window.ACE_RC_BANK = {"version":"2026-10-01","types":{"detail":"Detail","inference":"Inference","mainidea":"Main idea / purpose","function":"Function of a paragraph","except":"EXCEPT / NOT","tone":"Tone / attitude","application":"Application","vocab":"Vocabulary in context"},"passages":{"rc-o01":{"id":"rc-o01","title":"The Slow Work of Thin Air","topic":"high-altitude acclimatization","field":"physiology","source":"original","words":1370,"paragraphs":[["A traveler who arrives in La Paz, Bolivia, roughly 3,600 meters above sea level, is breathing air that still holds about 21 percent oxygen; what has changed is the pressure pushing those molecules into the lungs.","Barometric pressure falls with altitude, and because the partial pressure of a gas is its fraction times the total pressure, the oxygen available to the body falls with it.","At sea level the partial pressure of oxygen in dry air is about 160 millimeters of mercury; near the summit of Mount Everest, at nearly 8,850 meters, it is roughly one third of that.","The French physiologist Paul Bert proved this in 1878 with pressure chambers, showing that the misery of altitude came from a scarcity of oxygen molecules rather than from low pressure itself."],["The body cannot change the air, so it changes itself, and the adjustments it makes over hours, days, and weeks are called acclimatization.","The word is often confused with adaptation, and the difference matters.","Acclimatization is reversible and happens within one lifetime; adaptation is the work of natural selection across many generations and is written into a population's genes.","A climber from Chicago acclimatizes; a Tibetan herder is adapted."],["The first response arrives within minutes.","Small clusters of cells called the carotid bodies, lodged where each common carotid artery divides in the neck, monitor the oxygen in arterial blood.","When the partial pressure of oxygen drops, they fire more rapidly along the glossopharyngeal nerve to the brainstem, which answers by driving deeper and faster breathing.","Physiologists call this the hypoxic ventilatory response; it is why a newcomer feels breathless even while sitting still.","Extra ventilation pulls more fresh air into the alveoli and raises the oxygen pressure there, a real but partial repair."],["The repair has a cost.","Breathing hard blows off carbon dioxide faster than the tissues make it, and because carbon dioxide forms carbonic acid in blood, losing it leaves the blood more alkaline.","Chemoreceptors in the brainstem that respond to acidity sense this respiratory alkalosis and read it as a sign that breathing should slow.","Two signals now compete: the carotid bodies urging more breaths and the brainstem's acid sensors urging fewer.","In the first day or two the second signal acts as a brake, and ventilation rises less than the oxygen shortage alone would call for."],["The kidneys release the brake, but slowly.","Over the next several days they excrete bicarbonate, the main base in blood, into the urine, pulling the pH back toward normal.","As the alkalosis fades the brainstem stops objecting and ventilation climbs further, which is why the heaviest breathing often comes several days after arrival rather than on the first night.","The drug acetazolamide exploits this chemistry: developed as a diuretic, it blocks the enzyme carbonic anhydrase in the kidney, forcing bicarbonate out in the urine and producing a mild acidosis within hours.","The drug thus imitates several days of renal work: ventilation rises sooner and oxygen levels during sleep improve.","Its side effects are mostly harmless but memorable: tingling in the fingers and toes and a flat, metallic taste in carbonated drinks."],["Acclimatization matters because its absence hurts.","Acute mountain sickness, which commonly appears above about 2,500 meters in those who ascend too quickly, announces itself with a headache plus some mix of nausea, loss of appetite, fatigue, dizziness, and poor sleep.","Occasionally it progresses to fluid in the lungs or swelling of the brain, and both can kill.","The standard advice is therefore a schedule rather than a drug: above about 3,000 meters, raise the sleeping elevation by no more than 300 to 500 meters a day, rest every third or fourth day, and descend if symptoms worsen.","The schedule works because it gives the kidneys and the slower processes described below the time they need."],["A second adjustment takes shape inside the red cells, where hemoglobin's grip on oxygen is tuned by 2,3-bisphosphoglycerate, or 2,3-BPG, a small molecule the cells make as a side branch of their sugar metabolism.","When 2,3-BPG binds hemoglobin it loosens the grip, so oxygen unloads more readily in the tissues; on a graph of saturation against oxygen pressure, the curve shifts right.","Red cell 2,3-BPG rises within a day or two at altitude, and textbooks long presented this as a clean benefit.","The story is less tidy than that.","The alkalosis of early acclimatization shifts the same curve left, tightening the grip, and at extreme altitude the leftward pull wins.","That may be no accident: near a Himalayan summit the harder problem is loading oxygen in the lungs, not unloading it in the tissues, and a tighter grip helps with loading."],["The slowest adjustment is the growth of the red cell mass.","Specialized cells in the kidney sense low oxygen and release the hormone erythropoietin, or EPO, which tells the bone marrow to make more red cells.","EPO rises within hours of arrival and peaks within a day or two, then drifts back toward normal while the marrow keeps working; a measurable rise in circulating red cells takes weeks.","Travelers are told that their blood thickens within days, and in a sense it does, though not for the reason they imagine: plasma volume contracts early, so the same cells sit in less fluid.","The true expansion comes later and continues for months.","The oxygen-sensing switch behind EPO, a protein called hypoxia-inducible factor, earned William Kaelin, Peter Ratcliffe, and Gregg Semenza the 2019 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine."],["It is tempting to conclude that more red cells are simply better, and I think that is mistaken.","Each added cell carries oxygen, but it also thickens the blood, and thick blood moves sluggishly through small vessels and taxes the heart.","Past some point the slower flow cancels the extra carrying capacity, and oxygen delivery stops improving.","Native highlanders of the Andes have lived above 3,500 meters for thousands of years and carry noticeably more hemoglobin than sea-level populations.","A meaningful fraction develop chronic mountain sickness, first described in Peru in the 1920s by the physician Carlos Monge, in which the red cell count climbs so high that the blood becomes a burden.","Headaches, fatigue, bluish lips, and strain on the right side of the heart follow.","By a widely used definition, a hemoglobin concentration of 21 grams per deciliter or more in a man living at altitude marks the condition; a typical sea-level value is closer to 15."],["The Tibetan Plateau tells a different story at a similar height.","Tibetan highlanders above 4,000 meters tend to have hemoglobin concentrations close to sea-level values, well below those of Andeans at the same altitude, and chronic mountain sickness is comparatively rare among them.","They breathe more at rest and move more blood through their tissues, partly because they make more nitric oxide, a gas that relaxes vessel walls.","In 2010 several research groups reported that a variant of the gene EPAS1, which encodes one of the hypoxia-inducible factor proteins, is unusually common in Tibetans and linked to their lower hemoglobin.","Four years later a comparison of genomes showed that this stretch of DNA most likely entered the Tibetan lineage from Denisovans, an extinct group of archaic humans.","In effect, Tibetan bodies have turned down the very response that sea-level visitors turn up."],["The anthropologist Cynthia Beall, whose fieldwork spans both ranges, has called the two patterns distinct solutions to the same problem, and the contrast is instructive.","Andean physiology looks like a visitor's acclimatization carried on indefinitely: more red cells, then still more.","Tibetan physiology looks like something else, a settlement reached over a long time in which the body stops fighting the thin air and lives with it.","Which is better is not a question physiology alone can answer, and I am wary of any tidy verdict that one population won.","But the contrast should make us cautious about a popular assumption: that acclimatization is a short preview of what evolution would eventually deliver.","The visitor's body does what it can in the time it has, and some of what it does, pursued long enough, becomes a disease; the system was tuned for a journey of days and weeks, not for the generations of a home."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o01-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the amount of oxygen available to the body falls at high altitude because","choices":["the fraction of oxygen in the air drops as the air thins with elevation","total air pressure falls while the oxygen fraction stays the same","oxygen molecules are less able to cross into the blood in cold, dry air","low pressure acts on the body in ways unrelated to oxygen","the lungs take in less air with each breath at high elevation"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"the partial pressure of a gas is its fraction times the total pressure, the oxygen available to the body falls with it"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says the air still holds about 21 percent oxygen and that barometric pressure falls with altitude; since partial pressure is fraction times total pressure, it is the pressure, not the fraction, that drops.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: paragraph 1 says the fraction stays at about 21 percent.",null,"Outside the passage: nothing is said about cold air or the blood's ability to absorb oxygen.","This is the idea Paul Bert ruled out at the end of paragraph 1.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says breathing becomes deeper and faster, not shallower."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage credits Paul Bert with","choices":["discovering that the fraction of oxygen in air is about 21 percent","measuring the barometric pressure near the summit of Mount Everest","inventing the pressure chamber as a tool for studying altitude","showing that altitude sickness stems from too few oxygen molecules","proving that low air pressure itself, not oxygen, harms the body at elevation"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"the misery of altitude came from a scarcity of oxygen molecules rather than from low pressure itself"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says Bert used pressure chambers in 1878 to show that the misery of altitude came from a scarcity of oxygen molecules, not from low pressure itself.","distractorNotes":["Outside the passage: the 21 percent figure is stated as a fact, not as Bert's discovery.","Wrong detail: the Everest figure in paragraph 1 is not attributed to Bert.","Half right: Bert used pressure chambers, but the passage never says he invented them.",null,"Reversed: this is exactly the explanation Bert's work rejected."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author distinguishes acclimatization from adaptation in paragraph 2 primarily to","choices":["suggest that the two words are interchangeable in everyday speech","establish a contrast that the rest of the passage will develop","argue that a climber's adjustments are more impressive than a herder's","introduce the genetic evidence that will be presented in the following paragraph","explain why natural selection works faster at high altitude than at sea level"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"The word is often confused with adaptation, and the difference matters."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"acclimatization is a short preview of what evolution would eventually deliver"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says the difference matters, and the passage returns to that difference at the end, when it warns against treating acclimatization as a preview of evolution. The definition sets up the contrast the whole passage builds toward.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the author says the words are often confused, which is a reason to separate them.",null,"Outside the passage: the author never ranks the climber's adjustments against the herder's.","Wrong paragraph: the genetic evidence does not appear until paragraph 10, and paragraph 3 is about the carotid bodies.","Outside the passage: nothing is said about the speed of natural selection."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the carotid bodies","choices":["are located in the brainstem, where they regulate the depth of breathing","take several days to begin responding to a drop in oxygen pressure","release a hormone that signals the bone marrow to make more red cells","respond to a rise in the acidity of the blood by slowing the rate of breathing","sit where each common carotid artery divides and monitor arterial oxygen"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"lodged where each common carotid artery divides in the neck, monitor the oxygen in arterial blood"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 places the carotid bodies where each common carotid artery divides in the neck and says they monitor the oxygen in arterial blood.","distractorNotes":["Wrong location: paragraph 3 puts them in the neck and says they signal to the brainstem.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says the first response arrives within minutes.","Wrong organ: paragraph 8 says cells in the kidney release EPO.","Wrong sensor: paragraph 4 gives that job to chemoreceptors in the brainstem.",null]},{"id":"rc-o01-q05","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if a newcomer's kidneys could not excrete bicarbonate, the newcomer would most likely","choices":["continue to have ventilation held back by the alkaline brake","experience the heaviest breathing on the first night rather than later","develop a more severe metabolic acidosis than usual","stop breathing faster altogether within the first day or two of the stay","show a larger rise in 2,3-BPG inside the red cells"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"the second signal acts as a brake"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"The kidneys release the brake, but slowly."},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"As the alkalosis fades the brainstem stops objecting and ventilation climbs further"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says the kidneys release the brake by excreting bicarbonate, and only as the alkalosis fades does ventilation climb further. Take away the bicarbonate excretion and the alkalosis, and the brake, would remain.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: paragraph 5 says the heaviest breathing comes after the brake is released, not before.","Reversed: the problem in paragraph 4 is alkalosis, and keeping bicarbonate would keep the blood alkaline.","Too extreme: the carotid bodies still drive extra breathing; the brake only limits how much ventilation rises.","Unrelated: 2,3-BPG in paragraph 7 is not tied to the kidneys' handling of bicarbonate."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q06","type":"function","stem":"The author describes acetazolamide in paragraph 5 primarily to","choices":["show how a drug can hasten a correction the kidneys make on their own","argue that drugs are a poor substitute for a careful ascent schedule","warn readers that the drug's side effects outweigh its benefits","explain why diuretics were abandoned as a treatment for acute mountain sickness","introduce the role of carbonic acid in the respiratory alkalosis"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"The drug thus imitates several days of renal work"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says the drug exploits the same chemistry and imitates several days of renal work, so it is there as an example of speeding up what the kidneys already do.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong paragraph and too strong: paragraph 6 prefers a schedule, but paragraph 5 presents the drug favorably.","Reversed: paragraph 5 calls the side effects mostly harmless.","Outside the passage: the drug was developed as a diuretic, and nothing says diuretics were abandoned.","Wrong paragraph: carbonic acid is explained in paragraph 4, before the drug appears."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q07","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are named in the passage as features of acute mountain sickness EXCEPT:","choices":["headache","nausea","loss of appetite","dizziness","tingling"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"a headache","choice":0},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"loss of appetite","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"dizziness","choice":3},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"nausea","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":6,"quote":"tingling in the fingers and toes"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 lists headache, nausea, loss of appetite, fatigue, dizziness, and poor sleep. Tingling belongs to paragraph 5, where it is a side effect of acetazolamide.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 6 as the symptom that announces the illness.","Stated in paragraph 6 in the list of accompanying symptoms.","Stated in paragraph 6 in the list of accompanying symptoms.","Stated in paragraph 6 in the list of accompanying symptoms.",null]},{"id":"rc-o01-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the standard advice for climbers above about 3,000 meters includes","choices":["taking acetazolamide at the first sign that symptoms are worsening","descending by at least 500 meters after every third or fourth day","raising the sleeping elevation by no more than 300 to 500 meters a day","remaining at 2,500 meters until the heaviest breathing has passed","raising the sleeping elevation by about 300 meters every third or fourth day"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"raise the sleeping elevation by no more than 300 to 500 meters a day"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 spells out the schedule: no more than 300 to 500 meters of sleeping elevation a day, a rest every third or fourth day, and descent if symptoms worsen.","distractorNotes":["Mash-up: the advice is a schedule rather than a drug, and the response to worsening symptoms is descent.","Mash-up: the rest day comes every third or fourth day; no descent distance is given.",null,"Outside the passage: 2,500 meters is where mountain sickness commonly appears, not a holding point.","Mash-up: the limit is per day, and the third or fourth day is a rest day."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, when 2,3-BPG binds to hemoglobin, it","choices":["tightens hemoglobin's grip on oxygen and shifts the dissociation curve to the left","speeds the kidneys' excretion of bicarbonate into the urine","helps the lungs load oxygen more effectively near a Himalayan summit","loosens hemoglobin's grip so oxygen unloads more readily in the tissues","raises the partial pressure of oxygen inside the alveoli"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"it loosens the grip, so oxygen unloads more readily in the tissues"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says that when 2,3-BPG binds hemoglobin it loosens the grip, so oxygen unloads more readily in the tissues, and the curve shifts right.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: that is what the alkalosis does, not 2,3-BPG.","Wrong paragraph: bicarbonate excretion belongs to the kidneys in paragraph 5.","Reversed: paragraph 7 says a tighter grip helps with loading, and 2,3-BPG loosens it.",null,"Wrong paragraph: raising alveolar oxygen is the job of extra ventilation in paragraph 3."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q10","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that, near a Himalayan summit, a drug that shifted the oxygen curve further to the right would most likely","choices":["reverse the alkalosis that develops early in acclimatization","make it harder for the blood to pick up oxygen in the lungs","improve oxygen delivery by helping the tissues unload oxygen more readily","raise 2,3-BPG levels inside the red cells within a day or two","have no effect, because 2,3-BPG already dominates at extreme altitude"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":6,"quote":"the harder problem is loading oxygen in the lungs, not unloading it in the tissues, and a tighter grip helps with loading"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"it loosens the grip"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says a rightward shift means a looser grip, and that near a summit the harder problem is loading oxygen in the lungs, which a tighter grip helps. A further right shift would loosen the grip and work against loading.","distractorNotes":["Unrelated: the curve's position does not change blood pH; the passage runs the cause the other way.",null,"Half right: unloading would be easier, but paragraph 7 says unloading is not the harder problem at extreme altitude.","Reversed cause and effect: 2,3-BPG shifts the curve; the curve does not raise 2,3-BPG.","Contradicted: paragraph 7 says the leftward pull wins at extreme altitude."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q11","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the early thickening of a traveler's blood at altitude is mainly due to","choices":["a drop in plasma volume that packs the same cells into less fluid","the peak in erythropoietin, which arrives within a day or two of reaching altitude","a slowing of blood flow through the small vessels","a surge of new red cells released by the bone marrow","the rise in 2,3-BPG inside the circulating red cells"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"plasma volume contracts early, so the same cells sit in less fluid"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the blood thickens within days, but not for the reason travelers imagine: plasma volume contracts early, so the same cells sit in less fluid.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong cause: EPO does peak early, but paragraph 8 attributes the thickening to plasma volume.","Reversed: in paragraph 9, slower flow is a result of thick blood, not its cause.","Reversed: paragraph 8 says a measurable rise in circulating red cells takes weeks.","Wrong paragraph: 2,3-BPG in paragraph 7 changes hemoglobin's grip, not the blood's thickness."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q12","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a blood sample drawn three weeks after a traveler arrives at altitude would most likely show","choices":["a red cell count that has returned to its sea-level value","a red cell count that has reached its final size, since EPO has stopped","an EPO level below its early peak and a red cell count that has risen","an EPO level near its peak and a red cell count that has not yet changed","an EPO level higher than on the second day and about as many red cells"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"then drifts back toward normal while the marrow keeps working; a measurable rise in circulating red cells takes weeks"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"The true expansion comes later and continues for months."}],"why":"Paragraph 8 gives the timeline: EPO peaks within a day or two and drifts back toward normal, while a measurable rise in red cells takes weeks. At three weeks, EPO is past its peak and the count has started to climb.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: the red cell mass is growing at that point, not returning to baseline.","Too strong: paragraph 8 says the expansion continues for months.",null,"Wrong timing: EPO is past its peak by three weeks, and the count has had weeks to rise.","Reversed: EPO peaks within a day or two and then drifts back down."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the idea that more red cells are simply better is best described as","choices":["skeptical","resigned","enthusiastic","indifferent","bewildered"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"I think that is mistaken"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"Past some point the slower flow cancels the extra carrying capacity"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 opens by calling the idea tempting and then says plainly, I think that is mistaken, before explaining why thicker blood stops helping. That is a skeptic's voice, not a believer's.","distractorNotes":[null,"Wrong register: nothing in paragraph 9 suggests reluctant acceptance.","Reversed: the author rejects the idea rather than championing it.","Too weak: the author takes a clear position and argues for it.","Wrong register: the author explains the problem confidently, not in confusion."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q14","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the principle described in paragraph 9?","choices":["A farmer who plants more seeds in a field harvests more grain every year without limit","A library buys more books than its shelves can hold and stores the surplus in a basement","A runner who trains harder each week improves steadily until a sprained ankle forces a month of rest","A city adds buses to a route until the extra buses jam the streets and riders arrive later","A factory hires additional workers, and its output rises in direct proportion to the size of its staff"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"Past some point the slower flow cancels the extra carrying capacity, and oxygen delivery stops improving."},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"it also thickens the blood, and thick blood moves sluggishly"}],"why":"Paragraph 9's principle is that each added carrier also clogs the system, so past some point the extra capacity is cancelled by slower flow. Extra buses that jam the streets and slow riders down are the same pattern.","distractorNotes":["Contradicted: the principle is that benefit stops past some point, not that it grows without limit.","Wrong mechanism: the surplus is stored, not turned into a cost that cancels the benefit.","Wrong mechanism: the setback is a separate injury, not the added effort itself slowing the whole system.",null,"Contradicted: output rising in direct proportion is exactly what paragraph 9 says does not happen."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q15","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, each of the following is true of Tibetan highlanders EXCEPT:","choices":["They make more nitric oxide, which relaxes vessel walls.","Part of their DNA most likely came from Denisovans.","They carry far more hemoglobin than sea-level populations.","A variant of the gene EPAS1 is unusually common among them and linked to their hemoglobin.","They breathe more at rest."],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"They breathe more at rest","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"they make more nitric oxide, a gas that relaxes vessel walls","choice":0},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"is unusually common in Tibetans and linked to their lower hemoglobin","choice":3},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"most likely entered the Tibetan lineage from Denisovans","choice":1},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"hemoglobin concentrations close to sea-level values"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says Tibetan hemoglobin concentrations are close to sea-level values and well below those of Andeans. The other four statements all appear in paragraph 10.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 10, sentence 3 says they make more nitric oxide, a gas that relaxes vessel walls.","Paragraph 10, sentence 5 says this stretch of DNA most likely came from Denisovans.",null,"Paragraph 10, sentence 4 reports the EPAS1 variant and links it to their lower hemoglobin.","Paragraph 10, sentence 3 says this directly: they breathe more at rest."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q16","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 11, the word \"settlement\" most nearly means","choices":["the gradual sinking of material to the bottom of a liquid","an accommodation reached between two opposing pressures","a payment that ends a legal dispute between two parties","a final decision imposed from outside by an authority","a village founded by newcomers in a remote place"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"a settlement reached over a long time in which the body stops fighting the thin air and lives with it"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 describes Tibetan physiology as a settlement in which the body stops fighting the thin air and lives with it: an accommodation between the body and its surroundings, not a place or a payment.","distractorNotes":["Settling of particles is a lab meaning of the word that has nothing to do with this sentence.",null,"A legal settlement is another everyday meaning; nothing in the sentence involves a dispute between parties or money.","Nothing is imposed from outside; the body reaches the arrangement itself, over a long time.","This is the common sense of the word, and the Tibetans do live in villages, but the sentence describes a state of the body, not a place."]},{"id":"rc-o01-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Producing more red blood cells is the single most important change that lets a person survive at high altitude","Chronic mountain sickness shows that people who live high in the Andes are poorly suited to their home and should move lower","Climbers should take acetazolamide before every ascent because the kidneys correct the blood's chemistry too slowly","Tibetan highlanders prove that natural selection always finds the same solution that acclimatization reaches","The body's short-term answers to thin air are real but limited, and they differ from long-term genetic adaptation"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"Acclimatization is reversible and happens within one lifetime; adaptation is the work of natural selection"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"that acclimatization is a short preview of what evolution would eventually deliver"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":6,"quote":"the system was tuned for a journey of days and weeks, not for the generations of a home"}],"why":"The passage walks through the body's responses to thin air over hours, days and weeks, warns that more red cells is not simply better, and closes by separating acclimatization from adaptation. Only the answer covers both halves.","distractorNotes":["The author is openly skeptical that more red cells is simply better; that is the point of paragraph 9.","Too extreme and never said: the author refuses any tidy verdict about which population did better.","Acetazolamide is one detail in the passage, and the passage never says every climber should take it.","Reversed: paragraph 10 says Tibetans turned down the very response visitors turn up, a different solution.",null]}]},"rc-o02":{"id":"rc-o02","title":"The Chain and Its Weakest Link","topic":"trophic cascades and the difficulty of proving them","field":"ecology","source":"original","words":1356,"paragraphs":[["Remove a predator from a community and the effects should ripple down through everything it ate and everything its prey ate.","Ecologists call this a trophic cascade: a top predator's influence tumbles down through two or more feeding levels, the sign of the effect flipping at each step.","More predators mean fewer grazers, and fewer grazers mean more plants.","It is one of ecology's most satisfying ideas, simple to state and, in a few famous cases, demonstrated with something close to proof.","Yet the cleaner a cascade story sounds, the more tempting it is to tell it without checking whether every link actually held."],["The first of those cases began in 1963 on a rocky shore at Mukkaw Bay, on the outer coast of Washington's Olympic Peninsula.","Robert Paine, a zoologist at the University of Washington, began removing every ochre sea star, Pisaster ochraceus, from a section of intertidal rock, prying them loose and flinging them into deeper water.","A neighboring stretch of shore, left alone, served as the control.","The sea star is the main predator of the California mussel, and without it the mussels spread downward across rock that had held barnacles, limpets, chitons, and algae.","Within a few years the mussels had crowded most of these out, and the species count on the cleared plot fell from about fifteen to eight.","Paine published the result in 1966."],["In 1969 Paine gave the pattern a name that has stuck: the keystone species.","The metaphor comes from the wedge-shaped block at the top of an arch, small relative to the whole but holding every other stone in place.","A keystone predator is one whose effect on its community is far out of proportion to its abundance.","The sea stars were never numerous, but their steady predation arrested the mussels' advance and kept space open for everything else.","The method mattered more than the metaphor: Paine had not merely observed a correlation; he had removed the predator, watched the response, and compared it against an untouched plot a few meters away.","In 1980 Paine also supplied the phrase \"trophic cascade\" itself."],["The second case came from the Aleutian Islands of Alaska, where James Estes began studying sea otters in the early 1970s.","The fur trade had hunted otters nearly to extinction, and when an international treaty protected them in 1911 only scattered remnant colonies survived.","By the 1970s the otters had recovered around some islands but not others, which gave Estes a natural comparison.","Around islands with otters, the sea floor was a forest of kelp.","Around islands without them, the kelp was gone and the rock was carpeted with sea urchins, a condition divers call an urchin barren.","Sea otters eat urchins, urchins eat kelp, and so a predator two links away determines whether a kelp forest exists at all.","Estes and a colleague published the comparison in Science in 1974."],["The otter story later acquired a fourth link nobody had planned for.","In the 1990s, otter numbers around the western Aleutians collapsed, and in 1998 Estes and his colleagues reported that killer whales had apparently begun eating otters, possibly because their usual prey of seals and sea lions had declined.","As the otters vanished, urchins returned and the kelp thinned again.","This is perhaps the most instructive part of the story: a cascade is not a closed loop but an open chain, and a disturbance anywhere along it can be felt on the rocks near shore."],["No cascade story is told more often than the Yellowstone wolf story, and none is harder to pin down.","Gray wolves had been eliminated from Yellowstone National Park by the 1920s.","In 1995 biologists released fourteen wolves captured in Alberta, and in 1996 they added seventeen more from British Columbia, for a total of thirty-one founders.","By then the northern range elk herd numbered nearly twenty thousand, and the willows, aspen, and cottonwoods along the streams had been browsed to stubs for decades."],["Within a few years, William Ripple and Robert Beschta of Oregon State University reported that young willows and aspen in parts of the northern range were taller than they had been in decades.","Their explanation had two parts.","First, wolves were killing elk, so there were fewer mouths to browse the shoots.","Second, the surviving elk were afraid, avoiding streamside thickets and valley bottoms where wolves could ambush them, so plants in those risky places got a reprieve.","From there the story grew: taller willows would bring back beavers, beaver dams would raise water tables and create wetlands, songbirds would return, and stabilized banks would change the shape of the rivers.","In 2014 a short video titled \"How Wolves Change Rivers,\" narrated by the British writer George Monbiot, carried this version to tens of millions of viewers."],["Almost every piece of that account has since been contested, and the contest is where the lessons lie.","Elk numbers did fall, from nearly twenty thousand in the mid-1990s to a few thousand by the 2010s.","But wolves were far from the only new pressure.","Hunters in Montana took more than a thousand elk in some winters as the herd migrated north out of the park.","Grizzly bears and black bears, both increasing, took a large share of newborn calves each spring.","Cougars had quietly recolonized the park in the 1980s.","A severe drought in the early 2000s reduced the forage elk depend on.","Sorting the wolves' share out of that list is an accounting problem no one has fully solved."],["The second half of the hypothesis, the idea that fear alone reshaped elk behavior, has fared worse.","The phrase \"landscape of fear\" was introduced in 2001 to describe how Yellowstone's elk and bison might read the terrain for danger, and it was quickly adopted as the mechanism behind the plant recovery.","But when Matthew Kauffman and colleagues tested it in 2010, aspen stands where elk faced the highest risk from wolves were recovering no better than stands in safer places.","Tracking-collar studies found that an individual elk met wolves only occasionally and went back to feeding soon afterward, hardly the behavior of an animal too frightened to eat."],["Then there is the matter of water, which the tidy version leaves out entirely.","Willows along Yellowstone's small streams need a high water table, and the beavers whose dams once maintained it had been nearly absent for decades, partly because there were no tall willows for them to eat and build with.","A decade-long experiment by Colorado State University researchers fenced willow plots to keep elk out and, in some, built small dams to mimic the work of beavers.","Willows protected from browsing but left with a low water table did not reach the heights of the past, while those given both protection and water did.","Removing the browsers is not enough: the willows need the beavers and the beavers need the willows, a loop that wolves alone cannot restart."],["The claim that wolves changed the course of rivers is the boldest in the chain and rests on the thinnest evidence.","Stream channels were cut deep by decades of erosion while the willows were gone, and channel form responds to vegetation over spans far longer than the twenty years since the wolves returned.","The change in the rivers that the video describes remains a projection more than a measured result."],["None of this means the wolves did nothing.","Elk are fewer, some willow stands have recovered, and biologists still argue over how much credit the wolves deserve, which is exactly how a live scientific question should look.","What separates Yellowstone from Mukkaw Bay or the Aleutians is not the strength of the idea but the shape of the evidence.","Paine had a control plot, and Estes had islands with and without otters.","Yellowstone is a single reintroduction in a single park, with bears, cougars, hunters, drought, and beavers all changing at once, and nothing to compare it against.","The trophic cascade remains one of ecology's best ideas, and the honest way to admire it is to keep asking, at every link, whether the chain was tested or merely assumed."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o02-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, what happened on the plot at Mukkaw Bay after the sea stars were removed?","choices":["Barnacles replaced the mussels as the dominant animal on the rock","The number of species fell from about fifteen to eight","Kelp spread across rock that the mussels had once held","Sea urchins carpeted the rock once the sea stars were gone","The number of species roughly doubled as the mussels spread"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"the species count on the cleared plot fell from about fifteen to eight"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says that once the mussels crowded the other species out, the species count on the cleared plot fell from about fifteen to eight.","distractorNotes":["Reversed: the mussels crowded out the barnacles, not the other way around.",null,"Kelp belongs to the Aleutian otter story in paragraph 4, not to Mukkaw Bay.","Urchin barrens are the Aleutian result in paragraph 4; at Mukkaw Bay the mussels took over.","Reversed: diversity fell by about half, it did not double."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q02","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 3, the word 'arrested' most nearly means","choices":["detained","captured","halted","attracted","punished"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"their steady predation arrested the mussels' advance"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the predation 'arrested the mussels' advance and kept space open,' so the sea stars were stopping the mussels from spreading. The police meaning of the word is the trap.","distractorNotes":["The police sense of the word; sea stars do not detain mussels.","Sea stars do eat mussels, but the sentence is about stopping the mussels' advance, not seizing individuals.",null,"Opposite in spirit; the predation held the mussels back rather than drawing them in.","Imports a moral sense the sentence does not carry."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions 'an untouched plot a few meters away' in paragraph 3 primarily to","choices":["show how small the area Paine studied actually was","explain why the mussels spread downward across the rock","suggest that the sea stars soon recolonized the cleared rock","stress that Paine's result rested on an experiment with a control","argue that sea star removal had effects well beyond the boundaries of the cleared plot"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"compared it against an untouched plot a few meters away"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the method mattered more than the metaphor: Paine had not merely observed a correlation; he removed the predator and compared the result against an untouched plot. The untouched plot is the control, and that is the point being made.","distractorNotes":["The distance is not there to describe the study's size; the sentence is about method.","The mussels' spread is explained in paragraph 2 by the missing predator, not by the control plot.","The passage never says the sea stars recolonized the plot.",null,"Nothing in the passage describes effects spilling outside the cleared plot."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, which of the following characterized Aleutian islands that lacked sea otters?","choices":["Rock carpeted with sea urchins and stripped of kelp","Kelp forests that had grown unusually dense in the otters' absence","Remnant otter colonies protected by the 1911 treaty","Killer whales feeding on seals and sea lions","Mussel beds that crowded out other species"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"the kelp was gone and the rock was carpeted with sea urchins"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says that around islands without otters 'the kelp was gone and the rock was carpeted with sea urchins,' which divers call an urchin barren.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: kelp forests were found around islands with otters, not without them.","Remnant colonies are where otters survived; the question asks about islands without them.","Killer whales enter in paragraph 5, and their switch to otters is a 1990s event, not a feature of otter-free islands in the 1970s.","Mussel beds are the Mukkaw Bay result in paragraph 2."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q05","type":"inference","stem":"The passage implies that Estes's study differed from Paine's in that Estes","choices":["removed the predator from the study area himself","worked without any site to compare against","studied a predator that was more numerous than the sea star","published his results before the term keystone species existed","relied on a comparison he had not set up himself"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"which gave Estes a natural comparison"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"Paine had a control plot, and Estes had islands with and without otters"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says uneven otter recovery 'gave Estes a natural comparison,' and paragraph 12 contrasts Paine's control plot with Estes's islands. Paine created his comparison; nature handed Estes his.","distractorNotes":["That describes Paine, who pried the sea stars loose by hand.","The islands with and without otters were his comparison.","The passage says nothing about how numerous otters were relative to sea stars.","Keystone species was named in 1969; Estes published in 1974.",null]},{"id":"rc-o02-q06","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Estes and his colleagues reported in 1998 that","choices":["sea otters had been protected by a new international treaty","urchin barrens around the western Aleutians had turned back into kelp forest","killer whales had apparently begun to eat sea otters","seals and sea lions had recovered around the western Aleutians","sea otters had spread to the shore at Mukkaw Bay"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"killer whales had apparently begun eating otters"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says the 1998 report was that 'killer whales had apparently begun eating otters,' possibly because seals and sea lions had declined.","distractorNotes":["The treaty dates to 1911, in paragraph 4.","Reversed: as otters vanished, urchins returned and the kelp thinned.",null,"Reversed: the passage suggests seals and sea lions had declined, not recovered.","Mukkaw Bay is Paine's sea star site; otters are never placed there."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q07","type":"function","stem":"The author describes the killer whale episode in paragraph 5 primarily to","choices":["show that the sea otter cascade had been mistaken all along","explain why the fur trade nearly drove sea otters to extinction","argue that killer whales deserve the label keystone species","show that a cascade is an open chain, not a closed loop","demonstrate that kelp forests recover quickly once urchin numbers fall"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"a cascade is not a closed loop but an open chain"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 calls the episode 'the most instructive part of the story' because it shows 'a cascade is not a closed loop but an open chain' that a disturbance from elsewhere can reach.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: the episode extends the cascade by a link; it does not overturn it.","The fur trade is in paragraph 4 and has nothing to do with killer whales.","The passage never applies the keystone label to killer whales.",null,"Reversed: when the otters vanished, the kelp thinned."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, how many wolves in total were released in Yellowstone in 1995 and 1996?","choices":["fourteen","seventeen","twenty","thirty-one","nearly twenty thousand"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"for a total of thirty-one founders"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 gives fourteen wolves in 1995 and seventeen in 1996, 'for a total of thirty-one founders.'","distractorNotes":["Fourteen is the 1995 release alone.","Seventeen is the 1996 release alone.","Twenty does not appear in the passage; it is a rough mental sum that misses by eleven.",null,"Nearly twenty thousand is the size of the elk herd, not the number of wolves."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Ripple and Beschta's explanation for the taller willows and aspen had which two parts?","choices":["Fewer elk overall, and surviving elk that avoided risky places","Drought on the northern range and the return of beavers to its streams","Human hunting north of the park and rising bear predation on calves","Higher water tables and fewer cougars in the valley bottoms","Beaver dams and the return of songbirds to the thickets"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"wolves were killing elk, so there were fewer mouths to browse the shoots"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"the surviving elk were afraid, avoiding streamside thickets and valley bottoms"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 lays out the two parts: wolves killed elk so there were fewer mouths to browse, and the surviving elk were afraid and avoided streamside thickets and valley bottoms.","distractorNotes":[null,"Drought and beavers belong to the critics' side in paragraphs 8 and 10, not to Ripple and Beschta's explanation.","Hunting and bears are alternative pressures from paragraph 8, raised by critics, not by Ripple and Beschta.","Water tables are part of the Colorado State experiment in paragraph 10; cougars appear in paragraph 8.","These are downstream predictions in the extended story, not the two-part explanation itself."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q10","type":"except","stem":"According to paragraph 7, the extended version of the Yellowstone story predicted all of the following EXCEPT","choices":["the return of beavers to the streams","the return of songbirds","stabilized stream banks","rivers changing shape","a decline in grizzly bears"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"taller willows would bring back beavers","choice":0},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"songbirds would return","choice":1},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"stabilized banks","choice":2},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"change the shape of the rivers","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 7 lists beavers, wetlands, songbirds, stabilized banks, and reshaped rivers. Bears appear only in paragraph 8, and there they are increasing, not declining.","distractorNotes":["Stated: 'taller willows would bring back beavers.'","Stated: 'songbirds would return.'","Stated: 'stabilized banks would change the shape of the rivers.'","Stated: the banks 'would change the shape of the rivers.'",null]},{"id":"rc-o02-q11","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the popular version of the Yellowstone story is best described as","choices":["admiring","skeptical","indifferent","hostile","bewildered"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"which the tidy version leaves out entirely"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"the boldest in the chain and rests on the thinnest evidence"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"without checking whether every link actually held"}],"why":"The judging words are 'tidy version,' 'boldest in the chain,' 'thinnest evidence,' and 'a projection more than a measured result.' That is doubt with reasons, not dismissal.","distractorNotes":["The author admires the cascade idea and Paine's method, not the popular Yellowstone retelling.",null,"An indifferent writer would not spend five paragraphs testing the story link by link.","Too strong: the author says 'none of this means the wolves did nothing.'","The author is not confused; the critique is organized and specific."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q12","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are mentioned in the passage as pressures on Yellowstone's elk other than wolves EXCEPT","choices":["hunting by people north of the park in Montana","predation on newborn calves by bears","the recolonization of the park by cougars","a severe drought in the early 2000s","disease spreading from domestic cattle"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"Hunters in Montana took more than a thousand elk","choice":0},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"took a large share of newborn calves","choice":1},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"Cougars had quietly recolonized the park","choice":2},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":7,"quote":"A severe drought in the early 2000s","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 8 lists hunters, bears, cougars, and drought. Disease never appears anywhere in the passage.","distractorNotes":["Stated: hunters in Montana took more than a thousand elk in some winters.","Stated: grizzly and black bears took a large share of newborn calves.","Stated: cougars had recolonized the park in the 1980s.","Stated: a severe drought in the early 2000s reduced forage.",null]},{"id":"rc-o02-q13","type":"inference","stem":"The passage implies that if the 'landscape of fear' had been the main driver of plant recovery, then","choices":["aspen in risky places should have outgrown aspen in safe ones","elk numbers would have needed to fall below a few thousand","beavers would have returned before the willows grew tall","the wolves would have had to come from Alberta rather than British Columbia","the drought of the early 2000s would have had no effect on elk"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"aspen stands where elk faced the highest risk from wolves were recovering no better than stands in safer places"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 treats Kauffman's finding, that high-risk aspen stands were 'recovering no better than stands in safer places,' as evidence against the fear hypothesis. That only counts against it if the hypothesis predicted the risky stands would do better.","distractorNotes":[null,"The fear hypothesis is about behavior, not a threshold in elk numbers.","The passage says beavers need tall willows first, the opposite order.","Where the wolves came from has no bearing on the fear mechanism.","Nothing links the fear hypothesis to drought having no effect."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q14","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the Colorado State University experiment found that willows grew to the heights of the past only when they were","choices":["left unfenced but given a raised water table","fenced and also given a raised water table","fenced after live beavers had been returned to the streams","browsed lightly by elk during the winter months","fenced from elk but left with a low water table"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"those given both protection and water did"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says willows protected from browsing but left with a low water table did not reach the heights of the past, 'while those given both protection and water did.'","distractorNotes":["Half right: water alone is not what the passage credits; the successful plots had fences as well.",null,"The experiment used small built dams to mimic beavers, not live beavers.","Light browsing is never mentioned; the fences kept elk out.","This is the group that did not reach the past heights."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q15","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that the claim about rivers is weak chiefly because","choices":["the rivers in the northern range have changed in the opposite direction","beavers have never returned to any Yellowstone stream","stream channels respond more slowly than twenty years","the video that popularized it was narrated by a writer rather than a scientist","elk never browsed close enough to the rivers to affect them"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"channel form responds to vegetation over spans far longer than the twenty years since the wolves returned"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says channel form 'responds to vegetation over spans far longer than the twenty years since the wolves returned,' which is why the river change is called a projection rather than a measured result.","distractorNotes":["The passage says the change is unmeasured, not that it ran the other way.","Too strong and unsupported: the passage never says beavers are absent from every stream.",null,"The author never attacks Monbiot's credentials; the objection is about evidence and time.","Reversed: the passage says willows along the streams had been browsed to stubs for decades."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q16","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the kind of evidence the author says Yellowstone lacks?","choices":["Removing a fish from some ponds and leaving matched ponds untouched","Noting that deer declined in a park during the decade after coyotes arrived there","Filming a documentary about how sharks protect seagrass meadows","Counting the birds in a single forest over one summer","Building a computer model of plant growth after predators return"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"Paine had a control plot, and Estes had islands with and without otters"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"nothing to compare it against"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 says what Yellowstone lacks is a comparison: Paine had a control plot and Estes had islands with and without otters. Ponds with the predator removed beside matched untouched ponds is exactly that design.","distractorNotes":[null,"This is the Yellowstone problem itself: a single before-and-after with nothing to compare it against.","A documentary is a retelling, the very thing the author warns can outrun the evidence.","One forest, one summer, and no manipulation gives nothing to compare.","A model predicts; the author wants a tested comparison, not a projection."]},{"id":"rc-o02-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Wolves restored Yellowstone's willows, aspen, beavers, and rivers, confirming the cascade that Paine and Estes first described","The experiments at Mukkaw Bay and in the Aleutians showed that predators matter less than ecologists once believed","The cascade idea is sound, but Yellowstone's popular version outruns its evidence","Ecologists should abandon the term keystone species because the public misunderstands it","Elk declines in Yellowstone were caused entirely by hunters, bears, and drought rather than by wolves"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"the cleaner a cascade story sounds, the more tempting it is to tell it without checking whether every link actually held"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"not the strength of the idea but the shape of the evidence"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":6,"quote":"whether the chain was tested or merely assumed"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 sets the thesis, that clean cascade stories tempt us to skip checking the links, and paragraph 12 closes it: the idea is strong, Yellowstone's evidence is not shaped like Paine's or Estes's, and the honest way to admire the idea is to keep testing each link.","distractorNotes":["This is the popular version the passage spends five paragraphs contesting.","Reversed: both experiments showed that predators matter enormously.",null,"The author never argues against the term; it is presented approvingly.","Too strong: paragraph 12 says the wolves did not do nothing, and sorting out their share is unsolved."]}]},"rc-o03":{"id":"rc-o03","title":"Insulin and the Quarrel over Credit","topic":"the discovery of insulin and the dispute over credit","field":"history of science","source":"original","words":1385,"paragraphs":[["When news of the 1923 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine reached Toronto, one of the two winners reacted not with joy but with anger.","Frederick Banting learned that he would share the prize with John James Rickard Macleod, the professor of physiology in whose Toronto department the work had been done.","Banting considered refusing it.","Instead he announced that he would split his prize money with Charles Best, the student who had worked beside him, and Macleod responded by splitting his with James Collip, the biochemist who had purified the extract.","The record supports a less dramatic conclusion than the legend: insulin was the product of a team, and the instruments we use to assign credit were never built to describe one."],["The trail begins in 1869, when Paul Langerhans, a medical student in Berlin, described small clusters of cells scattered through the pancreas that looked nothing like the surrounding tissue, which makes digestive juice for the gut.","He did not know what the clusters did, and he did not guess.","In 1889, at Strasbourg, Oskar Minkowski and Joseph von Mering removed the pancreas from a dog and found that the animal developed every sign of diabetes, including urine so rich in sugar that it drew flies.","In 1893 the French histologist Edouard Laguesse named the clusters the islets of Langerhans and proposed that they released something directly into the blood, an internal secretion in the language of the day."],["A name came before a substance: the Belgian Jean de Meyer proposed insuline in 1909, and the English physiologist Edward Sharpey-Schafer proposed it again in 1916, both from the Latin insula, meaning island.","Extracting the hormone was harder than naming it, because injected pancreas extracts often caused fever, abscesses, and toxic reactions.","Georg Zuelzer in Berlin treated diabetic patients with a pancreatic extract around 1908 and saw their sugar fall before side effects forced him to stop.","Ernest Scott in Chicago in 1912 and Israel Kleiner in New York in 1919 each lowered the blood sugar of depancreatized dogs with extracts, and each stopped short of treating a patient.","Nicolae Paulescu in Bucharest reported in 1921 that his extract lowered the blood sugar of diabetic dogs, publishing in French months before the Toronto results appeared.","It is sobering that the substance sat in impure form in several laboratories for more than a decade before anyone could keep a patient alive with it."],["In the autumn of 1920 Banting was a struggling surgeon in London, Ontario, preparing a lecture on the pancreas, when he read an article about a patient whose pancreatic duct had been blocked by a stone.","In such cases the enzyme-producing tissue of the gland withers while the islets survive.","Early on October 31 he wrote himself a note: tie off the pancreatic ducts of dogs, wait for the digestive tissue to degenerate, and extract whatever the surviving islets contained.","His reasoning was that earlier extracts had failed because the gland's own digestive enzymes destroyed the hormone during preparation.","He took the idea to Macleod, an authority on carbohydrate metabolism, who was skeptical but agreed to provide laboratory space, ten dogs, and a student assistant for the summer of 1921."],["The assistant was Charles Best, a twenty-two-year-old with a fresh degree in physiology and biochemistry who could measure blood sugar in small samples, a skill Banting lacked.","The two began on May 17, 1921; Macleod left for Scotland in June, an absence Banting never let him forget.","On July 30, 1921, an extract of duct-ligated pancreas was injected into a depancreatized dog, and its blood sugar fell.","Banting and Best called the material isletin.","Over the following months they found that extracts of fetal calf pancreas worked, and then that whole adult beef pancreas soaked in alcohol worked too; it was the alcohol method that made the slow business of duct ligation unnecessary.","Banting's hypothesis had been the doorway rather than the destination; the method that finally mattered was close to what Scott had tried a decade earlier."],["When Macleod returned in September he pressed for better controls and repeated experiments, advice that Banting took as interference.","In December Macleod brought in Collip, a biochemist from the University of Alberta on sabbatical in Toronto.","Collip's contribution was purification.","By precipitating the extract in rising concentrations of alcohol he found that the active substance came out of solution near ninety percent while most contaminating protein stayed in solution.","He also showed that the extract made the liver store glycogen and that an overdose in rabbits produced convulsions that a dose of sugar could reverse, an early description of what would be called insulin shock."],["The first patient was Leonard Thompson, a fourteen-year-old boy who weighed about sixty-five pounds and was dying of diabetes in Toronto General Hospital.","On January 11, 1922, he received an injection of the Banting and Best extract.","His blood sugar fell somewhat, but sugar remained in his urine and a sterile abscess formed at the injection site; by any honest reading, the first trial failed.","On January 23 he received Collip's purified extract.","His blood sugar dropped sharply, the sugar in his urine nearly vanished, and the ketones that mark a body consuming its own fat disappeared.","Thompson lived another thirteen years."],["Success did not bring peace.","Within days of the second injection Collip told Banting and Best that he would not share his purification method, and an argument followed that, by the participants' later accounts, turned physical.","On January 25 the four signed an agreement to work together under Macleod's direction and to seek no patents independently.","Then the method failed: when Collip tried to make larger batches the extract lost its potency, and for weeks no one in Toronto could produce usable insulin.","Best recovered a working process in May.","That same month the university reached an agreement with Eli Lilly and Company of Indianapolis to produce insulin at scale."],["The patent question showed the group at its best.","Banting at first refused to put his name on any patent, holding that a physician should not profit from a remedy, so Best and Collip filed the application and his name was added later.","In 1923 the three assigned the United States patent to the Board of Governors of the University of Toronto for one dollar each.","The purpose was not income but control: the university could license manufacturers, require that every batch meet a standard of potency and purity, and keep any one company from cornering a drug that patients would need every day for life.","Whatever one makes of the men's quarrels, this decision was made with patients in view."],["The Nobel committee acted quickly, partly because the Danish physiologist August Krogh, himself a laureate, visited Toronto in late 1922, carried the method home, and nominated Banting and Macleod.","The prize was announced in October 1923.","Banting, who turned thirty-two that November, remains the youngest laureate in that category and was the first Canadian to win a Nobel Prize.","None of that softened his conviction that Macleod had taken credit for his idea, nor his insistence that Best belonged on the citation.","Macleod regarded the award as recognition of a department's achievement and quietly shared his money with the man who had made the extract safe to inject.","The two laureates never reconciled, and Macleod left Toronto for Scotland in 1928."],["What, then, was discovered, and by whom?","Banting supplied the idea and the stubbornness to test it, Best the daily laboratory work, Collip the purification that turned a toxic soup into a medicine, and Macleod the laboratory, the discipline, and the standing that got the work heard.","The historian Michael Bliss, whose 1982 study remains the standard account, reached much the same verdict from the notebooks and was not gentle with the summer experiments, which contain errors a more experienced investigator would have caught.","Yet the error-prone summer led somewhere, and the careful investigators before it had not.","Prizes honor names, and history remembers names; the discovery itself was made by a laboratory.","That is less a criticism of the Nobel committee than a limit of its instrument, and the Toronto quarrel is best read not as a contest with a rightful winner but as four men learning that a shared achievement cannot be divided."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o03-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, after Banting announced that he would split his prize money with Best, Macleod","choices":["refused any share of the prize","divided his own share of the prize money with Best","split his share of the money with Collip","briefly considered declining the award","publicly disputed Banting's claim to the discovery"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"Macleod responded by splitting his with James Collip"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says Macleod responded by splitting his prize money with James Collip, the biochemist who had purified the extract.","distractorNotes":["Macleod accepted the prize; nothing in the passage says he refused a share.","Best received money from Banting, not from Macleod.",null,"It was Banting who considered refusing the prize, not Macleod.","The passage never says Macleod publicly disputed Banting's claim."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q02","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the 1889 experiment of Minkowski and von Mering showed that","choices":["a dog without its pancreas developed diabetes","the islets release an internal secretion","the pancreas makes digestive juice","sugar in the urine could be reversed by a pancreatic extract","a stone blocking the pancreatic duct destroys the islets"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"removed the pancreas from a dog and found that the animal developed every sign of diabetes"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says that at Strasbourg in 1889 Minkowski and von Mering removed a dog's pancreas and the animal developed every sign of diabetes.","distractorNotes":[null,"The islet idea was Laguesse's proposal in 1893, not a result of the 1889 experiment.","Digestive juice is mentioned in describing Langerhans's work, not as a finding of this experiment.","No extract was given in 1889; the experiment removed the organ and watched diabetes appear.","The duct stone appears in paragraph 4, and there the islets survive rather than being destroyed."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q03","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions the flies in paragraph 2 primarily to","choices":["explain Laguesse's internal secretion idea","suggest the laboratory was poorly kept","show that Langerhans's clusters had a digestive function","make vivid how much sugar the dog's urine carried","contrast the dog's condition with that of Leonard Thompson"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"including urine so rich in sugar that it drew flies"}],"why":"The flies are a concrete picture of the sign named in the same sentence: urine so rich in sugar that it drew flies. The detail makes the dog's diabetes visible.","distractorNotes":["Laguesse's 1893 proposal is a separate sentence and is not tied to the flies.","The passage says nothing about the state of the laboratory.","The clusters are set apart from the digestive tissue, and the flies illustrate sugar loss, not digestion.",null,"Thompson appears in paragraph 7 and is never compared with the dog."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q04","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are named in the passage as investigators whose pancreatic extracts lowered sugar before 1922 EXCEPT:","choices":["Georg Zuelzer","Ernest Scott","Israel Kleiner","Nicolae Paulescu","Paul Langerhans"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"Georg Zuelzer in Berlin treated diabetic patients with a pancreatic extract around 1908 and saw their sugar fall","choice":0},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"Ernest Scott in Chicago in 1912 and Israel Kleiner in New York in 1919 each lowered the blood sugar of depancreatized dogs with extracts","choice":1},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"Israel Kleiner in New York in 1919 each lowered the blood sugar of depancreatized dogs with extracts","choice":2},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"Nicolae Paulescu in Bucharest reported in 1921 that his extract lowered the blood sugar of diabetic dogs","choice":3},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"He did not know what the clusters did, and he did not guess.","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 3 confirms Zuelzer, Scott, Kleiner, and Paulescu one by one. Langerhans, in paragraph 2, described the clusters in 1869 and did not even guess what they did; he made no extract.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 says Zuelzer treated patients with an extract and saw their sugar fall.","Paragraph 3 says Scott lowered the blood sugar of depancreatized dogs in 1912.","Paragraph 3 says Kleiner did the same in New York in 1919.","Paragraph 3 says Paulescu reported his extract lowered blood sugar in diabetic dogs in 1921.",null]},{"id":"rc-o03-q05","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that, by 1921, the chief obstacle to treating diabetes with a pancreatic extract was","choices":["ignorance of which organ controlled blood sugar","the impurity of the extracts rather than their absence","the lack of a name for the hormone being sought","the refusal of physicians to test extracts on patients","the absence of a reliable way to make a diabetic animal"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"injected pancreas extracts often caused fever, abscesses, and toxic reactions"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":6,"quote":"the substance sat in impure form in several laboratories for more than a decade"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the extracts caused fever, abscesses, and toxic reactions, and the author adds that the substance sat in impure form in several laboratories for more than a decade. The problem was purity, not existence.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 2 says the 1889 experiment had already tied the pancreas to diabetes.",null,"The name insuline existed by 1909, before the substance was isolated.","Zuelzer did treat patients; what stopped him was side effects, not refusal.","Removing the pancreas was already a known way to produce diabetes in a dog."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q06","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Banting proposed tying off the pancreatic ducts of dogs because he believed that","choices":["the islets would multiply once the ducts closed","Macleod had suggested the procedure","stones in the pancreatic duct were the usual cause of diabetes in people","the gland's own enzymes were destroying the hormone","duct ligation was the only way to obtain a usable extract"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"earlier extracts had failed because the gland's own digestive enzymes destroyed the hormone during preparation"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 gives his reasoning directly: earlier extracts had failed because the gland's own digestive enzymes destroyed the hormone during preparation, so letting that tissue degenerate first should protect it.","distractorNotes":["The passage says the islets survive when the duct is blocked, not that they multiply.","Macleod was skeptical of the idea; it was Banting's, not his.","The stone case was the article that gave Banting the idea; the passage never calls stones the usual cause of diabetes.",null,"Paragraph 5 says duct ligation turned out to be unnecessary, so it was not the only way."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q07","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, which finding made duct ligation unnecessary?","choices":["Whole adult beef pancreas soaked in alcohol gave a working extract","Extracts of fetal calf pancreas lowered blood sugar in dogs","Collip's alcohol precipitation removed the contaminating protein","Blood sugar could be measured in small samples","Macleod's demand for repeated experiments confirmed the summer results"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"it was the alcohol method that made the slow business of duct ligation unnecessary"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says whole adult beef pancreas soaked in alcohol worked, and that it was the alcohol method that made duct ligation unnecessary.","distractorNotes":[null,"Fetal calf pancreas worked, but the passage credits the alcohol method, not the fetal extracts, with ending duct ligation.","Collip's precipitation is in paragraph 6 and concerns purifying the extract, not how the gland was prepared.","Measuring blood sugar was Best's skill; it does not touch the duct question.","Macleod's demands are in paragraph 6 and are never linked to duct ligation."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q08","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions the rabbit experiments in paragraph 6 primarily to","choices":["explain why Collip refused to share his method","show that Collip's work went beyond purification","argue that the extract was too dangerous to give to patients","illustrate why the first Thompson injection failed","demonstrate that Macleod's demand for controls was justified"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"Collip's contribution was purification."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"He also showed that the extract made the liver store glycogen and that an overdose in rabbits produced convulsions"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 states that Collip's contribution was purification, then opens the next sentence with \"He also showed,\" adding the glycogen and overdose findings. The rabbits extend his contribution past purification.","distractorNotes":["Collip's refusal is in paragraph 8 and is never explained by the rabbits.",null,"The passage goes straight on to treat a patient; it does not argue the extract was too dangerous.","The first injection failed through impurity and an abscess, not an overdose.","Macleod's demand for controls is a separate sentence about Banting's resentment."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q09","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the injection Leonard Thompson received on January 11, 1922","choices":["used the extract that Collip had purified","cleared the ketones from his urine within a day","lowered his blood sugar sharply but produced convulsions and coma","had no measurable effect on his blood sugar","lowered his blood sugar only somewhat and caused an abscess"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"His blood sugar fell somewhat, but sugar remained in his urine and a sterile abscess formed at the injection site"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says his blood sugar fell somewhat, sugar remained in his urine, and a sterile abscess formed at the injection site. That was January 11; the sharp drop came on January 23.","distractorNotes":["Collip's purified extract was the second injection, on January 23.","The ketones disappeared after the January 23 injection, not the first.","The sharp drop belongs to January 23, and no convulsions are reported in Thompson.","The passage says his blood sugar fell somewhat, so there was a measurable effect.",null]},{"id":"rc-o03-q10","type":"inference","stem":"The passage implies that the agreement signed on January 25, 1922 was a response to","choices":["Macleod's return from Scotland the previous September","the loss of potency in Collip's larger batches","the dispute over Collip's purification method","the university's negotiations with Eli Lilly","Banting's refusal to put his name on a patent"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"Collip told Banting and Best that he would not share his purification method"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"signed an agreement to work together under Macleod's direction and to seek no patents independently"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 places the agreement right after Collip's refusal to share his method and the argument that followed, and its terms, working together and seeking no patents independently, answer exactly that dispute.","distractorNotes":["Macleod's return is in paragraph 6, months earlier, and is not connected to the agreement.","The batches lost potency after the agreement; the passage says \"Then the method failed.\"",null,"The Lilly arrangement came in May, after the agreement.","Banting's refusal concerns the patent application in paragraph 9, which came later."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q11","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, the inventors assigned the patent to the University of Toronto for all of the following reasons EXCEPT to","choices":["keep control over the drug in the university's hands","license the companies that manufactured insulin","require that every batch meet a standard of potency and purity","provide the university with a steady source of income","prevent any one company from cornering the drug"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"The purpose was not income but control","choice":0},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"the university could license manufacturers","choice":1},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"require that every batch meet a standard of potency and purity","choice":2},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"keep any one company from cornering a drug","choice":4},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"The purpose was not income but control","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the purpose was not income but control, then lists licensing manufacturers, batch standards of potency and purity, and keeping any one company from cornering the drug. Income is the one the passage rules out.","distractorNotes":["Control is named outright as the purpose in paragraph 9.","Licensing manufacturers is the first item in the list.","The potency and purity standard is the second item.",null,"Keeping any one company from cornering the drug is the third item."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q12","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 9, the word \"cornering\" most nearly means","choices":["trapping","monopolizing","turning","cheapening","discontinuing"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"keep any one company from cornering a drug that patients would need every day for life"}],"why":"The sentence is about keeping any one company from controlling a drug that patients need every day. To corner a market is to monopolize it, which fits; trapping is the word's everyday sense and does not.","distractorNotes":["Trapping is the everyday meaning of corner, but a company cannot trap a drug.",null,"Turning a corner is a different idiom and makes no sense here.","Cheapening is the opposite worry; the fear was control of supply, not low prices.","Discontinuing is not suggested; the concern is one company holding the supply."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q13","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, August Krogh","choices":["nominated Banting and Macleod after visiting Toronto","was the first Canadian to win a Nobel Prize","shared his prize money with Collip","served as the Nobel committee's Danish chairman in 1923","published the standard historical account of the discovery"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"visited Toronto in late 1922, carried the method home, and nominated Banting and Macleod"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says Krogh, a Danish physiologist and laureate, visited Toronto in late 1922, carried the method home, and nominated Banting and Macleod.","distractorNotes":[null,"Banting was the first Canadian laureate; Krogh was Danish.","Macleod shared his money with Collip, not Krogh.","The passage calls him a laureate who nominated the pair, not a committee chairman.","The standard account is Michael Bliss's 1982 study, in paragraph 11."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the Nobel committee's decision to honor Banting and Macleod is best described as","choices":["openly contemptuous of its unfairness","wholehearted agreement with its choice","measured, blaming the instrument rather than the committee","indifferent to who received the money","nostalgic for an era when scientific prizes were less bitterly contested"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":6,"quote":"That is less a criticism of the Nobel committee than a limit of its instrument"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"Prizes honor names, and history remembers names; the discovery itself was made by a laboratory."}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says the problem is less a criticism of the Nobel committee than a limit of its instrument, right after noting that prizes honor names while the discovery was made by a laboratory. That is a measured judgment, not an attack.","distractorNotes":["The author explicitly declines to criticize the committee; contempt is far too strong.","The author says a prize cannot represent a team discovery, so this is not wholehearted agreement.",null,"The whole final paragraph weighs who deserves credit; the author is not indifferent.","Nothing in the passage looks back to an earlier era of prizes."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q15","type":"application","stem":"Which of the following situations best illustrates the author's point in the final paragraph about prizes and shared achievements?","choices":["Two chemists working independently in different countries isolate the same compound and are jointly honored by a scientific society","A runner who wins a marathon gives her medal to the coach who trained her and to the club that sponsored her","A company lists every one of its three hundred employees as co-inventors on a single patent application","A student submits a teammate's results under his own name and is later exposed by the laboratory director","An award names two project leaders, though the result depended equally on an unnamed tester and designer"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"Prizes honor names, and history remembers names; the discovery itself was made by a laboratory."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":6,"quote":"a shared achievement cannot be divided"}],"why":"The final paragraph says prizes honor names while the discovery was made by a laboratory. An award that names two leaders and leaves out people the result depended on is the same mismatch in a new setting.","distractorNotes":["Two independent discoverers are not a team, and here the honor names both of them.","Voluntarily passing a medal along is a personal gesture, not a limit of the award itself.","Naming everyone is the opposite of the problem; the author's point is that prizes leave essential contributors out.","Taking credit for another's work is misconduct, which the passage never discusses.",null]},{"id":"rc-o03-q16","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that, compared with Banting and Best, earlier investigators such as Scott and Kleiner were","choices":["less honest in reporting their results","more careful in method but less successful in the end","more interested in naming the hormone than in extracting it","equally successful but denied credit by the Nobel committee","hampered mainly by the lack of a surgical assistant"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"Yet the error-prone summer led somewhere, and the careful investigators before it had not."},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"each stopped short of treating a patient"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 contrasts the error-prone summer, which led somewhere, with the careful investigators before it, who had not gotten there; paragraph 3 says Scott and Kleiner each stopped short of treating a patient.","distractorNotes":["Nothing in the passage questions anyone's honesty.",null,"The naming was done by de Meyer and Sharpey-Schafer, not by Scott or Kleiner.","They stopped short of treating a patient, so they were not equally successful, and the committee is never said to have denied them.","The passage gives no reason tied to assistants; Best's role was blood sugar measurement, not surgery."]},{"id":"rc-o03-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Banting deserved the Nobel Prize alone, and Macleod's share of it was an injustice","Collip's purification was the real discovery of insulin, and the quarrel obscured it","The discovery of insulin was delayed for decades by investigators who lacked the courage to test their extracts on patients","Insulin was a team achievement, and the quarrel over credit shows how poorly prizes represent such work","The University of Toronto's handling of the insulin patent set the standard for how universities should manage medical discoveries"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"insulin was the product of a team, and the instruments we use to assign credit were never built to describe one"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"the discovery itself was made by a laboratory"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":6,"quote":"a shared achievement cannot be divided"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 states the thesis outright, that insulin was the product of a team and that our instruments for assigning credit were never built to describe one, and paragraph 11 returns to it: the discovery was made by a laboratory, and a shared achievement cannot be divided.","distractorNotes":["The author sides with no single claimant and calls the quarrel a contest without a rightful winner.","Collip's purification is one of four contributions the passage weighs, not the whole discovery.","The passage blames impurity and side effects for the delay, not a lack of courage, and Zuelzer did treat patients.",null,"The patent is one paragraph and is praised, but it is not what the passage is about."]}]},"rc-o04":{"id":"rc-o04","title":"The Hand the Body Wants","topic":"handedness (chirality) in drug molecules","field":"chemistry","source":"original","words":1318,"paragraphs":[["Your two hands look nearly identical, yet no amount of turning will make a left hand fit a right glove.","Molecules can have the same property: one that cannot be superimposed on its own mirror image is called chiral, from the Greek word for hand, a term the physicist Lord Kelvin introduced in the 1890s.","In medicine, handedness turned out to be a matter of life and death, and later, a matter of marketing."],["The story begins in Paris in 1848 with Louis Pasteur, then in his mid-twenties.","Chemists already knew that tartaric acid, obtained from the crust inside wine casks, rotated plane-polarized light, light filtered so that its waves vibrate in a single plane.","A second form, chemically identical in every test, did not rotate the light at all, and no one could say why.","Pasteur crystallized a sodium ammonium salt of the inactive acid and, examining the crystals under a magnifying lens, noticed that they came in two shapes that were mirror images of each other.","With tweezers he sorted them into two piles, dissolved each separately, and found that one solution rotated light to the right and the other to the left by the same amount.","The inactive acid was not inactive at all; it was an equal mixture of two opposite forms whose effects cancelled."],["Such a fifty-fifty mixture is now called racemic.","The explanation came in 1874, when Jacobus van 't Hoff and Joseph Le Bel independently proposed that the four bonds of a carbon atom point toward the corners of a tetrahedron.","If the four attached groups are all different, they can be arranged in two ways that are mirror images and cannot be interconverted without breaking a bond.","Such a carbon is called a stereocenter, and the two mirror-image molecules are called enantiomers."],["Enantiomers are strange twins.","They share the same melting point, boiling point, solubility, and density, and ordinary laboratory methods cannot tell them apart.","They differ in exactly two circumstances: in the direction they rotate polarized light, and in how they interact with other chiral things.","The nose makes the point vividly: one enantiomer of carvone smells of spearmint while its mirror image smells of caraway."],["This matters in medicine because the body is itself overwhelmingly one-handed.","Proteins are built from amino acids of a single handedness, so every enzyme and every receptor is a chiral pocket, as particular about shape as a glove.","In 1933 the researchers Easson and Stedman offered a model still taught today: if a drug must touch its receptor at three points, only one enantiomer can line up all three at once, while its mirror image can manage two at best.","Pharmacologists call the better-fitting enantiomer the eutomer and its partner the distomer.","A distomer may do nothing, may do something milder, may do something different, or, in the worst case, may do harm."],["The worst case has a name.","Thalidomide went on sale in West Germany in 1957 as a sedative and was soon taken by pregnant women for morning sickness.","By 1961 physicians had linked it to a wave of severe birth defects, often malformed or missing limbs, and it was withdrawn late that year.","Thousands of children were affected worldwide, by common estimates near ten thousand.","The United States was largely spared because Frances Kelsey, a reviewer at the Food and Drug Administration, refused to approve the drug for lack of adequate safety data; a 1962 law then strengthened the agency's demands for evidence."],["Thalidomide has one stereocenter, and later animal studies indicated that one enantiomer carries the sedative effect while the other causes the birth defects.","It is tempting to conclude that selling only the safe hand would have averted the tragedy, but that conclusion is wrong.","The hydrogen on thalidomide's stereocenter is easily removed and replaced under the mild conditions of the bloodstream, so either pure enantiomer turns into a mixture of both within hours of being swallowed.","A single-enantiomer thalidomide would not have been a safe drug, because the body would have manufactured the harmful form on its own.","The drug has since returned under strict controls, approved by the FDA in 1998 for a painful complication of leprosy and later for a cancer of the bone marrow."],["Ibuprofen tells a gentler version of the same story.","Developed at the Boots company in Britain in the 1960s, it has mainly been sold as a racemate.","Only the S enantiomer blocks the enzyme that produces the signaling molecules behind pain and inflammation; the R enantiomer is nearly inert at that target.","Instead the body attaches the R form to a carrier molecule, inverts its stereocenter, and releases it as the active S form, a conversion that runs in only one direction and captures a large share of the R form, by many estimates more than half.","The supposedly inactive half of the tablet is in effect a slow-release supply of the active one.","A pure S version is sold in some countries, but the racemate remains the standard."],["In 1992 the FDA issued a policy statement encouraging developers to study each enantiomer of a new chiral drug separately, and single enantiomers became the norm for new chiral medicines.","The more interesting development was commercial.","A company whose racemic drug was nearing the end of its patent could isolate the eutomer and launch it as a new product under a new patent, a maneuver the industry calls a chiral switch.","The most famous example is esomeprazole, the S enantiomer of the stomach-acid drug omeprazole, approved in the United States in 2001 as the older drug's patent protection was running out.","Advertised directly to consumers as the purple pill, it went on to sell billions of dollars' worth a year."],["Here is where a careful reader should slow down.","Omeprazole is a prodrug: it does nothing until it reaches the acid-secreting cells of the stomach lining, where acid converts it into an active form that has no stereocenter at all.","At the site of action, in other words, the two enantiomers become the same molecule.","The difference lies in the liver, which breaks down the S enantiomer more slowly, so a given dose of esomeprazole delivers somewhat more drug to the stomach than the same dose of omeprazole.","That is a real difference in how the body handles the drug, but several widely cited trials compared 40 milligrams of the new product with 20 milligrams of the old one, and critics argued that much of the advantage could be had by simply raising the omeprazole dose.","I find it hard to read the esomeprazole episode as anything but a commercial achievement first and a scientific one a distant second."],["It would be unfair to dismiss every switch on that basis.","Bupivacaine, a local anesthetic, is dangerous to the heart in overdose, and the danger is greater for its R enantiomer.","The S enantiomer, sold alone as levobupivacaine, provides comparable numbing with a wider margin of safety, which is exactly the sort of benefit the three-point model would predict.","A chiral switch is neither a scientific advance nor a marketing trick by definition; each one must be judged by whether patients do measurably better on the single enantiomer than on the mixture, at matched doses."],["None of this should obscure how remarkable the underlying chemistry is.","Pasteur sorted crystals with tweezers because no other method existed.","Today chemists can build one enantiomer and not the other on an industrial scale using catalysts that are themselves chiral, work recognized by the 2001 Nobel Prize in Chemistry awarded to William Knowles, Ryoji Noyori, and Barry Sharpless.","For any new medicine it is now possible to ask which hand the body wants and to supply that hand alone.","Whether supplying it alone is worth paying for is a different question, and it should be answered by evidence rather than by the color of the pill."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o04-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, what did Pasteur notice about the crystals of the sodium ammonium salt?","choices":["They rotated light more strongly than tartaric acid did.","They were chemically different from ordinary tartaric acid.","They formed only when the solution was kept warm.","They could not be dissolved once they had been sorted.","They came in two mirror-image shapes."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"noticed that they came in two shapes that were mirror images of each other"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says Pasteur, looking through a magnifying lens, noticed that the crystals came in two shapes that were mirror images of each other. That observation is the whole starting point of the passage.","distractorNotes":["The crystals themselves were from the inactive acid; the passage says the solutions rotated light equally in opposite directions, not more strongly than tartaric acid.","Paragraph 2 says the inactive acid was chemically identical to tartaric acid in every test, the reverse of this choice.","The passage never mentions temperature or warming; this is outside the passage.","Paragraph 2 says he dissolved each pile after sorting, so this contradicts the text.",null]},{"id":"rc-o04-q02","type":"except","stem":"Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as part of Pasteur's work in 1848?","choices":["heating the crystals to separate the two forms","crystallizing a sodium ammonium salt of the inactive acid","sorting the crystals by hand with tweezers","measuring the rotation produced by each solution","examining the crystals under a magnifying lens"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"crystallized a sodium ammonium salt of the inactive acid","choice":1},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"With tweezers he sorted them into two piles","choice":2},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"found that one solution rotated light to the right and the other to the left","choice":3},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"examining the crystals under a magnifying lens","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 2 confirms the salt, the lens, the tweezers, and the measured rotations, but it never mentions heating. The separation was done by eye and by hand, so heating is the one you cannot confirm.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 2 says Pasteur crystallized a sodium ammonium salt of the inactive acid.","Paragraph 2 says he sorted the crystals into two piles with tweezers.","Paragraph 2 says he found one solution rotated light to the right and the other to the left.","Paragraph 2 says he examined the crystals under a magnifying lens."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, two enantiomers of the same compound differ in","choices":["their melting points and boiling points","the direction they rotate polarized light","the number of stereocenters they each contain","their solubility and density in water","the strength of their bonds to carbon"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"in the direction they rotate polarized light"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 lists exactly two circumstances in which enantiomers differ: the direction they rotate polarized light and how they interact with other chiral things. Everything else on this list is something they share.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 says enantiomers share the same melting point and boiling point.",null,"Enantiomers are mirror images of the same molecule; the passage never says they differ in number of stereocenters.","Paragraph 4 says they share the same solubility and density.","Bond strength is never discussed; this is outside the passage."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q04","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \"particular\" most nearly means","choices":["specific","individual","unusual","detailed","selective"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"as particular about shape as a glove"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 calls a receptor a chiral pocket that is as particular about shape as a glove. A glove is choosy about which hand it accepts, so here \"particular\" means selective or fussy, not specific or individual.","distractorNotes":["\"Specific\" is the common meaning of the word, but a glove is not specific about shape; it is choosy about shape.","\"Individual\" is another everyday sense of \"particular\" that does not fit a glove being picky.","Nothing in the sentence suggests the receptor is unusual; the comparison is about pickiness.","\"Detailed\" does not describe a glove's relationship to a hand.",null]},{"id":"rc-o04-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the three-point attachment model explains why","choices":["enzymes are built from amino acids of a single handedness","a drug's two enantiomers share the same melting point","receptors change shape when a drug binds to them","only one enantiomer can fully fit a receptor","the distomer of every drug is harmful to the body"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"only one enantiomer can line up all three at once, while its mirror image can manage two at best"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 describes the model: if a drug must touch its receptor at three points, only one enantiomer can line up all three, while the mirror image manages two at best. The model is about fit, not about what the distomer does.","distractorNotes":["That proteins are built from one-handed amino acids is stated earlier in the paragraph as a fact, not something the model explains.","Shared melting point is a physical property from paragraph 4, unrelated to the model.","The passage never says receptors change shape; this is outside the passage.",null,"Paragraph 5 says a distomer may do nothing, something milder, something different, or harm; \"every\" is far too strong."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q06","type":"function","stem":"The author ends paragraph 5 with the words \"may do harm\" primarily to","choices":["argue that racemic mixtures should never be approved for sale","introduce the thalidomide case that follows","explain why the body's proteins are one-handed","contrast ibuprofen's distomer with esomeprazole's","summarize the model proposed by Easson and Stedman"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"in the worst case, may do harm"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"The worst case has a name."}],"why":"The last line of paragraph 5 names the worst case a distomer can be, and paragraph 6 opens with \"The worst case has a name\" and tells the thalidomide story. The phrase is a bridge into that example.","distractorNotes":["The author later praises racemic ibuprofen and asks for case-by-case judgment, so a blanket ban is far too strong.",null,"One-handed proteins are explained at the start of paragraph 5, not by its closing words.","Ibuprofen and esomeprazole come in paragraphs 8 to 10, and the passage never contrasts their distomers.","The model is summarized in the third sentence; the closing sentence lists what a distomer can do, which goes beyond the model."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q07","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the United States was largely spared the thalidomide disaster because","choices":["the drug was never manufactured or sold anywhere outside West Germany","American physicians connected it to birth defects before it went on sale","a 1962 law banned the use of sedatives during pregnancy","the drug's single-enantiomer form was the only version sold there","an FDA reviewer withheld approval over inadequate safety data"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"refused to approve the drug for lack of adequate safety data"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says Frances Kelsey, a reviewer at the FDA, refused to approve the drug for lack of adequate safety data. The 1962 law came afterward and strengthened the agency's demands; it was not the reason.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 6 says thousands of children were affected worldwide, so the drug was clearly sold beyond West Germany.","The passage credits an FDA reviewer, not American physicians, and says the drug was never approved there.","The 1962 law strengthened demands for evidence; the passage never says it banned sedatives in pregnancy, and it came after the refusal.","Paragraph 7 says a single-enantiomer version would not have been safe, and the passage never says one was sold anywhere.",null]},{"id":"rc-o04-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if thalidomide had been sold only as its sedative enantiomer,","choices":["the harmful form would still have formed inside patients","the sedative effect would have been lost entirely","the FDA would have approved it for sale in 1957","the birth defects would have been prevented","the drug could not have returned for leprosy complications in 1998"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"either pure enantiomer turns into a mixture of both within hours of being swallowed"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"the body would have manufactured the harmful form on its own"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says either pure enantiomer turns into a mixture of both within hours, and that the body would have manufactured the harmful form on its own. So the sedative-only version would have become the dangerous mixture anyway.","distractorNotes":[null,"Nothing suggests the sedative effect disappears; the problem is that the harmful form reappears alongside it.","Kelsey's refusal was about missing safety data; the passage never ties FDA approval to which enantiomer was sold.","Paragraph 7 calls exactly this conclusion wrong.","The 1998 return is about strict controls for a leprosy complication; the passage never links it to the enantiomer sold."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q09","type":"application","stem":"Which of the following new situations best illustrates the problem described in paragraph 7?","choices":["A heartburn drug whose two enantiomers become the same active molecule once acid converts them","A painkiller whose inactive enantiomer is steadily converted by the body into the active enantiomer over several hours","An antibiotic sold as one pure enantiomer that becomes a fifty-fifty mixture in patients' blood","A crystalline drug whose harmful enantiomer can be removed by sorting its crystals by hand","A fragrance compound whose two enantiomers produce entirely different smells"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"either pure enantiomer turns into a mixture of both within hours of being swallowed"}],"why":"The problem in paragraph 7 is interconversion: a pure enantiomer turns into a mixture of both inside the body, so selling the safe hand alone does not help. An antibiotic that becomes a fifty-fifty mixture in the blood is the same situation with a new drug.","distractorNotes":["Two enantiomers becoming one achiral active form is the omeprazole case from paragraph 10, not interconversion between enantiomers.","One-way conversion of the inactive form into the active one is the ibuprofen case from paragraph 8, which the passage presents as helpful rather than a problem.",null,"Sorting crystals by hand is Pasteur's method from paragraph 2 and has nothing to do with what happens inside the body.","Different smells are the carvone example from paragraph 4, which illustrates that enantiomers interact differently with chiral things."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q10","type":"except","stem":"All of the following are stated in the passage about ibuprofen EXCEPT:","choices":["It was developed at the Boots company in Britain.","Only its S enantiomer blocks the target enzyme.","The body converts part of the R form into the S form.","The conversion of R to S runs in both directions.","A pure S version is sold in some countries."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"Developed at the Boots company in Britain in the 1960s","choice":0},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"Only the S enantiomer blocks the enzyme","choice":1},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"releases it as the active S form","choice":2},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"A pure S version is sold in some countries","choice":4},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"a conversion that runs in only one direction"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 confirms Boots, the S enantiomer as the active one, the R-to-S conversion, and the pure S product, but it says the conversion runs in only one direction. A two-way conversion is the opposite of what the passage states.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 8 says ibuprofen was developed at the Boots company in Britain in the 1960s.","Paragraph 8 says only the S enantiomer blocks the enzyme behind pain and inflammation.","Paragraph 8 says the body inverts the R form and releases it as the active S form.",null,"Paragraph 8 says a pure S version is sold in some countries."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q11","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the FDA's 1992 policy statement","choices":["required that all new drugs be sold as single enantiomers","banned the sale of racemic mixtures in the United States","approved esomeprazole for sale as a new product","introduced the term chiral switch into the industry","encouraged separate study of each enantiomer"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"encouraging developers to study each enantiomer of a new chiral drug separately"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the 1992 statement encouraged developers to study each enantiomer of a new chiral drug separately. Encouraging is softer than requiring, and the passage says nothing about bans.","distractorNotes":["\"Required\" and \"all\" are too strong; the passage says the statement encouraged study of each enantiomer.","Racemic ibuprofen remains the standard according to paragraph 8, so no ban occurred.","Esomeprazole was approved in 2001, nine years later, and the policy statement is not credited with that approval.","The passage says the industry calls the maneuver a chiral switch; it never attributes the term to the FDA.",null]},{"id":"rc-o04-q12","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if the liver broke down both enantiomers of omeprazole at the same rate,","choices":["omeprazole would no longer act as a prodrug","esomeprazole would offer little or no advantage","esomeprazole would deliver more drug to the stomach than before","the active form of the drug would regain its stereocenter","the 40 milligram trials would have shown a larger effect"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"the two enantiomers become the same molecule"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"The difference lies in the liver, which breaks down the S enantiomer more slowly"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says the two enantiomers become the same molecule at the site of action and that the difference lies in the liver, which breaks down the S enantiomer more slowly. Take away the liver difference and nothing is left to separate the two drugs.","distractorNotes":["Omeprazole is a prodrug because stomach acid activates it, according to paragraph 10; liver rates have nothing to do with that.",null,"Reversed: esomeprazole delivers more drug only because it is broken down more slowly, so equal breakdown would remove that extra delivery.","The active form lacks a stereocenter because of what acid does to it, not because of the liver.","Outside the passage, and backwards: with no liver difference, the trials would have shown less advantage, not more."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q13","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, critics of esomeprazole argued that","choices":["a higher dose of omeprazole could match much of its advantage","it was broken down by the liver faster than omeprazole","it caused more side effects than the older drug","its patent protection had already expired before the drug was approved","it failed to reach the acid-secreting cells of the stomach"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"critics argued that much of the advantage could be had by simply raising the omeprazole dose"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says widely cited trials compared 40 milligrams of the new drug with 20 milligrams of the old one, and critics argued that much of the advantage could be had by simply raising the omeprazole dose.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: paragraph 10 says the liver breaks down the S enantiomer more slowly, not faster.","Side effects are never mentioned; this is outside the passage.","It was omeprazole's patent that was running out, according to paragraph 9, not esomeprazole's.","Paragraph 10 says esomeprazole delivers somewhat more drug to the stomach, not less."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the esomeprazole chiral switch is best described as","choices":["enthusiastic approval","neutral indifference","measured skepticism","outright hostility","puzzled confusion"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"a commercial achievement first and a scientific one a distant second"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"That is a real difference in how the body handles the drug, but"}],"why":"The author grants that there is a real difference in how the body handles the drug, then calls the episode a commercial achievement first and a scientific one a distant second. Conceding the point and still doubting the value is measured skepticism.","distractorNotes":["The author doubts the scientific value of the switch; nothing in paragraph 10 is enthusiastic.","An author who tells the reader to slow down and then renders a judgment is not indifferent.",null,"Hostility is too strong; the author concedes a real difference and says in paragraph 11 that not every switch should be dismissed.","The author is not confused; the mechanism is laid out clearly and a verdict is given."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q15","type":"function","stem":"The author discusses bupivacaine in paragraph 11 primarily to","choices":["illustrate how the liver breaks down enantiomers at different rates","argue that local anesthetics should always be sold as racemates","prove that the three-point attachment model is incorrect","show that some chiral switches deliver a real benefit","compare the sales of levobupivacaine with those of esomeprazole"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"It would be unfair to dismiss every switch on that basis."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"provides comparable numbing with a wider margin of safety"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 opens by saying it would be unfair to dismiss every switch, then offers levobupivacaine as a single enantiomer with a wider margin of safety. The example exists to balance the skepticism of paragraph 10.","distractorNotes":["Liver metabolism is the omeprazole story in paragraph 10; paragraph 11 is about heart toxicity.","Reversed: the passage presents the single enantiomer, not the racemate, as the safer anesthetic.","Reversed: the passage says the benefit is exactly what the three-point model would predict.",null,"Sales figures are given only for esomeprazole; the passage never compares the two products commercially."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q16","type":"inference","stem":"It can be inferred from the passage that before the development of chiral catalysts,","choices":["no method existed that could distinguish one enantiomer from the other","producing a single enantiomer at industrial scale was difficult","Pasteur's method of sorting crystals was used in factories","drugs were never sold as single enantiomers","racemic mixtures were considered unsafe by regulators"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"Pasteur sorted crystals with tweezers because no other method existed."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"Today chemists can build one enantiomer and not the other on an industrial scale using catalysts that are themselves chiral"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 contrasts Pasteur's tweezers, used because no other method existed, with today's chiral catalysts that build one enantiomer on an industrial scale. The natural inference is that industrial-scale production of one hand was hard before those catalysts.","distractorNotes":["Too strong: paragraph 2 shows that polarized light could tell the two apart as early as 1848.",null,"The passage never says Pasteur's hand-sorting was used in factories; this is outside the passage.","Too strong: the passage never says single-enantiomer drugs were impossible before the catalysts, only that producing them at scale is now possible.","Paragraph 9 says the 1992 statement encouraged separate study; nothing says regulators considered racemates unsafe."]},{"id":"rc-o04-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Pasteur's discovery of mirror-image crystals was the most important event in the history of chemistry.","Chiral switches are marketing maneuvers that regulators should prohibit.","Handedness shapes how drugs act, but each single-enantiomer product must be judged on evidence.","Thalidomide and ibuprofen show that racemic drugs are always more dangerous than single enantiomers.","Enzymes and receptors are chiral because they are built from amino acids of a single handedness."],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"a matter of life and death, and later, a matter of marketing"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"each one must be judged by whether patients do measurably better on the single enantiomer than on the mixture"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"it should be answered by evidence rather than by the color of the pill"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 frames handedness as a matter of life and death and later of marketing; paragraph 11 says each switch must be judged by whether patients do better; paragraph 12 closes by asking for evidence rather than the color of the pill. The passage is about handedness mattering and the value being an evidence question.","distractorNotes":["Pasteur is the opening story, not the thesis, and the passage never ranks his discovery against all of chemistry.","Too strong: paragraph 11 says a switch is neither an advance nor a trick by definition, and the author praises levobupivacaine.",null,"Reversed and too strong: racemic ibuprofen is presented as perfectly good, and \"always\" contradicts paragraph 11.","That is a supporting fact from paragraph 5, not the main idea of the whole passage."]}]},"rc-o05":{"id":"rc-o05","title":"Eaters of Bacteria","topic":"bacteriophage therapy, its eclipse and its return","field":"microbiology","source":"original","words":1367,"paragraphs":[["In 1915, the English bacteriologist Frederick Twort noticed something odd on his culture plates.","Some bacterial colonies had turned glassy, as if dissolving, and material from a dissolved colony passed the effect to a fresh one.","Twort reported this in The Lancet, suggested a virus might be responsible, and then, short of money and distracted by war, set the question aside.","Two years later, at the Pasteur Institute in Paris, Felix d'Herelle described an invisible agent that destroyed the bacteria of dysentery and named it: bacteriophage, the eater of bacteria.","A long quarrel over credit followed, but the discovery itself was secure."],["A bacteriophage, or phage for short, is a virus that infects only bacteria.","They are thought to be the most abundant biological entities on Earth.","The classic phage looks like a tiny lunar lander: a protein head holding its genes, a tail, and gripping fibers.","Infection follows a sequence that microbiologists call the lytic cycle.","The phage attaches to a specific receptor molecule on the bacterial surface, injects its genome into the cell, and redirects the cell's own machinery to copy that genome and build new phage parts.","Within as little as half an hour, new viruses fill the cell, and phage enzymes break open the cell wall from the inside.","The cell bursts, releasing tens to hundreds of phages, each ready to begin again; a dense culture can clear within hours."],["Not every phage kills on arrival.","Some, called temperate phages, can instead insert their genome into the bacterial chromosome and lie quiet, copied with the host at each division, a state called lysogeny.","Such a dormant phage, a prophage, may wake after many generations, often when its host is damaged, and turn lytic.","Lysogeny has a darker side for medicine.","A prophage can carry genes that change its host's behavior, and the toxins behind cholera and diphtheria are encoded on genes acquired this way.","A temperate phage can also shuttle antibiotic resistance genes from one bacterium to another.","So a phage chosen for therapy should be strictly lytic: it must kill reliably, and it must not be able to settle in and hand its host new tricks."],["Phages are also choosy, and their choosiness cuts both ways.","Because a phage must recognize a particular receptor, it usually infects only certain strains within a single species, sometimes only a handful.","For the patient, this is a gift: a phage aimed at a wound infection will leave the trillions of helpful bacteria in the gut untouched, while a broad-spectrum antibiotic scorches friend and foe alike.","For the physician, it is a burden.","The infecting strain must be isolated, grown, and tested against a library of phages, which can take days a very sick patient may not have.","An antibiotic can be pulled from a shelf; a phage, in most cases, must be matched."],["The decades after d'Herelle's report were giddy ones.","He was treating patients by 1919, companies in Europe and the United States sold phage preparations for everything from dysentery to boils, and Sinclair Lewis built his 1925 novel Arrowsmith around a young doctor testing phages against plague.","The evidence, however, did not keep pace.","Many early trials had no control groups, some commercial products were found to contain little or no active phage, and the lysogeny problem was not yet understood.","In 1934, a long review in the Journal of the American Medical Association found most claims unsupported, and the field's reputation in the West never recovered.","Then came penicillin.","By the late 1940s, antibiotics were cheap, stable, broad, and backed by controlled trials, and most Western physicians saw no reason to look back."],["The story did not end everywhere.","In 1923, the Georgian microbiologist George Eliava, who had worked with d'Herelle in Paris, founded an institute in Tbilisi that became a center of phage work, and d'Herelle himself came to work there in the 1930s.","Eliava was executed in Stalin's purges in 1937, but the institute that now bears his name survived, and phage therapy became routine in Soviet medicine.","Short of antibiotics, Soviet physicians prescribed phages for wound and intestinal infections for decades, and the institute built one of the world's largest phage collections.","Most of it appeared in Russian journals Western scientists did not read, and much of it lacked the controlled design that Western medicine had come to demand, so it was easy to ignore."],["There was a scientific reason for caution too, the same one now troubling antibiotics: evolution.","Bacteria exposed to a phage soon produce mutants that the phage cannot infect, most often by altering or shedding the surface receptor the phage uses to attach.","Bacteria also carry internal defenses that chop up foreign DNA; CRISPR, now a gene-editing tool, began as a bacterial memory of past phage attacks.","Phage therapists answer with cocktails: mixtures of several phages that attack the same bacterium through different receptors, so that a mutant which escapes one phage is still caught by another.","There is a further, more hopeful twist.","Resisting a phage often costs the bacterium something: the discarded receptor may have been needed to cause disease or to pump out antibiotics, so a resistant mutant can end up newly vulnerable to drugs that had failed."],["That twist played out in the case that revived Western interest.","In late 2015, Tom Patterson, a professor at the University of California, San Diego, fell ill in Egypt with an infection of Acinetobacter baumannii, a bacterium notorious for resisting nearly every antibiotic.","By early 2016 he was in a coma and out of antibiotics, and his wife, the epidemiologist Steffanie Strathdee, began calling laboratories about phage therapy.","Within weeks, researchers at Texas A&M University and at a United States Navy laboratory had each screened their collections against his isolate and assembled cocktails that killed it.","With emergency permission from the Food and Drug Administration, his physicians infused phages into his abscess and then into his bloodstream in March 2016.","Patterson woke within days, and over the following months, with phages and antibiotics given together, the infection cleared.","Tellingly, the bacterium did evolve resistance to the phages, but the resistant form appeared more sensitive to an antibiotic in his regimen."],["The case was published in 2017, and the effect was immediate: UC San Diego opened a phage therapy center in 2018, requests poured into phage laboratories worldwide, and compassionate-use treatments have since been reported for lung infections, infected implants, and other infections antibiotics could no longer touch.","Yet an honest reader should notice what these reports are: single cases, tried because nothing else remained, and often published because they succeeded.","The patients who did not improve rarely make the news, and a therapy cannot be judged on its best days."],["The deepest obstacle may be regulatory, and it follows from the biology.","Drug approval normally rests on a fixed, well-characterized product made the same way every time.","A phage cocktail is none of those things: it is alive, it reproduces inside the patient, it may need reformulating as the bacterium changes, and the ideal mixture for one patient is wrong for the next.","United States regulators have so far granted case-by-case emergency permission rather than approving any phage as a medicine, while Belgium lets pharmacists prepare phages for individual patients under a defined quality standard.","The first phage products accepted by American regulators were not drugs at all but food sprays, the earliest approved in 2006 against Listeria on ready-to-eat meats."],["Whether phage therapy becomes ordinary medicine depends on work far less dramatic than a bedside rescue.","It will require randomized trials, of which few have been run, with mixed results; phage banks that match an isolate in hours, not days; and manufacturing standards for a product that evolves.","The need is not in doubt: antibiotic-resistant infections now cause more than a million deaths a year worldwide, and few new antibiotics are coming.","A century after Twort watched his colonies dissolve, the eaters of bacteria are back on the table.","They are not a miracle, and anyone who promises one has not read the history, but for the first time since the 1930s they have a fair chance to prove what they can do."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o05-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the agent d'Herelle described in 1917 destroyed the bacteria responsible for","choices":["dysentery","cholera","plague","boils","diphtheria"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"destroyed the bacteria of dysentery"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says d'Herelle described an invisible agent that destroyed the bacteria of dysentery. The other diseases do appear in the passage, but each is attached to a different idea.","distractorNotes":[null,"Cholera appears in paragraph 3 as a toxin carried on phage genes, not as d'Herelle's target.","Plague is the disease in Sinclair Lewis's novel in paragraph 5.","Boils are among the conditions commercial phage products were sold for in paragraph 5.","Diphtheria is paired with cholera in paragraph 3 as a phage-encoded toxin."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q02","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 1, the word 'secure' most nearly means","choices":["kept under guard","safe from theft","tightly fastened","emotionally confident","beyond dispute"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"but the discovery itself was secure"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 sets the quarrel over credit against the discovery itself, which was 'secure': the finding stood firm no matter who got the credit. The everyday sense of safety or locks does not fit a discovery.","distractorNotes":["Being guarded is the physical sense again; nothing in the sentence is being protected.","Safety from theft is the everyday sense of 'secure' and cannot describe a scientific finding.","Fastening is the physical sense of the word, which does not apply to a discovery.","Confidence describes a person, and the sentence is about the discovery, not the people.",null]},{"id":"rc-o05-q03","type":"except","stem":"Which of the following is NOT described in paragraph 2 as part of the lytic cycle?","choices":["The phage injects its genome into the cell.","The phage genome is inserted into the bacterial chromosome.","Phage enzymes break open the cell wall from the inside.","The cell's own machinery is redirected to build new phage parts.","The phage attaches to a receptor on the bacterial surface."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"attaches to a specific receptor molecule on the bacterial surface","choice":4},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"injects its genome into the cell","choice":0},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"redirects the cell's own machinery to copy that genome and build new phage parts","choice":3},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"phage enzymes break open the cell wall from the inside","choice":2},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"insert their genome into the bacterial chromosome"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 lists attachment, injection, hijacking of the cell's machinery, and bursting out. Inserting the genome into the chromosome is lysogeny, which paragraph 3 presents as the alternative to the lytic cycle.","distractorNotes":["Injection of the genome is stated in the same sentence of paragraph 2.",null,"Enzymes breaking the wall open from the inside is in paragraph 2, sentence 6.","Redirecting the cell's own machinery is stated in paragraph 2, sentence 5.","Attachment is the first step named in paragraph 2, sentence 5."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q04","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a temperate phage would be a poor choice for therapy even if it could kill the target bacterium because it","choices":["cannot recognize receptors on most bacterial strains","reproduces too slowly to clear a dense infection","might give the bacterium harmful new genes","has never been tested in a controlled clinical trial","would be destroyed by the bacterium's CRISPR defenses"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"A prophage can carry genes that change its host's behavior"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":7,"quote":"it must not be able to settle in and hand its host new tricks"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says a prophage can carry toxin genes and shuttle antibiotic resistance between bacteria, then concludes that a therapeutic phage must not 'settle in and hand its host new tricks.' The danger is gene transfer, not killing power.","distractorNotes":["Receptor specificity belongs to paragraph 4 and applies to all phages, not temperate ones in particular.","Speed of reproduction is never raised as a weakness of temperate phages.",null,"Trial history is discussed in paragraphs 5 and 11 and is never tied to temperate phages.","CRISPR appears in paragraph 7 as a general bacterial defense, not a special weakness of temperate phages."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, a phage's narrow host range benefits the patient because the phage","choices":["can be pulled from a shelf like an antibiotic","spares the helpful bacteria in the gut","cannot be resisted by the infecting bacterium","kills a wider range of strains than an antibiotic does","can be matched to the infecting strain within hours"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"will leave the trillions of helpful bacteria in the gut untouched"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 calls specificity a gift for the patient because a targeted phage leaves the helpful gut bacteria untouched, while a broad-spectrum antibiotic 'scorches friend and foe alike.'","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 says the opposite: an antibiotic comes off a shelf, while a phage must be matched.",null,"Paragraph 7 says bacteria do evolve resistance to phages.","Reversed: the phage's range is narrower than an antibiotic's, not wider.","Matching takes days in paragraph 4; hours is the goal paragraph 11 hopes for."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q06","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions Sinclair Lewis's novel Arrowsmith in paragraph 5 primarily to","choices":["explain why the 1934 review was so harshly critical","suggest that phage therapy was effective against plague","give an example of a controlled trial of phage therapy","show that novelists understood phage biology better than physicians did","illustrate how far early excitement about phages had spread"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"The decades after d'Herelle's report were giddy ones."},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"Sinclair Lewis built his 1925 novel Arrowsmith around a young doctor testing phages against plague"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 opens by calling the decades after d'Herelle 'giddy ones' and then stacks up examples: d'Herelle's patients, commercial products, and a popular novel. The novel is there to show that the excitement reached beyond the laboratory into popular culture.","distractorNotes":["The review is introduced three sentences later and is explained by the weak evidence, not by the novel.","The author never claims phages worked against plague; the novel is fiction.","A novel is not a trial, and the passage says early trials lacked control groups.","The passage makes no comparison between novelists and physicians.",null]},{"id":"rc-o05-q07","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, all of the following contributed to the decline of phage therapy in the West EXCEPT","choices":["the arrival of cheap, stable antibiotics","a critical review published in 1934","trials that lacked control groups","evidence that phages carried toxin genes","commercial products that contained little or no active phage"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"Many early trials had no control groups","choice":2},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"some commercial products were found to contain little or no active phage","choice":4},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"In 1934, a long review","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":7,"quote":"antibiotics were cheap, stable","choice":0},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"the lysogeny problem was not yet understood"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 names uncontrolled trials, inactive products, the 1934 review, and the arrival of antibiotics. It says the lysogeny problem was 'not yet understood' at the time, so toxin genes could not have driven the decline.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 ends with penicillin and the cheap, stable antibiotics of the late 1940s.","The 1934 review is in paragraph 5, sentence 5.","Stated in paragraph 5, sentence 4.",null,"Stated in the same sentence of paragraph 5."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the institute in Tbilisi","choices":["was founded by d'Herelle himself in the 1930s","was the first to test phage therapy in controlled clinical trials","closed after its founder was executed in 1937","was founded by a scientist who had worked with d'Herelle","published most of its findings in Western journals"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"George Eliava, who had worked with d'Herelle in Paris, founded an institute in Tbilisi"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says George Eliava, who had worked with d'Herelle in Paris, founded the Tbilisi institute in 1923. d'Herelle only visited, in the 1930s.","distractorNotes":["d'Herelle visited in the 1930s, but Eliava founded it in 1923.","Paragraph 6 says much of the Soviet work lacked controlled design.","Paragraph 6 says the institute survived Eliava's execution.",null,"Paragraph 6 says most of the work was published in Russian, in journals Western scientists did not read."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that Western scientists ignored the Soviet phage work mainly because","choices":["it was out of their sight and lacked rigorous design","the Eliava Institute refused to share its phage collection","it had been shown to be fraudulent after Eliava's death","the 1934 review had already proven that phages could not work","Soviet physicians used phages only for intestinal infections"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"much of it lacked the controlled design that Western medicine had come to demand, so it was easy to ignore"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 closes by saying the work was in Russian, in journals Western scientists did not read, and lacked controlled design, 'so it was easy to ignore.' Language and method, not disproof, kept it out of view.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage says the institute built a large collection, not that it refused to share it.","Nothing in the passage calls the Soviet work fraudulent.","The 1934 review found Western claims unsupported, which is not the same as proving phages cannot work.","Paragraph 6 names wound infections as well as intestinal ones."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q10","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, bacteria most often become resistant to a phage by","choices":["producing enzymes that destroy the phage's protein head","altering or losing the receptor the phage attaches to","using CRISPR to chop up the phage's DNA","pumping the phage out of the cell before it can replicate","acquiring resistance genes from a prophage"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"most often by altering or shedding the surface receptor the phage uses to attach"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says resistant mutants arise 'most often by altering or shedding the surface receptor the phage uses to attach.' DNA-chopping defenses and CRISPR are mentioned next as additional defenses, not the most common route.","distractorNotes":["The passage never mentions enzymes attacking the phage head.",null,"CRISPR is named in paragraph 7 as a further defense, not the most common one.","Pumps appear in paragraph 7 as a way bacteria expel antibiotics, not phages.","Prophages shuttle antibiotic resistance genes in paragraph 3, not resistance to phages."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q11","type":"application","stem":"Which new situation best illustrates the principle behind the phage cocktails described in paragraph 7?","choices":["A farmer rotates crops from year to year so that a pest adapted to one crop starves the next year","A hospital switches to a different antibiotic each month so that no single drug is overused","A laboratory screens fifty candidate phages against a patient's bacterial strain to find the single one that kills it fastest","A patient takes three drugs that hit a virus at different steps, so a mutant escaping one is stopped by the others","A doctor doubles the dose of an antibiotic when the first course fails to clear an infection"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"so that a mutant which escapes one phage is still caught by another"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 describes cocktails as several attackers used at once, through different routes, so that an escape from one is caught by another. Three antiviral drugs given together at different steps follow the same logic. Rotation and bigger doses are different strategies.","distractorNotes":["Crop rotation changes the target over time; a cocktail attacks the same target several ways at once.","Rotating one drug at a time is sequential, not several attackers at once.","Screening for the single best phage is the matching problem of paragraph 4, not a cocktail.",null,"A higher dose of the same agent gives a mutant nothing new to escape from."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q12","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the phages used to treat Tom Patterson were supplied by","choices":["his wife's own laboratory at UC San Diego","a university in Texas and a Navy laboratory","a company that sold phage products in the 1930s","the Food and Drug Administration's phage bank","the Eliava Institute in Tbilisi and a Belgian pharmacy"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"researchers at Texas A&M University and at a United States Navy laboratory"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says researchers at Texas A&M University and at a United States Navy laboratory each screened their collections against Patterson's isolate and assembled cocktails that killed it.","distractorNotes":["Strathdee is an epidemiologist who called laboratories; the passage never gives her a phage lab.",null,"Commercial products of the 1930s belong to paragraph 5, not to the 2016 case.","The FDA gave emergency permission; the passage never says it holds phages.","The Eliava Institute and Belgium appear in other paragraphs and had no part in this case."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q13","type":"inference","stem":"The passage implies that Patterson's recovery, by itself, cannot settle whether phage therapy works because","choices":["the Food and Drug Administration refused to approve the treatment","the bacterium never developed resistance to the cocktail","he also received antibiotics and his case was one patient","the phages were given only into the abscess and never reached his blood","his case was published without peer review"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"with phages and antibiotics given together"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"single cases, tried because nothing else remained, and often published because they succeeded"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 notes the phages and antibiotics were given together, and paragraph 9 warns that such reports are single cases, often published because they succeeded. One patient on two treatments cannot show which one worked.","distractorNotes":["The FDA granted emergency permission; it did not refuse.","Paragraph 8 says the bacterium did evolve resistance.",null,"Paragraph 8 says the phages went into his abscess and then into his bloodstream.","The passage says the case was published in 2017 and says nothing about review."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the future of phage therapy is best described as","choices":["cautiously optimistic","uncritically enthusiastic","nostalgic and regretful","openly dismissive","coldly indifferent"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"They are not a miracle, and anyone who promises one has not read the history, but for the first time since the 1930s they have a fair chance to prove what they can do."},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"a therapy cannot be judged on its best days"}],"why":"The last paragraph says phages are 'not a miracle' and warns against anyone who promises one, yet gives them 'a fair chance to prove what they can do.' Paragraph 9 adds that a therapy cannot be judged on its best days. Hope with the brakes on is cautious optimism.","distractorNotes":[null,"The author explicitly warns against promises of a miracle and against judging on best days.","The author looks forward to trials, not backward with regret.","A dismissive author would not say the need is not in doubt or give phages a fair chance.","Indifference does not fit an author who calls the need beyond doubt."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q15","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions food sprays in paragraph 10 primarily to","choices":["praise the Food and Drug Administration for its early approval in 2006","argue that phages are safer in food than in patients","highlight how little ground phages have gained as approved medicines","show that Listeria is the bacterium phages kill best","explain why Belgium adopted a different regulatory approach from the United States"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"rather than approving any phage as a medicine"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"were not drugs at all but food sprays"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 is about the regulatory obstacle. Right after saying no phage has been approved as a medicine in the United States, the author notes that the first accepted phage products were food sprays, 'not drugs at all,' which underlines how far phages still are from approval as medicine.","distractorNotes":["The sentence is pointed, not congratulatory; it stresses what has not happened.","The passage never compares safety in food with safety in patients.",null,"Listeria is simply the target of the spray; no claim about 'best' is made.","Belgium's approach is stated in the previous sentence and is not explained by the food sprays."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q16","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Belgium differs from the United States in that Belgium","choices":["lets pharmacists prepare phages for single patients","grants emergency permission on a case-by-case basis","requires a randomized trial before any phage may be used","has approved a phage cocktail as a licensed medicine","restricts phages to use on ready-to-eat foods"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"Belgium lets pharmacists prepare phages for individual patients under a defined quality standard"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 contrasts the United States, which grants case-by-case emergency permission, with Belgium, which lets pharmacists prepare phages for individual patients under a defined quality standard.","distractorNotes":[null,"Case-by-case emergency permission is the United States approach in the same sentence.","The passage calls for randomized trials in paragraph 11 but attributes no such requirement to Belgium.","Neither country is said to have approved a phage as a licensed medicine.","Food sprays are the United States example in the next sentence."]},{"id":"rc-o05-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Regulators should refuse to approve any medicine that is alive and changes inside the patient.","Bacteriophages are the most abundant organisms on Earth and will soon replace antibiotics.","Tom Patterson's recovery proved that phage cocktails can cure any antibiotic-resistant infection.","Phage therapy was abandoned in the West because the Soviet Union controlled the only phage collections.","Phage therapy, eclipsed by antibiotics, is returning with real promise and unresolved problems."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"bacteriophage, the eater of bacteria"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":7,"quote":"most Western physicians saw no reason to look back"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"the case that revived Western interest"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"they have a fair chance to prove what they can do"}],"why":"The passage walks from the discovery (paragraph 1) through the eclipse by antibiotics (paragraph 5), the revival after Patterson's case (paragraph 8), and the trials and regulatory problems still ahead (paragraphs 10 and 11). The answer captures the whole arc without overreaching.","distractorNotes":["The passage describes the regulatory problem but never argues that regulators should refuse.","Abundance is a detail of paragraph 2, and the passage never predicts phages will replace antibiotics.","Too strong: paragraph 9 says single cases cannot judge a therapy, and 'any' infection goes far beyond the passage.","Reverses the reason: the West turned to antibiotics, and the Soviet work was ignored, not withheld.",null]}]},"rc-o06":{"id":"rc-o06","title":"The Stripes That Moved the Continents","topic":"seafloor magnetic stripes and the acceptance of plate tectonics","field":"geology","source":"original","words":1369,"paragraphs":[["Few ideas in the Earth sciences have had a stranger career than continental drift.","In January 1912 the German meteorologist Alfred Wegener proposed that the continents had once been joined in a single landmass, which he later called Pangaea, and had since moved apart.","His book, The Origin of Continents and Oceans, appeared in 1915.","Wegener drew on several independent lines of evidence: the jigsaw fit of the South American and African coastlines, matching rock formations across the Atlantic, identical fossils on continents now an ocean apart, and signs of ancient glaciation in regions that today sit near the equator.","Any one of these could be explained away, but together they deserved a serious hearing."],["They did not get one.","Wegener could describe what had happened but not how, and the mechanism he offered, continents plowing through the ocean floor under tidal forces and a push away from the poles, did not survive scrutiny.","The Cambridge geophysicist Harold Jeffreys showed that these forces were far too weak to move anything.","At a 1926 symposium in New York, American geologists lined up to reject the hypothesis, some in openly mocking terms.","Wegener died on the Greenland ice sheet in 1930, and for three decades drift was treated in most English-speaking departments as a cautionary tale.","In hindsight the reaction looks less like healthy caution than a refusal to take good evidence seriously because it lacked a tidy explanation."],["The evidence that settled the matter came from the ocean floor, which in 1950 was almost entirely unmapped.","At the Lamont Geological Observatory in New York, Marie Tharp turned echo-sounding records into profiles of the Atlantic floor while Bruce Heezen collected the data at sea.","In 1952, plotting six profiles across the North Atlantic, Tharp noticed a deep notch at the crest of the Mid-Atlantic Ridge in every one and argued that it was a continuous rift valley down the center of the ridge.","Heezen at first dismissed the idea, but when earthquake epicenters from the ocean basins were plotted on the same map, they fell along the valley Tharp had drawn.","By 1957 the two had published a physiographic diagram of the North Atlantic floor, and later work traced the ridge and its rift around the globe as a mountain system roughly 65,000 kilometers long.","A rift valley is where crust is being pulled apart, and a rift that circles the planet is hard to reconcile with a static Earth."],["Harry Hess of Princeton supplied the idea that gave the rift its meaning.","By 1960 he had collected several puzzles: the ocean floor carried only a thin blanket of sediment, nothing truly ancient had been recovered from it, and heat flow was unusually high at the ridges.","His answer, circulated in 1960 and published in 1962, was that mantle convection brings hot material up beneath the ridges, where it forms new floor that spreads outward in both directions and is eventually carried back into the mantle at the deep trenches.","Hess called his paper an essay in geopoetry, and Robert Dietz, publishing independently in 1961, gave the process its lasting name: seafloor spreading."],["Spreading was still a hypothesis without a test, and the test came from an unlikely direction: the magnetism frozen into volcanic rock.","Basalt, the rock of the upper ocean crust, contains small grains of the iron oxide magnetite.","Above a threshold known as the Curie temperature, about 580 degrees Celsius for magnetite, the grains cannot hold a magnetic direction.","As a lava cools below that threshold, the grains lock in the direction and polarity of Earth's field at that moment, a property called thermoremanent magnetization.","Every basalt flow is therefore a compass reading taken on the day it solidified and kept ever since."],["Those readings revealed something odd.","In 1906 the French physicist Bernard Brunhes reported lavas in central France magnetized roughly opposite to the present field, and in 1929 Motonori Matuyama showed that reversed lavas in Japan and Manchuria were consistently older than normal ones.","The obvious conclusion, that Earth's field flips its polarity from time to time, was resisted for decades.","In the early 1960s Allan Cox, Richard Doell and Brent Dalrymple of the United States Geological Survey paired magnetic measurements of lava flows with potassium-argon dating of the same flows and built a calendar of reversals.","They named the most recent intervals after pioneers of geomagnetism: the Brunhes normal epoch, in which we live, the Matuyama reversed epoch before it, then the Gauss normal and Gilbert reversed epochs.","The Brunhes-Matuyama boundary is now placed at about 780,000 years ago, and reversals proved to come irregularly, typically a few hundred thousand years apart."],["In 1955 and 1956 a Coast and Geodetic Survey ship towing a magnetometer ran closely spaced lines off Washington, Oregon and California, and the map that Arthur Raff and Ronald Mason published in 1961 showed something nobody expected.","The field strength varied in long parallel bands, alternately slightly stronger and slightly weaker than the regional average, so that the floor looked striped like a zebra.","No one could say what had drawn the stripes."],["The explanation arrived in September 1963 in a short paper in Nature by Fred Vine, a graduate student at Cambridge, and his supervisor Drummond Matthews.","New basalt at a ridge records the field of the day, and spreading carries each strip away from the axis in both directions, so the floor should be a tape recording of the reversal history: alternating bands symmetric about the ridge crest, normal bands adding to the present field and reversed bands subtracting from it.","Lawrence Morley of the Geological Survey of Canada had reached the same conclusion independently earlier that year, but Nature and then an American journal rejected his manuscript, one reviewer remarking that the idea belonged at a cocktail party rather than in print."],["Even then, the reception was cool.","Lamont, which held an enormous archive of marine magnetic data, remained unconvinced.","Two developments in 1966 changed that.","First, Doell and Dalrymple identified a short normal interval inside the Matuyama epoch, the Jaramillo event, and once it was added to the calendar, seafloor profiles that had fit the model only roughly now fit it closely.","Second, in 1965 the research vessel Eltanin had crossed the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge on its nineteenth cruise towing a magnetometer, and when Walter Pitman and James Heirtzler plotted the profile, the anomalies on one side mirrored those on the other for hundreds of kilometers.","Folded about the ridge crest, the two halves of the Eltanin-19 profile matched peak for peak, and their spacing fit the reversal calendar if the floor spread at a few centimeters a year.","Shown at a conference in New York that November, the profile changed the minds of several prominent skeptics in the room."],["Once the stripes were accepted, the rest followed fast.","The distance of a dated reversal from the ridge divided by its age gave a spreading rate, and the rates measured in ocean after ocean were slow, steady and sufficient to carry Wegener's continents.","In 1967 and 1968 Jason Morgan, Dan McKenzie and Robert Parker, and Xavier Le Pichon described Earth's surface as a small number of rigid plates moving over a sphere, and the theory acquired its modern name, plate tectonics.","Drilling by the Glomar Challenger in the South Atlantic in 1968 and 1969 then found sediment on the basalt growing older with distance from the ridge at very nearly the predicted rate."],["The stripes are a direct physical record, written by the planet in its own rocks, of a process argued about for half a century.","The symmetry of the Eltanin-19 profile is, to my mind, among the most beautiful results in the Earth sciences: a single line of data that confirms spreading, reversals and drift at once, and that anyone can check with a ruler.","The rift valley was on Tharp's drafting table in 1952, Hess's essay was circulating in 1960, and the stripes were explained in 1963, yet most geologists did not come around until 1966 or later.","Caution has its place, but when the evidence is this clear, the delay says more about the habits of a profession than about the strength of the case."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-o06-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, which of the following was among the lines of evidence Wegener drew on for continental drift?","choices":["The unusually high heat flow measured at the mid-ocean ridges","Signs of ancient glaciation in regions now near the equator","Earthquake epicenters lined up along the Mid-Atlantic Ridge","The thin blanket of sediment covering the ocean floor","Parallel magnetic stripes on the floor of the Pacific Ocean"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"signs of ancient glaciation in regions that today sit near the equator"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 lists Wegener's evidence: the coastline fit, matching rocks, identical fossils, and signs of ancient glaciation in regions that now sit near the equator. The other choices are real details from the passage, but they belong to later discoveries by other people.","distractorNotes":["Heat flow at the ridges is one of Hess's puzzles in paragraph 4, not part of Wegener's case.",null,"The epicenters belong to Tharp and Heezen's rift valley in paragraph 3, decades after Wegener.","Thin sediment is a Hess puzzle from paragraph 4, not evidence Wegener used.","The stripes were found in the 1950s survey of paragraph 7; Wegener never saw them."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q02","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 1, the word 'hearing' most nearly means","choices":["the physical sense by which sound is perceived","a formal proceeding before a judge","a fair chance to be considered","a lecture delivered to an audience","a rumor passed from person to person"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"together they deserved a serious hearing"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 says the evidence 'deserved a serious hearing,' and paragraph 2 opens with 'They did not get one' and then describes geologists refusing the idea. So a hearing here is a fair chance to be considered, not the sense of hearing.","distractorNotes":["That is the everyday meaning of the word, but a body of evidence cannot literally be listened to.","No court or judge appears anywhere in the passage; the hearing is figurative.",null,"Wegener did give lectures, but the sentence is about how his evidence was received, not how it was delivered.","A rumor is unverified talk; the sentence is about evidence deserving serious consideration."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Harold Jeffreys's role in the debate over continental drift was to","choices":["show that the forces Wegener proposed were far too weak","organize the 1926 symposium that rejected the hypothesis","propose that convection in the mantle could carry the continents","argue that the fossil evidence could be explained away","supply the mechanism that Wegener's proposal had lacked"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"showed that these forces were far too weak to move anything"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says Jeffreys 'showed that these forces were far too weak to move anything.' He attacked Wegener's mechanism; he did not provide one of his own.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage names no organizer for the 1926 symposium and never places Jeffreys there.","Mantle convection is Hess's answer in paragraph 4, not anything Jeffreys proposed.","Paragraph 1 says any single line could be explained away, but that is the author's remark, not Jeffreys's argument.","Reversed: Jeffreys knocked down Wegener's mechanism rather than supplying a better one."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q04","type":"function","stem":"The author mentions earthquake epicenters in paragraph 3 primarily to","choices":["explain why Tharp was not allowed to collect data at sea","suggest that the Mid-Atlantic Ridge is volcanically active","show that the ocean floor was almost entirely unmapped in 1950","show what persuaded Heezen that the rift valley was real","demonstrate that the ridge system is 65,000 kilometers long"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"they fell along the valley Tharp had drawn"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says Heezen 'at first dismissed the idea, but when earthquake epicenters from the ocean basins were plotted on the same map, they fell along the valley Tharp had drawn.' The epicenters are there to show what changed his mind.","distractorNotes":["The passage never says why Tharp stayed ashore; it only says Heezen collected the data at sea.","Volcanic activity is not mentioned in paragraph 3; the epicenters tie to the rift, not to volcanoes.","The unmapped floor is the paragraph's opening claim and has nothing to do with the epicenters.",null,"The length of the ridge comes from later work in the next sentence, not from the epicenters."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the mid-ocean ridge and its central rift","choices":["were first noticed during the Eltanin's nineteenth cruise","form a mountain system roughly 65,000 kilometers long","were discovered by Harry Hess using a naval echo sounder","appear only in the six profiles Tharp plotted in 1952","were mapped completely before the Second World War"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"a mountain system roughly 65,000 kilometers long"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says later work 'traced the ridge and its rift around the globe as a mountain system roughly 65,000 kilometers long.'","distractorNotes":["The Eltanin crossed the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge in 1965 (paragraph 9); the rift was recognized in 1952.",null,"Hess supplied the idea that explained the rift (paragraph 4); Tharp and Heezen found it, and no echo sounder is tied to Hess.","Too narrow: the rift showed in all six profiles and was later traced around the globe.","Reversed: paragraph 3 says the floor was almost entirely unmapped in 1950."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q06","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that Hess's hypothesis accounted for the thin blanket of sediment on the ocean floor because","choices":["the deep trenches swallow sediment faster than it accumulates","the high heat flow at the ridges bakes the sediment away","the sediment settles mainly on the continents instead","the floor is continually renewed and so is never very old","Dietz had already shown that the sediment was thin in 1961"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"nothing truly ancient had been recovered from it"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"it forms new floor that spreads outward in both directions and is eventually carried back into the mantle at the deep trenches"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 pairs the puzzles (thin sediment, nothing ancient) with Hess's answer: new floor forms at the ridges and is carried back into the mantle at the trenches. If the floor is constantly made and destroyed it is young, and young floor has had little time to collect sediment.","distractorNotes":["The passage says the floor itself is carried into the trenches; it never says trenches remove sediment faster than it forms.","Heat flow is listed as a puzzle, not as a process that destroys sediment.","Nothing in the passage says sediment settles on the continents.",null,"Dietz is credited with the name seafloor spreading, not with any measurement of sediment."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q07","type":"except","stem":"According to paragraph 4, all of the following are true of Hess's proposal EXCEPT:","choices":["It was circulated in 1960, two years before it was published.","It invoked mantle convection beneath the mid-ocean ridges.","It had new floor carried back into the mantle at the trenches.","Hess himself described the paper as an essay in geopoetry.","Hess gave the process its lasting name, seafloor spreading."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"circulated in 1960 and published in 1962","choice":0},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"mantle convection brings hot material up beneath the ridges","choice":1},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"carried back into the mantle at the deep trenches","choice":2},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"Hess called his paper an essay in geopoetry","choice":3},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"Robert Dietz, publishing independently in 1961, gave the process its lasting name: seafloor spreading"}],"why":"Four choices come straight from paragraph 4. Its last sentence says it was Robert Dietz, not Hess, who gave the process the name seafloor spreading.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 4: 'circulated in 1960 and published in 1962.'","Stated in paragraph 4: 'mantle convection brings hot material up beneath the ridges.'","Stated in paragraph 4: new floor is 'eventually carried back into the mantle at the deep trenches.'","Stated in paragraph 4: 'Hess called his paper an essay in geopoetry.'",null]},{"id":"rc-o06-q08","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the Curie temperature is the temperature","choices":["at which basalt begins to melt into lava","at which Earth's magnetic field reverses its polarity","above which magnetite grains cannot hold a magnetic direction","below which potassium-argon dating of a lava flow becomes unreliable","at which hot material begins to rise beneath the ridges"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"the grains cannot hold a magnetic direction"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 defines it directly: above the Curie temperature, about 580 degrees Celsius for magnetite, 'the grains cannot hold a magnetic direction.'","distractorNotes":["Melting is never mentioned; the Curie temperature is about magnetism, not melting.","Reversals are a behavior of Earth's field (paragraph 6), not a temperature.",null,"Potassium-argon dating appears in paragraph 6 with no temperature attached to it.","That describes mantle convection from paragraph 4, not the Curie temperature."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that if an ancient basalt flow were reheated well above 580 degrees Celsius and then allowed to cool again, it would","choices":["record the field as it was at the time of the reheating","preserve its original compass reading unchanged","lose its magnetite grains entirely","record both the original and the new field at once","become impossible to date by the potassium-argon dating method"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"the grains cannot hold a magnetic direction"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"lock in the direction and polarity of Earth's field at that moment"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says that above the Curie temperature the grains cannot hold a direction, and that on cooling below it they lock in the field 'at that moment.' Reheating erases the old reading; cooling again writes a new one.","distractorNotes":[null,"Reversed: above the Curie temperature the grains cannot hold a direction, so the old reading is lost.","The passage says the grains lose their magnetic direction, not that they disappear.","The grains lock in the field 'at that moment,' a single reading, not two.","Nothing in the passage links the Curie temperature to potassium-argon dating."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q10","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Cox, Doell and Dalrymple built their calendar of reversals by","choices":["towing a magnetometer behind a research vessel across the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge","comparing the lavas of central France with those of Japan and Manchuria","folding seafloor magnetic profiles about the crest of the ridge","pairing magnetic measurements of lava flows with potassium-argon dates","drilling into the sediment that rests on the basalt of the South Atlantic"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"paired magnetic measurements of lava flows with potassium-argon dating of the same flows"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says they 'paired magnetic measurements of lava flows with potassium-argon dating of the same flows and built a calendar of reversals.'","distractorNotes":["That is the Eltanin survey in paragraph 9, worked up by Pitman and Heirtzler.","Brunhes and Matuyama studied those lavas decades earlier; the calendar came from dated flows.","Folding the profile is what revealed the Eltanin-19 symmetry in paragraph 9.",null,"Drilling is the Glomar Challenger work described in paragraph 10."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q11","type":"except","stem":"According to the passage, all of the following are true of geomagnetic reversals EXCEPT:","choices":["Brunhes reported reversely magnetized lavas in France in 1906.","Matuyama found that reversed lavas were older than normal ones.","The idea that the field flips was resisted for decades.","The Brunhes-Matuyama boundary is now placed near 780,000 years ago.","Reversals recur at a regular interval of about 780,000 years."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"In 1906 the French physicist Bernard Brunhes reported lavas in central France magnetized roughly opposite to the present field","choice":0},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"reversed lavas in Japan and Manchuria were consistently older than normal ones","choice":1},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"was resisted for decades","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"now placed at about 780,000 years ago","choice":3},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"reversals proved to come irregularly, typically a few hundred thousand years apart"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 confirms the other four choices. Its last sentence says reversals 'come irregularly, typically a few hundred thousand years apart,' so a fixed 780,000-year interval is contradicted; 780,000 years is the age of one boundary, not a repeating period.","distractorNotes":["Stated in paragraph 6: Brunhes, 1906, lavas in central France magnetized opposite to the present field.","Stated in paragraph 6: reversed lavas in Japan and Manchuria were 'consistently older than normal ones.'","Stated in paragraph 6: the conclusion 'was resisted for decades.'","Stated in paragraph 6: the boundary is 'now placed at about 780,000 years ago.'",null]},{"id":"rc-o06-q12","type":"function","stem":"The author describes Lawrence Morley's experience in paragraph 8 primarily to","choices":["suggest that Vine and Matthews had taken the idea from his rejected manuscript","explain why Nature was the most prestigious journal in the field","show that the magnetic stripes were first discovered in Canada","illustrate how unwelcome the idea was even when it was right","argue that cocktail parties are where good science is first discussed"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"one reviewer remarking that the idea belonged at a cocktail party rather than in print"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says Morley reached the same conclusion independently, yet two journals rejected it and a reviewer mocked it. The episode shows the field dismissing a correct idea, which feeds the author's larger point about slow acceptance.","distractorNotes":["The passage says Morley reached the conclusion 'independently,' which is the opposite of anyone borrowing it.","Nothing is said about Nature's prestige; it is simply where the Vine and Matthews paper appeared.","The stripes were found off Washington, Oregon and California (paragraph 7), not in Canada.",null,"The cocktail party remark was a reviewer's insult, not a recommendation from the author."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q13","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that a band of seafloor where the measured field is slightly stronger than the regional average most likely","choices":["formed when the field had the same polarity as today","lies directly beneath the crest of the mid-ocean ridge","is older than the reversed bands on either side of it","contains more magnetite than the neighboring bands","formed during the Matuyama reversed epoch"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"normal bands adding to the present field and reversed bands subtracting from it"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 explains that normal bands add to the present field and reversed bands subtract from it. A slightly stronger reading therefore marks floor that cooled when the field pointed the way it does now.","distractorNotes":[null,"Position relative to the crest tells you age, not polarity; stronger bands occur at many distances from it.","Age depends on distance from the ridge (paragraph 10), not on polarity; one neighbor is older and one is younger.","The passage ties the stripes to polarity, not to how much magnetite a band contains.","Reversed: the Matuyama epoch was reversed, and a reversed band subtracts from the present field."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q14","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the Eltanin-19 profile was striking because","choices":["it was the first magnetic survey ever run across an ocean ridge","it revealed the Jaramillo event for the first time","it showed the ocean floor spreading far faster than anyone had believed possible","it was gathered off the Pacific coast of North America","its anomalies on one side of the ridge mirrored those on the other"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"the anomalies on one side mirrored those on the other for hundreds of kilometers"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says that when Pitman and Heirtzler plotted the profile, 'the anomalies on one side mirrored those on the other for hundreds of kilometers,' and that folded about the crest the two halves matched peak for peak.","distractorNotes":["The passage never calls it the first; an earlier magnetometer survey appears in paragraph 7.","Doell and Dalrymple identified the Jaramillo event from lava flows, not from this profile.","The passage gives a rate of a few centimeters a year and calls spreading slow and steady (paragraph 10).","That is the Raff and Mason survey of paragraph 7; the Eltanin crossed the Pacific-Antarctic Ridge.",null]},{"id":"rc-o06-q15","type":"application","stem":"Which of the following best illustrates the principle described in paragraph 5?","choices":["A compass needle continuously swings to follow the field wherever it is carried","Wet cement poured over a leaf hardens and keeps the leaf's imprint for decades","A thermometer on a wall rises and falls with the temperature of the room","Iron filings line up along the field lines when a magnet is held beneath the paper","A weather vane turns to point into whatever wind is blowing at the moment"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"the grains lock in the direction and polarity of Earth's field at that moment"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"a compass reading taken on the day it solidified and kept ever since"}],"why":"Paragraph 5's principle is that a material locks in a record of conditions at the moment it solidifies and keeps that record afterward. Cement hardening around a leaf does exactly that; the other cases respond to present conditions and keep no record.","distractorNotes":["A compass tracks the current field and keeps no record, the opposite of locking in a reading.",null,"A thermometer responds to the present temperature and remembers nothing once it changes.","Filings show the field at this moment; move the magnet and the pattern changes with it.","A weather vane follows the present wind and keeps no record of past winds."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q16","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the geological profession's handling of continental drift is best described as","choices":["unreserved admiration","puzzled curiosity","measured criticism","bitter contempt","complete indifference"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"a refusal to take good evidence seriously because it lacked a tidy explanation"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"the delay says more about the habits of a profession than about the strength of the case"}],"why":"The judging words are in paragraph 2 ('less like healthy caution than a refusal to take good evidence seriously') and paragraph 11 ('says more about the habits of a profession'). The author faults the delay but grants that 'caution has its place,' so the criticism is measured rather than bitter.","distractorNotes":["Admiration is reserved for the evidence, especially the Eltanin-19 profile, not for the profession's response.","The author is not puzzled; the delay is explained as a habit of the profession.",null,"Too strong: 'caution has its place' and the respectful account of the scientists rule out contempt.","The author plainly cares, calling the evidence beautiful and the delay telling."]},{"id":"rc-o06-q17","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Seafloor magnetic stripes supplied the evidence that turned continental drift into plate tectonics","Alfred Wegener was the first scientist to propose that the continents had once been joined","Marie Tharp's discovery of the rift valley was unfairly credited to Bruce Heezen","Earth's magnetic field reverses its polarity at irregular intervals of a few hundred thousand years","The symmetric Eltanin-19 profile was the single piece of evidence that persuaded geologists to accept seafloor spreading"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"the test came from an unlikely direction: the magnetism frozen into volcanic rock"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"the floor should be a tape recording of the reversal history"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"The stripes are a direct physical record, written by the planet in its own rocks, of a process argued about for half a century"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"a single line of data that confirms spreading, reversals and drift at once"}],"why":"The passage opens with drift rejected for lack of a mechanism, then builds to the magnetic test (paragraph 5), the stripe prediction (paragraph 8) and the author's verdict in paragraph 11 that the stripes are the record that settled the argument. The answer covers that whole arc.","distractorNotes":[null,"True, but it is only paragraph 1; the passage is about how drift was finally proven.","The passage never says credit was taken from Tharp; it says Heezen was at first unconvinced.","A detail from paragraph 6, one piece of the story rather than its point.","Too strong: the Jaramillo event and the Glomar Challenger drilling also mattered."]}]},"rc-p01":{"id":"rc-p01","title":"How Tardigrades Survive Drying Out","topic":"cryptobiosis in tardigrades","field":"zoology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1680,"paragraphs":[["A square of moss on a north facing roof tile spends much of the summer as a brittle gray crust.","Crumble a pinch of it into a watch glass, add rainwater, and wait twenty minutes.","Under a dissecting scope the crust becomes a crowd.","Something plump hauls itself forward on eight stubby legs with the slow gait that earned it the name water bear.","Nothing in the dry crust looked alive.","Nothing in it was dead, either."],["Tardigrades are a phylum of their own, segmented invertebrates mostly between a tenth and half a millimeter long.","They have four pairs of clawed legs and a pair of stylets backed by a sucking pharynx.","They live in ocean sediment, in soil, and in the thin films of water that cling to moss and lichen.","That last habitat is the interesting one.","A tardigrade is an aquatic animal.","It walks, feeds, and reproduces only while surrounded by water, and the water in a moss cushion is gone by noon."],["The answer is cryptobiosis, from the Greek for hidden life.","A cryptobiotic animal is not dormant the way a hibernating mammal is, because hibernation is a slowed metabolism and cryptobiosis is, as far as any instrument can tell, no metabolism at all.","Biologists sort it by the insult that triggers it: freezing gives cryobiosis, lack of oxygen gives anoxybiosis, and a sudden rise in outside salt concentration gives osmobiosis.","Drying gives anhydrobiosis, and that is the one the tardigrade performs best."],["The change into the dried form, called a tun after the word for a small barrel, is orderly and takes hours.","The animal pulls its head and legs inward, folds its cuticle into lengthwise creases, and contracts until its surface area drops by roughly half, which slows the escape of the water still inside.","Body water falls from about eighty percent of fresh mass to a few percent or less.","Measured metabolic rate falls below a hundredth of one percent of the active rate, which is below the detection limit of the instruments pointed at it.","A tardigrade dried gradually, over several hours in air that is still fairly humid, usually survives.","The same animal dropped into dry air and desiccated within minutes usually does not, because the tun is a program that needs time to run, not a passive collapse."],["Ordinary drying wrecks an ordinary cell.","Water is not only the medium chemistry happens in.","It is a structural component.","A phospholipid membrane holds its spacing because water molecules hydrogen bond to the lipid head groups.","Pull the water out and the heads crowd together, the sheet stiffens into a different phase, and when water is added back the membrane cracks along the boundaries between phases and leaks its contents."],["The first molecule offered as an answer was trehalose, a sugar of two glucose units joined head to head.","Because both anomeric carbons are tied up in that linkage, trehalose is nonreducing: it has no free aldehyde group waiting to react with the amino groups of nearby proteins, so it can be packed around delicate machinery at high concentration without slowly browning it.","Under the water replacement idea, its hydroxyl groups take over the hydrogen bonds that departing water molecules leave behind, propping membrane head groups apart near their normal spacing."],["The second proposal is that trehalose turns the cell interior into a glass.","A glass here is not a windowpane but a physical state: a liquid dried or cooled so far past the point of easy molecular motion that it seizes up without ever arranging itself into a crystal.","It has the disorder of a liquid and the rigidity of a solid.","Diffusion through it is slowed by many orders of magnitude, so two molecules that would ruin each other on contact never travel far enough to meet.","A tun does not so much repair damage as refuse to let the reactions that cause it get started."],["Trehalose, though, cannot carry the whole account, and the field has been quietly uncomfortable about that for years.","Brine shrimp cysts and anhydrobiotic nematodes load themselves with the sugar, in some cases to a fifth of their dry weight.","Tardigrades accumulate far less, and in several species that dry and revive as reliably as any, trehalose is present only in traces or cannot be detected at all.","A molecule missing from an animal that performs the trick cannot be the whole explanation of the trick."],["What fills the gap appears to be proteins that break the usual rule about protein structure.","Most proteins fold into one dominant shape and do one job.","Intrinsically disordered proteins have no stable fold in solution, existing instead as a shifting ensemble of shapes, and they are promiscuous: rather than matching a single partner the way a key matches one lock, they drape themselves over many different partners and take whatever form suits the one they meet.","Three families are abundant in tardigrades, each named for where it sits and how it behaves in a hot test tube: the cytoplasmic, the secretory, and the mitochondrial abundant heat soluble proteins.","They stay in solution when boiled, which is itself a signature of disorder."],["Their behavior on drying is the striking part.","As water leaves, the cytoplasmic family condenses into a network of fibers and a gel that stiffens into the glassy state, so one molecule serves as both scaffold and vitrifying agent.","The clearest sign that they do real work is that the trick travels.","Move the tardigrade genes for them into yeast or bacteria, organisms that ordinarily die when dried, and the engineered cells survive desiccation at rates their unmodified relatives do not."],["Protection of the genome has its own machinery.","Tardigrades carry a chromatin binding protein nicknamed the damage suppressor, which associates with DNA and appears to shield it from the hydroxyl radicals that radiation and oxidative stress generate.","Human cells grown in culture with the tardigrade gene added show measurably less DNA breakage after an X ray dose.","Shielding is only half the story.","When a tun takes up water it switches on a vigorous repair response, and part of what looks like invulnerability from outside is a first hour of frantic proofreading within."],["Here the popular version drifts away from the biology.","Anhydrobiosis suspends metabolism.","It does not suspend chemistry, and it certainly does not suspend time.","Inside a tun, slowly and without enzymes, oxygen still attacks lipids, sugars still condense onto protein amino groups, amino acids slowly change their handedness, and the DNA backbone still breaks.","The glass slows all of this by orders of magnitude.","It does not stop it.","The consequence is measurable: the longer a population stays dry, the smaller the fraction that walks away on rewetting, and the longer the survivors take to get moving.","Revival after several years in a dry moss sample is documented.","The much repeated figure of more than a century rests on a single mid twentieth century report that no one has reproduced, and it is better read as folklore than as data."],["The rest of the folklore has the same shape: a real measurement with its conditions stripped off.","Tuns have been carried into low Earth orbit, exposed to vacuum and solar ultraviolet, and some came back.","Tuns have been held near absolute zero and above the boiling point of water, and some came back.","Tuns tolerate pressures many times those of the deepest ocean trench.","Every one of those sentences is true, and every one describes a tun, briefly, with a survival fraction well short of everything.","The active, hydrated animal is not impressive at all.","Left in water held a little above normal human body temperature it can die within a day, and it is eaten by mites and by other tardigrades."],["Seen from the animal's side, none of the extremes are the point.","Nothing in a tardigrade's history has ever asked it to survive orbit.","What its history asks, over and over, is that a film of water on a moss leaf vanish on Tuesday and return with Friday's rain.","The machinery that answers that, a cell interior converted into an inert glass, happens also to be indifferent to vacuum, to cold, and to much radiation, because all three do their damage by moving molecules around and there are no molecules free to move.","The tardigrade is not built to be indestructible.","It is built to be dry, and indestructibility is a side effect."],["That side effect is the part with an address in medicine.","A great deal of modern therapy is a fragile molecule in a cold room.","Vaccines, antibody drugs, clotting factors, and living cells are shipped and stored on refrigeration, and the chain that keeps them cold is expensive, power hungry, and the first thing to fail in a rural clinic or a disaster zone.","A preparation that could sit on a shelf as a dry powder and be brought back with sterile water would change the logistics of global immunization."],["The tardigrade suggests how to build one, and why the obvious attempt falls short.","Trehalose is already a common stabilizer in freeze dried products, but sugar alone gives a brittle glass that crystallizes over time or pulls moisture from the air and softens, and a softened glass lets molecules move again.","The lesson of the tun is that the sugar was never working alone.","Laboratory work has shown that adding a tardigrade disordered protein to a human clotting factor preparation lets it be dried and rewetted with much of its activity intact.","The unsolved problems are the ones the animal faces too: the drying has to be slow and controlled, and anything thicker than half a millimeter dries from the outside in.","An organ is not a tun, and no one should promise that it will become one soon."],["Back on the roof tile, the moss is gray again by afternoon.","The animals in it are not waiting, in any sense that involves an experience of waiting.","They are glass, and the glass keeps until the rain comes."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p01-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the water content of a tardigrade in the tun state falls to","choices":["a few percent of its fresh mass or less","one fifth of its fresh mass","less than a hundredth of one percent of its fresh mass","roughly half of its fresh mass","about eighty percent of its fresh mass"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"to a few percent or less"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 gives the number outright: body water drops from about eighty percent of fresh mass to a few percent or less. The other figures in that same paragraph belong to different measurements, which is what makes this one worth reading slowly.","distractorNotes":[null,"A fifth of dry weight is the trehalose load of brine shrimp cysts in paragraph 8, a different animal and a different quantity.","Below a hundredth of one percent is the metabolic rate inside a tun, not its water content.","Roughly half is how much the animal's surface area shrinks, not how much water it keeps.","Eighty percent is the starting point, the water content of an active animal, not where it ends up."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage indicates that a tardigrade dried within minutes in very dry air usually dies because","choices":["sudden drying tears the cuticle along its lengthwise creases","rapid water loss strips away the trehalose already stored in its tissues","tun formation is a program the animal needs hours to run","quick drying leaves too much water behind for a glass to form","the animal cannot take in enough oxygen while a tun is forming"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":6,"quote":"the tun is a program that needs time to run"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 sets the two schedules side by side. Dried gradually over several hours in humid air, the animal usually survives; dropped into dry air and desiccated in minutes, it usually does not, because the tun is a program that needs time to run rather than a passive collapse.","distractorNotes":["Those creases are part of normal tun formation in paragraph 4, not a failure point the passage describes.","The passage never ties drying speed to trehalose loss, and paragraph 8 says several species get by with almost none of the sugar.",null,"Leftover water is a problem for dried medical preparations in paragraph 16, not the reason fast drying kills the animal.","Oxygen supply is not the issue here; lack of oxygen appears in paragraph 3 only as a separate trigger, for anoxybiosis."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q03","type":"detail","stem":"The passage says trehalose is nonreducing, and treats that as important because the sugar therefore","choices":["can be stored at a fifth of the animal's total dry weight","has no free aldehyde group to react with protein amino groups","resists being broken down by the ordinary enzymes of the cell","arranges itself into a crystal as the last water is removed","cannot form hydrogen bonds with the head groups of a phospholipid membrane"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"no free aldehyde group waiting to react with the amino groups"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 defines the term as it goes: both anomeric carbons are tied up in the linkage, so there is no free aldehyde waiting to react with the amino groups of nearby proteins. That is what lets the sugar be packed in at high concentration without slowly browning the machinery it surrounds.","distractorNotes":["A fifth of dry weight describes brine shrimp cysts in paragraph 8, and it is a quantity rather than a consequence of being nonreducing.",null,"Enzyme resistance is never mentioned; the passage ties the nonreducing character to reactivity with proteins.","Paragraph 7 says the opposite, that the glassy state forms without ever arranging into a crystal.","This reverses paragraph 6, which says the sugar's hydroxyl groups take over exactly those hydrogen bonds."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q04","type":"detail","stem":"Which of the following does the passage offer as evidence that trehalose alone cannot account for tardigrade survival?","choices":["Trehalose crystallizes inside a tun within a few years of drying","Some tardigrade species revive reliably with little or no detectable trehalose","Trehalose is absent from the dried cysts and seeds of every other organism studied","Tardigrades store more trehalose than brine shrimp cysts do","Trehalose cannot enter the cytoplasm until a tun has already formed"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"trehalose is present only in traces or cannot be detected at all"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"cannot be the whole explanation of the trick"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 makes the argument directly. Brine shrimp cysts and anhydrobiotic nematodes load up on the sugar, tardigrades accumulate far less, and in several species that dry and revive as well as any, trehalose is present only in traces or cannot be found at all.","distractorNotes":["Crystallizing sugar is a problem for dried medical formulations in paragraph 16, not something reported inside a tun.",null,"The passage names two other organisms that are full of the sugar, so this is contradicted on the page.","Paragraph 8 says the reverse: the cysts load themselves with the sugar and tardigrades accumulate far less.","Nothing in the passage describes trehalose being kept out of the cytoplasm until a tun forms."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The protein nicknamed the damage suppressor is described as acting on","choices":["DNA, by binding chromatin and shielding it","the trehalose stored in the cytoplasm","the phospholipids of the cell membrane","the repair enzymes switched on at rewetting","the folded shapes of the heat soluble proteins"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"which associates with DNA and appears to shield it"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 introduces it as a chromatin binding protein that associates with DNA and appears to shield it from the hydroxyl radicals that radiation and oxidative stress generate.","distractorNotes":[null,"This protein binds DNA, and the passage never connects it to the sugar.","Membranes are handled by trehalose in paragraph 6, not by this protein.","Repair does switch on at rewetting in paragraph 11, but that is a separate response rather than what this protein binds.","The heat soluble families in paragraph 9 have no stable folded shape at all, and this protein is not described as acting on them."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, \"glass\" most nearly means","choices":["a rigid solid state that keeps a liquid's disorder","a small vessel used for holding and viewing a specimen","a crystalline solid formed as the last water is removed","a thin protective coating laid down over a membrane","a hard, transparent material of the kind used in windows"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"the disorder of a liquid and the rigidity of a solid"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"without ever arranging itself into a crystal"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 defines the word for the reader before using it: not a windowpane but a physical state, with the disorder of a liquid and the rigidity of a solid, in which diffusion is slowed by many orders of magnitude.","distractorNotes":[null,"A watch glass shows up in paragraph 1, but that is a piece of labware and has nothing to do with the state described here.","Paragraph 7 says the material seizes up without ever arranging itself into a crystal, so crystalline is the opposite of the sense meant.","Coating surfaces is what trehalose does in paragraph 6, while the glass is a state of the whole cell interior.","This is the everyday meaning, and the passage rules it out in the very sentence that introduces the term."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 9, \"promiscuous\" most nearly means","choices":["changing chemically whenever the surrounding water leaves","moving freely between the cytoplasm and the mitochondria","present in unusually large amounts inside the cell","able to bind many different partners rather than one","reproducing without regard to a particular mate"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"they drape themselves over many different partners"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 explains the word in the same sentence it appears: instead of matching one partner the way a key matches one lock, these proteins drape over many different partners and take whatever shape suits the one they meet.","distractorNotes":["The proteins do condense on drying in paragraph 10, but that is a change of state, not what the word means here.","The three family names say where each protein sits, not that any of them travels between compartments.","Abundance is built into the family names in that paragraph, but it is a separate property from the binding behavior this word describes.",null,"This is the everyday sense of the word, and the paragraph is describing molecular binding rather than mating."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly supports which statement about a tardigrade while it is in the tun state?","choices":["It repairs oxidative damage more slowly than an active animal does","It is chemically inert until water is added back to it","It loses the ability to revive at a steady, predictable rate","It accumulates damage that it cannot fix until water returns","It will survive indefinitely as long as it is kept dry"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"oxygen still attacks lipids"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":6,"quote":"It does not stop it."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"When a tun takes up water it switches on a vigorous repair response"}],"why":"Two paragraphs combine here. Paragraph 12 says damage keeps building in the dry state even though metabolism is suspended, and paragraph 11 says the repair response switches on when a tun takes up water. So the fixing waits for rehydration.","distractorNotes":["This assumes repair is still happening, only slower, but the passage places metabolism, and with it repair, at zero until water returns.","Paragraph 12 says the opposite: chemistry continues in the dry state even though metabolism does not.","Paragraph 12 says the surviving fraction falls the longer a sample stays dry, but it never claims the decline is steady or predictable.",null,"Paragraph 12 rules this out, since survival drops with time dried and the century long claim is treated as folklore."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Based on the passage, a hydrated, active tardigrade exposed to radiation would most likely be","choices":["less well protected than a tun, because its molecules are free to move","protected about as well as a tun, since the damage suppressor is always present","killed outright, since active animals carry no protective proteins","protected only if it had stored trehalose beforehand","unaffected, because radiation damage requires a dry cell interior"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"there are no molecules free to move"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":6,"quote":"The active, hydrated animal is not impressive at all."}],"why":"Paragraph 14 gives the reason the tun shrugs off vacuum, cold, and much radiation: all three do their damage by moving molecules, and in a glass nothing is free to move. A hydrated animal has no glass, so that particular shield is missing.","distractorNotes":[null,"The damage suppressor does help, but paragraph 14 credits the broad tolerance to the glassy state, which a hydrated animal does not have.","This overshoots. Paragraph 11 describes DNA shielding in tardigrade cells without restricting it to tuns.","Trehalose belongs to the drying response, and paragraph 8 notes that some species manage with almost none of it.","Nothing ties radiation damage to dryness; paragraph 11 has radicals arising from radiation and oxidative stress generally."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the central point of the passage?","choices":["Tardigrades are the most durable animals known, and no laboratory has yet matched their tolerances","Surviving drying is a chemical strategy that slows damage rather than a form of invulnerability","Experiments in low Earth orbit have produced most of what is known about drying","The tardigrade genome should become the standard model for studying radiation damage","A single sugar, trehalose, accounts for the ability of small animals to survive without water"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"The tardigrade is not built to be indestructible."},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":6,"quote":"indestructibility is a side effect"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"The glass slows all of this by orders of magnitude."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"refuse to let the reactions that cause it get started"}],"why":"The whole passage builds toward this. The tun works by turning the cell interior into a glass that keeps damaging reactions from getting started, which is a specific and limited trick, and paragraphs 15 and 16 argue that the trick, not toughness, is what dried medicines might borrow.","distractorNotes":["Paragraphs 12 and 13 argue against this framing, calling the famous tolerances real measurements with their conditions stripped off.",null,"Orbital exposure appears once, in paragraph 13, as an example of overstated folklore rather than as a main source of knowledge.","The passage never recommends a model organism, and radiation is a side topic inside the drying story.","Paragraph 8 takes this apart, since several species dry and revive with almost no detectable trehalose."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 8 serves mainly to","choices":["establish trehalose as the central molecule of anhydrobiosis","argue that brine shrimp survive drying better than tardigrades do","compare the drying speed of tardigrades with that of nematodes","introduce the three families of heat soluble proteins by name","show that the leading single molecule explanation is incomplete"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"cannot carry the whole account"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"cannot be the whole explanation of the trick"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 exists to open a gap. It grants that trehalose does real work in other animals, then points out that tardigrades carry far less and some carry none, which is exactly what makes the disordered proteins of paragraph 9 necessary.","distractorNotes":["That is paragraph 6's job, and paragraph 8 pulls in the opposite direction.","The comparison is about how much sugar each organism stores, not about which one dries better.","Drying speed is a paragraph 4 topic and is not the comparison being drawn here.","The three families are named in paragraph 9, after this paragraph has cleared the ground for them.",null]},{"id":"rc-p01-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["A medical problem, then a survey of the organisms that might solve it, then a proposed trial","A definition, then a debate between two research camps, then a verdict in favor of one camp","An observation, then its mechanism, then a correction of a popular overstatement, then a use for it","A popular claim, then the experiments that were said to confirm it, then a call for more study","A history of the field, then the single discovery that changed it, then the questions that remain open"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"Under a dissecting scope the crust becomes a crowd."},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":1,"quote":"The answer is cryptobiosis"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"Here the popular version drifts away from the biology."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"That side effect is the part with an address in medicine."}],"why":"The passage opens with moss on a roof tile, works through the tun, trehalose, the glassy state, and the disordered proteins, spends paragraphs 12 through 14 walking the indestructibility story back, and closes on dried medicines.","distractorNotes":["The cold chain problem arrives late, in paragraph 15, rather than at the start, and no trial is proposed.","Trehalose and the disordered proteins are presented as partial and complementary answers, not as rival camps with a winner named.",null,"The passage does the reverse of confirming the popular claim, and it ends with an application rather than a call for study.","There is no history of the field, and no single discovery is presented as the turning point."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the claim that tardigrades have revived after more than a century is best described as","choices":["cautiously hopeful","angrily dismissive","openly admiring","plainly skeptical","entirely neutral"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":9,"quote":"better read as folklore than as data"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":9,"quote":"no one has reproduced"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 grants that revival after several years is documented, then says the century long figure rests on one report nobody has reproduced and is better read as folklore than as data. That is doubt, stated calmly.","distractorNotes":["Hope would suggest the author expects the claim to hold up, but the passage files it under folklore.","The tone stays measured, and the author faults the evidence rather than the people repeating the claim.","Admiration is not the note here; the author sets the claim aside rather than celebrating it.",null,"Neutrality would mean reporting the claim without judging it, and the author judges it in the same sentence."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In discussing what the tardigrade's chemistry might do for medicine, the author's stance is best described as","choices":["doubtful that dried preparations will ever be more than a curiosity","indifferent to whether the approach ever reaches patients","interested in the approach and candid about its limits","convinced that room temperature storage of tissue is close at hand","impatient with researchers who have failed to solve the problem"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"would change the logistics of global immunization"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":6,"quote":"no one should promise that it will become one soon"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 treats the sugar plus protein route as worth building on, reports a clotting factor dried and rewetted with much of its activity intact, then lists what is unsolved and ends by saying an organ is not a tun and nobody should promise that it will be one soon.","distractorNotes":["Doubt that deep does not fit a paragraph reporting a clotting factor dried and rewetted with most of its activity intact.","Paragraph 15 spends its length on rural clinics and disaster zones, which shows the author cares about reaching patients.",null,"The closing sentence of paragraph 16 refuses exactly this optimism about anything thicker than half a millimeter.","No blame is placed on researchers; the obstacles are described as the same ones the animal itself faces."]},{"id":"rc-p01-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Some tuns have survived exposure to the vacuum of low Earth orbit","An active tardigrade can be killed by water a little warmer than the human body","Certain tardigrade species contain little or no detectable trehalose","Yeast or bacteria given tardigrade genes can survive drying better than usual","A tun protects itself by running its metabolism at a low but steady rate"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"exposed to vacuum and solar ultraviolet, and some came back","choice":0},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":7,"quote":"it can die within a day","choice":1},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"trehalose is present only in traces or cannot be detected at all","choice":2},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"the engineered cells survive desiccation at rates their unmodified relatives do not","choice":3},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"no metabolism at all","choice":4},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"below the detection limit of the instruments","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraphs 3 and 12 are explicit that cryptobiosis is no measurable metabolism at all rather than a slowed one, and paragraph 7 credits the protection to a glass that keeps reactions from starting. Each of the other four statements sits on the page.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 13 reports tuns carried into low Earth orbit and exposed to vacuum, with some coming back.","Paragraph 13 says a hydrated animal left in water a little above normal body temperature can die within a day.","Paragraph 8 says trehalose is present only in traces, or not detectable at all, in several reliable species.","Paragraph 10 says engineered yeast and bacteria survive desiccation at rates their unmodified relatives do not.",null]},{"id":"rc-p01-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for the physical reason a dried membrane leaks when water is added back to it should turn to","choices":["paragraph 3","paragraph 14","paragraph 7","paragraph 11","paragraph 5"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"the membrane cracks along the boundaries between phases and leaks its contents"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 walks through the whole sequence: water normally hydrogen bonds to the lipid head groups, removing it lets the heads crowd together and the sheet shift phase, and on rewetting the membrane cracks at the phase boundaries and leaks.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 defines cryptobiosis and its varieties without touching membranes.","Paragraph 14 argues that the extreme tolerances are a side effect of drying, with no membrane detail.","Paragraph 7 describes the glassy state, which concerns diffusion through the cell interior rather than membrane structure.","Paragraph 11 is about DNA shielding and the repair response at rewetting.",null]}]},"rc-p02":{"id":"rc-p02","title":"How Concrete Sets and How It Fails","topic":"the chemistry of Portland cement and concrete","field":"materials chemistry","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1703,"paragraphs":[["By weight, people make more concrete than any other manufactured thing.","It fills foundations, bridge decks, dams, and the floors of nearly every building taller than a house, and most people never ask how it works.","The answer is chemical, and stranger than the material looks.","Concrete does not dry into a solid.","It grows one."],["The story starts in a quarry and a kiln.","The binder in ordinary concrete is Portland cement, a powder made by grinding and burning roughly four parts limestone to one part clay or shale.","Limestone is calcium carbonate, and the clay supplies silica, alumina, and iron.","That mixture, called raw meal, enters the high end of a long steel tube lined with firebrick, tilted a few degrees and turning slowly, and tumbles downhill toward a flame.","Near nine hundred degrees Celsius the limestone gives up its carbon dioxide and becomes calcium oxide, or quicklime.","This step is called calcination, and it is the source of both the usefulness and the trouble of cement."],["What turns that lime into cement happens farther down the tube, where the temperature reaches about fourteen hundred and fifty degrees.","There the quicklime and the silica and alumina from the clay fuse partly into a melt and crystallize into new compounds, leaving the kiln as dark nodules the size of gravel called clinker.","Four phases matter.","Tricalcium silicate, known in the trade as alite, makes up half to two thirds of the clinker and does most of the early work.","Dicalcium silicate, called belite, makes up something like a fifth and works slowly.","Tricalcium aluminate and a calcium aluminoferrite fill out the rest, the first alarmingly quick to react and the second responsible for the gray color."],["Clinker is cooled and ground to a powder finer than flour, with a small amount of gypsum, usually three to five percent by mass, ground in.","The gypsum is not filler.","Without it the tricalcium aluminate would react within minutes and seize the batch into a useless stiff mass, a failure called flash set.","Sulfate from the gypsum reaches the aluminate first and builds needles of a hydrated calcium sulfoaluminate called ettringite around each grain, which slows the aluminate down and buys the hours a crew needs to place the batch."],["Add water and the real reaction begins.","The calcium silicates do not dissolve and then dry.","They react with the water and precipitate a new solid, a poorly crystalline calcium silicate hydrate that chemists write as C-S-H and everyone else can call the glue.","It grows outward from every cement grain as a dense tangle of sheets and fibers that locks the sand and stone together.","The same reaction releases calcium hydroxide, a crystalline mineral called portlandite, which is not much of a glue but which matters enormously later."],["The two silicates set the pace.","Alite hydrates quickly and supplies most of the strength gained in the first week, which is why the trade tests cylinders at twenty eight days but watches them at three and seven.","Belite hydrates slowly and keeps adding strength for months and years, so a structure quietly gets stronger long after the forms come off.","A cement heavier in belite runs cool and gains late, an advantage in a massive dam, where the heat of reaction would crack the mass as it cooled."],["Here the common intuition fails.","Concrete does not harden by drying out.","Water is a reactant, built permanently into the C-S-H, and hydration stops when the water runs out, whether or not the surface looks dry.","Curing, as the word is used on a site, means keeping the water in: misting the slab, covering it with wet burlap, ponding it.","Concrete that is still green, soft enough to take a thumbprint and not yet carrying load, is exactly the concrete most easily ruined by a hot dry afternoon.","Roughly four parts water to ten parts cement by mass is enough to hydrate all of it.","Crews routinely add more, because a wetter mix is easier to place, and every extra measure leaves a capillary pore behind once it evaporates.","Those pores are where strength goes, and they are the roads by which everything that later attacks the concrete gets in."],["The kiln, meanwhile, has a problem no engineer can design away.","Roughly half a tonne of carbon dioxide leaves the limestone for every tonne of clinker made, purely as stoichiometry, before a single unit of fuel is counted.","Firing the kiln and grinding the product push the total to something near eight tenths of a tonne.","More than half of the emissions of cement are therefore not a combustion problem but a chemical one: the carbon was in the rock, and making the material means taking it out.","Cement production is commonly put at seven or eight percent of human carbon dioxide emissions, well above aviation."],["Concrete is strong in compression and weak in tension, so it is nearly always cast around steel bars.","The pairing works for two unrelated reasons.","The first is mechanical: the two expand and contract with temperature at almost the same rate, so they do not tear apart on a hot afternoon.","The second is chemical, and it comes from the portlandite.","The pore water in hardened concrete is saturated with calcium hydroxide and alkali, which gives it a pH between about twelve and a half and thirteen and a half.","In liquid that alkaline, steel grows a thin, tight oxide film over itself and stops corroding.","Engineers say the steel is passive.","The concrete is not merely armor.","It is a chemical environment that keeps the bar from rusting."],["Air undoes this slowly.","Carbon dioxide is only about four hundredths of one percent of the atmosphere, but it dissolves in the pore water and reacts with the calcium hydroxide to make calcium carbonate.","The concrete turns a little of itself back into limestone.","Carbonated concrete is slightly harder and slightly denser, and as a material it is sound.","What it has lost is alkalinity, and the pH behind the carbonation front falls below about nine.","As long as unreacted portlandite remains the pH holds, so the portlandite acts as an alkaline reserve, a stock of protection that the air spends.","The front moves inward roughly in proportion to the square root of time, so a depth reached in ten years takes forty to double.","That arithmetic is the logic behind the specified cover, the twenty to fifty millimeters between the outer face and the nearest bar."],["When the front reaches the steel, the film goes and the bar begins to rust.","Rust is the slow failure mode of the modern built environment, and it is mechanical as much as chemical, because iron oxides occupy several times the volume of the metal they replace.","The expanding rust cracks the cover along the line of the bar and eventually pushes slabs of it off, which is called spalling.","Chloride from sea spray or road salt does the same work faster and more locally, breaking the passive film in pits without waiting for the pH to fall.","A bridge deck rarely fails because the concrete was crushed.","It fails because the steel inside it grew."],["Because the carbon is in the limestone, the most direct way to cut emissions is to use less clinker in each cubic meter.","Industry has done this for decades by blending in supplementary materials, cementitious or pozzolanic.","Fly ash from coal-fired boilers and ground slag from blast furnaces both react in the presence of water and lime to make more C-S-H.","A newer blend grinds together clinker, limestone, and clay calcined at roughly eight hundred degrees, a temperature that drives water out of the clay without decomposing any carbonate, and can replace about half the clinker.","Roman builders made durable marine mortar from lime and volcanic ash two thousand years ago on much the same principle, with no steel inside it, and some of their harbor works still stand."],["The catch is chemical, and it deserves more attention than it usually gets.","A pozzolan makes its extra glue by consuming calcium hydroxide.","The reaction that adds strength is the reaction that spends the alkaline reserve, so a binder with much less portlandite in it has less protection to give the steel when the carbonation front arrives.","Against that, the same blends usually produce a finer and less connected pore structure, which slows carbon dioxide on its way in.","Which effect wins is not a matter of principle but of the mix, the cover, the curing, and the exposure, and for a given structure the honest answer is that it has to be worked out rather than assumed."],["Supply is the other limit.","Fly ash exists because coal is burned for electricity and slag exists because iron is smelted, so the two most established substitutes for a high-carbon material are byproducts of other high-carbon industries, and they grow scarcer as those industries clean up or close down.","Suitable clay is common almost everywhere, but a plant still has to be built to calcine it."],["Other routes are under development, none yet ordinary.","Capturing the carbon dioxide at the kiln stack would address process and fuel emissions together, at a cost in energy and equipment.","Precast pieces can be cured in a carbon dioxide atmosphere, which stiffens them and locks a little gas back into mineral form, though the amount is modest against what the kiln released.","Binders based on magnesium, or on alkali-activated slag, avoid the limestone problem in other ways and raise their own unsettled questions."],["Underneath all of it sits an accounting question.","Emissions are counted per tonne of cement, but service is counted in years of standing.","A binder that cuts emissions by a third and halves the life of the structure has gained nothing, because the replacement pour will emit again, and a plain mix with generous cover and patient curing may beat a clever one that is rushed.","The chemistry of concrete is not a problem waiting for one substitute.","It is a set of trades among the heat that makes the binder, the water that grows it, and the air that slowly spends it."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p02-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, calcination is the step in which","choices":["limestone sheds its carbon dioxide and becomes quicklime","the clay contributes the silica, alumina, and iron the kiln will need","gypsum is interground so the aluminate will not stiffen the batch too quickly","the ground powder meets water and begins precipitating the binding solid","quicklime and silica fuse and crystallize into gravel-sized nodules"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"the limestone gives up its carbon dioxide and becomes calcium oxide, or quicklime"},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"This step is called calcination"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 names this step outright. Near nine hundred degrees the limestone loses its carbon dioxide and becomes calcium oxide, or quicklime, and the passage calls that calcination.","distractorNotes":[null,"The clay does supply those oxides, but that is the makeup of the raw meal, not the reaction the passage names.","Interground gypsum belongs to paragraph 4, after the clinker has already been made and cooled.","That is hydration, which happens once water is added on the job, not inside the kiln.","That is the hotter clinkering step farther down the kiln, which paragraph 3 treats separately."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage indicates that gypsum is ground in with the clinker in order to","choices":["give the finished powder its characteristic gray color","hold water in the mix so that hydration can continue for longer","raise the proportion of alite so the cement gains strength sooner","keep the tricalcium aluminate from stiffening the batch almost at once","replace part of the clinker and lower the emissions of the finished cement"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"the tricalcium aluminate would react within minutes and seize the batch"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says the gypsum is not filler. Without it the tricalcium aluminate would react within minutes and seize the batch, a failure the passage calls flash set.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 credits the gray color to the calcium aluminoferrite phase, not to the gypsum.","Keeping water available is curing, which paragraph 7 puts in the hands of the crew after the concrete is placed.","Alite content is set in the kiln, and paragraph 6 treats early strength as a matter of clinker composition rather than anything gypsum does.",null,"That describes the supplementary materials of paragraph 12, while the three to five percent of gypsum is there to control setting."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, most of the strength a concrete gains in its first week comes from","choices":["alite, the most abundant phase in clinker","the calcium aluminoferrite that colors the powder","belite, the slower of the two calcium silicates","portlandite crystallizing out of the pore water","ettringite needles built around the cement grains"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"supplies most of the strength gained in the first week"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"makes up half to two thirds of the clinker"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the two silicates set the pace and gives the first week to alite. Paragraph 3 has already put alite at half to two thirds of the clinker.","distractorNotes":[null,"The ferrite phase is mentioned only for the gray color it gives the powder.","Belite is the other silicate, but the passage gives it months and years rather than the first week.","Portlandite is released by the reaction, and paragraph 5 says plainly that it is not much of a glue.","Ettringite is what slows the aluminate down so the batch stays workable, and the passage never calls it a source of strength."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage puts the pH of the pore water in sound hardened concrete closest to","choices":["4.5","7.0","9.0","13.0","14.0"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"between about twelve and a half and thirteen and a half"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 gives a range of about twelve and a half to thirteen and a half for the pore water, and thirteen is the only value here inside it.","distractorNotes":["That is acidic, the opposite of the strongly alkaline liquid paragraph 9 describes.","Neutral water would not grow the protective film, and the passage stresses how alkaline the pore water is.","Below about nine is the value paragraph 10 gives for concrete that has already carbonated.",null,"The passage tops the range out near thirteen and a half, not at the extreme end of the scale."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q05","type":"detail","stem":"For every tonne of clinker produced, the passage says the limestone itself gives off about","choices":["eight tenths of a tonne of carbon dioxide","half a tonne of carbon dioxide","a full tonne of carbon dioxide","a fifth of a tonne of carbon dioxide","seven or eight percent of a tonne of carbon dioxide"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"Roughly half a tonne of carbon dioxide leaves the limestone"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 separates two sources. Roughly half a tonne comes out of the rock as stoichiometry alone, before any fuel is counted.","distractorNotes":["That is the total once the fuel for firing and the energy for grinding are added in, not the share from the rock.",null,"The passage keeps the share from the limestone well under a tonne, at roughly half.","The passage never gives this figure, and it would make the share coming from the rock look far smaller than it is.","Seven or eight percent is cement's share of human carbon dioxide emissions, a different figure entirely."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, \"green\" most nearly means","choices":["inexperienced or newly trained","rich in plant material or organic matter","freshly placed and not yet hardened","produced with unusually low emissions","tinted by the iron in the clinker"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"soft enough to take a thumbprint and not yet carrying load"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 defines the word in the same sentence: soft enough to take a thumbprint and not yet carrying load. It describes how young the pour is.","distractorNotes":["That is the everyday sense of the word, and it does not fit a sentence about a slab that still takes a thumbprint.","Nothing organic is involved, since the passage is describing cement, sand, stone, and water.",null,"Emissions do come up, but not until paragraph 8, and here the word describes a slab that has just been placed.","Paragraph 3 credits the gray color to the ferrite phase, and nothing in the passage is green in color."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 10, \"reserve\" most nearly means","choices":["a rule written into a specification","a spare batch held back for repairs","an area of land protected from building","a cautious, understated manner","a stored supply that gets used up"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"a stock of protection that the air spends"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 supplies the sense itself: the portlandite is a stock of protection that the air spends. It is an amount on hand that carbonation draws down.","distractorNotes":["Cover depth is specified later in the paragraph, but the reserve is a quantity of portlandite rather than a written requirement.","Nothing is being held back for later, since the portlandite is already in place and is consumed where it sits.","The land sense is a real dictionary meaning, but the passage is describing a chemical stock inside the concrete.","That is the personality sense of the word, which has no place in a sentence about how long the pH holds.",null]},{"id":"rc-p02-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly supports which statement about a fresh slab left uncovered on a hot, dry day?","choices":["It will carbonate before its steel has a chance to become passive","It will never set at all, since the cement cannot react without cover","It may stop gaining strength while some of its cement is still unreacted","It needs a larger share of gypsum to make up for the water it has lost to the air","It will set faster and therefore end up stronger than one kept damp"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"hydration stops when the water runs out, whether or not the surface looks dry"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"most easily ruined by a hot dry afternoon"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says hydration stops when the water runs out, whether or not the surface looks dry. A slab that loses its water early leaves cement unreacted and stops gaining.","distractorNotes":["Carbonation belongs to paragraph 10 and works over years, not over a single afternoon.","Hydration starts as soon as water is added, so the slab does set. The passage says the reaction stops early, not that it never begins.",null,"Gypsum controls how fast the aluminate reacts, and the passage never ties it to how much water a slab keeps.","This is the intuition the paragraph is written to correct, since drying is not hardening and losing water early costs strength."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage best supports which inference about the Roman marine mortar it mentions?","choices":["It was fired in kilns that reached the same temperature as a modern clinker kiln","It was immune to carbonation because volcanic ash blocks carbon dioxide entirely","Its long life shows that lime and ash binders are stronger than any modern cement","A loss of alkalinity would not have threatened it as it threatens modern members","Roman builders had worked out why steel stays passive in alkaline surroundings"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"some of their harbor works still stand"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"has less protection to give the steel"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":9,"quote":"It is a chemical environment that keeps the bar from rusting."}],"why":"Paragraph 12 describes a binder of lime and volcanic ash, and paragraph 9 explains that alkalinity matters because of the steel cast inside modern concrete. A mortar with no steel in it has nothing for a falling pH to endanger.","distractorNotes":["Only the Portland kiln is given a temperature, about fourteen hundred and fifty degrees, and Roman firing is never described.","Nothing in the passage says volcanic ash blocks carbon dioxide, and paragraph 13 says only that finer pores slow it down.","The passage calls the mortar durable, which is a claim about lasting rather than about out-performing modern cement in strength.",null,"The passive film is presented as modern engineering language, and the passage credits the Romans with a working principle instead."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["Concrete is overused, and the volume poured each year ought to be cut sharply","Corrosion of reinforcing steel is the leading cause of building collapse worldwide","Ancient builders understood durability far better than modern engineers manage to","Blended binders have made ordinary Portland cement obsolete for new construction","One chemistry sets cement's usefulness, its carbon cost, and its slow decay"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"the source of both the usefulness and the trouble of cement"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"not a problem waiting for one substitute"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"a set of trades among the heat that makes the binder"}],"why":"The passage follows one chain. The calcination that makes the binder is the step that releases the carbon dioxide, and the portlandite that protects the steel is the compound the air slowly spends. Every proposal at the end is weighed against that chain.","distractorNotes":["The passage never argues for building less, and the closing paragraph is about how binders should be compared.","Rust is called the slow failure mode of the built environment, which is not the same as a worldwide count of collapses.","The Roman mortar appears once to illustrate a principle, not as a verdict on modern engineering.","Paragraph 13 presents the blends as a trade with an unsettled outcome, not as a replacement that has already won.",null]},{"id":"rc-p02-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 7 is included mainly to","choices":["correct a common misunderstanding and show what it costs","compare the two calcium silicates that set the pace","introduce the carbonation reaction that arrives later on","explain why cement kilns have to be fired so hot","show that site crews are careless with the mixes they place"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"Here the common intuition fails."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"Concrete does not harden by drying out."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":8,"quote":"Those pores are where strength goes"}],"why":"The paragraph opens by saying the common intuition fails, replaces drying with hydration, and then follows the error to the extra water and the capillary pores it leaves behind.","distractorNotes":[null,"That comparison is the work of paragraph 6, which comes just before this one.","Carbonation is introduced in paragraph 10. What paragraph 7 introduces is the pore network that later lets it in.","Kiln temperature is handled in paragraphs 2 and 3, while paragraph 7 is about what happens after the cement leaves the plant.","The passage explains that a wetter mix is easier to place, describing the tradeoff rather than faulting anyone for it."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"The passage is organized mainly by","choices":["opening with a failure and working backward to its causes","moving through the four clinker phases one at a time","setting ancient construction against modern construction throughout","following the material forward in time, then testing proposals against it","alternating a chemical claim with a rebuttal of that claim in every paragraph"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"The story starts in a quarry and a kiln."},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"Add water and the real reaction begins."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"Air undoes this slowly."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"Other routes are under development, none yet ordinary."}],"why":"The passage runs quarry, kiln, clinker, hydration, curing, then emissions and decades of slow carbonation. Only after all that does it take up substitutes, supply, and the accounting.","distractorNotes":["The failure arrives late, in paragraphs 10 and 11, after the chemistry that explains it has been laid out.","The four phases are named once, in paragraph 3, and two of them are barely mentioned again.","Roman mortar occupies a single sentence rather than framing the whole passage.",null,"There is pushback in paragraphs 7 and 13, but most paragraphs simply carry the account forward."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the carbon dioxide that comes out of the limestone is best described as","choices":["matter of fact about an unavoidable piece of chemistry","indignant at an industry that has refused to act","confident that cleaner fuel will shortly remove the whole problem","dismissive of a figure that is often exaggerated","puzzled by a source of emissions nobody has explained"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"a problem no engineer can design away"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"purely as stoichiometry"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 calls it a problem no engineer can design away and explains it as stoichiometry. The author gives the size of it plainly, without blaming anyone or promising a fix.","distractorNotes":[null,"The paragraph assigns no blame and says instead that the carbon was in the rock to begin with.","Fuel is only part of the total, and the passage says more than half of the emissions are chemical rather than combustion.","The author treats the figure as serious, comparing cement's share with that of aviation.","The origin is explained exactly, in one sentence about where the carbon was."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 13, the author's attitude toward blended binders is best described as","choices":["openly enthusiastic about a settled improvement","interested but unwilling to generalize from principle","resigned to the idea that nothing much can be improved here","hostile, on the grounds that they endanger structures","indifferent to which binder a given project chooses"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"not a matter of principle but of the mix"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"has to be worked out rather than assumed"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 gives the blends a real benefit, a finer pore structure, and a real cost, a smaller alkaline reserve, then says which effect wins has to be worked out for the particular mix and exposure.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph opens with a catch and never treats the question as settled.",null,"The author is weighing an option rather than giving one up, and the closing paragraph still looks for better practice.","The danger is conditional here, and the same paragraph credits the blends with slowing carbon dioxide down.","Indifference would not fit a paragraph that says the catch deserves more attention than it usually gets."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports each of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Chloride can break the protective film without any fall in pH","Carbonated concrete is denser than the concrete it formed from","Concrete reaches its final strength once its surface is dry","Belite goes on adding strength for months after a structure is finished","Cover depth is specified because the carbonation front advances slowly"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"keeps adding strength for months and years","choice":3},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"without waiting for the pH to fall","choice":0},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"slightly harder and slightly denser","choice":1},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":8,"quote":"That arithmetic is the logic behind the specified cover","choice":4},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"whether or not the surface looks dry","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"a structure quietly gets stronger long after the forms come off","choice":2}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says the opposite. Water is a reactant, and hydration stops when the water runs out, whether or not the surface looks dry, so a dry surface is not a finished reaction.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 11 says chloride breaks the film in pits without waiting for the pH to fall, so this one is supported.","Paragraph 10 calls carbonated concrete slightly harder and slightly denser, so this one is supported.",null,"Paragraph 6 gives belite months and years of continued gain, so this one is supported.","Paragraph 10 ties the specified cover to a front that advances with the square root of time, so this one is supported."]},{"id":"rc-p02-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for the reason a very large dam might be built with a slow-gaining cement would find it in which paragraph?","choices":["Paragraph 4","Paragraph 5","Paragraph 6","Paragraph 10","Paragraph 13"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"an advantage in a massive dam"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 contrasts the two silicates and closes on the dam: a cement heavier in belite runs cool, and the heat of reaction would otherwise crack a mass that size as it cooled.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 is about gypsum and flash set, a matter of minutes rather than of heat in a massive pour.","Paragraph 5 describes hydration and names its products, but the dam example is not there.",null,"Paragraph 10 covers carbonation and cover depth, long after any heat of reaction has gone.","Paragraph 13 weighs blended binders and says nothing about heat in a large pour."]}]},"rc-p03":{"id":"rc-p03","title":"How the Deep Ocean Makes Its Own Light","topic":"bioluminescence in the deep ocean","field":"marine biology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1669,"paragraphs":[["The deep ocean is usually described in the language of absence.","It is cold, it is black, the pressure is crushing, and no plant or alga can photosynthesize there.","Most of that holds up.","Below about 1,000 meters, sunlight has been absorbed and scattered away to nothing, and the water stays a few degrees above freezing down to the seafloor.","But the blackness deserves a second look.","The deep sea is not a place without light.","It is a place where nearly all the light is made by the animals living in it."],["Sunlit water has a light source every animal shares and no animal controls.","In the deep, light is a possession.","An animal that can make a photon can put it where it wants, when it wants, in the color it wants, and every other animal in range has to interpret it.","Four questions follow: how the light is made, why it keeps being invented, what it is used for, and why the answers took so long to gather."],["The chemistry is simple in outline and endlessly varied in detail.","A molecule called a luciferin is the fuel, and an enzyme called a luciferase is the machine.","The luciferase binds the luciferin and adds oxygen to it, producing a strained intermediate that comes apart on its own.","The product lands in an excited electronic state, meaning one of its electrons is briefly held higher than it wants to be.","When that electron falls back down, the energy leaves as a photon.","The payout is taken as light instead of heat."],["Because so little energy escapes as heat, bioluminescence is often called cold light, and the phrase is literal.","Some organisms run it as a conventional enzyme, turning over fuel to make a steady glow.","Others use a photoprotein, loaded in advance with its luciferin and its oxygen and held, cocked, until a trigger arrives.","The jellyfish protein aequorin works this way, and its trigger is calcium: when calcium floods in, the stored reaction fires at once and the animal flashes.","That same jellyfish supplies a green fluorescent protein, which absorbs the blue light made beside it and re-emits it as green."],["Bioluminescence is not a family trait handed down from one ancient luminous ancestor.","It has been invented independently at least 40 separate times across the tree of life, in bacteria, dinoflagellates, jellyfish, worms, crustaceans, squid, and fish.","The luciferases are not related to one another.","They have been assembled again and again out of whatever enzymes happened to be present.","The luciferins, by contrast, are few.","One of them, coelenterazine, turns up in animals from many unrelated phyla, and in a great many of them it is not made at home.","It is eaten.","Animals take it in with their food and route it to their light organs, so the hard part of evolving light is often not the fuel but the machine that burns it."],["Why should the same solution keep arising?","Part of the answer is that the starting materials are ordinary.","Cells are already full of molecules that oxidize readily, and an oxidation leaving its product in an excited state is not exotic chemistry.","It is chemistry that usually wastes the excitation as heat.","Any protein rigid enough to hold that excited state can be co-opted into a crude luciferase, and selection can sharpen a crude one.","Even so, making light is not free.","A flash spends fuel, oxygen, and protein, and for a small animal where meals are sparse, that is expensive.","The trait keeps evolving because in the dark it buys something worth the price."],["What it buys depends on the physics of seawater.","Clear water transmits blue and blue-green light far better than red or violet, so a signal near 470 to 490 nanometers travels the farthest, and the great majority of marine bioluminescence falls in that window.","Deep-sea eyes have followed, with visual pigments tuned to the same band and little sensitivity to anything else.","A few fish have exploited the gap.","The stoplight loosejaw makes and sees red light, using a pigment derived from ingested chlorophyll to push its vision into a range its prey cannot detect."],["The most widespread use of the light is not a signal at all.","It is camouflage.","In the upper several hundred meters a faint blue haze of sunlight still filters down, and anything swimming in it shows from below as a silhouette.","Counterillumination erases the silhouette.","Rows of light organs along the belly emit light downward at the intensity of the water overhead, so a predator looking up sees uniform dim blue where a body should be.","The organs are small optical instruments, with reflectors behind the light source, lenses in front of it, and filters that adjust the color."],["The lie has to be a good one.","Too little light and the silhouette survives.","Too much and the animal becomes a lamp.","Several counterilluminating animals adjust their output as the surrounding light changes, dimming as a cloud crosses the surface or as they sink deeper, and some can match the weaker light of a moonlit night.","The disguise also works in one direction only, because it is aimed down.","Whatever an animal gains from below, its own lights are still a beacon to anything beside it or above it."],["Other uses of light are meant to be seen.","The burglar alarm hypothesis describes a defense that works by making the attacker conspicuous.","A dinoflagellate being grazed by a copepod flashes, and the flash does the copepod no harm.","It advertises the copepod to a fish, which eats it.","The grazer is removed by a third party the dinoflagellate never touches.","The same logic is proposed for larger prey: an animal seized in the dark may throw a repeating display that a bigger predator can home in on, so the captor's safest move is to let go."],["Further tricks fill out the repertoire.","Some shrimp and squid discharge a luminous cloud instead of ink, leaving a glowing distraction where the animal used to be.","Certain worms and brittle stars shed glowing body parts that flash after they detach.","Some animals smear luminous material onto an attacker, marking it in a way it cannot easily wash off."],["Light is also used for hunting and for conversation.","The deep-sea anglerfish dangles an illuminated lure in front of its own mouth, turning the visual advantage of darkness into a trap.","Smaller crustaceans release timed trains of glowing droplets in patterns particular to their species, so a signal readable by the right mate is meaningless to the wrong one."],["Many animals do not make their own light at all.","They farm bacteria that do.","The Hawaiian bobtail squid hatches without a single symbiont and recruits its bacteria from the seawater around it within hours.","Ciliated appendages on the developing light organ beat seawater toward a set of pores, mucus traps passing cells, and chemical filters winnow the catch until one species is left inside.","The bacteria then hold their light in reserve until they are crowded.","Each cell releases a small signaling molecule, and only when enough cells are packed together for it to build up do they switch on the genes for luciferase.","This density check is called quorum sensing, and it keeps a drifting bacterium from spending energy to glow alone."],["The partnership is maintained rather than merely established.","Each dawn the squid expels about 95 percent of the bacteria in its light organ into the water, and the survivors regrow through the day so that a full population is ready for the following night.","Other partnerships have gone further still.","The bacteria in the lures of some deep-sea anglerfish have lost large stretches of their genomes, including genes they would need in order to live on their own, the signature of a symbiont confined for a very long time with nowhere else to go."],["That leaves the fourth question: how much of this is there?","The field spent decades underestimating it for reasons that had nothing to do with the animals.","Early sampling was done with nets, and nets are brutal to the animals most likely to be luminous.","Jellyfish and comb jellies arrive at the surface as shreds, and a shredded animal cannot be scored as luminous.","Animals that survive often exhaust their light after handling and arrive dark.","Submersibles brought a problem of their own, since floodlights bright enough to film by wash out any glow.","The resulting census tilted toward tough-bodied crustaceans and fish.","Once video surveys began counting animals in place under dim red light, the numbers moved sharply: in well-studied stretches of the water column, roughly three quarters of the animals observed proved capable of making light."],["Caution is still warranted.","A display recorded once in the dark tells an observer what an animal did, not what it was for, and a plausible story about function is easy to construct and hard to test.","Laboratory work supports the burglar alarm in certain pairings, yet many deep-sea displays have never been watched by anyone who knew which predator was nearby.","Function may also be multiple: a light organ can serve as camouflage on one dive and as a signal on another, and its present use need not be the reason it first appeared.","The claim worth defending is the narrow one, that light is common and is made for reasons, rather than a confident purpose assigned to every flash."],["What survives all that hedging is still a reversal of the ordinary picture.","The largest habitat on the planet by volume is not an empty dark space with occasional sparks in it.","It is a place where most of the animals carry their own light, where the same few fuels have been harnessed by unrelated machinery over and over, and where a photon is the common currency of hiding, hunting, warning, and courtship.","The deep sea is dark only in the sense that its light has to be earned."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p03-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the photon in a bioluminescent reaction leaves the molecule at the moment when","choices":["oxygen is first bound and added by the luciferase enzyme","calcium floods in and reaches the luciferin stored in a photoprotein","a green fluorescent protein absorbs blue light and re-emits it as green light","an electron in the reaction product falls back to its lower energy state","heat generated by the reaction escapes into the surrounding water"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"When that electron falls back down, the energy leaves as a photon."}],"why":"Paragraph 3 walks the reaction in order: oxygen is added, a strained intermediate comes apart, the product lands in an excited state, and the light leaves when that electron falls back down. The photon is the last step, not the first.","distractorNotes":["Oxygen goes in earlier in the sequence, which sets the reaction up rather than releasing the light.","Calcium is the trigger for the photoprotein in paragraph 4, so it starts the stored reaction instead of being the instant light departs.","That is the color shift in paragraph 4, which recolors light that already exists.",null,"Paragraph 3 says the payout is taken as light instead of heat, so escaping heat is not where the photon comes from."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage gives which reason that most marine bioluminescence falls in the blue-green range?","choices":["Blue-green light carries farther than other colors through clear seawater","Blue-green light is the cheapest color for a luciferase to produce","Blue-green light is invisible to the majority of deep-sea predators","Blue-green light is the color of the sunlight that still reaches 1,000 meters","Blue-green light can be produced without using any oxygen"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"transmits blue and blue-green light far better than red or violet"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"travels the farthest"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 ties the color straight to the physics of water: blue and blue-green travel far better than red or violet, so signals near 470 to 490 nanometers reach the farthest.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage never ranks colors by what they cost to make; the reason it gives is how far the light travels.","The opposite holds in paragraph 7, where deep-sea eyes are tuned to that same band, which is why one fish using red is remarkable.","Paragraph 1 says sunlight is gone below about 1,000 meters, so there is no sunlight down there to match.","Oxygen is required by the reaction in paragraph 3 no matter what color comes out."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q03","type":"detail","stem":"The passage describes the luciferases of different luminous groups as","choices":["variations on a single enzyme inherited from one ancient luminous ancestor","proteins that most animals obtain from their food rather than build themselves","unrelated proteins assembled again and again from enzymes already present","identical in structure but different in the kind of fuel they will accept","made only by bacteria and passed to animals through symbiosis"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"The luciferases are not related to one another."},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"assembled again and again out of whatever enzymes happened to be present"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says plainly that the luciferases are not related to one another and have been put together repeatedly out of whatever enzymes were on hand. The fuel is what gets shared, not the enzyme.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 opens by rejecting a single luminous ancestor.","Diet supplies coelenterazine, the fuel, in paragraph 5, not the enzyme that burns it.",null,"Not being related to one another is exactly the point, so identical structure is what they lack.","Bacterial partnership is one route among several in paragraphs 13 and 14, not the only source."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q04","type":"detail","stem":"Quorum sensing, as the passage presents it, keeps symbiotic bacteria from","choices":["being taken up by a host species other than the one they belong in","growing beyond the space that is available inside the light organ","losing the genes they would need in order to live independently","being expelled from the light organ when morning comes","making light when they are too sparse for it to be worth the cost"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":7,"quote":"keeps a drifting bacterium from spending energy to glow alone"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 describes a density check: the signaling molecule builds up only when cells are crowded, and only then do the luciferase genes switch on, which spares a lone drifting cell the cost of glowing.","distractorNotes":["Host selection is handled earlier in paragraph 13 by the mucus and the chemical filters, not by the density check.","Paragraph 13 links the signal to switching on light genes, not to limiting how many cells there are.","Genome loss belongs to the anglerfish symbionts in paragraph 14 and is not governed by this signal.","The dawn expulsion in paragraph 14 is something the squid does, and the density check does not prevent it.",null]},{"id":"rc-p03-q05","type":"detail","stem":"Each dawn, the light organ of the Hawaiian bobtail squid","choices":["vents most of its bacteria into the water and regrows them through the day","takes in an entirely fresh population of bacteria from the surrounding seawater","digests the bacteria it holds and absorbs the nutrients they contain","swaps its bacteria for a second species that is better suited to daylight","shuts its bacteria down by withdrawing the oxygen they need"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"expels about 95 percent of the bacteria in its light organ"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"the survivors regrow through the day"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 puts a number on it: about 95 percent of the bacteria are expelled each dawn, and the survivors regrow during the day so the organ is full again by night.","distractorNotes":[null,"Recruitment from seawater happens once, in the hours after hatching, as paragraph 13 describes.","Paragraph 14 says the bacteria are expelled into the water and says nothing about digesting them.","The winnowing in paragraph 13 leaves essentially one species, and no second species appears anywhere in the passage.","Oxygen withdrawal is never mentioned; the reset described there is a physical expulsion."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 6, \"expensive\" most nearly means","choices":["commanding a high price in a market","unusually difficult for an animal to obtain","demanding in energy and materials","wasteful of the heat a reaction produces","uncommon among the smallest animals"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":7,"quote":"A flash spends fuel, oxygen, and protein"}],"why":"The sentence lists what a flash spends, which is fuel, oxygen, and protein, and then calls that expensive for an animal where meals are sparse. The cost is metabolic, not monetary.","distractorNotes":["That is the everyday money sense of the word, and no money is involved anywhere in paragraph 6.","Paragraph 6 calls the starting materials ordinary, so the cost is in spending them, not in getting hold of them.",null,"Paragraph 3 makes the point that this reaction gives up very little heat, so waste heat is not the cost being named.","The sentence is about what one flash costs, not about how many animals have the trait."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 13, \"recruits\" most nearly means","choices":["trains for a specialized task","enlists by force","produces internally from its own tissue","draws in from an outside supply","selects from among its own offspring"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"recruits its bacteria from the seawater around it"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"hatches without a single symbiont"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 says the squid hatches with no symbionts and gathers its bacteria out of the seawater around it within hours. The word is about taking in cells from outside the body.","distractorNotes":["No training happens, since the bacteria already carry the machinery to make light.","Nothing in paragraph 13 suggests force; the squid draws in cells that are already drifting past.","The squid hatches without a single symbiont, so the bacteria cannot come from its own tissue.",null,"The bacteria are free-living cells in the seawater, not the squid's young."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q08","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 9 most strongly suggests that an animal using counterillumination must also be able to","choices":["see the light that its own belly organs produce","sense how bright the water above it is","recognize the species of a predator approaching it","produce red light in addition to blue-green light","stop glowing entirely whenever it is threatened"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"adjust their output as the surrounding light changes"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"at the intensity of the water overhead"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says these animals adjust their output as the surrounding light changes, dimming as a cloud passes or as they sink. Matching a background that shifts means the animal has to register what the background is doing.","distractorNotes":["The organs aim light downward, away from the animal's own eyes, and the passage never asks it to view its own output.",null,"Paragraph 9 is about matching background light; recognizing a species belongs to the courtship signals in paragraph 12.","Red light is the unusual case of one fish in paragraph 7, not a requirement for this disguise.","Going dark would restore the very silhouette the disguise exists to erase."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q09","type":"inference","stem":"It can be inferred from paragraph 15 that counts of luminous animals based on net collections","choices":["were reliable for jellyfish but not for fish","were abandoned as soon as submersibles became available","overstated the trait, because handled animals flash repeatedly","matched the later video counts once the badly damaged specimens were set aside","understated how common light is, especially among soft-bodied animals"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":2,"quote":"The field spent decades underestimating it"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"a shredded animal cannot be scored as luminous"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":7,"quote":"tilted toward tough-bodied crustaceans and fish"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 says nets shred the animals most likely to be luminous and that a shredded animal cannot be scored as luminous, which tilted the census toward tough-bodied groups. Those counts had to come in low.","distractorNotes":["Jellyfish and comb jellies are precisely what the nets destroy, so those counts are the least trustworthy.","The passage says submersibles brought a problem of their own and gives no timeline for retiring nets.","Repeated handling leaves animals dark, which pushes a count down rather than up.","The numbers moved sharply once video counting began, which is the opposite of a match.",null]},{"id":"rc-p03-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the central point of the passage?","choices":["Light production is chemically simpler than the variety of its biological uses would suggest.","The deep ocean is a hostile place that only a few highly specialized animals have managed to colonize.","Partnership with luminous bacteria is the main way that deep-sea animals obtain the light they use.","Making light is normal in the deep sea, and what is known about it has been shaped by how it was sampled.","Improvements in underwater imaging have overturned most of what biologists once believed about the open ocean."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":7,"quote":"nearly all the light is made by the animals living in it"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":2,"quote":"for reasons that had nothing to do with the animals"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":3,"quote":"most of the animals carry their own light"}],"why":"The passage opens by correcting the picture of a lightless deep sea, works through how light is made, why it keeps appearing, and what it does, then explains in paragraphs 15 and 16 why the count was long too low. Both halves of that answer are the spine of the piece.","distractorNotes":["True as far as it goes, and it is one thread in the middle of the passage rather than the point the whole piece builds toward.","The first paragraph rejects exactly this empty-desert picture of the deep sea.","Symbiosis gets two paragraphs among many routes to light and is treated as one arrangement, not the main one.",null,"Video methods revise one number in paragraph 15, and the passage claims nothing that sweeping."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q11","type":"function","stem":"The main purpose of paragraph 15 is to","choices":["argue that nets should no longer be used in deep-sea research","explain why the collecting methods, not the animals, produced a low early count","describe the physical damage that deep-sea animals suffer during capture and handling","show that submersibles solved the problems the earlier net surveys had created","introduce the claim that most luminous animals in the ocean are gelatinous"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":2,"quote":"for reasons that had nothing to do with the animals"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"nets are brutal to the animals most likely to be luminous"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":6,"quote":"Submersibles brought a problem of their own"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 asks how much bioluminescence there is and answers that the field undercounted it for reasons that had nothing to do with the animals. Everything in it, the nets, the handling, the floodlights, serves that explanation.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph explains what nets distort, but it never calls for retiring them.",null,"The damage is evidence inside the argument rather than the reason the paragraph exists.","The paragraph says submersible floodlights created a problem of their own.","Gelatinous animals appear as an example within the argument, not as the paragraph's claim."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["An assumption is corrected, four questions are posed and answered, and the conclusions are then qualified.","A single hypothesis is introduced, weighed carefully against two competing explanations, and then rejected on the evidence.","One organism is followed closely from start to finish, and general principles are drawn from it at the end.","A history of deep-sea exploration is traced from the earliest nets through modern video methods.","The uses of light are listed one after another, each one supported by a separate experiment."],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"But the blackness deserves a second look."},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"Four questions follow"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"That leaves the fourth question"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"Caution is still warranted."}],"why":"Paragraph 1 corrects the picture of a lightless deep sea, paragraph 2 sets out four questions, the body answers them in that order, and paragraph 16 pulls back on how far the answers can be pushed.","distractorNotes":[null,"No hypothesis is rejected; the closing paragraphs keep the general claim while limiting the specific ones.","The bobtail squid gets close attention in the middle, but the passage is built around questions rather than around one animal.","Methods come up only near the end, and only to explain why one number was wrong.","The uses are described, but no experiments are reported for any of them."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude in paragraph 16 toward explanations of what a particular flash is for is best described as","choices":["dismissive of the idea that these displays serve any biological function at all","confident that the purpose of each display has now been worked out","careful, holding the general claim while reserving judgment case by case","frustrated with the researchers who study deep-sea behavior","indifferent to whether such explanations can ever be tested"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"The claim worth defending is the narrow one"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"easy to construct and hard to test"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 keeps the broad claim that light is common and made for reasons, and it withholds only the confident purpose assigned to any single flash. That is caution, not doubt about the whole subject.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph keeps the claim that light is made for reasons; what it resists is naming the reason for every flash.","The paragraph says a plausible story is easy to construct and hard to test, which is the opposite of confidence.",null,"No researcher is criticized; the difficulty belongs to the setting, since displays happen unobserved in the dark.","Testability is precisely what the paragraph cares about."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 6, the author treats the repeated independent evolution of light as","choices":["a coincidence that remains unexplained","an outcome that ordinary cell chemistry makes unsurprising","evidence that the trait must have a single ancient origin after all","a puzzle that undermines current evolutionary thinking","a rare event that required unusual conditions each time it happened"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"the starting materials are ordinary"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"is not exotic chemistry"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 answers its own question by pointing to ordinary ingredients: cells are already full of molecules that oxidize readily, and any protein rigid enough to hold the excited state can be pressed into service. The repetition is presented as expected, not mysterious.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph offers an explanation rather than leaving the pattern unaccounted for.",null,"Paragraph 5 rules out a single luminous ancestor, and paragraph 6 explains why none is needed.","Nothing is presented as overturning evolutionary thinking; building a trait from available parts is treated as routine.","The paragraph stresses that the starting materials are ordinary, which is the argument against rarity."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports each of the following statements about light organs EXCEPT:","choices":["Some contain reflectors, lenses, and filters that shape the light leaving them.","Some are stocked with bacteria instead of making the light themselves.","The output of some can be adjusted as conditions around the animal change.","Some must shed the considerable heat their reaction produces.","Some are aimed downward so that an animal's silhouette disappears."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"with reflectors behind the light source, lenses in front of it, and filters","choice":0},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"They farm bacteria that do.","choice":1},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"adjust their output as the surrounding light changes","choice":2},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"because it is aimed down","choice":4},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"Because so little energy escapes as heat","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraphs 3 and 4 make the point that almost no energy leaves as heat, which is why the passage calls it cold light. There is no meaningful heat to shed, so that statement is the one the passage does not support.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 8 lists these optical parts by name.","Paragraphs 13 and 14 describe light organs filled with symbiotic bacteria.","Paragraph 9 describes animals dimming and brightening as the surrounding light shifts.",null,"That is the counterillumination described in paragraph 8."]},{"id":"rc-p03-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wanted to know why the bacteria inside some anglerfish lures could not survive on their own would find that explained in","choices":["paragraph 5","paragraph 9","paragraph 11","paragraph 13","paragraph 14"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"including genes they would need in order to live on their own"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 is where the anglerfish symbionts appear, and it says they have lost large stretches of their genomes, including the genes they would need in order to live on their own.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 covers the independent origins of luminous chemistry, not any particular partnership.","Paragraph 9 is about matching the background light while counterilluminating.","Paragraph 11 covers luminous clouds and shed glowing body parts.","Paragraph 13 explains how a squid gathers and screens its bacteria, but the genome loss appears in the paragraph after it.",null]}]},"rc-p04":{"id":"rc-p04","title":"How Resistance Genes Travel","topic":"antibiotic resistance and horizontal gene transfer","field":"microbiology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1680,"paragraphs":[["Penicillin reached general clinical use in the early 1940s, and within a decade hospitals were reporting infections that shrugged it off.","Staphylococci that died reliably in the mid 1940s were surviving by the early 1950s, having acquired an enzyme that cleaves the central ring of the drug.","The speed of that reversal set the pattern for nearly every antibiotic since.","A compound arrives, works spectacularly, and then loses ground, sometimes in a matter of years.","The usual explanation is that bacteria evolve quickly because they divide quickly.","That explanation is true as far as it goes, and it is also incomplete in a way that matters a great deal for how the problem is managed."],["The familiar account runs through mutation and selection.","A bacterium copying its chromosome makes an occasional error.","Most errors are harmful or meaningless, but a few alter the shape of a drug's target, the permeability of the cell envelope, or the activity of a pump that expels foreign molecules.","In the presence of the drug, the cell carrying that change leaves more descendants than its neighbors.","Because a population in rich medium can double every twenty minutes, a rare variant can come to dominate within days.","This is ordinary vertical descent: the gene passes from parent to offspring, and the lineage carrying it grows."],["Vertical descent by itself, though, cannot account for what clinicians actually see.","Resistance to a newly introduced drug often shows up in several unrelated species at once, in the same ward or the same stretch of river, carrying the same gene with the same sequence.","Independent mutation does not produce identical answers in lineages that have not shared an ancestor for hundreds of millions of years.","Something is moving the gene itself."],["Bacteria pass genes sideways as well as down, a process called horizontal gene transfer.","Three routes do most of the work.","In conjugation, two cells make direct contact.","A donor carrying a conjugative plasmid extends a protein appendage called a pilus, which binds a recipient and draws the two cells together.","An enzyme called a relaxase then nicks one strand of the plasmid at a particular sequence, the origin of transfer, and that single strand is threaded through a channel into the recipient.","Both cells synthesize the complementary strand, and the recipient becomes a donor in its own right.","The whole exchange can finish in minutes."],["The plasmids that travel this way are circular DNA molecules separate from the chromosome, replicating on their own schedule.","A single plasmid may carry a dozen resistance determinants along with genes for metal tolerance, virulence, and its own transfer machinery.","Some are narrow in their tastes and live within one genus.","Others are far less particular, establishing themselves in hosts as distantly related as Escherichia and Pseudomonas.","A cell that receives one of these acquires an entire package in a single event, which is why a susceptible strain can become multidrug resistant without passing through any intermediate stage."],["The second route requires no contact at all.","Dying bacteria release DNA into their surroundings, and some species can take that naked DNA across the envelope and recombine it into their own chromosome.","The capacity is called competence, and in several species it switches on under stress or once the local population reaches a certain density.","The phenomenon was described in the late 1920s, when heat killed pneumococci were shown to transmit a heritable trait to living cells, an experiment that eventually identified DNA as the carrier of heredity.","The same machinery that once settled a foundational question now distributes resistance genes in the throat and the gut."],["The third route conscripts viruses.","Bacteriophages package their own genome into new particles, and occasionally the packaging machinery takes a piece of host DNA by mistake.","When such a particle infects another cell, it injects bacterial genes instead of viral ones.","Generalized transduction moves more or less any fragment of the host chromosome; specialized transduction moves the segments that lie alongside a phage's integration site.","Neither is efficient per particle.","Seawater and sediments, however, hold phages in numbers that defeat ordinary intuition, so even a rare event, repeated at that scale, becomes a routine feature of microbial life."],["Arriving is not the same as being used.","A gene that lands in a new cell without a promoter in front of it sits inert.","Integrons solve that problem, and they are worth understanding because they explain how resistance accumulates rather than merely appears.","An integron is a genetic filing system: a gene encoding an integrase, a recombination site where new material is inserted, and a single promoter aimed at that site.","Incoming gene cassettes, small circular units carrying one gene apiece, are cut in and placed under the resident promoter.","A fresh cassette inserts nearest the promoter, so the most recent addition is expressed most strongly, while older ones sit further back in the array."],["Integrons themselves are not mobile, but they ride on things that are.","Class 1 integrons in particular sit inside transposons that hop onto plasmids, and those plasmids conjugate.","The result is a modular system in which a cassette picked up anywhere can be filed, expressed, and carried across species lines.","Arrays holding five or six cassettes are common in clinical isolates."],["All of this raises an obvious question: where do the genes come from in the first place?","The intuitive answer, that antibiotics created them, is simply wrong.","Bacteria recovered from a deep cave chamber in New Mexico, cut off from the surface for something on the order of four million years, carry resistance to multiple modern drug classes, including compounds first made in a laboratory.","DNA extracted from permafrost sediments tens of thousands of years old contains recognizable genes for enzymes that destroy penicillin type drugs.","The reason is not mysterious.","Many antibiotic scaffolds in clinical use were isolated from soil microbes, which produce these compounds in competition with their neighbors and must protect themselves from what they make."],["The standing pool of such genes in soil and gut communities is large enough to have earned a name, the resistome, and it is ancient.","What the antibiotic era did was not to invent resistance.","It applied intense selection to a reservoir that was already there, and it rearranged the contents of that reservoir, pulling genes out of the chromosomes of soil organisms, onto plasmids, and into the species that infect people."],["This distinction, between resistance arising and resistance spreading, is the one most often collapsed in public discussion, and collapsing it leads to bad predictions.","Arising is a matter of chemistry and chance, and it has been going on for as long as microbes have been making antibiotics.","Spreading is a matter of ecology: how much drug is present, how often cells meet, how mobile the genetic element is, and how many hosts it will tolerate.","Only the second of those is something human behavior can push on.","Hospitals, feedlots, and sewage systems are not laboratories where new resistance is invented.","They are mixing vessels where existing resistance is concentrated and moved."],["There is a tempting corollary to all this.","Resistance is supposed to be costly.","A pump that continuously expels molecules burns energy; an altered ribosome may translate slightly less efficiently; a plasmid adds DNA that must be copied at every division.","In the absence of the drug, a resistant cell should therefore fall behind a susceptible one, and resistance should drain out of a population once prescribing stops.","The reasoning is sound and the premise is often true.","The conclusion, unfortunately, does not follow as reliably as it should."],["Several things blunt it.","Compensatory mutations elsewhere in the genome restore the lost efficiency while leaving the resistance gene intact, so the cost can shrink toward nothing within a few hundred generations, and the strain that emerges is both resistant and fit.","Co-selection links determinants that would otherwise be independent: a plasmid carrying resistance to an abandoned drug may also carry resistance to one still in heavy use, or tolerance to copper or a disinfectant, so ordinary hospital and agricultural practice keeps selecting for the entire package.","Some plasmids carry toxin antitoxin systems, pairing a durable toxin with a short lived antidote, so a daughter cell that fails to inherit the plasmid poisons itself.","And some resistance genes appear to cost very little from the beginning."],["The empirical record matches that picture rather than the tidy one.","Where national programs have cut the use of a whole drug class sharply and sustained the cut, resistance in some target organisms has declined measurably.","Elsewhere, the same kind of restriction has produced years of flat numbers.","A gene that has become fixed in a population, with its cost paid down and its carriage tied to other advantages, does not vanish because prescribing habits change.","Reversal happens, but it is slow, partial, and specific to the organism, and no one should plan around it as though it were a switch on a wall."],["None of this argues against stewardship.","It argues for a more honest version of it.","If the aim is to restore an earlier state, stewardship will look like failure.","If the aim is to slow the movement of genes that already exist, and to keep the drugs that still work from being spent on infections that do not need them, then the tools follow: diagnostics that separate bacterial from viral illness before a prescription is written, narrow agents rather than broad ones once the organism is known, courses no longer than they need to be, infection control that stops a resistant strain from meeting new hosts, and sanitation that keeps gut plasmids out of the water supply.","These measures buy time rather than turning back the clock, and time is what a slow drug development pipeline needs most."],["Treating resistance as a contaminant to be cleaned up misreads what it is.","It is a standing feature of microbial life, older than medicine by a wide margin, and the realistic goal is to manage the traffic rather than to clear the road."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p04-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, what happens immediately after the relaxase nicks one strand of a conjugative plasmid?","choices":["One strand of the plasmid passes into the recipient through a channel","The recipient degrades the incoming DNA before replication begins","The pilus retracts and the donor permanently loses its plasmid","The plasmid inserts itself into the recipient chromosome at the origin of transfer site","A virus particle packages the plasmid and carries it across"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"that single strand is threaded through a channel into the recipient"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 walks through conjugation in order: contact, pilus, relaxase nick at the origin of transfer, then the single nicked strand threaded through a channel into the recipient. The answer is the next step in that sequence.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage never mentions the recipient destroying incoming DNA during conjugation; it says the recipient builds the complementary strand and becomes a donor itself.","The passage says the recipient becomes a donor in its own right, which means the donor keeps a copy rather than losing the plasmid.","The origin of transfer is where the relaxase cuts, not a site where the plasmid integrates into the chromosome; plasmids in this passage replicate separately from the chromosome.","Packaging into a virus particle is transduction, described in paragraph 7, not conjugation."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage indicates that the gene cassette most recently added to an integron is","choices":["silenced until the older cassettes are excised","placed farthest from the promoter and expressed weakly","expressed most strongly because it sits closest to the promoter","copied onto a separate plasmid before any of it can be expressed","given a dedicated promoter of its own by the integrase"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"A fresh cassette inserts nearest the promoter"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"the most recent addition is expressed most strongly"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 explains that a fresh cassette inserts nearest the single resident promoter, so the newest arrival is expressed most strongly and older cassettes sit further back in the array.","distractorNotes":["Nothing in the passage requires older cassettes to be removed; they simply sit further back and are expressed less strongly.","This reverses the arrangement in paragraph 8. The newest cassette goes nearest the promoter, and the older ones are the ones sitting further away.",null,"The passage says cassettes are filed into the integron and expressed there. Movement onto a plasmid happens to the whole integron, and it is not a requirement for expression.","The point of the integron is that one shared promoter serves the array. A cassette arrives without a promoter of its own, which is exactly the problem integrons solve."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q03","type":"detail","stem":"As the passage describes them, toxin antitoxin systems on a plasmid work because","choices":["the toxin kills neighboring susceptible cells and clears competitors from the surrounding population","the toxin speeds the transfer of the plasmid into new hosts","the system lowers the energy cost of carrying the plasmid","the toxin destroys any competing plasmid that enters the cell","the antidote is short lived, so a cell losing the plasmid dies"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"a durable toxin with a short lived antidote"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"a daughter cell that fails to inherit the plasmid poisons itself"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 pairs a durable toxin with a short lived antidote. When a daughter cell fails to inherit the plasmid, the antidote disappears first and the lingering toxin kills that cell, which is why the plasmid is hard to shed.","distractorNotes":["The passage describes the toxin acting on the cell that lost the plasmid, not on neighboring cells.","Transfer speed is a property of the conjugation machinery in paragraph 4, not of the toxin antitoxin system.","Paragraph 13 states that a plasmid adds DNA that must be copied at every division, so it adds cost rather than lowering it. Compensatory mutations, not toxin systems, are what reduce cost here.","Nothing in the passage describes the toxin attacking other plasmids; the target is the cell that failed to inherit this one.",null]},{"id":"rc-p04-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, specialized transduction moves","choices":["any fragment of the host chromosome, with roughly equal probability for each","the chromosomal segments lying next to where the phage integrates","only plasmids that carry resistance cassettes","naked DNA released into the surroundings by cells that have died","the phage genome alone, with no bacterial DNA"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"specialized transduction moves the segments that lie alongside a phage's integration site"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 draws the contrast directly: generalized transduction moves more or less any fragment, while specialized transduction moves the segments that lie alongside a phage's integration site.","distractorNotes":["That describes generalized transduction, which the passage names in the same sentence as the contrast.",null,"Plasmids move by conjugation in paragraph 4. The passage does not restrict transduction to plasmids.","Uptake of naked DNA from the surroundings is transformation, described in paragraph 6.","If only the phage genome moved, no bacterial gene would travel at all, and the passage is explaining how bacterial genes ride along."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q05","type":"detail","stem":"Which of the following is offered in the passage as evidence that resistance genes existed before the antibiotic era?","choices":["Soil bacteria shed their resistance genes within a few generations once antibiotics are withdrawn","Clinical isolates commonly carry integrons holding five or six cassettes","Bacterial populations in rich medium can double every twenty minutes","Cave bacteria sealed off for millions of years resist several modern drug classes","Permafrost bacteria grow faster than bacteria taken from the surface"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"cut off from the surface for something on the order of four million years"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"carry resistance to multiple modern drug classes"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 gives two pieces of evidence, and this is one of them: bacteria from a New Mexico cave chamber cut off from the surface for roughly four million years resist multiple modern drug classes.","distractorNotes":["The passage argues the opposite in paragraphs 13 through 15. Resistance often persists after a drug is withdrawn rather than disappearing quickly.","That detail is true and appears in paragraph 9, but it describes how much resistance accumulates in current clinical strains, not how old resistance is.","That figure comes from paragraph 2 and explains how fast a variant can spread, which is a different question.",null,"The passage mentions permafrost DNA carrying penicillin destroying enzyme genes, not anything about growth rates."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 11, \"reservoir\" most nearly means","choices":["a tank constructed to hold water for later use","a species that carries a pathogen without ever becoming ill from it","an existing supply of genes that selection can draw from","a laboratory collection of preserved bacterial strains","a barrier that keeps genes from moving between species"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"applied intense selection to a reservoir that was already there"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"The standing pool of such genes in soil and gut communities"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 calls the standing pool of resistance genes in soil and gut communities the resistome and says the antibiotic era applied selection to a reservoir that was already there. The word names a pre existing supply, not a container.","distractorNotes":["This is the everyday dictionary sense. Nothing is being stored as water here, and the passage is talking about genes in living communities.","This is a real biological use of the word in the study of infection, but the passage is describing a pool of genes, not a host species that stays healthy.",null,"The pool described sits in soil and gut communities in the wild, not in a curated laboratory collection.","The paragraph says the era pulled genes out of soil chromosomes and into human pathogens, so the reservoir is a source of movement, not a barrier against it."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 15, \"fixed\" most nearly means","choices":["repaired after having been damaged","held at a single location on the chromosome","set in advance by the prescribing physician","established across a population and not easily lost","attached permanently to a plasmid so that it cannot be shed"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"A gene that has become fixed in a population"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"does not vanish because prescribing habits change"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 says a gene that has become fixed in a population, with its cost paid down, does not vanish when prescribing habits change. The word describes a gene that has become a settled part of the population.","distractorNotes":["This is the most common everyday meaning, but the sentence is about a gene spreading through a population, not about something broken being mended.","This confuses the gene's position in a genome with its frequency in a population, which is what the sentence is tracking.","The paragraph is about what happens in bacterial populations regardless of prescribing, and prescribing habits are named as the thing that changes without dislodging the gene.",null,"Plasmid carriage is one reason a gene persists, described in paragraph 14, but the sentence is about the gene's standing in the population as a whole, not its physical attachment."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage implies that if resistance genes moved only from parent to offspring, then","choices":["resistance would appear far sooner after a drug is introduced","plasmids would carry more cassettes than they currently do","different lineages would be expected to arrive at different genetic solutions","resistance would still be expected to appear as the same sequence in distant species","resistance would be confined to the species that manufacture antibiotics"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"Independent mutation does not produce identical answers in lineages"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"carrying the same gene with the same sequence"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"Something is moving the gene itself."}],"why":"Paragraph 3 argues that independent mutation cannot produce identical answers in unrelated lineages, which is why the shared sequence points to transfer. Turn that around and vertical descent alone would leave each lineage with its own separate solution.","distractorNotes":["Nothing suggests vertical descent alone would be faster. Paragraph 5 notes that transfer can deliver a whole package at once, which is the faster route.","Cassette counts come from paragraph 9 and depend on integrons capturing incoming genes, which is horizontal movement, not vertical descent.",null,"This is exactly the observation paragraph 3 says vertical descent cannot explain, which is why the author concludes the gene itself is moving.","Paragraph 10 says producer organisms protect themselves, but the passage never claims resistance would be limited to them without transfer."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Based on the passage, what can be inferred about a resistance gene that carries almost no fitness cost?","choices":["It must have arrived by transduction rather than by conjugation or by transformation","It requires a compensatory mutation before it can be expressed at all","It will spread in hospital wards but not in soil communities","It cannot be carried on a plasmid alongside other resistance genes","Withdrawing the drug would be an unusually weak way to reduce it"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"some resistance genes appear to cost very little from the beginning"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"resistance should drain out of a population once prescribing stops"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"with its cost paid down"}],"why":"Paragraphs 13 and 14 hang the whole case for reversal on the idea that resistance is costly, and paragraph 14 lists low cost genes as one reason withdrawal disappoints. If there is little cost to escape, removing the drug removes little pressure against the gene.","distractorNotes":["Cost has nothing to do with which of the three transfer routes delivered the gene; the passage never links the two.","Compensatory mutations reduce an existing cost, as paragraph 14 says. A gene that already costs little has no need of one, and the passage never ties them to expression.","The passage places resistance genes in soil communities from the start, in paragraphs 10 and 11, so this contradicts the text.","Paragraph 5 describes plasmids carrying a dozen determinants at once, and nothing excludes a low cost gene from riding along.",null]},{"id":"rc-p04-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the central point of the passage?","choices":["Agricultural antibiotic use is the single cause of resistance in human infections, and restricting it would solve the problem","Resistance genes are ancient and spread chiefly by gene transfer, so stewardship should aim at slowing that spread rather than reversing it","Because bacteria divide quickly, mutation and selection within single lineages account for nearly all the resistance clinicians encounter","Conjugation, transformation, and transduction are the three routes of horizontal gene transfer, and each contributes to the spread of resistance","Since resistance cannot be reversed once it appears, the only realistic response is to keep developing new antibiotics faster than the old ones fail"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"and it is ancient"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"rearranged the contents of that reservoir"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"slow the movement of genes that already exist"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":2,"quote":"manage the traffic rather than to clear the road"}],"why":"The passage moves from a puzzle about speed, through the transfer mechanisms, to the evidence that resistance is ancient, and lands in paragraphs 15 and 16 on stewardship as a way to slow movement rather than restore an earlier state. That arc is the main idea.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 12 names feedlots as one mixing vessel among several, alongside hospitals and sewage, so the passage never makes agriculture the single cause.",null,"Paragraph 1 calls this explanation true as far as it goes but incomplete, and paragraph 3 says it cannot account for what clinicians see.","This is accurate and occupies several paragraphs, but it is the machinery the argument uses, not the conclusion the passage reaches.","Paragraph 15 says reversal does happen, though slowly and partially, and paragraph 16 endorses stewardship rather than abandoning it for new drugs."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q11","type":"function","stem":"The author's main purpose in describing the cave bacteria and the permafrost DNA is","choices":["to correct the assumption that resistance genes arose in the antibiotic era","to show that resistance is more severe in natural settings than it is in hospitals","to argue that soil bacteria should be mined for the next generation of drugs","to explain how integrons capture and file incoming gene cassettes","to warn that thawing permafrost will release new pathogens"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"where do the genes come from in the first place"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"that antibiotics created them, is simply wrong"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 opens by asking where the genes come from and says flatly that the intuitive answer, that antibiotics created them, is wrong. The cave and the permafrost are the evidence brought in to overturn that assumption.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage does not compare severity between settings. Paragraph 12 calls hospitals and feedlots mixing vessels, which is about concentration, not severity.","Paragraph 10 notes that many antibiotic scaffolds came from soil microbes, but that is background, not a proposal for drug discovery.","Integrons are explained in paragraph 8, several paragraphs earlier, and for a different reason.","Nothing in the passage predicts a release of pathogens from thawing. The permafrost appears only as a record of ancient DNA."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes the relationship between paragraph 13 and paragraph 14?","choices":["Paragraph 13 gives a single example and paragraph 14 generalizes from it to a broader rule","Both paragraphs describe the same mechanism from two different angles","Paragraph 13 raises a question that paragraph 14 declines to answer","Paragraph 14 supplies fresh experimental evidence for the claim in paragraph 13","Paragraph 13 builds a sound expectation and paragraph 14 explains why it often fails"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"resistance should drain out of a population once prescribing stops"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":6,"quote":"does not follow as reliably as it should"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":1,"quote":"Several things blunt it."}],"why":"Paragraph 13 builds the fitness cost argument and grants that its reasoning is sound, then says the conclusion does not follow reliably. Paragraph 14 opens with the phrase about what blunts it and lists the specific mechanisms that do so.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 13 presents a general argument rather than a single example, and paragraph 14 narrows to specific mechanisms rather than widening.","The two paragraphs describe opposing forces, not one mechanism seen twice.","Paragraph 14 answers the question paragraph 13 leaves open, in four distinct ways.","Paragraph 14 offers mechanisms that undercut the expectation, not evidence that supports it. The empirical record arrives in paragraph 15.",null]},{"id":"rc-p04-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the common habit of treating resistance arising and resistance spreading as one thing is best described as","choices":["amused by it","indifferent, since the distinction makes no practical difference to patients","approving, since simplification helps public understanding","critical, because merging the two leads to unreliable expectations","alarmed, predicting an imminent epidemic because of the confusion"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"collapsing it leads to bad predictions"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 says the distinction is the one most often collapsed in public discussion and that collapsing it leads to bad predictions. That is measured criticism, not amusement or panic.","distractorNotes":["There is no humor in the paragraph. The language is corrective and plainly serious.","The paragraph argues the opposite, that the distinction determines what human action can actually change.","The author treats the simplification as a source of bad predictions, so approval is the reverse of the stance taken.",null,"The tone is analytical rather than alarmed, and the passage makes no prediction of an imminent epidemic."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward antibiotic stewardship is best described as","choices":["skeptical that stewardship is worth the effort it requires","supportive, provided its goal is understood as slowing spread rather than reversing it","confident that strict stewardship will restore susceptibility within about a decade","neutral, presenting stewardship without offering any evaluation of it","dismissive of diagnostics and infection control as minor measures next to new drug development"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"None of this argues against stewardship."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"It argues for a more honest version of it."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"slow the movement of genes that already exist"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 states outright that none of this argues against stewardship and that it argues for a more honest version, then says the measures buy time rather than turning back the clock. That is support with a corrected goal.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph opens by denying exactly this reading and goes on to list the tools stewardship should use.",null,"Paragraph 15 says reversal is slow, partial, and organism specific, which rules out confidence in a decade long restoration.","The author evaluates stewardship directly, calling for a more honest version of it and naming which aims will and will not work.","Diagnostics and infection control head the author's own list of tools, and new drug development is presented as needing the time stewardship buys."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Some plasmids can establish themselves in distantly related host genera","A phage particle can inject bacterial DNA into a new host cell","Competence allows a cell to take up DNA released into its surroundings by dying bacteria","Integrons move between cells on their own, without help from other elements","Compensatory mutations can reduce the cost of carrying a resistance gene"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"Integrons themselves are not mobile, but they ride on things that are.","choice":3},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"hosts as distantly related as Escherichia and Pseudomonas","choice":0},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"it injects bacterial genes instead of viral ones","choice":1},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"take that naked DNA across the envelope","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"The capacity is called competence","choice":2},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"the cost can shrink toward nothing within a few hundred generations","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says plainly that integrons themselves are not mobile and that they ride on transposons which hop onto plasmids. The other four statements each restate something the passage asserts.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 says some plasmids establish themselves in hosts as distantly related as Escherichia and Pseudomonas, so this is supported.","Paragraph 7 describes a particle that injects bacterial genes instead of viral ones, so this is supported.","Paragraph 6 defines competence as the capacity to take up naked DNA released by dying bacteria, so this is supported.",null,"Paragraph 14 says compensatory mutations restore lost efficiency so the cost shrinks toward nothing, so this is supported."]},{"id":"rc-p04-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wants to know how a resistance gene that arrives without a promoter can still be switched on should look in the paragraph that discusses","choices":["the integron and its gene cassettes","the steps of conjugation and the origin of transfer","the cave and permafrost evidence","the difference between resistance arising and resistance spreading","the stewardship measures near the end"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"without a promoter in front of it sits inert"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"Integrons solve that problem"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 raises exactly that problem, saying a gene that lands without a promoter sits inert, and then presents the integron with its single shared promoter as the solution.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 4 explains how DNA physically gets into a new cell, which is the step before the expression problem arises.","Paragraph 10 addresses the age and origin of resistance genes, not how an arriving gene is expressed.","Paragraph 12 separates two processes conceptually and does not deal with promoters.","Paragraph 16 lists practical measures and says nothing about gene expression."]}]},"rc-p05":{"id":"rc-p05","title":"Volcanic Ash and the Zero Threshold","topic":"volcanic ash and aviation safety","field":"geology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1697,"paragraphs":[["The word ash does more harm than good.","It invites a picture of the soft gray powder left in a fireplace after the wood has burned.","Volcanic ash is none of that, and nothing in it has burned.","It shares almost nothing with soot except a color and a habit of hanging in the air.","A pilot who treats ash as smoke will reason about it wrongly.","An engineer who treats it as pulverized rock thrown to cruising altitude will reason about it correctly."],["What a volcano ejects is magma torn apart by its own gas.","Water and carbon dioxide dissolved in the melt come out of solution as the magma rises and the pressure falls, and the bubbles expand until the liquid between them cannot hold together.","The melt shatters.","The fragments cool within seconds, far too quickly for their atoms to settle into the ordered lattice of a crystal, so they are quenched into glass, a liquid structure frozen in place.","Fragments smaller than two millimeters across are called ash by convention.","They are angular, hard, and abrasive enough to scour a windscreen from clear to frosted in minutes."],["Size decides how far ash travels.","Coarse fragments fall out near the vent.","The fine tail behaves differently.","A particle ten micrometers across, roughly a seventh the width of a human hair, settles through still air at a few millimeters per second, so a cloud injected into the winds at nine kilometers can drift for days and cross an ocean before gravity takes much of it out.","Aviation's problem lies almost entirely in that fine tail, the part that leaves the volcano's neighborhood and arrives where nobody expects it."],["Inside a jet engine the trouble starts with temperature.","Air taken in at the front is compressed and burned with fuel in the combustor, where the gas exceeds 1,400 degrees Celsius before it reaches the turbine.","Silicate glass needs nothing like that much heat to lose its shape.","Depending on its composition, volcanic ash begins to soften and flow near 1,100 degrees.","Ash drawn into the intake passes from cold air into a region hundreds of degrees above its own softening range in well under a second.","It does not burn.","There is nothing in it left to burn.","It melts."],["Molten ash does not stay molten.","It is carried into the first stage of the turbine, where the metal is deliberately held cooler than the gas around it by air bled from the compressor and blown out through hundreds of small holes in every vane and blade.","Glass touching those cooler surfaces sticks and freezes onto them.","The cooling holes plug, so the metal beneath runs hotter than it was designed to run.","The deposit also narrows the passage the gas must squeeze through, and a turbine whose throat has been narrowed pushes back against the compressor ahead of it.","The airflow breaks down, the flame goes out, and the engine stops."],["The recovery is the strange part, and it is why the two most famous encounters ended with everyone alive.","A dead engine cools.","Glass and metal contract at different rates, the brittle layer cracks and flakes away in the airflow, and a shaft still turning in the wind can be relit.","The procedure this implies runs against instinct: descend out of the cloud, giving up altitude in the dark, wait, and try again."],["On 24 June 1982 a British Airways Boeing 747 flying from Kuala Lumpur toward Perth entered ash from Mount Galunggung in Java at night.","The weather radar showed nothing, because such radar is built to detect water droplets and is effectively blind to dry ash.","The crew saw a glow on the leading edges, smelled something acrid in the cabin, and then watched all four engines flame out.","The airplane glided for roughly sixteen minutes and lost about 25,000 feet before the engines relit one at a time.","It landed at Jakarta with a windscreen sandblasted nearly opaque."],["On 15 December 1989 a new Boeing 747-400 descending toward Anchorage flew into a cloud from Mount Redoubt that had been reported but had not reached the crew usefully.","All four engines failed again.","The airplane fell about 14,000 feet before the crew got them running and landed safely.","Nobody was hurt.","The repair bill, which included four replaced engines, came to roughly 80 million dollars.","Two aircraft, seven years apart, had shown that a hazard invisible to radar could silence a wide-body at cruise."],["The institutional answer took shape from the late 1980s into the 1990s as the International Airways Volcano Watch, run under the International Civil Aviation Organization.","Nine Volcanic Ash Advisory Centres divide the globe between them, at London, Toulouse, Anchorage, Washington, Montreal, Buenos Aires, Darwin, Wellington and Tokyo.","When a volcano erupts, the responsible center issues an advisory: polygons drawn on a chart showing where ash is expected at given flight levels now and at six, twelve and eighteen hours ahead."],["The polygons come out of a dispersion model, which is only as good as what it is told about the eruption.","It needs source parameters: the height of the column, the mass leaving the vent each second, and the range of particle sizes.","Only height is observed directly, by ground radar or satellite.","The mass rate is usually inferred from height through an empirical relation in which height rises as roughly the fourth root of the rate.","Read the other way, that relation punishes error: mass goes as the fourth power of height, so a column overestimated by a quarter overstates the ash by nearly a factor of two and a half.","The model then releases millions of notional particles into the winds of a weather forecast and follows them."],["Direct observation is thinner than it sounds.","The standard satellite technique compares brightness at two infrared wavelengths near eleven and twelve micrometers.","The difference between the two channels comes out positive over water cloud and negative over ash, which lets an analyst tell one from the other.","The method goes blind when ash lies beneath a meteorological cloud, when ice condenses onto the particles and dresses them as weather, and when the layer is very thin.","It also says little about how much ash sits at any one altitude, because a satellite looks down through a whole column and cannot resolve it into layers."],["The rule built on all this was short and, for a long time, unarguable: avoid ash.","Not avoid dense ash, not avoid ash above some stated amount.","Avoid it.","The guidance was written when nobody could say what exposure an engine could survive, and in the absence of a number the only defensible number is zero.","The people who wrote it deserve sympathy.","They had two total power losses, no laboratory data, and a duty not to gamble with passengers.","But a rule of that shape quietly makes a claim it cannot support.","The model produces a field of concentrations fading from thick to nothing, with no edge anywhere in it, and the rule needs an edge.","So an edge was drawn wherever the outermost contour fell, everything inside it forbidden and everything outside clear.","The rule was conservative in the engineer's sense of that word: it moved the whole weight of the uncertainty onto the side of not flying, and left the question of how much ash is too much permanently unasked."],["For nearly two decades nobody had much reason to ask it.","Certifying an engine against ash would mean expensive testing with a material that varies from one volcano to the next, in order to publish a number that could mainly expose the manufacturer to blame.","While eruptions closed a thinly flown corner of Alaska or the Pacific for a day or two, the cost of not knowing stayed small, and someone else paid it."],["Iceland ended that.","Eyjafjallajokull opened as a quiet flank fissure on 20 March 2010, and on 14 April the activity shifted beneath the summit ice cap.","Meltwater reaching the magma chilled and shattered it violently, producing an unusually large proportion of very fine ash, and the weather that month carried it southeast toward the busiest airspace in the world.","Applying the rule as written, authorities closed most of European airspace from 15 to 23 April.","About 100,000 flights were canceled, more than ten million travelers were stranded, and the industry's own estimate of its losses came to roughly 1.7 billion dollars.","No airplane was damaged, because almost none flew."],["The closure achieved what two engine failures had not.","Within days, research aircraft carrying particle counters were flying alongside the plume, and ground-based lidar stations were measuring the ash layers passing over Europe.","Much of the closed airspace turned out to hold concentrations far below anything the accident record suggested was dangerous.","Engine manufacturers, pressed to state a tolerance they had never been obliged to state, produced one.","Within weeks the binary chart had become a graded one: below two milligrams of ash per cubic meter of air, ordinary operations; between two and four, flight permitted with the manufacturer's agreement and added inspections; above four, closed."],["Two ideas came with the numbers, and both outlast them.","The first is that what harms an engine is a dose, the concentration multiplied by the minutes spent inside it, so a brief crossing of a thick layer and a long cruise through a thin one are not different in kind.","The second is that an advisory should carry its own uncertainty on its face, which is why centers now run their models repeatedly and publish the spread.","When Grimsvotn erupted in May 2011 with an initial column taller than anything Eyjafjallajokull produced, cancellations ran into the hundreds rather than the hundreds of thousands.","The science had not become certain.","It had become quantitative, which is a different and more useful thing.","The lesson worth keeping is not that the 2010 closure was an overreaction.","It is that a threshold of zero is not the absence of a judgment about risk.","It is a judgment made silently, by people who had not been given the data to make it any other way."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p05-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, ash particles have the structure of a glass rather than a crystal because","choices":["they are ground against one another inside the vent until their edges become rounded","dissolved gas keeps crystals from forming at any temperature","meltwater quenches them at the moment they leave the vent","they are melted a second time inside a jet engine","they cool too fast for their atoms to reach an ordered lattice"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"far too quickly for their atoms to settle into the ordered lattice of a crystal"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 says the fragments cool within seconds, far too quickly for their atoms to settle into the ordered lattice of a crystal, so they freeze as glass. Speed of cooling is the whole reason.","distractorNotes":["The passage calls the fragments angular, not rounded, and never mentions grinding inside the vent.","Dissolved gas is what tears the magma apart in paragraph 2. It is not offered as a reason crystals fail to form.","Meltwater appears only in the 2010 account in paragraph 14, where it explains fine fragmentation rather than glassy structure.","Melting inside an engine comes in paragraph 4, long after the particle is already glass.",null]},{"id":"rc-p05-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage gives the temperature, in degrees Celsius, at which volcanic ash begins to soften and flow as approximately","choices":["400","1,400","2,000","1,100","700"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"begins to soften and flow near 1,100 degrees"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 states that, depending on composition, ash begins to soften and flow near 1,100 degrees Celsius. The 1,400 figure in the same paragraph belongs to the gas in the combustor.","distractorNotes":["Far too low. Paragraph 4 puts the softening point up near 1,100 degrees.","This is the combustor gas, which paragraph 4 says exceeds 1,400 degrees. That is the number the softening point is contrasted against, not the softening point itself.","No figure this high appears anywhere, and the point of the paragraph is that the engine is already hotter than ash needs.",null,"Paragraph 4 gives one specific number for softening, and it is not this one."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to paragraph 5, a glass deposit in the first turbine stage does which two things?","choices":["Cooling holes become blocked and the gas passage is narrowed","The blades melt outright and the shaft seizes","The fuel supply is contaminated and the flame is put out directly","The compressor blades are worn thin and the intake is choked","The turbine spins faster and overspeeds the shaft"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"The cooling holes plug"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"narrows the passage the gas must squeeze through"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says the cooling holes plug, so the metal beneath runs hotter than designed, and the deposit narrows the passage the gas must squeeze through. Those are the two effects named.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage says the metal runs hotter than intended. It never says the blades melt or the shaft seizes.","Fuel appears in paragraph 4 only as what burns in the combustor, and the flame goes out because the airflow breaks down.","Wear on the compressor is not what paragraph 5 describes. The damage there is glass sticking to hot turbine parts.","The paragraph describes the opposite. A narrowed throat chokes the flow until the engine stops."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the weather radar on the 1982 flight showed nothing because","choices":["it had been switched off earlier in the flight to save electrical power","ash gives back a signal identical to heavy rain","it is built to detect water droplets and cannot see dry ash","the ash cloud was still below the airplane","the crew were watching the engine instruments instead"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"built to detect water droplets and is effectively blind to dry ash"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 explains that weather radar is designed to detect water droplets and is effectively blind to dry ash. The radar was working. It simply cannot see this hazard.","distractorNotes":["Nothing in paragraph 7 says the radar was off. The limitation described is what the radar can detect.","If ash looked like rain, the radar would have shown something. Paragraph 7 says it showed nothing at all.",null,"Paragraph 7 says the airplane entered the ash, so the cloud was not beneath it.","The crew noticed the glow and the smell. The radar limit is stated as a property of the radar, not of their attention."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q05","type":"detail","stem":"Under the graded scheme in paragraph 15, flight through air carrying three milligrams of ash per cubic meter is","choices":["prohibited outright","treated exactly as ordinary operations","allowed only above a stated flight level","permitted with the manufacturer's agreement and added inspections","permitted only for aircraft fitted with one particular type of engine"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":5,"quote":"between two and four, flight permitted with the manufacturer's agreement and added inspections"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 sets ordinary operations below two milligrams per cubic meter and closure above four. Three falls in the middle band, where flight is allowed with the manufacturer's agreement and extra inspections.","distractorNotes":["Prohibition begins above four milligrams per cubic meter, and three is below that.","Ordinary operations apply below two milligrams per cubic meter, and three is above that.","The bands in paragraph 15 are set by concentration, not by altitude.",null,"No such restriction appears. The condition named is the manufacturer's agreement, not the engine model."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 2, \"quenched\" most nearly means","choices":["soaked in water so that it cools evenly all the way through","extinguished, the way a flame is put out by smothering","satisfied, the way a thirst is relieved by drinking","hardened by repeated cycles of heating and hammering","cooled so fast that a disordered structure is locked in"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"quenched into glass, a liquid structure frozen in place"}],"why":"In paragraph 2 the word sits between rapid cooling and a liquid structure frozen in place, so it means cooling so fast that the disorder of the melt is trapped rather than resolving into crystals.","distractorNotes":["Water appears elsewhere in the passage, but paragraph 2 says the fragments cool in air within seconds.","This is the everyday sense. The paragraph is about cooling, and the passage insists nothing in ash is burning.","A real dictionary sense that has nothing to do with a sentence about atoms failing to reach a crystal lattice.","Repeated heating is the opposite of what happens. The fragment gets one very fast cooling and keeps what it leaves.",null]},{"id":"rc-p05-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 12, \"conservative\" most nearly means","choices":["careful with money and therefore unwilling to pay for engine testing","placing the whole burden of uncertainty on the restrictive side","resistant to new technology of any kind","cautious in a way that is always correct","traditional, in keeping with long established practice"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":10,"quote":"moved the whole weight of the uncertainty onto the side of not flying"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 defines the word inside the sentence: the rule moved the whole weight of the uncertainty onto the side of not flying. That is the engineering sense the author is naming.","distractorNotes":["The cost of testing is raised in paragraph 13, which is a separate point from what the word means here.",null,"Paragraph 12 is about how a safety margin was set, not about attitudes toward technology.","The paragraph separates caution from this word and argues the rule was not simply correct.","Long practice is not the sense here. The author supplies the meaning in the same sentence."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q08","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 6 most strongly supports which conclusion about ash damage to an engine?","choices":["Some of it can undo itself once the engine stops making heat","It is unlikely to occur if the engine is throttled back in advance","It is permanent, so a damaged engine must be replaced before flight continues","It comes mainly from abrasion rather than from melting","It grows worse the longer an engine is left to cool"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"A dead engine cools."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"the brittle layer cracks and flakes away in the airflow"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says a dead engine cools, the glass and metal contract at different rates, and the brittle layer cracks and flakes away so the engine can be relit. The damage partly reverses itself.","distractorNotes":[null,"Throttle setting is never discussed. Paragraph 6 describes what happens after the engines have already failed.","Engines were replaced after the 1989 flight, but paragraph 6 explains why a deposit can shed in flight and allow a relight.","Abrasion damages the windscreen in paragraphs 2 and 7. The engine damage in paragraphs 5 and 6 comes from melted glass.","Paragraph 6 says a cooling engine sheds its deposit, so cooling time helps rather than harms."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Which conclusion about advisory boundaries follows most directly from paragraph 10?","choices":["They are measured directly by radar and satellite at each flight level","They are drawn deliberately wider than the model output to add a safety margin","They are recalculated only after an eruption has ended","Particle size has no measurable effect on where the model sends the ash","A small error in column height can shift the forecast ash load sharply"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"mass goes as the fourth power of height"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"overstates the ash by nearly a factor of two and a half"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says mass goes as the fourth power of height, so a column overestimated by a quarter overstates the ash by nearly a factor of two and a half. An uncertain input becomes a much more uncertain output.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 10 says only the height is observed directly. The mass and the spread are modeled.","Widening for safety is not described in paragraph 10. The question of where the edge falls comes later, in paragraph 12.","The advisories in paragraph 9 look six, twelve and eighteen hours ahead, so they are issued while the eruption continues.","Particle size is listed in paragraph 10 as one of the source parameters the model requires.",null]},{"id":"rc-p05-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best states the central point of the passage?","choices":["Volcanic ash is dangerous to aircraft chiefly because it is abrasive enough to blind a crew in flight","Aviation met an unmeasured hazard with a blanket ban until a costly closure forced measurement","Dispersion models are now accurate enough to make ash advisories reliable","The 1982 and 1989 failures show that flying near any volcano is unsafe","Iceland's volcanoes pose a uniquely severe threat to European air travel"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":10,"quote":"left the question of how much ash is too much permanently unasked"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"The closure achieved what two engine failures had not."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":8,"quote":"a threshold of zero is not the absence of a judgment about risk"}],"why":"The passage moves from what ash is, to what it does inside an engine, to a rule of avoid all ash written without data, to the 2010 closure that forced concentration numbers into existence. That arc is the point.","distractorNotes":["Abrasion is real in the passage, but it is one detail. The engine failures and the rule built on them carry the argument.",null,"Paragraph 10 stresses how sensitive the models are to an uncertain input, and paragraph 16 says the science became quantitative rather than certain.","The two flights are used to explain where the rule came from, not to argue that flight near volcanoes is unsafe.","Iceland is the case that forced the change, but nine centers cover the whole globe and the problem is framed as a global one."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q11","type":"function","stem":"The first paragraph serves mainly to","choices":["establish that ash clouds are visible to a crew well before they are entered","clear away an everyday association before the real material is described","introduce the two accidents that the rest of the passage explains","argue that pilots are less well informed than engineers about volcanoes","explain why ash is hard to detect from a satellite"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":1,"quote":"The word ash does more harm than good."},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"It invites a picture of the soft gray powder"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":6,"quote":"will reason about it correctly"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 spends its whole length separating volcanic ash from fireplace ash and soot, so that the reader stops picturing something burned and soft before paragraph 2 describes pulverized rock.","distractorNotes":["The passage says the opposite in paragraph 7, where the radar showed nothing and the encounter happened at night.",null,"The accidents arrive in paragraphs 7 and 8. The opening is about what ash is and is not.","The pilot and engineer sentences contrast two mental pictures of ash, not the competence of two professions.","Satellite detection is the subject of paragraph 11 and is not raised in the opening."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["A list of eruptions arranged from earliest to most recent","A comparison of two competing explanations for the engine failures","A single problem stated at the start and then deliberately left unresolved at the end","A substance, then a mechanism, then a rule, then the event that revised it","A history of one regulatory body told through its nine centers"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"What a volcano ejects is magma torn apart by its own gas."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"Inside a jet engine the trouble starts with temperature."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"The rule built on all this was short"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":1,"quote":"Iceland ended that."}],"why":"Paragraphs 1 through 3 describe the material, 4 through 6 the mechanism inside an engine, 7 through 13 the accidents and the rule they produced, and 14 through 16 the eruption that forced the rule to be rewritten.","distractorNotes":["Eruptions do appear in date order, but they occupy a small part of a passage that moves from substance to mechanism to regulation.","Only one mechanism is offered anywhere: melted silicate glass freezing onto cooler turbine metal.","The closing paragraphs resolve the problem with graded thresholds and the idea of a dose.",null,"The nine centers occupy one paragraph. This is not an institutional history."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude in paragraph 12 toward the people who wrote the avoid all ash rule is best described as","choices":["openly scornful of their unwillingness to gather evidence","indifferent, since the rule has since been replaced","understanding of their position while critical of what the rule hid","admiring, with no reservations about the rule itself","puzzled that anyone would restrict flight over a hazard they could not see"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"The people who wrote it deserve sympathy."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":7,"quote":"quietly makes a claim it cannot support"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 says those people deserve sympathy and had a duty not to gamble with passengers, then argues in the same breath that the rule makes a claim it cannot support and leaves a question permanently unasked.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 12 states plainly that these people deserve sympathy and faced a real duty.","Indifference does not fit a paragraph that argues at length about what the rule left unasked.",null,"The sympathy is genuine, but the same paragraph says the rule quietly makes a claim it cannot support.","The author treats the hazard as real throughout and questions only the shape of the rule."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In the final paragraph, the author's attitude toward the 2010 closure is best described as","choices":["unwilling to call it a mistake, since the number needed was missing","firmly convinced that it was a clear overreaction by regulators who were too cautious","certain that it prevented a disaster that would otherwise have taken place","neutral, offering the reader no view of the episode at all","regretful that airlines were not consulted before the airspace was closed"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":7,"quote":"not that the 2010 closure was an overreaction"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":9,"quote":"had not been given the data to make it any other way"}],"why":"The last paragraph says directly that the lesson worth keeping is not that the closure was an overreaction, and then reframes a threshold of zero as a silent judgment made without data.","distractorNotes":[null,"The final paragraph rejects this reading in so many words.","The passage says no airplane was damaged because almost none flew. It does not claim a disaster was averted.","The author does take a position, calling a threshold of zero a judgment made silently.","Consultation is never raised. The gap the author names is data, not process."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage presents all of the following as limits on knowing where ash is and how much of it is there EXCEPT","choices":["Weather radar returns no signal from dry ash","A satellite cannot separate a column into layers","The mass leaving the vent is inferred rather than measured","Forecast winds are unavailable to the modeling centers","The satellite method fails when ash sits under ordinary cloud"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"into the winds of a weather forecast","choice":3},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"effectively blind to dry ash","choice":0},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"cannot resolve it into layers","choice":1},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"The mass rate is usually inferred from height","choice":2},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"when ash lies beneath a meteorological cloud","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 10 says the model releases its particles into the winds of a weather forecast, so forecast winds are exactly what the centers do have. The other four limits appear in paragraphs 7, 10 and 11.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 7 gives this as the reason the radar showed nothing in 1982.","Paragraph 11 says a satellite looks down through a whole column and cannot resolve layers.","Paragraph 10 says the mass rate is inferred from column height rather than observed.",null,"Paragraph 11 lists ash beneath a meteorological cloud as a case where the method goes blind."]},{"id":"rc-p05-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wanted to find out how air taken from the compressor is used to keep turbine metal cool should look in which paragraph?","choices":["Paragraph 2","Paragraph 4","Paragraph 5","Paragraph 9","Paragraph 11"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"air bled from the compressor and blown out through hundreds of small holes"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 is the one that describes air bled from the compressor and blown out through hundreds of small holes in every vane and blade to hold the metal cooler than the gas.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 2 is about how ash forms and what it is made of.","Paragraph 4 gives the temperatures, but the cooling air and the holes appear one paragraph later.",null,"Paragraph 9 covers the advisory centers, not engine hardware.","Paragraph 11 is about satellite detection."]}]},"rc-p06":{"id":"rc-p06","title":"Plaque as a Community, Not Debris","topic":"the oral microbiome and the biofilm model of dental caries","field":"microbiology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1698,"paragraphs":[["A few hours after a careful brushing, a fingernail drawn along the gumline will come away with a soft, pale smear.","For most of the twentieth century, dentistry treated it as debris, an accumulation of food residue, shed cells, and stray bacteria whose only interesting property was that it could be scraped off.","The account that replaced it is less tidy and far more useful.","Dental plaque is not a pile of leftovers.","It is a community with architecture, and the disease it can produce is better understood as a change in local conditions than as an invasion."],["A biofilm is a population of microorganisms attached to a surface and held in a matrix that the cells themselves secrete.","In plaque that matrix is mostly polysaccharide, with protein and extracellular DNA woven through it, and it is not packing material.","It fixes cells at stable addresses so that neighbors remain neighbors, and it slows the movement of small molecules into and out of the film.","It turns a smear of bacteria into a structure with an inside and an outside."],["Assembly follows a reliable order.","Within minutes of a cleaning, salivary proteins adsorb onto the exposed enamel and form the acquired pellicle, a conditioning film of proline-rich proteins, statherin, mucins, and enzymes only a fraction of a micrometer thick.","The first colonizers, mostly oral streptococci such as Streptococcus sanguinis, Streptococcus gordonii, and Streptococcus oralis, along with Actinomyces species, do not bind bare enamel at all.","They bind receptors displayed on the pellicle.","Later arrivals are poor at binding pellicle and instead attach to cells already in place, a specific cell-to-cell recognition called coaggregation.","Fusobacterium nucleatum, a long spindle-shaped anaerobe, is the great connector of the mouth, able to bind both early streptococci and late colonizers that will bind almost nothing else.","Over several days the film thickens and diversifies; more than seven hundred bacterial species have been catalogued across the human mouth."],["Sucrose holds a special place in this system.","Bacterial enzymes called glucosyltransferases split the sugar and polymerize its glucose into glucans, sticky branched polymers that make up much of the plaque matrix.","The reaction needs no cellular energy, because the energy is already stored in the bond joining the two halves of sucrose.","Glucans glue cells to the tooth and to one another and supply fresh binding sites for newcomers.","A sucrose-rich diet therefore does two things at once: it feeds acid production, and it builds the scaffolding that holds the acid producers against the enamel."],["Because the matrix slows diffusion, a film far thinner than a millimeter develops steep internal gradients.","Oxygen is consumed near the outer surface, leaving the depths anaerobic and hospitable to organisms that could not survive in open saliva.","Acid made in those depths is not immediately washed away.","Microelectrode measurements show that the pH inside plaque can sit more than a full unit below the pH of the saliva a millimeter away.","The consequence is the central fact of the model: the tooth surface does not experience the chemistry of the mouth.","It experiences the chemistry of the film pressed against it."],["Once plaque was recognized as a community, the next question was which members matter.","The specific plaque hypothesis, dominant from the 1970s onward, answered that only a few do, and named Streptococcus mutans and the lactobacilli.","The case was reasonable.","Both groups are strongly acidogenic, producing acid quickly from dietary sugar, and both are aciduric, meaning they keep growing and metabolizing at pH values that stall their neighbors.","The framing was attractive for a second reason: it made tooth decay an infectious disease with a named agent, and infectious diseases come with a familiar playbook of identify, target, and eliminate."],["The evidence never quite cooperated.","Lesions appear in mouths from which mutans streptococci cannot be recovered, and plenty of decay-free mouths carry them in abundance.","When sequencing replaced culturing, other capable acid producers turned up in active lesions, among them lactobacilli, bifidobacteria, Scardovia wiggsiae, certain Actinomyces, and the yeast Candida albicans.","Streptococcus mutans remains a competent troublemaker and a serviceable marker of risk, but it proved neither necessary nor sufficient.","A model resting on one organism cannot absorb that many exceptions."],["The ecological plaque hypothesis offered a different account.","Its claim is that the species dominating a site are the species that local conditions favor, and that caries follows a shift in those conditions rather than the arrival of a villain.","Frequent fermentable carbohydrate means frequent acid, and the acid is itself the selective pressure.","Species that tolerate low pH keep growing while their acid-sensitive neighbors slow, decline, or are crowded out.","The organisms that best survive the acid are the ones that make the most of it, so the output of the film turns more acidic still.","The community is plastic, and its makeup at any moment is a record of the conditions that have been acting on it."],["What is lost in that shift matters as much as what is gained.","Several of the acid-sensitive residents are actively protective.","Some run the arginine deiminase system or produce urease, pathways that release ammonia and push local pH upward.","Others generate hydrogen peroxide that holds competitors in check.","A sustained acid challenge therefore removes brakes at the same time that it adds accelerators, which is part of why the shift, once established, tends to hold."],["The time course of a single sugar exposure is well mapped.","Plaque pH begins to fall within two to five minutes, reaches a minimum of roughly 4.5 to 5.5 within about five to twenty minutes, and then climbs back toward a resting value near 6.7 as saliva buffers and clears the acid, a recovery that commonly takes twenty to sixty minutes.","Enamel begins to dissolve below a critical pH near 5.5.","Exposed root dentin, which carries less mineral, begins to dissolve closer to 6.2.","That curve explains why frequency outranks quantity.","Sweets eaten at one sitting drive the pH down once.","The same sweets taken in small bites across an afternoon restart the descent again and again and hold the film under the threshold for hours."],["Underneath the microbiology sits a plain chemistry.","Enamel is about ninety-six percent mineral by weight, a carbonated form of hydroxyapatite built from calcium, phosphate, and hydroxide.","When the fluid inside plaque falls below the critical pH, it becomes undersaturated with respect to that crystal and mineral dissolves out of the surface.","When pH recovers and the fluid is again supersaturated, calcium and phosphate move back in.","Neither direction is an event.","Both run all day, in every mouth, and what decides the fate of a tooth is the budget, the running difference between the two.","An early lesion, the chalky white spot visible on a dried tooth, is subsurface mineral loss beneath a surface layer that is still intact, and while that layer holds, the loss can be repaid."],["Saliva is the other half of the ledger, and it does more than rinse.","Its bicarbonate and phosphate buffer acid, and its flow clears sugar.","It carries calcium and phosphate at concentrations that keep it supersaturated with respect to enamel, while statherin and proline-rich proteins keep that fluid from precipitating mineral where none is wanted.","When flow fails, through certain medications, radiation to the head and neck, or an autoimmune disease of the glands, decay can become rapid and widespread in a person whose diet and bacteria have not changed at all."],["Fluoride was long explained as something that makes a tooth stronger from the inside, built into the enamel during development.","The part of the story that carries the most weight is narrower and happens at the crystal surface, largely after the tooth has erupted.","When fluoride ion is present in the fluid around a crystal during a recovery period, it adsorbs to the surface and is taken up as new mineral forms, producing a fluoridated apatite that does not dissolve until roughly pH 4.5.","Lowering that threshold by about a unit shortens the part of every acid cycle in which the crystal loses ground, and fluoride also speeds the repair itself.","Concentrated applications leave deposits resembling calcium fluoride that release fluoride when acid arrives, a reservoir switched on by the very condition it answers.","At high concentration fluoride also inhibits bacterial enolase and the pump that expels protons, though that effect is secondary.","What the chemistry recommends is a low concentration present often."],["All of this bears on a phrase that circulates widely, that tooth decay is an infection a person catches.","The phrase is not entirely wrong.","Strains do move between people, and transmission from caregiver to infant is well documented.","But the organisms involved are ordinary residents of nearly every human mouth, and their mere presence predicts very little.","Calling the condition an infection aims treatment at eradication, and no one can sterilize a mouth, nor would clearing out the resident community be desirable, since much of it competes with the acid makers and buffers their acid.","The more defensible statement is that the cast is already assembled, and that diet, saliva, and fluoride decide which members prosper."],["The ecological account should not be oversold in its turn.","It describes a mechanism convincingly and still predicts an individual's next five years poorly.","Enamel structure, salivary flow and composition, genetics, fluoride exposure, and the particular community a person started with all contribute, and no current risk assessment separates high risk from low risk with much confidence.","The model has earned its standing less by settling questions than by changing which questions look worth asking."],["Those questions now shape practice.","If a lesion is a balance that has tipped rather than a hole that has appeared, then the earliest lesions are candidates for repair rather than removal, and arresting one counts as a result.","Advice shifts from scrubbing harder toward reducing how often the film is fed, keeping fluoride present, and protecting saliva.","The soft film that returns by evening is not evidence of failure but the ordinary state of a warm wet surface that is never sterile.","The useful question is not how to abolish it, but what it is being given, and how often."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p06-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the first bacteria to settle on a freshly cleaned tooth attach to","choices":["glucans left behind from earlier sucrose exposure","receptors displayed on a film of adsorbed salivary proteins","strands of extracellular DNA within the plaque matrix","bare enamel crystal uncovered by the cleaning","the surfaces of Fusobacterium nucleatum cells already in place"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"do not bind bare enamel at all"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"They bind receptors displayed on the pellicle."}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the first colonizers do not bind bare enamel at all. They bind receptors displayed on the acquired pellicle, the film of salivary proteins that adsorbs within minutes of a cleaning.","distractorNotes":["Glucans are built later from dietary sucrose and are matrix material, not the surface the first colonizers land on.",null,"Extracellular DNA is listed in paragraph 2 as part of the matrix, not as the attachment site for the first colonizers.","The passage says the opposite in the same sentence: those species do not bind bare enamel, which is why the pellicle matters.","That reverses the order in paragraph 3: later arrivals bind cells already in place, and Fusobacterium nucleatum is the connector between early and late groups."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage indicates that exposed root dentin begins to lose mineral","choices":["only once plaque pH drops below about 4.5","near pH 6.7, the resting value of plaque","at a pH above the threshold for enamel","only where salivary flow has already failed","at the same pH as enamel, since both are apatite"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"begins to dissolve closer to 6.2"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"below a critical pH near 5.5"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 gives two thresholds: enamel near pH 5.5 and exposed root dentin closer to 6.2. Since 6.2 is the higher number, root dentin starts dissolving earlier in the pH drop.","distractorNotes":["About 4.5 is the figure from paragraph 13 for fluoridated apatite, not for root dentin.","6.7 is the resting pH plaque climbs back toward, not a point at which mineral is lost.",null,"Paragraph 12 links failed flow to rapid decay, but paragraph 10 sets the root dentin threshold without any such condition.","The passage gives root dentin a different threshold and explains it by its lower mineral content."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, bacteria can build glucans without spending cellular energy because","choices":["the glucose is taken from proteins already in the pellicle","saliva supplies the phosphate the reaction consumes","the bond joining sucrose already holds the needed energy","glucosyltransferases work only in the anaerobic depths of the film","the acid released during the reaction drives the polymer forward"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"the energy is already stored in the bond joining the two halves of sucrose"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 states it plainly: the reaction needs no cellular energy because the energy is already stored in the bond that joins the two halves of sucrose.","distractorNotes":["The pellicle is a protein film; paragraph 4 says the glucose comes from sucrose.","Salivary phosphate appears in paragraph 12 in connection with buffering and mineral supply, not with glucan synthesis.",null,"Anaerobic depths come from paragraph 5 and describe oxygen gradients, not the energetics of glucan building.","Acid is a separate product of sugar fermentation, and the passage never makes it the driver of glucan formation."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q04","type":"detail","stem":"Which of the following does the passage describe as a way resident bacteria push the local pH upward?","choices":["releasing ammonia through urease or arginine deiminase","generating hydrogen peroxide","polymerizing glucose from sucrose into branched glucans","consuming oxygen in the outer layer of the film","adsorbing fluoride onto the enamel crystal surface"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"pathways that release ammonia and push local pH upward"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 names the arginine deiminase system and urease as pathways that release ammonia and push local pH upward.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 9 does credit some residents with hydrogen peroxide, but its stated job is holding competitors in check, not raising pH.","Glucan building is described in paragraph 4 as matrix construction, with no effect on pH given.","Oxygen consumption in paragraph 5 creates anaerobic depths; the passage ties it to which species can live there, not to pH.","Fluoride uptake belongs to paragraph 13 and is chemistry at the crystal surface rather than something the bacteria do."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The passage states that mineral containing fluoride resists dissolving until the pH falls to roughly","choices":["4.5","5.0","5.5","6.2","6.7"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"does not dissolve until roughly pH 4.5"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 says fluoride taken up during repair produces a fluoridated apatite that does not dissolve until roughly pH 4.5, which shortens the part of each acid cycle that costs mineral.","distractorNotes":[null,"5.0 sits inside the range plaque pH dips to after sugar, but the passage never uses it as a dissolution threshold.","5.5 is the threshold for ordinary enamel, the number fluoride improves on.","6.2 is the threshold paragraph 10 gives for exposed root dentin.","6.7 is the resting pH plaque returns to after a sugar exposure, from paragraph 10."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 8, \"plastic\" most nearly means","choices":["able to be molded by hand into a chosen shape","made of a synthetic polymer","fixed in form once it has assembled","reshaped by the conditions acting on it","artificial rather than naturally occurring"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"The community is plastic"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"a record of the conditions that have been acting on it"}],"why":"The sentence right after the word explains it: the community's makeup at any moment is a record of the conditions that have been acting on it. The word points to something shaped by its surroundings.","distractorNotes":["This keeps the molding idea but adds someone doing the molding, while the passage credits the local conditions.","The literal material sense does not fit; the matrix described in paragraph 2 is a polysaccharide the cells make themselves.","This is the reverse of the point, since the whole paragraph describes a membership that changes as conditions change.",null,"That is a common dictionary sense of the word, but nothing in paragraph 8 contrasts natural with artificial."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 11, \"budget\" most nearly means","choices":["a fixed limit on how much mineral a tooth is allowed to lose","a schedule setting when demineralization happens","the total mineral a tooth carries when it erupts","the running net balance between two opposing processes","an allowance of sugar a person can take in each day"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":6,"quote":"the budget, the running difference between the two"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says mineral loss and mineral return both run all day, and then calls the budget the running difference between the two. It is a net balance, not a cap or a plan.","distractorNotes":["A cap implies some allowance being enforced, and the passage describes a running total instead.","The passage explicitly says neither direction is an event, which rules out a set schedule.","The starting amount of mineral is not what the word tracks; the sentence is about the difference between loss and return over time.",null,"The passage does discuss how often sugar arrives in paragraph 10, but the word here labels a mineral balance, not a diet limit."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The description of gradients in paragraph 5 most strongly supports which conclusion?","choices":["Oxygen is absent from every layer of a mature plaque film","The species inside plaque are spread evenly through the film","Plaque must grow thicker than a millimeter before enamel is at risk","Saliva loses the ability to buffer acid once a biofilm has formed","A saliva pH reading can understate the acid at the tooth surface"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"more than a full unit below the pH of the saliva a millimeter away"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"the tooth surface does not experience the chemistry of the mouth"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 reports that pH inside plaque can sit more than a full unit below the saliva a millimeter away, and it closes by saying the tooth meets the chemistry of the film rather than of the mouth. A saliva reading would therefore look better than conditions at the surface.","distractorNotes":["Oxygen is consumed near the outer surface, which leaves the depths anaerobic; the passage does not empty the whole film of oxygen.","Gradients are the point of the paragraph, and they mean the film is not uniform.","The paragraph says the gradients form in a film far thinner than a millimeter, so thickness of that scale is not required.","Paragraphs 10 and 12 both show saliva buffering and clearing acid even though a film is present.",null]},{"id":"rc-p06-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that an approach aimed at wiping out as much of the plaque community as possible would risk","choices":["leaving enamel permanently unable to take mineral back up","converting sucrose into a form bacteria cannot use","stopping the pellicle from forming after a cleaning","removing residents that raise pH and check acid makers","raising the critical pH of enamel above 6.2"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"nor would clearing out the resident community be desirable"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"removes brakes at the same time that it adds accelerators"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 describes acid-sensitive residents that release ammonia and hydrogen peroxide, and paragraph 14 says clearing out the resident community would not be desirable because much of it competes with the acid makers and buffers their acid.","distractorNotes":["Remineralization in paragraph 11 depends on pH and on calcium and phosphate in the fluid, not on which bacteria are present.","Nothing in the passage connects removing bacteria to changing the chemistry of sucrose itself.","The pellicle is made of salivary proteins and forms within minutes of any cleaning, so bacteria are not required for it.",null,"Critical pH is a property of the mineral in paragraph 11, and 6.2 belongs to root dentin rather than enamel."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["Decay reflects a shift in a resident community, not the arrival of a pathogen","Streptococcus mutans is the one organism responsible for nearly all tooth decay","Brushing the film away is the most effective defense a person has against decay","Salivary flow by itself decides whether a given tooth will decay","Fluoride has made the study of oral bacteria largely unnecessary in dentistry"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"a change in local conditions than as an invasion"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"rather than the arrival of a villain"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":6,"quote":"diet, saliva, and fluoride decide which members prosper"}],"why":"The passage moves from plaque as a structured community, through the failure of a single-organism explanation, to the ecological account and the running mineral balance it implies. The closing paragraphs make that reframing explicit.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 7 shows this claim failing, calling the organism neither necessary nor sufficient.","Paragraph 16 shifts advice away from scrubbing harder and toward how often the film is fed.","Saliva is one contributor among several, and paragraph 15 lists others that matter as well.","Fluoride gets one paragraph as surface chemistry, and the passage never sets it against studying the community."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 7 functions primarily to","choices":["recommend that every patient be tested for Candida albicans","argue that culturing gives more reliable results than sequencing","identify the organism most useful for judging a person's risk","explain how the matrix keeps acid pressed against the enamel","show why an explanation built on one organism could not hold"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"The evidence never quite cooperated."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"A model resting on one organism cannot absorb that many exceptions."}],"why":"Paragraph 7 lists findings the earlier model could not absorb: lesions without mutans streptococci, decay-free mouths full of them, and other acid producers found by sequencing. It closes by saying a model resting on one organism cannot carry that many exceptions.","distractorNotes":["Candida albicans appears only in a list of acid producers, and the passage makes no testing recommendation.","The passage credits sequencing with revealing what culturing missed, which is the reverse of this claim.","The paragraph does call the organism a serviceable marker, but that is a concession made in passing, not the work the paragraph does.","That is the job of paragraph 5, not paragraph 7.",null]},{"id":"rc-p06-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes how paragraphs 6 through 8 are arranged?","choices":["A chemical mechanism is laid out and then applied to one patient's history","A clinical recommendation is given and then supported with laboratory measurements","A popular belief is stated and then shown to be entirely false","Two rival explanations are described and then combined into a single model","An earlier explanation is presented, tested against evidence, and replaced"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"The specific plaque hypothesis, dominant from the 1970s onward"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"The evidence never quite cooperated."},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"The ecological plaque hypothesis offered a different account."}],"why":"Paragraph 6 presents the specific plaque hypothesis and grants that its case was reasonable, paragraph 7 sets the evidence against it, and paragraph 8 supplies the ecological account in its place.","distractorNotes":["The passage never follows an individual patient.","No recommendation appears in these paragraphs; advice waits until paragraph 16.","Too strong: the passage keeps Streptococcus mutans as a real troublemaker and a serviceable marker of risk.","The ecological account replaces the earlier one rather than merging with it.",null]},{"id":"rc-p06-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the specific plaque hypothesis is best described as","choices":["fair to its reasoning yet unconvinced","dismissive of it as careless thinking","alarmed that it still directs most treatment","eager to see it restored to favor","indifferent to whether it was ever accepted"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"The case was reasonable."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"it proved neither necessary nor sufficient"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 says the case was reasonable and lays out the evidence behind it, and paragraph 7 then explains calmly why that case does not cover the findings. The author gives the idea its due and still sets it aside.","distractorNotes":[null,"Too harsh: the passage says outright that the case was reasonable.","No claim is made that this model still governs treatment; paragraph 16 describes practice shaped by the newer account.","The passage moves past this model rather than arguing for its return.","The author spends two full paragraphs weighing the idea, which is not indifference."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 15, the author's stance toward the ecological account is best described as","choices":["neutral, since the paragraph makes no judgment either way","supportive but careful not to overstate what it can do","impatient with researchers who keep studying saliva","doubtful that it captures any real mechanism","confident that it now predicts an individual's risk accurately"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"should not be oversold in its turn"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":2,"quote":"describes a mechanism convincingly and still predicts an individual's next five years poorly"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 opens by warning against overselling the account, grants that it describes a mechanism convincingly, notes that it predicts an individual's next five years poorly, and credits it with changing which questions look worth asking.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph offers a clear judgment: valuable, and not yet predictive.",null,"Saliva appears in the list of contributors, and nothing in the paragraph criticizes studying it.","The same paragraph says the account describes a mechanism convincingly, so doubt about the mechanism is the wrong read.","This is the claim the paragraph explicitly denies."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports each of the following statements about saliva EXCEPT:","choices":["It contains proteins that keep mineral from precipitating where none is wanted.","It neutralizes plaque acid about as fast as bacteria can produce it.","Its flow clears sugar from the mouth.","It carries calcium and phosphate at levels that keep it supersaturated with respect to enamel.","Its bicarbonate and phosphate buffer acid."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"a recovery that commonly takes twenty to sixty minutes","choice":1},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"Its bicarbonate and phosphate buffer acid","choice":4},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"its flow clears sugar","choice":2},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"keep it supersaturated with respect to enamel","choice":3},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"keep that fluid from precipitating mineral where none is wanted","choice":0}],"why":"Paragraph 12 supports the first four points directly. The fifth runs against the passage: paragraph 5 says acid made in the depths of the film is not immediately washed away, and paragraph 10 gives recovery times of twenty to sixty minutes.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 12 credits statherin and proline-rich proteins with exactly this, so it is supported.",null,"Paragraph 12 states this, so it is supported and not the exception.","Paragraph 12 states this, so it is supported and not the exception.","Paragraph 12 states this, so it is supported and not the exception."]},{"id":"rc-p06-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for why sucrose, more than sugar in general, helps hold the film against the tooth should turn to","choices":["paragraph 4","paragraph 3","paragraph 10","paragraph 2","paragraph 13"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"Sucrose holds a special place in this system."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"builds the scaffolding that holds the acid producers against the enamel"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 is the one that singles out sucrose, explaining how glucosyltransferases turn it into glucans that glue cells to the tooth and to one another.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 3 covers the order in which species arrive, not what diet contributes.","Paragraph 10 follows pH after a sugar exposure, which is about acid rather than attachment.","Paragraph 2 defines a biofilm and its matrix in general terms and never names a dietary sugar.","Paragraph 13 is about fluoride at the crystal surface."]}]},"rc-p07":{"id":"rc-p07","title":"What Dissolving Carbon Does To Shells","topic":"ocean acidification and calcifying organisms","field":"ocean chemistry","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1681,"paragraphs":[["Roughly a quarter of the carbon dioxide human activity has released into the atmosphere never stayed there.","It crossed the sea surface and dissolved.","That transfer is usually described as a favor, the ocean taking on part of the problem and slowing the warming of the air.","The description is accurate as far as it goes.","What it leaves out is that the ocean does the favor by chemistry, and the chemistry does not leave the water unchanged."],["The first steps are simple enough to write on a napkin.","Carbon dioxide gas dissolves in seawater, and a small fraction of it combines with water to form carbonic acid.","Carbonic acid is weak and unstable, and it promptly sheds a hydrogen ion, leaving a bicarbonate ion.","A much smaller fraction of that bicarbonate sheds a second hydrogen ion and becomes a carbonate ion.","In pure water that sequence would simply make the water more acidic.","Seawater is not pure water, and the part that matters begins one step later."],["Seawater already carries a standing stock of carbonate ions, and carbonate is an eager scavenger of hydrogen.","Nearly every hydrogen ion released by dissolving carbon dioxide is intercepted by a carbonate ion already there, and the two combine into another bicarbonate ion.","The bookkeeping is worth following slowly.","Total dissolved inorganic carbon goes up.","Bicarbonate goes up sharply.","Hydrogen ion concentration goes up slightly, which is what a falling pH records.","And carbonate ion concentration goes down.","Adding carbon to seawater subtracts carbonate from it, and that inversion is the engine of the whole problem."],["Carbonate matters because many marine organisms build their hard parts out of it.","A calcifier draws a calcium ion and a carbonate ion out of solution and lays the pair down as calcium carbonate: a coral skeleton, a clam shell, the chalky plates of a single celled alga, the spines of a sea urchin.","Scarcer carbonate means a crystal that once assembled readily must be coaxed into place."],["Chemists summarize that difficulty with one number, the saturation state, written as omega.","It compares the actual product of calcium and carbonate concentrations against the product at equilibrium with solid calcium carbonate.","Where omega exceeds one the water is supersaturated and a crystal left in it tends to grow.","Where omega is below one the water is undersaturated and an unprotected crystal tends to dissolve.","Calcium is abundant and varies little, so omega rises and falls with carbonate."],["Deeper water is colder, under greater pressure, and loaded with carbon dioxide from matter decomposing as it sinks, so omega falls with depth.","The depth at which it passes through one is the saturation horizon.","Above that boundary shells are thermodynamically safe; below it the only thing keeping a living animal's shell intact is its own covering of tissue or organic film.","The horizon sits at very different depths in different basins.","In the North Atlantic, where surface water sinks relatively recently and carries little accumulated respiratory carbon, it lies thousands of meters down.","In the North Pacific, whose deep water is the oldest in the world ocean and has spent centuries collecting respired carbon dioxide, it can sit within a few hundred meters of the surface."],["That boundary is not fixed, and it has been moving in one direction.","As atmospheric carbon dioxide works into the upper ocean, the depth at which omega falls below one shoals, migrating upward toward the light.","In parts of the North Pacific the change has been measured in tens of meters over a few decades.","Where seasonal upwelling already drags deep water onto a continental shelf, the two can meet, and water corrosive to shell arrives in the shallow, sunlit places where larvae are trying to build one."],["Calcium carbonate is also not a single material.","It crystallizes in more than one arrangement, and two dominate marine biology: calcite and aragonite.","They share an identical chemical formula and differ in the internal geometry of the crystal, and that geometry sets the solubility.","Aragonite is roughly half again as soluble as calcite in seawater, so in any water column the aragonite saturation horizon sits shallower than the calcite horizon.","An organism's choice of crystal is not a matter of taste.","It decides which portion of the ocean is hospitable to it at all."],["The division cuts across the tree of life almost arbitrarily.","Reef building corals lay down aragonite.","So do pteropods, the small swimming snails called sea butterflies, eaten by everything from herring to whales.","Coccolithophores, the single celled algae whose plates turn stretches of the North Atlantic milky in bloom season, build calcite, as do the foraminifera whose shells accumulate on the seafloor.","Many molluscs use both: an adult oyster's outer shell is largely calcite, while the first shell a larval oyster assembles is aragonite.","A third form, calcite with magnesium substituted into part of the crystal, is more soluble still than aragonite, and it is what coralline algae and sea urchins use."],["Here the most common phrase in public discussion of the subject does damage.","The ocean is repeatedly said to be turning to acid.","It is not.","Surface seawater has a pH near 8.1, comfortably on the alkaline side of neutral, and it has fallen from roughly 8.2 since the industrial era began.","No plausible emissions trajectory takes the open ocean below 7.","Acidification names a direction of travel, not a destination, in the way a bath cooling from scalding to merely hot is cooling.","The imprecision is not harmless: a reader who pictures a sea of vinegar and later learns the truth may throw out the subject entirely.","But the correction is no reprieve.","Shells do not wait for pH 7.","They answer to a saturation state that can cross below one while the water is still alkaline."],["Nor do organisms sit passively in the water around them.","Most calcifiers do not precipitate skeleton directly out of seawater.","They wall off a small internal space, a calcifying fluid held between tissue and skeleton, and pump hydrogen ions out of it, holding its pH well above the pH outside.","These mechanisms are genuinely effective.","Laboratory populations of some corals, some coccolithophores and several crustaceans have held calcification rates steady under carbon dioxide levels far beyond what the coming century should deliver, and a few have raised them."],["Such results are sometimes reported as evidence that the concern was overblown.","They are better read as evidence that calcification has a price and that the price is climbing.","Pumping protons against a steeper gradient is expensive, and the energy must come from somewhere in an animal that is also feeding, growing, defending itself and reproducing.","The trade shows up in measurements that rarely make headlines: shells of normal size but lower density, larvae slower to reach settlement, adults that keep their skeletons and release fewer eggs.","A shellfish hatchery on the Pacific Northwest coast can lose most of a batch of oyster larvae when upwelled, undersaturated water enters its intake, not because the water dissolves them but because they cannot build fast enough in the window when they must build."],["The picture is uneven rather than uniform, and careful accounts of it resist a single verdict.","Response varies between species, between populations of one species, and between seasons.","Seagrass meadows and kelp forests pull carbon dioxide out of the water during daylight and can lift the local saturation state enough to shelter the animals among them, then give much of it back after dark.","Estuaries swing across a wider chemical range in a day than the open ocean is projected to cross in a century, and the organisms there are built for the ride.","None of this contradicts the chemistry.","It means the chemistry is a boundary condition ecology plays out against, not a script it has to read."],["To see what happens when that boundary condition shifts quickly, the usual place to look is an interval about 56 million years ago, preserved in the geological record and known as the Paleocene Eocene Thermal Maximum.","A large quantity of carbon entered the ocean and atmosphere, the planet warmed by several degrees, and the seafloor recorded the consequence with unusual clarity.","In deep sea sediment cores the pale carbonate ooze that had piled up for millions of years gives way abruptly to a band of brown clay, then returns over the next tens of thousands of years.","Carbonate did not stop being produced.","It dissolved on the seafloor as the saturation horizon rose through the sediment, and a substantial fraction of deep dwelling foraminifera species went extinct."],["The event is instructive, and it is also asked with some regularity to carry more weight than it can bear.","It shows that the carbonate system responds to a large carbon injection as theory predicts, and that recovery is slow, since the processes that restore balance work over tens of thousands of years.","What it does not show is the present rate.","Estimates place that ancient release on the order of several thousand years, against roughly two centuries for the modern one, and rate is nearly the whole question, because the ocean's slow buffers only help if they are given time.","Nor can the record resolve a decade: sediment accumulates slowly and burrowing animals stir it, so a fifty year event can smear into invisibility.","The pH values come from proxies such as boron isotopes in fossil shells, which carry real uncertainty.","And the species that filled that ocean are not the species in this one."],["What survives the caveats is a narrower claim than the headlines make and a firmer one.","The chemistry is not in dispute: carbon dioxide entering seawater raises dissolved carbon and lowers carbonate, and it is doing so faster than anything the record can resolve.","The saturation horizons are rising, and that is measured rather than modeled.","What remains open is the biology, meaning which organisms will pay the climbing price out of reserves they can spare, which will pay it out of growth and reproduction they cannot, and which will simply move."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p07-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, aragonite differs from calcite in that aragonite","choices":["carries magnesium substituted into part of its crystal","has a chemical formula unlike that of calcite","dissolves more readily in seawater than calcite does","is the material of coccolithophore plates and foraminifera shells","stays saturated to a greater depth in the water column"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"roughly half again as soluble as calcite in seawater"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 makes the whole comparison turn on solubility. The two forms share a formula, differ in the internal geometry of the crystal, and aragonite is roughly half again as soluble in seawater.","distractorNotes":["That describes the third form the passage names later, magnesium bearing calcite, not aragonite.","The passage says the two share an identical chemical formula and differ only in crystal geometry.",null,"Those are the calcite builders the passage lists, which is the opposite grouping.","This reverses the result: because aragonite is more soluble, its saturation horizon sits shallower, not deeper."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage attributes the shallow saturation horizon of the North Pacific mainly to","choices":["the unusually high pressure of that particular ocean basin","the long residence of its deep water, which has collected respired carbon dioxide","the seasonal upwelling that drags deep water up onto the continental shelf each summer","the number of aragonite building organisms living in its surface water","a calcium concentration much lower there than in other basins"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"has spent centuries collecting respired carbon dioxide"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 contrasts the two basins directly. North Pacific deep water is the oldest in the world ocean and has spent centuries collecting carbon dioxide from decomposition, so its saturation horizon can sit within a few hundred meters of the surface.","distractorNotes":["Pressure rises with depth everywhere, so it cannot explain why one basin differs from another.",null,"Upwelling is what carries that water onto a shelf, not what made the water corrosive in the first place.","The passage never connects how many calcifiers live above a spot to how deep its horizon lies.","The passage says calcium is abundant and varies little, which is why omega tracks carbonate instead."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q03","type":"detail","stem":"The passage names which organisms as builders of the magnesium bearing form of calcite?","choices":["reef building corals","pteropods, the small swimming snails of polar water","coccolithophores","foraminifera","coralline algae and sea urchins"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":6,"quote":"it is what coralline algae and sea urchins use"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 closes by naming a third crystal form, calcite with magnesium substituted into part of it, and says coralline algae and sea urchins are the organisms that use it.","distractorNotes":["Corals are listed as aragonite builders at the start of that paragraph.","Pteropods are grouped with corals as aragonite builders.","Coccolithophores are given as builders of ordinary calcite.","Foraminifera are also placed in the ordinary calcite group.",null]},{"id":"rc-p07-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, deep sea sediment cores record the ancient warm interval as","choices":["a thickening of the pale carbonate ooze","an abrupt band of brown clay set between layers of carbonate ooze","a dense layer of intact foraminifera shells","a slow darkening spread evenly across many millions of years of accumulated sediment","a gap in which no sediment accumulated at all"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"gives way abruptly to a band of brown clay"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 describes the core signature plainly. Pale carbonate ooze that had piled up for millions of years gives way abruptly to brown clay, then returns over the following tens of thousands of years, because the carbonate was dissolving on the seafloor.","distractorNotes":["This reverses what happened: the ooze stops and clay takes its place.",null,"The passage says the opposite, that a substantial fraction of deep dwelling foraminifera species went extinct.","The passage calls the change abrupt, with the recovery, not the onset, taking tens of thousands of years.","Sediment kept accumulating. What changed is that the carbonate in it dissolved."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The passage gives the pH of present day surface seawater as","choices":["slightly below 7","exactly 7, the neutral point","about 7.5, down from roughly 8 over the last century","near 8.1, down from roughly 8.2","near 8.2 and essentially unchanged"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"a pH near 8.1"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"it has fallen from roughly 8.2"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 gives both numbers. Surface seawater sits near 8.1, down from roughly 8.2 since the industrial era began, which keeps it on the alkaline side of neutral.","distractorNotes":["That would put seawater on the acid side, which the passage says no plausible trajectory reaches.","Neutral is exactly what the passage says surface seawater is not.","Both numbers are wrong and they overstate the shift the passage actually reports.",null,"The passage reports a drop of about a tenth of a unit, so the value has not held steady."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, \"shoals\" most nearly means","choices":["gathers into dense groups","becomes sandy and shallow, like a sandbar","splits apart into separate layers","slows to a gradual crawl","moves nearer to the surface"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"shoals, migrating upward toward the light"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 is tracking a depth. The boundary is migrating upward toward the light, measured in tens of meters over a few decades, so the word means climbing toward the surface.","distractorNotes":["That is the fish sense of the word, and nothing in the sentence concerns animals grouping together.","A shoal as a sandbar is a different sense, and the sentence is about a chemical boundary, not sediment.","The passage describes one boundary moving, not a boundary dividing in two.","The sentence measures distance, tens of meters, rather than any rate of motion being slow.",null]},{"id":"rc-p07-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 12, \"expensive\" most nearly means","choices":["scarce and hard to obtain","demanding of the animal's energy","costly in terms of money and trade","elaborate in its design","damaging to nearby tissue"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"the energy must come from somewhere"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 defines the word in the very next clause: the energy must come from somewhere in an animal that is also feeding, growing, defending itself and reproducing. The cost is metabolic.","distractorNotes":["Scarcity in this passage belongs to carbonate in the water, not to the pumping the word describes.",null,"The monetary sense is the dictionary one, but the sentence is about where an animal's energy comes from.","The passage is not commenting on how intricate the mechanism is.","The passage never says the pumping harms tissue, only that it draws on a limited energy supply."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly supports which statement about a parcel of seawater that has taken up extra carbon dioxide?","choices":["It holds more dissolved carbon overall yet less of the ion a shell builder needs","It holds less dissolved carbon, because the carbon departs as bicarbonate","It holds more carbonate than before, since carbonate is one of the products of the reaction","It has turned acidic and will dissolve any shell that is placed into it","Its calcium concentration has fallen in step with its carbonate concentration"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"Total dissolved inorganic carbon goes up."},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":7,"quote":"carbonate ion concentration goes down"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"many marine organisms build their hard parts out of it"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 walks the bookkeeping through one line at a time. Total dissolved inorganic carbon rises and bicarbonate rises sharply, while carbonate, the ion a calcifier draws on, goes down.","distractorNotes":[null,"Total dissolved inorganic carbon goes up in that paragraph, and bicarbonate is where most of it ends up rather than leaving.","Carbonate is consumed in the passage's account, because it mops up the freed hydrogen ions.","The water stays alkaline, and whether a shell dissolves depends on saturation state rather than on the water being acidic.","The passage says calcium is abundant in seawater and varies little."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q09","type":"inference","stem":"A species is found in the laboratory to hold its shell growth rate unchanged under high carbon dioxide. Based on the passage, the author would most likely treat that result as","choices":["proof that the species is untouched by the change in seawater chemistry","probably a measurement error, given what the chemistry predicts","leaving open what the species gave up to hold that rate","evidence that the species has permanently adapted to any future condition","a result that will hold equally for every species sharing its habitat"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"calcification has a price and that the price is climbing"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"shells of normal size but lower density"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 handles exactly this case. The author accepts steady laboratory rates and reads them as a cost met somewhere else, showing up in shell density, larval timing, or eggs released.","distractorNotes":["The author treats a steady rate as a sign that a cost is being met elsewhere, not as a sign of no effect.","The author accepts these laboratory results and calls the mechanisms behind them genuinely effective.",null,"The passage ties compensation to circumstances such as food supply rather than to a permanent solution.","The passage says response varies between species and even between populations of one species."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["Added carbon dioxide lowers the carbonate shell builders need, a settled chemical result whose biological effects remain open","The ocean is on course to become acidic enough that marine shells will dissolve outright in open water within a century","Marine calcifiers have evolved mechanisms that will carry them through the change without meaningful cost to growth or reproduction","The record of an ancient warming interval shows in fine detail what the coming century will do to the ocean and its marine life","Concern about seawater chemistry has been exaggerated, largely by the imprecise language that reporters and scientists use in public"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":8,"quote":"Adding carbon to seawater subtracts carbonate from it"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"raises dissolved carbon and lowers carbonate"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"What remains open is the biology"}],"why":"The passage runs one chemical result, less carbonate, out through organisms, depth, popular wording, coping mechanisms and the fossil record. The closing paragraph states the balance directly: the chemistry is not in dispute, the biology is what remains open.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage denies this outright, saying no plausible trajectory takes the open ocean below 7 and tying shell trouble to saturation state instead.","Coping mechanisms are described, but the passage insists they carry a cost that is climbing.","The passage says the ancient record cannot show what happens at the present rate.","The author does criticize the phrasing, then refuses to let the correction count as reassurance."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q11","type":"function","stem":"The author's main purpose in the paragraph that takes up the phrase about acid is to","choices":["show that popular reporting on seawater chemistry is on the whole trustworthy and precise","argue that pH is a misleading way to describe any chemical change in a solution","correct a common misstatement while denying that the correction is cause for relief","establish that the open ocean will eventually fall below neutral on the pH scale","explain why a cooling bath and a warming ocean behave in much the same way"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"It is not."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":8,"quote":"But the correction is no reprieve."}],"why":"Paragraph 10 spends half its length correcting the slogan and the other half refusing the comfort that correction might offer, closing with shells answering to a saturation state while the water is still alkaline.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph faults the popular phrasing rather than defending it.","The author uses pH values freely and objects only to one careless slogan.",null,"The passage states the opposite, that no plausible emissions trajectory takes the open ocean below 7.","The bath appears only as an analogy for direction of travel, not as a claim about baths."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"How does the paragraph on the energy cost of calcification relate to the paragraph on internal chemical control that comes just before it?","choices":["It supplies the laboratory evidence for the mechanism just described","It grants the evidence and rereads what that evidence means","It disputes the accuracy of the measurements just reported","It shifts the subject from living organisms to the fossil record","It restates the earlier point using a different set of organisms"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"sometimes reported as evidence that the concern was overblown"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"They are better read as evidence"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 reports that some corals, coccolithophores and crustaceans hold calcification rates steady. Paragraph 12 does not deny those results, it rereads them as evidence that calcification has a price and that the price is climbing.","distractorNotes":["The laboratory evidence sits in the earlier paragraph, and the later one reads it differently.",null,"The author accepts the measurements and questions only the conclusion drawn from them.","The fossil record does not enter the passage until two paragraphs later.","The later paragraph adds a new claim about cost rather than repeating the old one."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the claim that the ocean is turning to acid is best described as","choices":["accepting it as a simplification that serves the public well","dismissing the whole subject as overstated","indifferent to how the chemistry gets described","alarmed that the phrase badly understates the danger","correcting the wording but insisting the worry stands"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"It is not."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":8,"quote":"But the correction is no reprieve."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":9,"quote":"Shells do not wait for pH 7."}],"why":"In paragraph 10 the author calls the phrase false and calls the imprecision harmful, then immediately says the correction is no reprieve. Both halves of that move belong to the attitude.","distractorNotes":["The author calls the imprecision harmful rather than useful.","The author rejects the slogan yet explicitly refuses to treat the correction as a reprieve.","The author gives a full paragraph to why the wording matters, which is not indifference.","The objection is that the phrase overstates what is happening to pH, not that it understates it.",null]},{"id":"rc-p07-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In the discussion of the ancient warming interval, the author's attitude toward that record as evidence is best described as","choices":["appreciative of what it shows and wary of how far it is stretched","convinced that it settles the question of how fast the present change is moving","skeptical that an event of that kind occurred in the way described","impatient with the scientists who spend their careers reading sediment cores","confident that modern proxies have removed what uncertainty once remained"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"The event is instructive"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"asked with some regularity to carry more weight than it can bear"},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"What it does not show is the present rate."}],"why":"Paragraph 15 opens by calling the event instructive and also asked to carry more weight than it can bear, then lists what it cannot show: the present rate, a single decade, and pH values free of proxy uncertainty.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage says the opposite, that what the record does not show is the present rate.","The author treats the event as well recorded and genuinely instructive.","The author draws on that work respectfully and questions only how it gets used.","The passage says the proxy values carry real uncertainty."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Dissolved calcium in seawater changes far less than dissolved carbonate does","A shell can be at risk in water that is still alkaline by ordinary measures","Open ocean pH is expected to fall below neutral within the coming century","Sediment cores cannot resolve a change that lasts only a few decades","Some organisms raise the pH of an internal fluid in order to build their skeletons"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"Calcium is abundant and varies little","choice":0},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":10,"quote":"can cross below one while the water is still alkaline","choice":1},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":5,"quote":"a fifty year event can smear into invisibility","choice":3},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"holding its pH well above the pH outside","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"No plausible emissions trajectory takes the open ocean below 7.","choice":2}],"why":"Paragraph 10 states the opposite of this choice: no plausible emissions trajectory takes the open ocean below 7. Each of the other four traces back to a specific line elsewhere in the passage.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 says calcium is abundant and varies little, which is why omega tracks carbonate.","Paragraph 10 makes exactly this point when it says shells do not wait for pH 7.",null,"Paragraph 15 says a fifty year event can smear into invisibility in a core.","Paragraph 11 describes calcifiers pumping hydrogen ions out of a calcifying fluid to hold its pH up."]},{"id":"rc-p07-q16","type":"detail","stem":"In which paragraph does the passage describe habitats whose surrounding water chemistry departs from the open ocean pattern?","choices":["paragraph 6","paragraph 9","paragraph 11","paragraph 13","paragraph 15"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"can lift the local saturation state enough to shelter the animals"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"Estuaries swing across a wider chemical range in a day"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 is the one about local settings. Seagrass meadows and kelp forests lift the saturation state around them during daylight, and estuaries swing across a wider chemical range in a day than the open ocean is projected to cross in a century.","distractorNotes":["That paragraph maps how saturation changes with depth and between ocean basins, not between habitats.","That paragraph sorts organisms by which crystal form they build.","That paragraph covers chemistry an organism controls inside itself, not the water around it.",null,"That paragraph weighs what the geological record can and cannot show."]}]},"rc-p08":{"id":"rc-p08","title":"Sleep and Waste Clearance in the Brain","topic":"sleep and brain waste clearance","field":"neuroscience","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1696,"paragraphs":[["Every organ faces the same housekeeping problem.","Cells burn fuel, discard proteins, and leave behind molecules that serve no further purpose.","In most of the body the solution is the lymphatic system, a network of thin vessels that gathers the fluid seeping out of capillaries and returns it to the blood.","The brain is the conspicuous exception.","No lymphatic vessels run through the tissue itself, and yet the brain is the most metabolically expensive organ a person owns, consuming roughly a fifth of the body's energy while making up about a fiftieth of its mass.","Something has to take out the trash."],["The brain does have fluid of its own.","Cerebrospinal fluid is made mainly by the choroid plexus, a ribbon of tissue inside the ventricles, at a rate of roughly half a liter a day in an adult.","Since only about 150 milliliters are present at any one moment, the whole supply is replaced several times over in a day.","That fluid fills the ventricles and bathes the outer surface of the brain and cord.","What it does not obviously do is reach the inside.","Between the cells of the brain lies a maze of gaps only a few tens of nanometers wide, and for most of the twentieth century anything crossing that maze was assumed to move by diffusion alone."],["A route to the interior had been visible long before anyone knew what to make of it.","When an artery dives from the surface into the brain, it carries a sleeve of the surface space down with it, so that the vessel travels inside a channel of its own, a pipe running through a slightly wider pipe.","The outer wall of that channel is built from astrocytes, the star-shaped glial cells whose broad flattened endfeet tile the vessel surface almost completely.","Studded into the endfoot membrane facing the vessel is aquaporin-4, a channel protein that lets water cross the astrocyte far faster than it otherwise could.","The circuit proposed from this anatomy runs as follows: cerebrospinal fluid driven inward along the sleeves around arteries, pushed through the tissue, and collected along the channels around veins, carrying dissolved waste out with it.","Because glial cells were doing the work of a lymphatic system, the route was named glymphatic."],["That the idea waited until this century to be tested is a matter of method, not imagination.","Nearly everything known about the space between brain cells had come from electron microscopy, which requires tissue to be chemically fixed, dehydrated, and sliced.","Fixation collapses those spaces, and the earliest measurements accordingly put the extracellular fraction near zero.","A hardened slab cannot show fluid moving either, because in a dead brain none is.","Two methods changed that.","The first, real-time iontophoresis, releases a small charged tracer from a pipette tip and records its arrival at a second tip about a hundred micrometers away, and the shape of that arrival curve reports how much open space the tracer had to cross and how twisted its path was.","The second, two-photon laser scanning microscopy, sends infrared light through a window in the skull and follows fluorescent tracers a few hundred micrometers deep in a living animal whose heart is beating and whose brain waves are recorded at the same time."],["The result, when it came, was arresting.","In mice, extracellular space made up about 14 percent of brain volume during quiet waking and about 23 percent during natural sleep, an increase of roughly 60 percent.","Fluorescent tracer placed in the cerebrospinal fluid spread into sleeping brains far more readily than into waking ones, and labeled amyloid-beta, a protein fragment that builds up in Alzheimer's disease, cleared about twice as fast from a sleeping brain.","The shift tracked state rather than clock: mice anesthetized with a mixture of ketamine and xylazine showed the sleeping pattern even in the middle of their active period.","Waking mice given drugs that block adrenergic receptors showed much of the same expansion, which pointed at norepinephrine, the arousal signal broadcast across the brain by a small brainstem nucleus called the locus coeruleus, as a candidate controller of the space itself."],["Aquaporin-4 supplied the molecular hook.","Mice engineered to lack the channel took up less tracer from the cerebrospinal fluid and cleared labeled protein more slowly, and in aged or injured brains the channel drifts off the endfoot membrane and scatters over the cell.","A story with a named protein, a named cell, a named transmitter, and a named disease is a story that travels."],["Human work followed the same arc.","Magnetic resonance imaging of sleeping volunteers has captured large, slow pulses of cerebrospinal fluid surging into the fourth ventricle roughly every twenty seconds during deep non-rapid-eye-movement sleep, each pulse locked to a slow electrical wave in the cortex and to a dip in blood volume.","Keeping people awake for a single night raises the concentration of amyloid-beta and of the protein tau in their cerebrospinal fluid the next morning.","And poor sleep, tracked over years, is associated with a higher risk of dementia."],["From there the account escaped into public speech in a compressed form: the brain washes itself at night, a rinse cycle, a nightly power wash, a janitorial shift that runs while the lights are off.","The image is vivid, memorable, and further ahead of the evidence than most of those repeating it realize."],["Begin with what the central experiments measure.","A tracer appears farther inside a sleeping brain than a waking one over the same interval.","That is a difference in transport, and transport is not the same thing as flow.","A tracer can spread along a channel faster than diffusion would carry it without any net movement of fluid at all: if the artery inside the channel widens and narrows with every heartbeat, the fluid around it sloshes back and forth, and that sloshing mixes a tracer outward even though the fluid ends each cycle where it began.","Calculations of what it would take to push fluid in bulk through the dense mesh filling the extracellular space return pressure differences larger than any measured in a living brain."],["Then there is the anesthesia.","Much of the evidence compares waking animals with anesthetized ones, on the reasonable-sounding premise that a drug producing slow electrical waves is a fair stand-in for sleep.","It is not one.","Anesthetics change heart rate, blood pressure, breathing, body temperature, and the diameter of the vessels themselves, and every one of those variables can move a tracer on its own.","Slow waves on a recording are a feature sleep and certain anesthetics happen to share, not proof that the two states share a mechanism."],["The tracer is a third complication.","Getting a labeled molecule into the cerebrospinal fluid usually means injecting a volume of liquid into a closed skull, which raises the pressure inside it.","Some fraction of the movement seen afterward may be the experiment pushing its own tracer along.","Work that varies the injected volume and the speed of injection finds that both change the result, which is what one would expect if part of the signal came from the needle."],["Replication has been uneven in a way that a settled finding would not be.","Independent laboratories have both confirmed and failed to find the dependence on aquaporin-4, and the disagreement traces in part to differences in mouse strain, anesthetic, and what was counted as clearance.","At least one group, measuring the disappearance of a dye rather than the entry of one, reported clearance slower during sleep and anesthesia than during waking.","That disagreement is live."],["The last stretch of the popular story is the longest leap.","Suppose sleep really does speed the removal of amyloid-beta.","The inference that poor sleep therefore causes Alzheimer's disease still needs the arrow to point in one direction, and the disease is perfectly capable of pointing it the other way.","Alzheimer's pathology reaches the brainstem and hypothalamic cell groups that build and stabilize sleep early, often years before memory fails, so broken sleep in a person later diagnosed may be an early symptom rather than a cause.","Rising amyloid after a sleepless night fits more of the protein being made during the extra hours awake just as well as less of it being removed."],["None of this makes the underlying observation wrong, and it is worth being exact about what stands.","That the extracellular space of the brain changes size with behavioral state is a real measurement, made with more than one method, and a genuinely surprising one, because the geometry of the tissue had been treated as a constant.","That solutes move differently through a sleeping brain than through a waking one is also well supported.","What has not been shown is the part the metaphor supplies for free: a directed, pressure-driven current that sweeps waste out of the brain, and whose failure produces disease."],["Saying what would settle a question is a better test of a hypothesis than listing what supports it.","Three measurements would do most of the work.","The first is a direct reading of the speed and direction of fluid in a perivascular channel of an awake, undisturbed animal, with nothing injected, fine enough to tell a steady current of micrometers per second from sloshing that averages to zero.","The second is an intervention that changes perivascular transport and nothing else, leaving sleep architecture, blood pressure, breathing, and vessel tone where they were, then watching long enough to see whether protein builds up.","The third is a measurement in people: the disappearance rate of one labeled protein across a night of sleep and a matched day of waking, in the same person."],["Until those exist, the honest version of the finding is narrower than the headline and more interesting than it sounds.","The brain's fluid economy has a daily rhythm that nobody had documented twenty years ago, tied to the same arousal chemistry that governs attention, and what that rhythm accomplishes is an open question.","The appetite for a tidy story is the real hazard here.","Sleep does a great many things for a brain, and it does not need a rinse cycle to earn its place."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p08-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the brain accounts for roughly what share of the body's energy use?","choices":["About one fiftieth","About one twentieth","About one tenth","About one fifth","About one half"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"consuming roughly a fifth of the body's energy"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 gives two numbers side by side: the brain uses roughly a fifth of the body's energy while making up about a fiftieth of its mass. The question asks about energy, so a fifth is the one to take.","distractorNotes":["That is the share of body mass the passage gives the brain, not its share of energy.","No such figure appears, and it is smaller than the share the passage actually states.","This is in the neighborhood of the real answer but not the number given, which is one fifth.",null,"The passage never puts the brain's energy use anywhere near half of the body's total."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage describes the increase in extracellular space between quiet waking and sleep in mice as roughly","choices":["twofold","23 percent","14 percent","9 percent","60 percent"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"an increase of roughly 60 percent"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 reports about 14 percent during quiet waking and about 23 percent during sleep, then names the change directly as an increase of roughly 60 percent.","distractorNotes":["Twofold is how much faster labeled amyloid-beta cleared from a sleeping brain, not how much the space grew.","That is the size of the space during sleep, not the size of the change.","That is the size of the space during quiet waking, not the size of the change.","This is the arithmetic gap between the two percentages, but the passage reports the change as a percentage of the waking value.",null]},{"id":"rc-p08-q03","type":"detail","stem":"Which structure does the passage name as the source of the arousal signal implicated in setting the size of the extracellular space?","choices":["The choroid plexus in the ventricles","The fourth ventricle","A small brainstem nucleus","The astrocyte endfeet","The arachnoid membrane"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"a small brainstem nucleus called the locus coeruleus"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 names norepinephrine, broadcast across the brain by a small brainstem nucleus called the locus coeruleus, as a candidate controller of the space itself.","distractorNotes":["The choroid plexus makes cerebrospinal fluid, which is paragraph 2, and it is never described as releasing an arousal signal.","The fourth ventricle is where slow fluid pulses were imaged in sleeping people, not a source of norepinephrine.",null,"Endfeet build the wall of the perivascular channel and carry aquaporin-4; they do not broadcast the signal.","The arachnoid membrane bounds the fluid space at the surface and plays no part in the signal described."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q04","type":"detail","stem":"In the imaging of sleeping volunteers, each slow pulse of cerebrospinal fluid coincided with","choices":["a rise in body temperature","a burst of rapid eye movement","a rise in heart rate","a dip in blood volume","an injection of tracer into the skull"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"and to a dip in blood volume"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says each pulse into the fourth ventricle was locked to a slow electrical wave in the cortex and to a dip in blood volume.","distractorNotes":["Body temperature appears in paragraph 10 as something anesthetics change, not as part of the human imaging result.","The pulses were recorded during deep non-rapid-eye-movement sleep, which is the opposite of what this choice names.","Heart rate is listed in paragraph 10 among the variables anesthetics change; the human result names blood volume.",null,"Injected tracer is a complication of the animal work in paragraph 11, not a feature of the human imaging."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the earliest measurements of the space between brain cells were misleading because","choices":["the tracer molecules used were too large to enter the gaps","chemical fixation collapsed the spaces being measured","the animals studied were anesthetized rather than asleep","the samples came only from aged brains","the microscope could not detect fluorescent labels"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"Fixation collapses those spaces"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 explains that electron microscopy requires fixing the tissue, that fixation collapses those spaces, and that early measurements therefore put the extracellular fraction near zero.","distractorNotes":["Tracer size is never raised as the problem; the trouble was what preparing the tissue did to it.",null,"Anesthesia is a separate objection, raised in paragraph 10 about much later experiments.","Aged brains come up in paragraph 6, where aquaporin-4 shifts position, not as the source of the early error.","Fluorescent labels belong to the two-photon method described afterward, not to the early electron-microscope work."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 5, \"arresting\" most nearly means","choices":["striking","halting","preliminary","disputed","reassuring"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"The result, when it came, was arresting."},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"a genuinely surprising one"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 uses the word to introduce a set of numbers the author plainly finds remarkable, so the sense is striking rather than anything to do with stopping.","distractorNotes":[null,"This is the everyday sense of arrest, but nothing in the sentence stops or is stopped.","The paragraph reports finished measurements, not a first look.","Disagreement arrives much later in the passage; here the author is describing how the result landed.","The result is offered as surprising, not as comforting."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 6, \"a story that travels\" most nearly describes a story that","choices":["moves physically from one tissue to another","becomes harder to test as time passes","changes shape every time it is retold","spreads easily and gets repeated widely","applies just as well to other organs"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"is a story that travels"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"the account escaped into public speech in a compressed form"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 lists what the account has going for it, a named protein, a named cell, a named transmitter, and a named disease, and then says it travels, meaning it catches on and gets repeated.","distractorNotes":["The sentence is about the account of aquaporin-4, not about anything moving through tissue.","Testability is the worry of later paragraphs; this sentence is about how readily the story caught on.","Distortion in retelling is a reasonable guess, but the sentence stresses the appealing parts that carry the story along.",null,"Nothing in the paragraph extends the account beyond the brain."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q08","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 9 suggests that a tracer reaching deeper into a sleeping brain is compatible with which of the following?","choices":["Fluid that oscillates with the heartbeat and returns to where it began","Fluid that has stopped moving entirely during sleep","A tracer that binds to the walls of the channel","A pressure difference across the tissue larger than any yet measured in a living brain","Diffusion running at exactly the rate seen in waking"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"the fluid ends each cycle where it began"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 explains that a pulsating artery makes the fluid around it slosh back and forth, and that sloshing mixes a tracer outward even though the fluid ends each cycle where it began. Deeper spread therefore does not require net flow.","distractorNotes":[null,"If nothing moved, the tracer would not get farther in; the paragraph offers back-and-forth motion, not stillness.","Binding is never mentioned, and a tracer stuck to a wall would spread less, not more.","The paragraph says pressures that large have not been measured, which argues against bulk flow rather than supporting the observation.","The point of the paragraph is that the tracer outruns plain diffusion, so an unchanged diffusion rate cannot account for it."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage's treatment of amyloid-beta levels after a sleepless night implies that measuring a concentration by itself cannot separate","choices":["one protein from another in the same fluid","sleep from anesthesia as a brain state","how much is produced from how much is removed","cortical waves from brainstem activity","a measurement in mice from a measurement in people"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"fits more of the protein being made during the extra hours awake"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"just as well as less of it being removed"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 says a rise after a sleepless night fits more of the protein being made during the extra hours awake just as well as less of it being removed, so the number alone does not settle which is happening.","distractorNotes":["The passage names both amyloid-beta and tau, but the ambiguity it raises is about where a change in amount comes from.","That confusion is real and appears in paragraph 10, but it is a different problem from what a concentration can show.",null,"No such contrast is drawn anywhere in the passage.","Species differences are not what this paragraph is weighing."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the central point of the passage?","choices":["Sleep clears waste from the brain, and disrupted sleep therefore causes Alzheimer's disease","Electron microscopy is an unreliable way to study any living tissue","A real finding about sleep and brain fluid has been stretched into a mechanism it has not demonstrated","The brain makes up for its lack of lymphatic vessels through the channels that run around its arteries and veins","Popular writing about science cannot be trusted to describe laboratory work"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"further ahead of the evidence than most of those repeating it realize"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"What has not been shown is the part the metaphor supplies for free"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"narrower than the headline"}],"why":"The passage builds the case for the finding in paragraphs 1 through 7, works through the objections in paragraphs 9 through 13, marks off what stands in paragraph 14, and then names what would settle the rest.","distractorNotes":["This is the compressed public version the passage sets out to qualify, not the author's own position.","Paragraph 4 makes a narrower version of this point in passing, and it serves the larger argument rather than being it.",null,"This is the proposal under examination, and the passage spends most of its length on how much of it has actually been shown.","The author objects to one particular overstatement, not to science writing in general."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 4 is included mainly in order to","choices":["show that electron microscopy has no remaining use in biology","argue that the two techniques it describes give identical results","establish that the extracellular space is far larger than anyone had expected it to be","introduce the tracer injection problem that paragraph 11 develops","explain why the proposal could not be tested until living tissue could be watched"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"a matter of method, not imagination"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"because in a dead brain none is"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 opens by saying the delay was a matter of method rather than imagination, then shows why fixed tissue could not answer the question and what two live-tissue techniques made possible.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph criticizes what fixation does to fluid spaces, not the method as a whole.","The two are described as measuring different things, one the open space and the path, the other tracer movement in a living animal.","The measured sizes arrive in paragraph 5; this paragraph is about how measurement became possible at all.","Injection artifacts are a separate objection raised later, not the work this paragraph is doing.",null]},{"id":"rc-p08-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["A history of one laboratory, told in the order in which its experiments were run","Two rival theories compared point by point throughout","A definition of terms followed by one extended example","A case built for a proposal, then objections to it, then what would decide it","A popular claim refuted, with a replacement theory offered in its place"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"The circuit proposed from this anatomy runs as follows"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"Begin with what the central experiments measure."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"Saying what would settle a question is a better test"}],"why":"Paragraphs 1 through 7 assemble the case, paragraphs 9 through 13 raise the objections one at a time, paragraph 14 separates what stands from what does not, and paragraph 15 names three measurements that would settle it.","distractorNotes":["No laboratory or researcher is named anywhere, and the order is logical rather than chronological.","Only one proposal is under examination, and nothing is set up as its rival.","Terms are defined along the way, but the passage never settles into a single example.",null,"The author questions how far the claim has been carried and offers no competing mechanism."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the rinse cycle image in paragraph 8 is best described as","choices":["contempt for the researchers who produced the underlying data","wariness that it claims more than the evidence supports","confidence that it captures the finding accurately","indifference to how the public describes science","regret that the finding was ever published"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"further ahead of the evidence than most of those repeating it realize"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 grants that the image is vivid and memorable, then says it runs further ahead of the evidence than most of those repeating it realize. That is caution about the phrasing, not hostility toward the work.","distractorNotes":["The author treats the underlying research respectfully; the complaint is aimed at the compressed retelling.",null,"The paragraph ends by saying the image gets ahead of the evidence.","The author cares enough about the framing to spend most of the passage correcting it.","Paragraph 14 calls the observation real and surprising, so there is no regret about the work itself."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 14, the author's attitude toward the measured change in the size of the extracellular space is best described as","choices":["treating it as solid and genuinely surprising","dismissing it as an artifact of anesthesia","accepting it only if the link to disease holds up","regarding the change as too small to matter","withholding judgment until other laboratories confirm it"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"a real measurement, made with more than one method, and a genuinely surprising one"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 calls the state-dependent change a real measurement, made with more than one method, and a genuinely surprising one, while reserving the author's doubt for the directed current the metaphor adds.","distractorNotes":[null,"Anesthesia is a complaint about certain comparisons in paragraph 10, and the author does not apply it to this measurement.","The author keeps the two apart: this measurement stands on its own, and the disease claim is the part not shown.","The reported change is roughly 60 percent, and the author calls the result arresting.","The paragraph says the measurement has already been made with more than one method."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Aquaporin-4 sits in the astrocyte membrane that faces the vessel","No lymphatic vessels run through brain tissue itself","Drugs that block adrenergic receptors put waking mice to sleep","A single night without sleep raises amyloid-beta in the cerebrospinal fluid","Two-photon microscopy can follow tracers in an animal whose heart is beating"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"Studded into the endfoot membrane facing the vessel is aquaporin-4","choice":0},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"No lymphatic vessels run through the tissue itself","choice":1},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":3,"quote":"raises the concentration of amyloid-beta","choice":3},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":7,"quote":"a living animal whose heart is beating","choice":4},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"showed much of the same expansion","choice":2}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says those drugs produced much of the same expansion of the extracellular space in waking mice, which is not the same as making the animals sleep. The other four statements are stated outright in paragraphs 1, 3, 4, and 7.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 places aquaporin-4 in the endfoot membrane facing the vessel.","Paragraph 1 states this as the brain's peculiarity.",null,"Paragraph 7 reports this for people kept awake a single night.","Paragraph 4 describes the method working in a living animal with a beating heart."]},{"id":"rc-p08-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for the passage's explanation of how the arrival curve of a charged tracer reveals the amount of open space would turn to","choices":["paragraph 2","paragraph 4","paragraph 6","paragraph 9","paragraph 15"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":6,"quote":"that arrival curve reports how much open space the tracer had to cross"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 describes real-time iontophoresis, where a charged tracer is released from one pipette tip and detected at a second, and says the shape of that arrival curve reports the open space and how twisted the path was.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 2 covers where cerebrospinal fluid is made and where it goes, not how the space is measured.",null,"Paragraph 6 is about aquaporin-4 and why the account caught on.","Paragraph 9 discusses what tracer movement does and does not prove, not the measuring technique.","Paragraph 15 lists measurements that have not been made yet."]}]},"rc-p09":{"id":"rc-p09","title":"Intercalation and the Runaway Cell","topic":"lithium ion batteries and thermal runaway","field":"electrochemistry","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1693,"paragraphs":[["In a teardown lab the steel can of a spent cylindrical cell is cut away, and almost nothing inside looks like a source of power.","What comes out is a tight spiral of foil that unrolls into a strip about a meter long and as wide as a finger, two coated metal ribbons with a sheet of white plastic thinner than a hair between them, everything damp with a clear organic liquid."],["Every rechargeable battery that came before worked by taking metal apart and putting it back together.","In a lead acid cell the active material on both plates turns to lead sulfate on discharge and back again on charge, and a nickel cadmium cell does much the same with cadmium.","The first rechargeable lithium cells pushed the idea to its limit, using a sheet of lithium metal itself, the lightest metal there is, as the negative electrode."],["Plating is an untidy process.","Metal does not return as a smooth even layer; it builds up preferentially wherever the surface already bulges, because a bump concentrates the electric field around it, and each cycle leaves the surface rougher than the last.","With lithium the roughness takes the form of whiskers and mossy growths called dendrites, which creep outward like frost across a window.","Some snap off and are lost.","Others keep going until they touch the opposite electrode, shorting a sealed can of lithium metal and flammable solvent.","Cells of that design were withdrawn from sale."],["The idea that rescued the technology was to stop making and unmaking metal altogether.","In an intercalation electrode lithium is never reduced to metal at all.","It arrives as an ion, slots into a gap already waiting inside a crystal, and later leaves the same way.","The crystal is called a host, which here does not mean anything living but a rigid framework that takes in guest ions between its layers and gives them back without being consumed.","Graphite is the standard host on the negative side, its carbon sheets stacked like the pages of a closed book, and a layered metal oxide is the host on the positive side.","The cell runs by rocking lithium from one host to the other and back."],["The numbers are worth holding onto.","A fully charged graphite electrode holds one lithium for every six carbon atoms, and that much lithium swells the graphite by roughly a tenth of its volume, a strain the stacked sheets absorb without coming apart.","A common positive electrode is a layered oxide of nickel, manganese, and cobalt.","Another is lithium iron phosphate.","A cell built from graphite and a layered oxide rests near 3.7 volts and is charged to about 4.2.","The powders are spread onto foils only tens of micrometers thick: copper on the negative side, which resists attack at low voltage, and aluminum on the positive side, which survives high voltage."],["None of this settles what the lithium travels through.","Water is out of the question.","Every solvent has a voltage window, the span between the voltage at which it begins to break down at one electrode and the voltage at which it breaks down at the other, and the window for water is a little over one volt wide.","A lithium ion cell runs at nearly four.","So the electrolyte is a lithium salt, usually lithium hexafluorophosphate, dissolved in a blend of organic carbonate solvents.","Those solvents carry ions well and tolerate high voltage.","They are also flammable, with flash points at or below room temperature."],["A carbonate electrolyte should not survive contact with charged graphite either, and strictly speaking it does not.","During the first charge, performed at the factory under controlled conditions, a thin skin of decomposition products builds up on the graphite surface.","This layer, the solid electrolyte interphase, is the quiet hero of the design: it conducts lithium ions but not electrons, so once it covers the surface the reaction that built it shuts itself off.","Forming it permanently locks away a few percent of the cell's lithium.","The layer is nanometers thick, chemically fragile, and the first thing to give way when a cell gets hot."],["The white film is the separator, and its two jobs pull against each other.","Mechanically it must keep the electrodes apart.","Chemically it must not obstruct the ions.","So it is a porous polyolefin membrane, typically ten to twenty five micrometers thick and about forty percent empty space by volume, with pores well under a micrometer across.","Many are built as three bonded layers, polypropylene outside and polyethylene in the middle, so that the polyethylene melts near 130 degrees Celsius and its pores close, choking off ion flow, while the higher melting polypropylene holds the sheet together for another thirty degrees."],["Intercalation was supposed to retire dendrites, and in ordinary use it does.","But intercalation has a speed limit.","Ions must not only reach the graphite surface, they must work their way into it, and that is slow when the cell is cold, slow when the charging current is high, and slow when the graphite is already nearly full.","If ions arrive faster than the graphite can take them in, they accept an electron at the surface instead and plate out as lithium metal after all.","Given enough of those mossy deposits, some will reach across.","A stray metal particle left in during manufacturing can short a cell unaided."],["What follows is a sequence that is by now well characterized.","Somewhere between roughly 80 and 120 degrees Celsius the solid electrolyte interphase begins to break down, exposing charged graphite to liquid electrolyte and giving off heat.","That heat drives the next step rather than waiting for it.","Near 130 degrees the separator's shutdown layer closes its pores, which helps only if the cell can now cool faster than it is warming.","If it cannot, the separator itself melts and shrinks back from the edges above about 160 degrees, and the electrodes meet across an area far larger than any dendrite could bridge.","The internal short dumps the cell's stored charge as heat in seconds."],["Above roughly 200 degrees the positive electrode starts to come apart, and a layered oxide that comes apart gives off oxygen.","That detail separates a battery fire from an ordinary solvent fire.","The cell now holds fuel, an oxidizer, and a heat source inside one sealed steel can, and no longer needs the room's air in order to burn.","Pressure climbs until a scored disc in the can lets go, and the cell vents a jet of hot gas that is itself flammable: hydrogen, carbon monoxide, small hydrocarbons, and vaporized solvent, with hydrogen fluoride from the fluorine bearing salt.","Crews flood such fires mainly to cool the neighboring cells."],["The word runaway is doing precise work there.","Heat production inside the cell climbs steeply with temperature, while the cell's ability to shed heat into its surroundings grows only in proportion to how much hotter it is than the room.","Below a certain temperature removal wins and a warm cell cools off.","Above it production wins, every degree of warming buys further warming, and nothing done from outside can reach the reaction."],["It is tempting to read that cascade as a malfunction, and popular coverage usually does.","The framing is misleading.","Every step is the same chemistry the cell performs on purpose, running with nothing left to hold it back.","A charged cell is by design a metastable arrangement, a strong reducing agent and a strong oxidizing agent parked tens of micrometers apart and kept from reacting by a plastic film and a few nanometers of surface chemistry.","That separation is the entire safety strategy.","Engineers do not claim to have removed the hazard.","They claim something more modest, that they have made its onset rare and its spread slow."],["This is also why the familiar complaint, that these cells are dangerous because they store so much energy, is only half right.","A good lithium ion cell holds something like 250 to 300 watt hours per kilogram, seven or eight times what a lead acid battery of the same mass holds, and that figure is why the technology displaced everything else.","The stored electrical energy is not even the largest term: when a cell burns, the combustion of its electrolyte and electrode materials releases several times more energy than the charge it was holding.","Density and severity are two readings of a single property."],["One response is to pick a less energetic chemistry.","Lithium iron phosphate binds its oxygen inside phosphate groups, far more tightly than a layered oxide does, so the positive electrode does not readily give up oxygen and the onset of runaway sits at a markedly higher temperature.","Such cells are genuinely harder to set off.","They also sit at a lower voltage and store meaningfully less energy for their mass, which in a vehicle means either a shorter range or a heavier pack.","The safer chemistry is not simply the better one."],["Most of the remaining work happens at the pack, where the compromises are easiest to see.","A management system watches each group of cells and refuses to charge a cold one quickly, because cold and fast is exactly when plating begins.","Cells are spaced apart, wrapped in mats that swell and insulate when heated, and seated on cooling plates, and vents are aimed so that hot gas leaves through the floor of a vehicle rather than into the cabin.","Each of those measures costs volume and mass that could have carried more cells, so every firebreak is paid for out of the range the chemistry was chosen to deliver."],["The newest packs bond cells directly into the structure and delete the module walls between them, buying back several percent of usable volume and removing a firebreak in the same stroke.","Designers accept that trade only when they can show, by setting one cell alight on a test bench, that its neighbors stay cool.","The goal is narrower than the public version of it: not a cell that cannot burn, but a pack in which one cell's bad day stays one cell's bad day."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p09-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, taking lithium into graphite changes the graphite's volume in which way?","choices":["It swells by roughly a tenth.","It shrinks by roughly a tenth.","It swells by about half.","It roughly doubles.","It stays exactly the same, which is the whole point of intercalation."],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"swells the graphite by roughly a tenth of its volume"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 gives the figure outright: one lithium for every six carbons at full charge, and that much lithium swells the graphite by roughly a tenth of its volume.","distractorNotes":[null,"Right size, wrong direction. Lithium moving in swells the graphite rather than shrinking it.","Half is the wrong number. The passage says roughly a tenth, small enough for the stacked sheets to absorb.","Too extreme. Nothing in the passage describes a volume change anywhere near that large.","Intercalation does keep the framework intact, but the passage still reports a real swelling of about a tenth."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage says lead acid cells and nickel cadmium cells share which feature?","choices":["They use a lithium salt dissolved in carbonate solvents.","They hold lithium ions between stacked carbon sheets.","They shut down when a plastic membrane melts near 130 degrees Celsius.","They depend on a thin surface layer that conducts ions but blocks electrons.","Their active material is broken down on discharge and rebuilt on charge."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"taking metal apart and putting it back together"},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"turns to lead sulfate on discharge and back again on charge"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 groups the older chemistries by one habit. The electrode material is chemically taken apart as the cell discharges and put back together as it charges.","distractorNotes":["A carbonate electrolyte belongs to the lithium ion cell described in paragraph 6.","That is intercalation, the later idea introduced in paragraph 4, not how these older cells work.","The melting membrane is the separator of a lithium ion cell in paragraph 8.","The surface layer is the solid electrolyte interphase of paragraph 7, part of the lithium ion design.",null]},{"id":"rc-p09-q03","type":"detail","stem":"In the three layer separator the passage describes, the polyethylene layer is responsible for","choices":["keeping the membrane about forty percent empty by volume","closing its pores near 130 degrees and choking off ion flow","raising the temperature at which the positive electrode gives off oxygen","carrying electrons between the two electrodes","holding the sheet together for about thirty degrees more"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"the polyethylene melts near 130 degrees Celsius and its pores close, choking off ion flow"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 splits the two jobs. The polyethylene in the middle melts near 130 degrees Celsius so its pores close and ion flow stops, while the outer polypropylene keeps the sheet intact longer.","distractorNotes":["Forty percent empty space describes the membrane as a whole, not something one layer contributes.",null,"Oxygen release above roughly 200 degrees is a property of the positive electrode in paragraph 11, not of the separator.","A separator exists to keep the electrodes from touching, so carrying electrons between them is exactly what it prevents.","That is the polypropylene's role, which holds on for roughly another thirty degrees."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, forming the solid electrolyte interphase costs the cell","choices":["a few percent of its lithium, permanently","the ability to use a carbonate electrolyte after the first charge","roughly a tenth of its volume","about forty percent of its internal space","most of its capacity to take charge in the cold"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"permanently locks away a few percent of the cell's lithium"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says the layer builds during the factory's first charge and that making it permanently locks away a few percent of the cell's lithium.","distractorNotes":[null,"The interphase is what lets the carbonate electrolyte keep working, so it does not retire it.","A tenth of the volume is graphite's swelling in paragraph 5, a different number in a different place.","Forty percent is the separator's empty space in paragraph 8.","Trouble charging in the cold comes from intercalation's speed limit in paragraph 9, not from forming this layer."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The passage indicates that once the separator melts and pulls back from the edges, what happens next is that","choices":["a scored disc opens and the cell vents","the pores close and ion flow stops","charged graphite is exposed to liquid electrolyte for the very first time","the electrodes meet across a much larger area","the positive electrode begins giving off oxygen"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"the electrodes meet across an area far larger than any dendrite could bridge"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 runs the steps in order. The shutdown layer closes near 130 degrees, and if the cell keeps warming the separator itself melts above about 160, letting the electrodes meet over an area no dendrite could match.","distractorNotes":["Venting is driven by rising pressure in paragraph 11, after the internal short has already dumped the cell's charge.","That is the earlier step near 130 degrees, and it is the opposite effect.","Charged graphite meets electrolyte first, when the interphase breaks down between about 80 and 120 degrees.",null,"Oxygen release comes later, above roughly 200 degrees, in paragraph 11."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 4, \"host\" most nearly means","choices":["a large number of something","an organism that shelters another organism","a framework that holds ions without being consumed","a liquid in which a salt is dissolved","an electrode made of metal that gives up its electrons readily"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"a rigid framework that takes in guest ions"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"gives them back without being consumed"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 defines the word on the spot: not anything living, but a rigid framework that takes in guest ions between its layers and gives them back without being used up.","distractorNotes":["A real dictionary sense of the word, but the passage is naming a crystal, not a quantity.","The passage rules this sense out in the same sentence where it introduces the word.",null,"That describes the carbonate solvent of paragraph 6, not the crystal.","That describes the lithium metal electrode of paragraph 2, which is exactly what intercalation replaced."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 6, \"window\" most nearly means","choices":["a pore in the separator that ions pass through","the gap between two stacked sheets of carbon","the voltage range a solvent can survive","an opening in the steel can that relieves pressure","a short stretch of time in which something is possible"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"Every solvent has a voltage window"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"the span between the voltage at which it begins to break down"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 builds the meaning as it uses the term: the span between the voltage where a solvent starts breaking down at one electrode and where it breaks down at the other. Water's span is a little over a volt and the cell needs nearly four.","distractorNotes":["The separator's pores appear in paragraph 8 and are never called a window.","The space between carbon sheets is where lithium sits in paragraph 4.",null,"That is the scored vent disc of paragraph 11.","The everyday sense of the word, but this paragraph is measuring volts rather than minutes."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly suggests that charging a cold cell at a high current is risky because","choices":["the interphase thickens and locks away more of the cell's lithium","lithium plates onto the graphite instead of entering it","the separator's pores close before the cell is full","the electrolyte freezes and stops carrying ions","the positive electrode starts releasing oxygen at low temperature"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"plate out as lithium metal after all"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"cold and fast is exactly when plating begins"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 supplies the mechanism. Working into graphite is slow when the cell is cold and slow when the current is high, and ions that arrive faster than the graphite can take them in accept an electron at the surface and plate out as metal.","distractorNotes":["The interphase is built during the factory's first charge in paragraph 7, not during cold fast charging.",null,"Pore closure is a heat triggered shutdown near 130 degrees, which is the opposite situation.","The passage never mentions the electrolyte freezing. The limit it describes is how fast lithium can work its way into graphite.","Oxygen release needs roughly 200 degrees, nowhere near a cold cell."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Based on paragraph 13, a fully charged cell sitting idle on a shelf is best described as","choices":["held apart from a reaction its own chemistry favors","already undergoing slow thermal runaway at room temperature","gradually consuming its separator with every passing day","chemically stable, since its reactants cannot reach one another","safe because its electrolyte will not burn"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"a metastable arrangement"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"kept from reacting by a plastic film"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 calls a charged cell metastable: a strong reducing agent and a strong oxidizing agent kept from each other by a plastic film and a few nanometers of surface chemistry. Blocked is not the same as having no reason to react.","distractorNotes":[null,"Runaway in this passage is a rapid cascade above a crossing temperature, not something a room temperature cell is doing.","Nothing in the passage describes the separator wearing away during storage or ordinary use.","Metastable is the passage's own word, and it means the reaction is favored but blocked, not that there is nothing to block.","Paragraph 6 says plainly that the carbonate solvents are flammable."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the main point of the passage?","choices":["Thermal runaway begins with the interphase and ends when the cell vents.","Lithium cells fail mainly because manufacturers economize on the separator film that keeps the electrodes apart.","Lithium iron phosphate should replace layered oxides everywhere they are now used.","Intercalation cured the plating problem, but the energy it unlocked is also what makes failure severe.","The separator is the only thing standing between a cell and a fire."],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"Intercalation was supposed to retire dendrites, and in ordinary use it does."},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"that figure is why the technology displaced everything else"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"Density and severity are two readings of a single property."}],"why":"The passage moves from why intercalation replaced plating to what a cell does when it overheats, then states the link in paragraph 14: density and severity are two readings of a single property.","distractorNotes":["That is a fair summary of paragraphs 10 and 11 alone, not of the whole passage.","The passage argues the opposite, that the cascade is ordinary chemistry running unchecked rather than a cut corner.","Paragraph 15 calls the safer chemistry a different point on the same curve and names what it costs.",null,"Too narrow and too absolute. The interphase, the management system, cell spacing and vent paths are all described as barriers too."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 12 is included mainly in order to","choices":["explain why the failure keeps going on its own","describe how a management system tracks cell temperature","show that reaction rates can be measured with precision","catalog the gases a venting cell releases","recommend lithium iron phosphate for vehicles"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"The word runaway is doing precise work there."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"every degree of warming buys further warming"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 sets heat produced against heat removed. Above a certain temperature production wins and every degree of warming buys further warming, which is what makes the word runaway accurate.","distractorNotes":[null,"Management systems appear in paragraph 16.","The temperature dependence is used to explain a feedback, not to make a point about measurement.","The gases are listed in paragraph 11.","No such recommendation is made, and paragraph 15 is careful to name what that chemistry costs."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"How do the early paragraphs of the passage relate to the later ones?","choices":["The early ones give the history and the later ones give a procedure the reader is meant to follow step by step.","The early ones build the cell part by part; the later ones follow those same parts failing.","The early ones describe one chemistry and the later ones describe a rival chemistry.","Both stretches make the same argument from two different sets of numbers.","The early ones raise a problem the later ones report as solved."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":6,"quote":"The cell runs by rocking lithium from one host to the other and back."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"the solid electrolyte interphase begins to break down"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"the separator itself melts"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"the positive electrode starts to come apart"}],"why":"The passage introduces the foils, the hosts, the electrolyte, the interphase and the separator in turn, then walks those same components through the heating sequence in paragraphs 10 and 11.","distractorNotes":["There is history in paragraph 2, but the passage never hands the reader a procedure.",null,"Lithium iron phosphate gets one paragraph near the end and is not what the later paragraphs are about.","The two stretches do different work, one descriptive and one causal.","Nothing is reported as solved. The passage closes on a compromise and a deliberately narrow goal."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the popular habit of calling thermal runaway a malfunction is best described as","choices":["admiring of the clarity it brings","neutral, taking no position","corrective but not scornful","alarmed","dismissive of the concern itself"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"The framing is misleading."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"Every step is the same chemistry the cell performs on purpose"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 says the framing is misleading and then explains what is actually going on, calmly and without mocking anyone. That is a correction rather than an attack.","distractorNotes":["Nothing in the paragraph praises the popular framing.","Calling a framing misleading is taking a position.",null,"The paragraph is measured and explanatory rather than alarmed.","The author corrects how the hazard is described, not the idea that there is a hazard."]},{"id":"rc-p09-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In reporting what engineers claim about the hazard, the author comes across as","choices":["doubtful that any of the safety work accomplishes much","certain that the hazard has been engineered out of existence","indifferent to whether the claim turns out to hold","impatient with how slowly the field is moving","approving of a claim kept deliberately narrow"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":7,"quote":"They claim something more modest"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":3,"quote":"The goal is narrower than the public version of it"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 calls the engineers' claim more modest and lets it stand: onset made rare, spread made slow. That word reads as approval of a claim that does not overreach.","distractorNotes":["The passage treats those measures as real and effective within limits, not as empty.","The paragraph states directly that engineers do not claim to have removed the hazard.","Indifference would not single the claim out as modest, which is a favorable word here.","The passage says nothing at all about the pace of the work.",null]},{"id":"rc-p09-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage describes all of the following as pack level measures EXCEPT","choices":["mats that swell and insulate as they heat","aiming the vents so that hot gas leaves through the floor rather than the cabin","setting cells apart from one another","refusing to charge a cold pack quickly","flooding the pack with inert gas"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"Cells are spaced apart","choice":2},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"wrapped in mats that swell and insulate when heated","choice":0},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"hot gas leaves through the floor of a vehicle rather than into the cabin","choice":1},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"refuses to charge a cold one quickly","choice":3},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"Crews flood such fires mainly to cool the neighboring cells.","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 16 lists spacing, swelling mats, cooling plates, aimed vents and a management system that limits cold fast charging. An inert gas flood never appears; the only flooding in the passage is water used to cool neighboring cells in paragraph 11.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 16 describes mats that swell and insulate when heated.","Paragraph 16 says vents are aimed so hot gas leaves through the floor of a vehicle.","Paragraph 16 lists cell spacing directly.","Paragraph 16 says the management system refuses to charge a cold cell quickly.",null]},{"id":"rc-p09-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wanted to know why a lithium ion cell cannot use water as its electrolyte should look in","choices":["paragraph 5","paragraph 11","paragraph 2","paragraph 6","paragraph 4"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"Water is out of the question."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"the window for water is a little over one volt wide"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 rules water out and gives the reason: water's voltage window is a little over one volt wide and the cell runs at nearly four.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 gives the cell's numbers, including its voltages, but not why water is unsuitable.","Paragraph 11 covers the positive electrode giving off oxygen and the cell venting.","Paragraph 2 covers the plating chemistries that came before lithium ion.",null,"Paragraph 4 explains intercalation and what a host is."]}]},"rc-p10":{"id":"rc-p10","title":"The Trade Beneath the Forest Floor","topic":"mycorrhizal fungi and nutrient exchange in forests","field":"ecology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1683,"paragraphs":[["Pull a fine root from a temperate forest floor, rinse the soil away, and set it under a microscope.","What comes into focus is often not a plant surface at all.","The last few millimetres are either wrapped in fungal tissue or riddled with it, and that boundary between two kingdoms is where most of the tree's mineral nutrition takes place.","The arrangement is called a mycorrhiza, from the Greek for fungus and root, and the great majority of land plant families form one.","For most trees, the organ that takes up phosphate from soil is not a root.","It is a partnership."],["Forest botanists recognised the relationship in the 1880s, when the pale sheath on the root tips of pines and beeches was reinterpreted as a partner rather than an infection.","Fossils have since pushed the association far deeper into the past.","Plant remains roughly four hundred million years old, preserved in silica from the earliest land ecosystems, contain branched structures that resemble the feeding organs modern fungi build inside plant cells.","The symbiosis looks about as old as land plants themselves, which suggests plants reached dry ground with fungi rather than recruiting them later."],["Two associations dominate, and they differ in a way worth picturing.","In an arbuscular mycorrhiza, the fungus breaches the wall of a cortex cell and grows inside it, branching over and over into a structure shaped like a miniature tree.","That structure is the arbuscule, and it is the exchange surface.","The plant cell does not simply give way.","Its own membrane stretches inward around every branch, so the two organisms stay separated by two membranes and never share cytoplasm.","Other hyphae swell into vesicles that store lipid.","The fungi that do this form one small lineage with only a few hundred described species, yet they partner with most vascular plants."],["An ectomycorrhiza never enters a living cell.","The fungus instead sheathes the short root tip in a dense mantle of hyphae, often thick enough to see with a hand lens, then pushes a lattice of flattened hyphae between the outer root cells.","That lattice is the Hartig net, and it does the job the arbuscule does by wrapping cells rather than entering them.","Thousands of fungal species form ectomycorrhizas, and many are the familiar mushrooms of forest floors: boletes, amanitas, chanterelles and truffles.","Only about two percent of seed plant species take this route, but those few include the pines, oaks, beeches, birches and eucalypts that build most of the world's forests."],["The reason a tree pays for either arrangement comes down to geometry and to one stubborn nutrient.","Phosphate barely moves in soil.","It binds to mineral surfaces and diffuses so slowly that a root strips the millimetre or two around itself within days, then sits in its own depletion zone, ringed by soil it cannot reach.","A fine root is perhaps two to five hundred micrometres across and a root hair ten to twenty.","A fungal hypha is two to ten micrometres across, finds purchase in pores far too narrow for a root hair to enter, and extends centimetres past the depleted shell.","A single gram of forest soil can hold tens to hundreds of metres of it.","Per unit of carbon spent, hyphae buy far more absorbing surface than roots do."],["What travels back along those hyphae is, above all, phosphate, taken up at the tip, strung into chains called polyphosphate for the journey, and released at the exchange interface.","Nitrogen moves too, as ammonium and as amino acids.","Many ectomycorrhizal fungi secrete enzymes that break down protein in leaf litter, which lets their trees tap nitrogen locked inside dead tissue, while arbuscular fungi rely far more on nitrogen already freed into the soil.","The fungus also delivers water, zinc and copper, and can blunt attacks from root pathogens."],["The plant pays in carbon, and it pays twice over.","Sugar crosses the interface as simple six carbon units.","Lipid crosses as well, which is the more surprising half of the bill.","Arbuscular fungi cannot manufacture their own fatty acids and must be handed them by a host, which is a large part of why for decades no one could grow one to completion on a laboratory plate without a plant, until researchers supplied a fatty acid directly.","On the far side the fungus converts what it receives into storage compounds, so sugar in fungal tissue stays dilute and the gradient keeps pulling more across."],["That bill is not trivial.","Estimates of the share of a plant's net photosynthetic production that ends up in its fungal partners commonly run from four to twenty percent, with the higher figures in seedlings and poor soils.","Experiments that sever the phloem by girdling a trunk show the flow falling away within days, which means the fungi live on sugar made that week, not on stored reserves.","A colonised root system is, in the language physiologists use, a powerful sink, and sugar made up in the leaves is drawn down to it rather than kept in the shoot."],["Given a trade that large, the obvious question is where the carbon ends up.","The tool that answers it is the labelled atom.","Seal a branch or a whole seedling in a bag, feed it carbon dioxide built from an uncommon isotope of carbon, and every sugar the plant makes for the next several hours carries a tag instruments can detect at very low concentrations.","Wait, then harvest the plants growing nearby and look for the tag.","Some designs label two neighbouring plants with different isotopes at once, so traffic can be measured in both directions."],["Separating the fungal route from the soil takes a second trick.","Investigators grow the donor and the receiver in compartments divided by a mesh whose pores admit hyphae but exclude roots, and they sometimes rotate the mesh cylinder on a schedule to shear any hyphae that cross.","Compare the intact setup with the severed one, and the difference belongs to the fungal connection.","Run these designs and labelled carbon does turn up in neighbouring plants, across species as well as within them, and shaded receivers often show more of it than sunlit ones."],["This is the point where care is required, because a tag in a neighbour is not automatically carbon in a neighbour.","Much of what the instruments detect sits in fungal hyphae wrapped around and threaded through the receiving root, which is fungal tissue, not plant tissue.","Carbon that reaches the fungus and stops there has not moved between plants at all.","The stricter tests look for the tag above ground, in the receiver's stem and leaves, and by that measure the quantity that clearly crosses into plant tissue is usually a small fraction of what the receiver fixes for itself."],["The network is also not the only road.","Labelled carbon can leak from roots into soil water and be taken up by a neighbour directly, be respired underground as carbon dioxide and fixed again above ground, travel in litter as leaves fall and rot, or pass through a graft where two roots have fused.","Each of these is real, none requires a fungal conduit, and pulling them apart in a forest rather than a pot is genuinely difficult."],["Suppose all of that is handled and real transfer is demonstrated.","A further claim is still being smuggled in when the result is described as one tree feeding another, because purpose is a separate question from movement.","The fungus is not an inert pipe running between two trees.","It is a third organism with an interest of its own, usually joined to many hosts at once, and it can hold carbon in its own hyphae, spend it exploring new soil, or pour it into spores.","Flow may be set by the fungus, or simply by which end of the connection is hungrier."],["Evolution sharpens the objection.","Natural selection acts on the donor, and a trait that costs a tree carbon while benefiting a competitor three metres away should be whittled down over generations unless the donor gets something back: a better fungal partner, a return flow later, or the survival of a relative.","No such account is needed for leakage down a gradient, which happens whenever a tissue rich in sugar is plumbed to one that is short of it.","The plain explanation is the one to beat."],["One group of plants shows how far the conduit can be pushed.","Ghost pipe and a number of orchids carry no usable chlorophyll and make no sugar at all.","They live on carbon drawn out of fungi that are attached at the same time to photosynthetic trees, which is as clear a demonstration as anyone could want that plant carbon can cross a fungus into another plant.","It also cuts the other way.","These plants return nothing.","The route says nothing about generosity at either end."],["That distinction has largely been lost outside the laboratory.","The image of the forest as a web of nurture, with old trees knowingly provisioning seedlings and warnings passing from root to root, has become the standard way the subject is told, and the appeal is easy to see.","Some of it is defensible as metaphor.","But the confident language of intention has run well ahead of the measurements, and recent surveys of the literature have found that several of the most repeated claims rest on a handful of studies, many run in pots rather than forests, and that sources cited for the strongest statements often do not say what those statements report."],["None of this diminishes the science.","What is not in doubt is plain enough: the symbiosis is ancient, nearly universal, and structurally elegant.","The trade of mineral nutrients for sugar and fat is measured and quantified.","Carbon does move through fungal tissue between root systems.","What stays open is how much of that movement matters to a growing forest, and whether anything resembling provisioning happens at all.","The gap is where the interesting work sits, and a bargain that old was never going to need the decoration."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p10-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, when a fungus builds an arbuscule inside a cell of the root cortex, the plant cell","choices":["dissolves its own wall so the two organisms can mix freely","wraps the fungus in a dense sheath of its own tissue","folds its membrane inward around every fungal branch","fills with stored lipid that the fungus later draws on","dies off, leaving a cavity that the fungus then occupies"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"Its own membrane stretches inward around every branch"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"The plant cell does not simply give way."}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says the plant cell does not simply give way: its own membrane stretches inward around every branch, which keeps the two organisms separated by two membranes. The fungus gets inside the wall, but never inside the cytoplasm.","distractorNotes":["The fungus does breach the wall, but paragraph 3 is explicit that the two never share cytoplasm, so nothing mixes freely.","A sheath around the outside is the ectomycorrhizal mantle from paragraph 4, and that mantle is fungal tissue, not plant tissue.",null,"Lipid storage happens in vesicles, which paragraph 3 names as a separate structure formed by other hyphae.","Nothing in paragraph 3 says the colonised cell dies, and a dead cell could not stretch its membrane around the branches."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage describes the Hartig net as","choices":["a lattice of flattened hyphae between the outer root cells","a strand of hyphae running from a root tip out into open soil","a dense fungal sheath covering the outside of a short root tip","a swollen hypha that stores lipid on behalf of the fungus","a branched fungal organ that grows inside a cortex cell"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"That lattice is the Hartig net"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"pushes a lattice of flattened hyphae between the outer root cells"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 names the Hartig net as the lattice of flattened hyphae the fungus pushes between the outer root cells, and points out that it does the arbuscule's job by wrapping cells instead of entering them.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 5 does describe hyphae reaching out into soil, but the passage never gives that reach the name Hartig net.","That is the mantle, which paragraph 4 describes as a separate structure sitting on the outside of the root tip.","That is a vesicle, which paragraph 3 attributes to the arbuscular type rather than the ectomycorrhizal one.","That is the arbuscule from paragraph 3, and paragraph 4 opens by saying an ectomycorrhiza never enters a living cell."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q03","type":"detail","stem":"The passage gives the diameter of a fungal hypha as roughly","choices":["20 to 100 micrometres","10 to 20 micrometres","2 to 10 micrometres","1 to 2 millimetres","200 to 500 micrometres"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"A fungal hypha is two to ten micrometres across"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 lines the three figures up in a row, and the hypha is the smallest of them at two to ten micrometres. That is the whole point of the comparison.","distractorNotes":["No structure in paragraph 5 is given this width, so this choice has nothing behind it.","Ten to twenty micrometres is the figure paragraph 5 gives for a root hair, not for a hypha.",null,"A millimetre or two is the width of the depletion zone around a root in paragraph 5, not the width of a hypha.","Two to five hundred micrometres is the figure paragraph 5 gives for a fine root."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, many ectomycorrhizal fungi can reach nitrogen held in dead tissue because they","choices":["release enzymes that break down protein in leaf litter","capture nitrogen from air trapped inside the root mantle","take up ammonium faster than a root hair can take it up","move nitrogen along the hypha as chains of polyphosphate","trade nitrogen with arbuscular fungi on neighbouring roots"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"secrete enzymes that break down protein in leaf litter"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 draws the line between the two association types right here: ectomycorrhizal fungi secrete enzymes that break down protein in litter, while arbuscular fungi lean on nitrogen already freed into the soil.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 6 never mentions nitrogen being taken from air, and no part of the passage describes nitrogen fixation.","Paragraph 6 does say nitrogen moves as ammonium, but that is the form it travels in, not the reason litter nitrogen becomes available.","Polyphosphate is how paragraph 6 says phosphate travels, and swapping it onto nitrogen is a one word change.","No exchange between the two fungal types appears anywhere in the passage, so this is outside its scope."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The passage explains that an arbuscular fungus has never been grown to completion on a plate without a plant because the fungus","choices":["can store lipid only while it is attached to a living root","loses the ability to take up phosphate when no root is present","needs a mantle of root tissue in order to produce its spores","cannot make fatty acids and must be handed them","depends on carbon dioxide that living roots release into soil"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"cannot manufacture their own fatty acids and must be handed them by a host"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 calls lipid the more surprising half of the bill. Arbuscular fungi cannot manufacture their own fatty acids, and that dependence is given as a large part of why they cannot be raised to completion without a host.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 mentions vesicles storing lipid but never makes storage itself conditional on a living root.","Phosphate uptake is the fungus's own job in paragraph 6, and nothing ties it to the presence of a root.","Mantles belong to the ectomycorrhizal type in paragraph 4, and the passage never links them to spore production.",null,"Soil carbon dioxide appears in paragraph 12 as a route a label can take, not as something a fungus must be fed."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 5, \"purchase\" most nearly means","choices":["a payment made for a service","a claim on a shared resource","an advantage won by bargaining","something acquired by buying","a secure physical hold"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"finds purchase in pores far too narrow for a root hair to enter"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 is about size and reach: the hypha finds purchase in pores too narrow for a root hair. The word is being used for getting a grip on a space, not for buying anything.","distractorNotes":["This is the everyday meaning of the word, and the surrounding trade language makes it tempting, but pores are not paid for.","The passage does describe an exchange, but this particular sentence is purely about the size of the openings a hypha can enter.","Paragraph 5 is describing physical access to soil pores, not negotiation between the partners.","Nothing is being acquired here. The sentence is about where the hypha can fit.",null]},{"id":"rc-p10-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 8, \"sink\" most nearly means","choices":["a basin into which a liquid drains away and is lost","a steady decline in value over a long period","a destination that draws supply in","a slow settling downward through soil","a place where unwanted material collects"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"a powerful sink, and sugar made up in the leaves is drawn down to it"}],"why":"Watch what the word is doing in paragraph 8. Sugar made up in the leaves is drawn down to the colonised root rather than kept in the shoot, so a sink here is the end of the line that demand pulls supply toward.","distractorNotes":["This is the common household meaning, and paragraph 8 is not describing plumbing or drainage.","Nothing in paragraph 8 is losing value. Sugar is being pulled toward the root, not devalued.",null,"The movement in paragraph 8 is set by demand at the root, not by anything sifting downward through soil.","Sugar arriving at the root is the tree's own product being spent on a partner, not waste being dumped."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage suggests that finding a tag in a neighbouring plant's root is weaker evidence than finding it in that plant's leaves because","choices":["a root carries fungal tissue that may hold the tag itself","the tag breaks down before it can travel up into a shoot","the fungus strips the tag out of a root before it is harvested","leaves are the only tissue instruments can measure accurately","a root holds far less carbon overall than a leaf holds"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"which is fungal tissue, not plant tissue"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"The stricter tests look for the tag above ground"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 makes the distinction that drives the whole second half of the passage: much of what the instruments pick up in a receiving root sits in fungal hyphae wrapped around and through it. Tissue above ground cannot be fungal, so it is the stricter test.","distractorNotes":[null,"Nothing in the passage says the tag degrades, and the isotope is described as detectable at very low concentrations.","No removal of the tag by the fungus appears anywhere in the passage, so this invents a mechanism.","Paragraph 9 says instruments detect the tag at very low concentrations, with no suggestion that roots are unreadable.","The passage never compares how much carbon a root and a leaf hold, and that would not settle whose tissue the tag is in."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The example of ghost pipe and certain orchids most strongly supports which conclusion?","choices":["Chlorophyll turns out to be unnecessary for the majority of land plants","Fungi favour hosts that have given up photosynthesis","Trees raise their sugar output when a network drains carbon from them","A plant can take from a network without giving back","Most plants in a forest obtain their carbon from other plants"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":5,"quote":"These plants return nothing."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":6,"quote":"The route says nothing about generosity at either end."}],"why":"Paragraph 15 says these plants make no sugar and return nothing, and the author uses them to show that carbon crossing a fungus says nothing about generosity at either end. A route can be used in one direction only.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 15 limits the missing chlorophyll to ghost pipe and a number of orchids, not to most land plants.","The passage never says fungi prefer these plants, only that these plants can live off fungi already attached to trees.","Nothing in the passage describes a donor increasing its output in response to a drain.",null,"These plants are presented as one unusual group, and paragraph 1 says most plants take up their own nutrients through a partnership."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Ectomycorrhizal and arbuscular partners differ mainly in their fungal lineages","The trade is well documented, the sharing between trees far less so","Isotope labelling has settled the question of how carbon moves between trees","Fungal networks join the trees of a forest into a single system of mutual care","Phosphate scarcity is the one force that has shaped the evolution of land plants"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":17,"sentence":3,"quote":"The trade of mineral nutrients for sugar and fat is measured and quantified."},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":5,"quote":"What stays open is how much of that movement matters to a growing forest"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"the confident language of intention has run well ahead of the measurements"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":6,"quote":"It is a partnership."}],"why":"The passage builds the partnership carefully, then spends its second half separating what the measurements show from what the popular story claims. Paragraph 17 states the split directly.","distractorNotes":["The structural contrast is real, but it fills two paragraphs and is not what the passage is arguing.",null,"Paragraph 11 and paragraph 17 both say the opposite: labelling raises a question the field has not closed.","This is the popular account the author pushes back on in paragraph 16, not the position the passage argues for.","Phosphate gets one paragraph as the reason the partnership pays, and the passage never claims it is the single driver of plant evolution."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 12 is included mainly in order to","choices":["argue that soil water supplies most of the nutrients in a forest","point out other ways a tag could reach a neighbouring plant","explain why root grafts form between trees of the same species","describe the way fallen leaves decompose on a forest floor","show that the fungal route carries more carbon than the soil route does"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"The network is also not the only road."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"none requires a fungal conduit"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 lists soil water, respired carbon dioxide, litter and root grafts, then says none of them requires a fungal conduit. Its job is to keep a tag in a neighbour from being read automatically as network traffic.","distractorNotes":["Soil water is named as one possible path for a tag, not as the forest's main nutrient supply.",null,"Grafts appear as one item on a list of alternative routes, and the passage never explains how or why they form.","Litter is mentioned in a single clause as a route, and paragraph 6 covers what litter contributes without describing decay.","Paragraph 12 makes no comparison of quantities. It says the routes are hard to tell apart, which is the opposite of ranking them."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"The passage as a whole is organized as","choices":["a problem raised in the opening and fully solved by the closing paragraph","a comparison of two fungal groups, followed by a defence of one of them","a series of experiments presented in the order they were carried out","a chronological history of research into the fungi of forest soils","an account of a partnership, then a limit on what it shows"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":6,"quote":"It is a partnership."},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"The reason a tree pays for either arrangement"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"This is the point where care is required"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":5,"quote":"What stays open is how much of that movement matters"}],"why":"The first eight paragraphs build the symbiosis and price it. Paragraphs 9 and 10 give the method. From paragraph 11 on, the passage tests how far the results can be pushed, and paragraph 17 leaves the question open.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 17 says plainly that the central question stays open, so nothing is solved at the close.","The two groups are compared, but the author never takes a side between them.","The labelling work fills two paragraphs and is described by design rather than by date.","Only paragraph 2 is historical, and the rest of the passage is not arranged by date.",null]},{"id":"rc-p10-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the popular image of the forest as a web of nurture is best described as","choices":["alert to its appeal but unconvinced by it","indifferent to whether the image is accurate","fully persuaded by the case that has been made for it","openly contemptuous of everyone who repeats it","amused that researchers have allowed the phrase to spread so widely"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"the appeal is easy to see"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"the confident language of intention has run well ahead of the measurements"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 grants that the appeal is easy to see and that some of the image is defensible as metaphor, then says the language of intention has run well ahead of the measurements. That is acknowledgement followed by doubt.","distractorNotes":[null,"Accuracy is the author's central concern, which is what paragraphs 11 through 14 are built to test.","Paragraph 16 states the opposite, that the confident language has outrun what has been measured.","The author is critical of the claims, not of the people, and paragraph 16 concedes the image has real appeal.","Paragraph 16 treats the overreach as a serious problem with the literature rather than as something funny."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In the final paragraph, the author treats the unanswered questions about carbon sharing as","choices":["a sign that the whole field has been oversold","evidence that the symbiosis itself has been misdescribed","a reason to set the subject aside for the time being","a problem that no instrument will ever be able to settle","an opening for further work rather than a flaw"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":17,"sentence":1,"quote":"None of this diminishes the science."},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":6,"quote":"The gap is where the interesting work sits"}],"why":"Paragraph 17 lists what is not in doubt, names what stays open, and then says the gap is where the interesting work sits. The uncertainty is framed as the place to go next.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 17 opens by saying none of this diminishes the science, so the criticism is aimed at one claim, not the field.","Paragraph 17 calls the symbiosis ancient, nearly universal and structurally elegant, which is the part it treats as settled.","The closing sentence points toward more work on the question, not away from the subject.","No such limit is claimed. The passage describes methods that are getting stricter, not methods that are failing.",null]},{"id":"rc-p10-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports each of the following statements EXCEPT","choices":["Some of the fungi that form mycorrhizas produce mushrooms on a forest floor","Fungi steer carbon toward the seedlings most closely related to the donor","Phosphate moves through soil slowly enough to leave a root short of it","There are plants that live without making any sugar of their own","A share of what a tree makes in a given week ends up in fungal tissue"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"diffuses so slowly that a root strips the millimetre or two around itself within days","choice":2},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"many are the familiar mushrooms of forest floors","choice":0},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":3,"quote":"the fungi live on sugar made that week","choice":4},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":2,"quote":"make no sugar at all","choice":3},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"Flow may be set by the fungus, or simply by which end of the connection is hungrier.","choice":1},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"several of the most repeated claims rest on a handful of studies","choice":1}],"why":"Paragraph 14 raises a return to a relative only as one of the things a donor would need to get back for the behaviour to persist. The passage never reports that fungi actually route carbon by kinship, and paragraph 13 says flow may be set by the fungus or by simple demand.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 supports this by naming boletes, amanitas, chanterelles and truffles as ectomycorrhizal fungi.",null,"Paragraph 5 supports this: phosphate diffuses so slowly that a root creates its own depletion zone within days.","Paragraph 15 supports this with ghost pipe and a number of orchids, which make no sugar at all.","Paragraph 8 supports this with the girdling result, which shows the fungi living on sugar made that week."]},{"id":"rc-p10-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wants to know how investigators let hyphae, but not roots, pass between two plants should look in which paragraph?","choices":["Paragraph 5","Paragraph 8","Paragraph 9","Paragraph 10","Paragraph 12"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"a mesh whose pores admit hyphae but exclude roots"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 is the one that describes the mesh whose pores admit hyphae and exclude roots, along with the rotating cylinder used to shear the hyphae that cross.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 compares the widths of roots, root hairs and hyphae, but describes no apparatus.","Paragraph 8 covers the carbon budget and the girdling result, not the design of a transfer experiment.","Paragraph 9 explains how a plant is labelled with an isotope, which is the step before the barrier is set up.",null,"Paragraph 12 lists routes other than the fungal one, which is a different concern from blocking roots."]}]},"rc-p11":{"id":"rc-p11","title":"From Bacterial Memory to Gene Editing","topic":"bacterial adaptive immunity and gene editing","field":"molecular biology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1693,"paragraphs":[["In the late 1980s a team sequencing a bacterial gene ran past its end and found something nobody could explain.","Downstream of the gene sat a short sequence, roughly thirty base pairs long, repeated again and again.","Between one copy of the repeat and the next lay a stretch of similar length that was not repeated at all, and every one of those intervening stretches was different.","The arrangement was far too regular to be an accident.","The finding was published, noticed by a few specialists, and then left alone for fifteen years."],["As sequencing grew cheap the same pattern surfaced everywhere.","Something like four in ten sequenced bacteria carried such an array, and the large majority of archaea did too.","By 2002 the arrangement had a formal name, clustered regularly interspaced short palindromic repeats, and an acronym that has since escaped the laboratory.","Two details hinted that the arrays were doing work.","They sat beside a tight cluster of genes encoding proteins that looked like nucleases and helicases, the enzymes that cut and unwind nucleic acids.","And the nonrepeating stretches between the repeats, called spacers, differed from strain to strain within one species."],["The answer arrived around 2005, when several groups stopped comparing spacers with the host genome and began comparing them with everything else in the sequence databases.","The matches were not bacterial.","They came from bacteriophages, the viruses that prey on bacteria, and from plasmids, the small mobile circles of DNA that pass between cells.","A spacer was a fragment of something that had once tried to get in.","An array was a ledger of past intrusions, kept in the order they happened."],["A ledger implies a use, and within two years the use was demonstrated.","In a dairy bacterium used to ferment yogurt and cheese, cultures were challenged with a phage and the survivors sequenced.","The survivors had added new spacers, and those spacers matched the attacking phage.","Deleting a single spacer from a resistant strain cost it protection against precisely the phage that spacer matched, and nothing else.","Adding a spacer to a strain that had never met the phage handed it protection it had not earned.","The defense was sequence specific, heritable, and acquired during an encounter rather than carried from birth."],["That last property is what made the result startling.","Bacteria were already known to defend themselves, mainly with restriction enzymes, which cleave any DNA carrying a particular short sequence unless the cell has chemically marked its own copies as friendly.","That defense is blunt and fixed: the same weapon, aimed the same way, in every generation.","The arrays described something else.","A cell met a specific virus, kept a souvenir of it, and passed that souvenir to descendants who would know the virus on sight without ever having met it."],["The cycle has three stages, and keeping them separate prevents most of the confusion around the subject.","In adaptation, the cell samples an invader and files a new spacer.","In expression, the array is transcribed and cut into small guide RNAs.","In interference, a guide loaded into a protein finds the matching sequence in an invading genome and cuts it.","Only the third stage resembles editing, and only the first two explain how the system knows what to aim at."],["Adaptation is the rarest of the three and the least understood.","A pair of conserved proteins, Cas1 and Cas2, form a complex that captures a short piece of foreign DNA, called a protospacer, and installs it in the array.","The insertion is not random.","New spacers go in at one particular end, beside a stretch called the leader, so the array accumulates chronologically with the most recent threat at the front.","Each insertion also duplicates one copy of the repeat, which is why the alternating pattern survives every time the record grows."],["Expression begins with a single long transcript.","The cell reads the whole array, repeats and spacers together, into one RNA molecule and then chops it into units.","Each mature guide carries about twenty nucleotides of spacer derived sequence, the part that will pair with a target, attached to a piece of repeat derived sequence that the protein grips.","In the best studied version a second small RNA, made elsewhere in the same locus, pairs with the repeat portion and is required both for chopping the long transcript and for the guide to function."],["Interference is where the numbers get specific.","The loaded protein does not read the genome from one end to the other.","It collides with DNA at random, and at each collision it checks for a motif just two or three bases long.","Only when it finds one does it pry the double helix open and offer its guide to the exposed strand.","Pairing begins at the ten or twelve bases nearest the motif, a stretch called the seed, and if those fail to match the protein lets go within milliseconds.","If they match, pairing runs the length of the guide, the displaced strand loops out, and two nuclease domains inside the protein cut one strand each.","The product is a clean break across both strands, three base pairs from the motif."],["That short motif is the hinge of the whole system.","It is called the protospacer adjacent motif.","In the most widely used version it is any base followed by two guanines, sitting immediately beside the target on the strand the guide does not pair with.","The motif is no part of the guide, and the requirement looks at first like a design flaw, since only sequences sitting next to the right two letters can be attacked.","It is not a flaw.","The spacer stored in the array is flanked by repeat sequence rather than by the motif, so a guide never finds the motif beside the cell's own copy of the record.","The motif licenses the cut, and by licensing it only outside the array it keeps the cell from shredding its own memory.","The same rule guides adaptation, where the capture complex prefers fragments carrying the motif."],["Popular accounts reach immediately for scissors, and the metaphor does more harm than good.","In a bacterium the cut really is the end of the story: the invading genome has no future, the break destroys it, and most bacteria carry no pathway for stitching such a break back together.","In a nucleated cell the break is the beginning.","The cell notices the damage and repairs it, and what the experimenter gets is not the cut but whatever the repair machinery decides to do with it.","The scissors image describes the easy half of the job and skips the half that settles the outcome."],["The step that turned a defense system into an instrument was smaller than the publicity suggests.","By 2012 it was clear that the two RNAs could be fused into one synthetic molecule, a single guide, and that the protein would work perfectly well with the fusion.","That left an enzyme whose specificity lived entirely in twenty nucleotides of RNA.","Earlier targeting tools had the same ambition but read DNA with protein, which meant building a new protein for every new site.","Retargeting the newer system means ordering a different short strand of RNA."],["What happens after the break depends on which repair pathway arrives first.","The fast route rejoins the ends directly and often loses or gains a base or two along the way, which shifts the reading frame and silences the gene.","That is why gene knockouts are now routine.","The accurate route copies from a template, so a supplied donor sequence can install a chosen change, but it works mainly in dividing cells and is far less efficient.","One response has been to avoid double strand breaks entirely: editors that nick one strand and chemically convert a single base, and editors that nick one strand and write a short new sequence from an RNA template carried by the guide."],["Specificity is the other honest worry.","Twenty bases is long enough to be unique in a mammalian genome in principle, but pairing tolerates mismatches, particularly at the end farthest from the motif, where one or two mismatched bases may not be enough to make the protein release.","A guide that behaves this way is promiscuous: it pairs where it was not sent and cuts sites that resemble its target without matching it.","The countermeasures are real and partial.","Engineered variants hold DNA less tightly and so demand a better match, shortened guides are less forgiving of mismatches, and a pair of nicking enzymes aimed at opposite strands severs the helix only where both bind.","Finding rare off target events among billions of cells remains a measurement problem of its own."],["Delivery is the harder problem and the less glamorous one.","Taking cells out of a patient, editing them in a dish and returning them works, and it is where the first approved uses have landed, because the cells can be inspected before they go back.","Editing a tissue inside the body is another matter.","The most common viral vector carries roughly four and a half kilobases, and the gene for the standard enzyme alone runs about four, leaving no room for the promoter and guide it needs, which has pushed the field toward smaller enzymes borrowed from other bacteria.","Lipid particles sidestep the size limit but drain overwhelmingly to the liver.","And because these enzymes come from bacteria most people have already met, many patients carry antibodies and T cells primed against them."],["The arc is worth holding in view.","A pattern that looked like sequencing noise turned out to be a filing system, the filing system a memory, and the memory a program that anyone can retype in twenty letters.","None of it was designed for human purposes, which is exactly why it works so well and exactly why it resists domestication.","Phages already carry proteins that shut the system down, a reminder that it has been under test far longer than it has been in use.","The instrument on the bench is a borrowed one, still carrying the constraints of the cell it came from."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p11-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, where does a newly captured spacer end up in the array?","choices":["At one end of the array, beside the leader sequence","At the end of the array farthest from the leader sequence","Inside the repeat that sits closest to the neighboring genes","In a second array kept separate from the original one","Distributed at random among the spacers already stored there"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"New spacers go in at one particular end, beside a stretch called the leader"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says outright that new spacers go in at one particular end, beside a stretch called the leader, which is what makes the array chronological.","distractorNotes":[null,"Half right: it is one particular end, but paragraph 7 places it beside the leader rather than away from it.","Spacers sit between repeats, and paragraph 7 has each insertion duplicate a repeat rather than break into one.","No second array appears anywhere in the passage; the record grows inside the one array.","Paragraph 7 states directly that the insertion is not random, and then names the position."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage states that when the bases nearest the motif fail to pair with the guide, the protein","choices":["lets go of the DNA within milliseconds","files the mismatched sequence away as a new spacer","calls in a second protein to finish the search for a target","opens a longer stretch of the helix and tries the match again","cuts one strand of the helix and leaves the other intact"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"the protein lets go within milliseconds"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 describes the seed, the ten or twelve bases nearest the motif, and says that if those fail to match the protein lets go within milliseconds.","distractorNotes":[null,"Filing a spacer belongs to adaptation in paragraph 7 and is carried out by a different pair of proteins.","No second protein is described at this step, and the two nuclease domains named in paragraph 9 are inside the same protein.","Paragraph 9 runs the other way: the helix is opened first, and a failed seed ends the attempt.","Single strand nicking shows up in paragraph 13 as a deliberate laboratory design, not as the response to a failed seed match."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q03","type":"detail","stem":"In the phage experiment described, removing a single spacer from a resistant strain caused that strain to","choices":["lose protection against every phage it had previously resisted","resist a wider range of phages than it had resisted before","stop adding any new spacers to its array from then on","take up a replacement spacer from the surrounding medium","lose protection only against the phage that spacer matched"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"cost it protection against precisely the phage that spacer matched, and nothing else"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 reports that deleting one spacer cost the strain protection against precisely the phage that spacer matched, and nothing else.","distractorNotes":["Too sweeping: paragraph 4 stresses that the loss was confined to the matching phage.","This reverses the finding: the deletion cost protection instead of broadening it.","The experiment measured protection, and the passage never claims that deletion shuts adaptation down.","Paragraph 4 has spacers added during infection or by the experimenters, never picked up to fill a deletion.",null]},{"id":"rc-p11-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the trouble with the most common viral vector is that","choices":["its capacity leaves no room for the promoter and guide beside the enzyme gene","it works only in cells that are actively dividing","it delivers almost all of its cargo to the liver","patients have usually been exposed already to the bacteria it comes from","it has to be given over and over again before a useful number of cells are edited"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"leaving no room for the promoter and guide it needs"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 gives the numbers: the vector carries roughly four and a half kilobases and the enzyme gene alone runs about four, so the promoter and guide do not fit.","distractorNotes":[null,"Dependence on dividing cells belongs to the template copying repair route in paragraph 13.","Draining to the liver is the drawback of lipid particles in that same paragraph, offered as the alternative.","Prior exposure in paragraph 15 is to the bacteria the enzymes come from, not to the vector.","Repeat dosing is never mentioned; the stated obstacle is size."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The passage reports that the repeat arrays sit next to","choices":["a long stretch of DNA that encodes no protein at all","the genes a cell uses to mark its own DNA as friendly rather than foreign","a cluster of genes for proteins resembling nucleases and helicases","a duplicated copy of the whole bacterial chromosome","the plasmids that carry DNA between bacterial cells"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"a tight cluster of genes encoding proteins that looked like nucleases and helicases"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 names this as one of the two details that hinted the arrays were doing work: they sat beside a tight cluster of genes for proteins that looked like nucleases and helicases.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 2 says those neighboring genes encode proteins, which is exactly why they were suggestive.","Marking one's own DNA as friendly belongs to the restriction enzyme system in paragraph 5, used there as a contrast.",null,"No duplicated chromosome appears in the passage; paragraph 7 duplicates a single repeat.","Plasmids appear in paragraph 3 as a source of spacer sequence, not as a neighbor of the array."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 10, \"licenses\" most nearly means","choices":["explains","postpones","measures","conceals","permits"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":7,"quote":"The motif licenses the cut, and by licensing it only outside the array"}],"why":"In paragraph 10 the motif licenses the cut, meaning it gives the cut permission to proceed, and withholding that permission inside the array is what protects the cell's own record.","distractorNotes":["The motif does not account for the cut, it allows it; the sentence is about permission rather than explanation.","The motif does not delay the cut, it is the condition that lets the cut happen at all.","Nothing is being quantified in that sentence, which is about whether cutting may go ahead.","The motif sits in plain view beside the target, and hiding it would defeat the checkpoint entirely.",null]},{"id":"rc-p11-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 14, \"promiscuous\" most nearly means","choices":["present in many copies","shortened at one end","not selective enough","easily broken down","unusually quick to act"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"it pairs where it was not sent and cuts sites that resemble its target without matching it"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 defines the word in the same sentence: a promiscuous guide pairs where it was not sent and cuts sites that merely resemble its target.","distractorNotes":["Copy number is never discussed; the sentence concerns a single guide pairing in the wrong place.","Shortened guides turn up in the same paragraph as one of the fixes, so this trades the problem for the remedy.",null,"Nothing about the guide breaking down appears in that paragraph, which is describing binding behavior.","Speed is not at issue there; the sentence is about where a guide binds, not how fast."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q08","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 10 most strongly supports which conclusion about a bacterium whose stored spacer happened to sit beside the recognition motif?","choices":["It would need a second copy of the motif on the opposite strand before cutting","It would lose the ability to recognize the matching phage","It could no longer transcribe its array into guide RNAs","It would risk having its own record cut by one of its own guides","It would stop capturing new spacers from invading DNA"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"a guide never finds the motif beside the cell's own copy of the record"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":7,"quote":"it keeps the cell from shredding its own memory"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 explains that the stored spacer is flanked by repeat sequence rather than by the motif, which is what keeps a guide from attacking the array. Put the motif there and that protection is gone.","distractorNotes":["The passage describes one motif, on the strand the guide does not pair with, and never a second.","Recognition comes from the twenty nucleotide guide, which would be unchanged; what changes is whether the array can be attacked.","Transcription is described in paragraph 8 and does not depend on the motif in any way.",null,"Capture is described as preferring fragments that carry the motif, and nothing ties it to what is already stored."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Which conclusion about the same enzyme acting in a bacterium and in a nucleated cell does the passage best support?","choices":["A bacterium repairs a double strand break more precisely than a nucleated cell does","An identical cut can lead to different outcomes depending on the cell that receives it","The enzyme cuts less accurately once it is working inside a nucleated cell","The enzyme would require a different recognition motif in order to work inside a nucleated cell","A nucleated cell destroys the enzyme before it can ever reach its target"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"In a bacterium the cut really is the end of the story"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"In a nucleated cell the break is the beginning."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"whatever the repair machinery decides to do with it"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says the cut ends the story in a bacterium but only begins it in a nucleated cell, where the repair machinery decides what the experimenter actually gets.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 11 says most bacteria have no pathway for rejoining such a break, so they do not repair it more precisely.",null,"Paragraph 11 is about what follows the cut, not about cutting accuracy, which is raised separately in paragraph 14.","The motif is a property of the enzyme and its guide, and the passage never makes it depend on the host cell.","The immune recognition in paragraph 15 concerns patients carrying antibodies, not a cell destroying the enzyme at the target."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["Gene editing will stay impractical until viral vectors can be engineered to carry a much larger cargo","Repeated sequences found in bacterial genomes had no known function for fifteen years after they were first reported","A bacterial record of past infection was decoded and then borrowed as a tool that still carries its original constraints","Bacterial defenses turn out to be more sophisticated than the immune memory found in vertebrate animals","The recognition motif is the single feature that matters most in the whole system, in bacteria and in the laboratory alike"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"An array was a ledger of past intrusions"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"the memory a program that anyone can retype in twenty letters"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"The instrument on the bench is a borrowed one, still carrying the constraints of the cell it came from."}],"why":"The passage moves from an unexplained pattern to a memory system to a laboratory instrument, and closes in paragraph 16 by calling that instrument borrowed and still bound by the cell it came from.","distractorNotes":["Vector capacity is one obstacle named in paragraph 15, not the claim the passage is built to make.","True, and drawn from paragraphs 1 and 2, but far too narrow to carry sixteen paragraphs.",null,"The passage compares the two kinds of defense without ranking bacteria above animals.","Paragraph 10 does call the motif the hinge of the system, but one mechanism is not the point of the whole piece."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 5 is included mainly in order to","choices":["establish that bacteria are attacked by plasmids as well as by viruses","explain how restriction enzymes pick out the sequences they cut","argue that restriction enzymes protect a cell less effectively than arrays do","introduce the three stages of the cycle that the paragraph immediately after it goes on to list","show what sets the array system apart from the defense bacteria were already known to have"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"That last property is what made the result startling."},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"That defense is blunt and fixed"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"The arrays described something else."}],"why":"Paragraph 5 sets a blunt, fixed, inherited defense against a specific memory of a specific attacker, and that contrast is what makes the earlier result startling.","distractorNotes":["That the invaders include both phages and plasmids is already established back in paragraph 3.","Recognition gets one clause there, as setup for the contrast rather than as the point of the paragraph.","The paragraph draws a difference in kind, blunt and fixed against specific and remembered, and never ranks the two.","The three stages arrive in paragraph 6, and paragraph 5 does not preview them.",null]},{"id":"rc-p11-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes the way the passage is organized?","choices":["A laboratory technique is described first, then traced back to the organism it came from, then set against the rival techniques it displaced","A controversy is introduced, the evidence on both sides is weighed, and one side is finally endorsed","A series of experiments is presented in the order performed, each one correcting an error made in the one before it","An unexplained observation is reported, its function is worked out, its mechanism is detailed, and its move into the laboratory is assessed","A general principle is stated at the outset and then illustrated with examples of steadily increasing difficulty"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":1,"quote":"found something nobody could explain"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"A ledger implies a use, and within two years the use was demonstrated."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"The cycle has three stages"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"The step that turned a defense system into an instrument"}],"why":"The passage opens on a puzzle in paragraph 1, settles what the arrays are for by paragraph 4, spends paragraphs 6 through 10 on mechanism, and then weighs the laboratory version in paragraphs 11 through 16.","distractorNotes":["The passage runs the other way, from the bacterial observation forward to the technique, and rival tools get a single sentence in paragraph 12.","The author does push back on a popular metaphor in paragraph 11, but no two sided controversy is set up and weighed.","Experiments appear early, and the later work builds on the earlier work rather than correcting it.",null,"The opening offers a puzzle rather than a principle, and nothing is laid down as a rule to be illustrated."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the habit of comparing the protein to a pair of scissors is best described as","choices":["impatient with it, because it drops the part that settles the result","neutral, on the grounds that the comparison is accurate as far as it goes","alarmed by it, as a claim that has pushed researchers into unsafe work","amused by a figure of speech that no specialist takes seriously","enthusiastic about a phrase that makes a hard idea accessible to readers"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"the metaphor does more harm than good"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"skips the half that settles the outcome"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says the metaphor does more harm than good and ends by charging it with describing the easy half of the job and skipping the half that settles the outcome.","distractorNotes":[null,"Calling the image harmful is the opposite of neutral.","Too strong: the complaint is that the image is incomplete, and no researcher is accused of unsafe work.","Nothing in paragraph 11 is playful, and the author treats the metaphor as a live problem.","The author presents the phrase as a source of confusion, not as a helpful simplification."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In describing the measures taken against cutting at unintended sites, the author is best described as","choices":["dismissive, treating the measures as amounting to very little in practice","resigned, treating further progress on the problem as unlikely","puzzled, treating the measures as working for reasons nobody has established","candid, treating the measures as genuine and the problem as still open","confident, treating the problem as one the measures have brought under control"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"The countermeasures are real and partial."},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":6,"quote":"remains a measurement problem of its own"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 calls the countermeasures real and partial in the same breath, lists three of them, and then notes that finding rare off target events is still a measurement problem of its own.","distractorNotes":["The author calls the countermeasures real and describes three of them without belittling any.","The author leaves the problem open rather than predicting that it will not improve.","Paragraph 14 explains exactly why each measure works, so nothing there is presented as mysterious.",null,"The word partial, and the unsolved detection problem at the end, rule out any sense of control."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports each of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Cutting both strands of the helix is not required in order to change a single base.","A guide that matches its target perfectly will cut that sequence wherever it occurs.","The order of the spacers in an array carries information about when each threat was met.","Some viruses that infect bacteria can shut the system down.","The same protein can be aimed at a new target without altering the protein itself."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"the array accumulates chronologically with the most recent threat at the front","choice":2},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"Retargeting the newer system means ordering a different short strand of RNA.","choice":4},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"editors that nick one strand and chemically convert a single base","choice":0},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"Phages already carry proteins that shut the system down","choice":3},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"only sequences sitting next to the right two letters can be attacked","choice":1}],"why":"Paragraph 10 makes a perfect match insufficient on its own: only sequences sitting next to the motif can be attacked, so a flawless guide is stopped wherever the motif is missing.","distractorNotes":["Supported, so not the exception: paragraph 13 describes editors that nick one strand and convert a base.",null,"Supported, so not the exception: paragraph 7 has new spacers enter at the leader end, making the array chronological.","Supported, so not the exception: paragraph 16 notes that phages carry proteins that shut the system down.","Supported, so not the exception: paragraph 12 says retargeting means ordering a different short strand of RNA."]},{"id":"rc-p11-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for an explanation of why the repeats stay evenly spaced as the record grows should turn to","choices":["paragraph 13","paragraph 7","paragraph 1","paragraph 2","paragraph 9"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"which is why the alternating pattern survives every time the record grows"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 supplies the reason: each insertion also duplicates one copy of the repeat, so the alternating pattern survives every time a spacer is added.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 13 concerns what a cell does after a break, a different subject altogether.",null,"Paragraph 1 describes the alternating pattern as it was first seen, without saying what maintains it.","Paragraph 2 reports how widespread the arrays are and what sits beside them.","Paragraph 9 is about finding and cutting a target, not about the structure of the array."]}]},"rc-p12":{"id":"rc-p12","title":"The Heart's Own Current and the Pacemaker","topic":"cardiac conduction and the artificial pacemaker","field":"physiology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1687,"paragraphs":[["A heart lifted out of the body, kept warm and oxygenated, goes on beating with no brain attached and nothing telling it when to start.","That observation contains the whole subject.","The signal behind each beat is manufactured inside the heart, by a small population of cells that behave nothing like the muscle around them.","Every cardiac device ever built stands in for that internal source."],["Ordinary working muscle cells are electrically patient.","Each holds a steady negative voltage and waits for a wave of charge to arrive from a neighbor and shove it past threshold.","Such cells make an excellent relay and a hopeless starter."],["Pacemaker cells never settle.","Their membranes are leaky in a particular way: the moment one reaches its most negative point, near minus sixty millivolts, channels swing open that admit a slow trickle of positive charge, and the cell starts climbing again.","Physiologists named this the funny current, since those channels opened on the way down rather than on the way up.","At roughly minus forty millivolts the cell fires, and its upstroke is carried mostly by calcium rather than by the fast sodium rush that drives working muscle.","Then it falls back and begins again."],["Several tissues do this at different speeds.","The sinoatrial node, a flattened strip about a centimeter long in the wall of the right atrium near the mouth of the superior vena cava, drifts to threshold sixty to one hundred times a minute.","The atrioventricular junction manages forty to sixty.","The Purkinje fibers in the ventricular walls are slower still, twenty to forty.","Since all of them are excitable, the fastest wins: its wave resets the others one step short of their own finish.","The node leads by speed alone, not by rank, and the slower sites wait underneath as backups."],["The wave leaving the node spreads across both atria like a ripple in a shallow dish, then meets a wall.","A ring of fibrous tissue separates atria from ventricles and carries no current.","One door leads through it, the atrioventricular node, low in the right atrium.","Beyond that door the signal enters the bundle of His, splits into right and left bundle branches, and fans through the Purkinje network, which hands it to ventricular muscle near the apex.","The ventricles therefore contract from the bottom upward, wringing blood toward the great vessels."],["At the door the signal slows.","Conduction through the atrioventricular node takes about a tenth of a second, glacial next to the Purkinje fibers, and the slowness is built in.","Its cells are thin, they are joined by comparatively few of the protein channels that pass current from cell to cell, and their upstroke depends on calcium.","The pause buys the ventricles their last measure of filling.","Without it, the two floors of the pump step on each other."],["The delay does a second job that is easy to overlook.","It rations.","Flutter runs the atria at about three hundred impulses a minute, and fibrillation is faster still and carries no order at all.","The ventricles cannot follow either rhythm and survive, and nothing in the ventricles is equipped to refuse.","The node refuses on their behalf, passing only a fraction.","A narrow, slow, easily exhausted passage is poor design for speed and excellent design for protection.","The most conspicuous bottleneck in the system is also its finest safety feature."],["When conduction along that route falters, clinicians call it a block, and the word misleads anyone who hears it as a plug in a pipe.","Nothing is obstructed.","A block is a failure of the signal to arrive, late or not at all, at tissue perfectly able to contract the moment it gets the news.","The grades matter, because they are separate problems sharing a name."],["In first degree block every impulse gets through, only late: the interval from the start of atrial activity to the start of ventricular activity stretches past two hundred milliseconds.","In the second degree pattern known as Wenckebach, that interval lengthens a little more with each beat until one impulse fails and a beat is dropped.","Both usually sit in the node itself, both turn up in healthy people, particularly in well trained athletes at rest and in almost anyone asleep, and neither alone is a reason to do anything.","It is tempting to read every irregular tracing as a device waiting to be implanted.","Most blocks are a finding, not a disease."],["The dangerous ones look different.","In the second degree pattern called Mobitz type II, beats drop without warning and without any preceding stretch, which places the fault below the node, in the His and Purkinje wiring, where tissue tends to fail further.","In complete block nothing crosses.","The atria carry on at their own pace while the ventricles fall back on whatever escape rhythm they can raise, often only twenty to forty beats a minute, prone to pausing long enough that the person faints where they stand.","That is the problem the artificial pacemaker was built to solve."],["The idea is older than any technology able to carry it out decently.","Investigators in the late nineteenth century had driven an animal heart at any rate they chose, and by the early 1930s a physician had assembled a hand cranked device with a needle electrode that he called an artificial pacemaker.","Practical machines arrived in the 1950s, and they worked, which was the remarkable part.","They were also brutal.","Pacing ran through electrodes laid on the chest wall, so the current had to cross skin and muscle, which meant burns and pain severe enough that patients were sedated.","The units were the size of a cabinet on wheels and ran on wall current."],["Wall current was the flaw that broke the design open.","In the autumn of 1957 a power failure in Minneapolis cut electricity to a hospital where children with surgically injured conduction systems were being paced, and a surgeon there asked a local repairman who looked after the hospital equipment to build something independent of the grid.","The answer, produced in about four weeks and adapted from a published circuit for a transistorized metronome, was a battery powered box small enough to wear, wired through the chest wall to the heart."],["Getting the whole apparatus inside took two more years.","The first fully implanted unit went into a patient in Stockholm in 1958.","It failed after a few hours and a second failed after days, though the recipient went on to receive more than two dozen devices and outlived both the engineer and the surgeon.","American implants followed in 1960.","The limit was now chemical.","Those units ran on zinc and mercuric oxide cells that gave twelve to twenty four months of service and vented hydrogen gas as they discharged, so the case could not be sealed.","Body fluid crept in and shorted the circuits."],["Two answers were tried.","One was nuclear: a pellet of plutonium warm from its own decay driving a thermoelectric converter, which lasted decades but carried a regulatory burden nobody wanted.","The other, introduced in 1972, was the lithium iodine cell, which used a solid electrolyte, produced no gas at all, and could therefore be sealed inside a welded titanium can.","Service stretched to seven or ten years, and a sealed can shielded the circuit permanently.","Within a decade the implanted unit could sense the patient's own beats and hold its pulse when one arrived, be reprogrammed through the skin, and report on its own condition."],["Here the story takes an unexpected turn.","The generator, the part that looked hard, became the reliable part: batteries now announce their decline months ahead.","The stubborn problem turned out to be the lead, the insulated wire running from the can through a vein into the heart, the one component that has never stopped failing.","A lead must flex with every heartbeat, on the order of one hundred thousand times a day and forty million times a year, anchored at one end to a device that does not move and at the other to a muscle that does.","Conductors fracture, classically where the lead is pinched between the collarbone and the first rib, and insulation cracks and lets current escape."],["The tip has troubles of its own.","A pacing pulse counts only if it is strong enough to capture, meaning that it actually provokes a contraction rather than fading at the electrode, and the energy needed is not fixed.","Scar tissue forms around a fresh tip over the first weeks and drives the requirement up, sometimes far enough that a lead working at implant fails a month later.","The fixes were small and elegant.","Tips shrank to a few square millimeters, concentrating the same current into a higher density, and the metal was made to release a steroid slowly into the surrounding tissue.","Lower capture energy meant smaller pulses, and smaller pulses meant a longer battery."],["Taking an old lead out is harder than putting a new one in.","The tissue reaction that anchors the tip also binds the lead to the vein wall and to the valve it passes, and after a few years the wire is no longer a separate object in the body.","Locking stylets and cutting sheaths exist for the job, but the vein the lead has fused to tears easily.","This is much of why self contained capsules, with no wire leaving the heart, have drawn such interest, and equally why enthusiasm for them has stayed measured rather than triumphant.","A device with no lead cannot suffer a lead fracture.","It will still have to come out someday."],["Meanwhile the devices have been learning the lesson the node taught first.","A dual chamber pacemaker senses activity in the atrium, waits, and then paces the ventricle, and the interval it waits is programmable, usually between about one hundred twenty and two hundred milliseconds.","Engineers spent five decades building a machine that could reproduce, in titanium and software, the tenth of a second that a knot of unremarkable tissue on the floor of the right atrium has been giving away for nothing."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p12-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the Purkinje fibers in the ventricular walls reach threshold on their own at a rate of about","choices":["sixty to one hundred times a minute","one hundred twenty to two hundred times a minute","forty to sixty times a minute","twenty to forty times a minute","three hundred times a minute"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"The Purkinje fibers in the ventricular walls are slower still, twenty to forty."}],"why":"Paragraph 4 lists the pacemaker sites in order from fastest to slowest, and the Purkinje fibers come last at twenty to forty times a minute.","distractorNotes":["That is the sinoatrial node's rate, given first in the same list.","Those numbers are the programmable waiting interval in paragraph 18, which is measured in milliseconds rather than beats.","That is the rate of the atrioventricular junction, the middle member of the list.",null,"Three hundred a minute is the atrial rate in flutter from paragraph 7, not any pacemaker site's own pace."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage explains that conduction through the atrioventricular node is slow partly because","choices":["its cells are joined by comparatively few of the channels that pass current between cells","its cells cannot reach threshold on their own and must wait to be driven from above","a ring of fibrous tissue runs through the middle of the node and blocks part of the current","its cells depend on the same fast inward sodium rush that drives ordinary working muscle","the wave has to cross the whole of both atria before any part of it arrives there"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"joined by comparatively few of the protein channels that pass current from cell to cell"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 gives three built in reasons for the delay: the cells are thin, they are joined by comparatively few of the channels that pass current from cell to cell, and their upstroke depends on calcium.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 4 says the atrioventricular junction reaches threshold on its own at forty to sixty a minute, so this reverses the passage.","The fibrous ring in paragraph 5 surrounds the route and leaves one door through it, and the passage never places it inside the node.","Paragraph 6 says the opposite, that the upstroke there depends on calcium rather than on the fast sodium rush.","That spread across the atria is described in paragraph 5, but it happens before the node and is not offered as a reason the node itself is slow."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the zinc and mercuric oxide cells in the earliest implanted units created a design problem because they","choices":["could not deliver enough current to trigger a contraction of the chamber","gave off a gas as they discharged, so the case could not be sealed","lost all of their remaining capacity suddenly and with no warning at all","needed a thermoelectric converter to turn their heat into usable current","could only be recharged by a coil held against the skin for hours"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":6,"quote":"vented hydrogen gas as they discharged, so the case could not be sealed"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 says those cells vented hydrogen gas as they discharged, which is why the case could not be sealed. Body fluid then crept in and shorted the circuits.","distractorNotes":["Capture and the energy a pulse needs come up in paragraph 16, and the passage never blames the early cells for weak output.",null,"Paragraph 13 describes those cells as short lived, twelve to twenty four months, rather than abrupt, and paragraph 15 says modern batteries give months of notice.","The thermoelectric converter belongs to the nuclear unit in paragraph 14, which was a different answer to the same problem.","Recharging never appears in the passage. Paragraph 14 mentions reprogramming through the skin, which is a different thing entirely."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage states that a pacing lead is flexed roughly","choices":["one thousand times a day","one million times a day","forty million times a day","one hundred thousand times a year","one hundred thousand times a day"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"on the order of one hundred thousand times a day and forty million times a year"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 gives both figures in one sentence, about one hundred thousand flexes a day and forty million a year. The daily figure is the one asked for here.","distractorNotes":["That is far below the number given, and the passage ties the flexing to every single heartbeat.","No such figure appears, and a million beats a day is far beyond any rate the passage describes.","Forty million is the yearly figure from that same sentence, not the daily one.","This swaps the two units. One hundred thousand is the daily count in paragraph 15.",null]},{"id":"rc-p12-q05","type":"detail","stem":"Which two changes to the electrode tip does the passage credit with lowering the energy a pulse needs?","choices":["a welded titanium can and a solid electrolyte sealed inside it","a locking stylet and a sheath that cuts a path along the body of the lead","a larger tip surface and a stiffer, thicker conductor running to it","a much smaller tip and the slow release of a steroid into nearby tissue","an atrial sensor and a waiting interval the operator can program"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"Tips shrank to a few square millimeters"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"the metal was made to release a steroid slowly into the surrounding tissue"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 names both fixes: tips shrank to a few square millimeters, which concentrates the same current into a higher density, and the metal was made to release a steroid slowly into the surrounding tissue.","distractorNotes":["Those belong to the battery and its case in paragraph 14, which is a different part of the device.","Those are the lead extraction tools from paragraph 17 and have nothing to do with the energy a pulse needs.","Paragraph 16 says the tips got smaller rather than larger, precisely so the same current is concentrated into a higher density.",null,"That describes the dual chamber timing in paragraph 18 rather than anything done to the tip."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 3, \"leaky\" most nearly means","choices":["damaged in a way that allows charge to escape","unable to hold any voltage across the membrane at all","joined to neighboring cells by channels that stay open","steadily admitting a small inward current","liable to tear open under mechanical pressure"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"channels swing open that admit a slow trickle of positive charge"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 uses the word for a membrane that admits a slow trickle of positive charge as soon as the cell reaches its most negative point. It is a normal working feature of these cells, not damage.","distractorNotes":["The everyday sense of a leak is something escaping through a flaw, but in paragraph 3 the charge moves inward and nothing is broken.","The cell does hold a voltage. It simply will not hold still at one value, since it climbs toward threshold instead.","Channels between neighboring cells appear in paragraph 6, where their scarcity slows the node, and that is a separate idea.",null,"This borrows the plumbing sense of a leak. Nothing in paragraph 3 involves pressure or tearing."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 16, \"capture\" most nearly means","choices":["to record the chamber's electrical activity for later review","to hold the tip of the lead firmly against the chamber wall","to succeed in triggering a contraction","to soak up the energy of the pulse without responding to it","to take over pacing duty from the sinoatrial node permanently"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"meaning that it actually provokes a contraction rather than fading at the electrode"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 defines the word right where it uses it: a pulse captures when it actually provokes a contraction rather than fading at the electrode.","distractorNotes":["Recording is a fair everyday sense of the word, but the passage is describing a pulse the device sends out, not data it takes in.","Anchoring the tip is discussed in paragraph 17, where scar tissue binds the lead, and the word capture is never used for it.",null,"This is the opposite of the passage's meaning, since a pulse that fades at the electrode is exactly one that failed to capture.","Taking over from the sinoatrial node describes an escape rhythm in paragraph 10, while capture in paragraph 16 refers to a single pulse."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q08","type":"inference","stem":"Suppose the sinoatrial node in a particular heart slowed to about thirty impulses a minute. The passage most supports which expectation?","choices":["The ventricles would stop entirely until the node recovered its usual speed.","The atrioventricular junction would be expected to set the rate instead.","The fibrous ring would begin to carry current from the atria downward.","The Purkinje fibers would speed up to about sixty impulses a minute.","The heart would by definition be in complete block from that moment on."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"Since all of them are excitable, the fastest wins"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"The atrioventricular junction manages forty to sixty."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":6,"quote":"the slower sites wait underneath as backups"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says the fastest site wins and the slower sites wait underneath as backups. At thirty a minute the node is no longer fastest, so the junction, listed at forty to sixty, would reach threshold first.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 describes the slower sites as backups, which is exactly why a slowed node does not stop the heart.",null,"Paragraph 5 says the fibrous ring carries no current, and nothing in the passage suggests that ever changes.","The rates in paragraph 4 are each site's own pace, and nothing lets a slower site run faster than its own.","Paragraph 10 defines complete block as nothing crossing from atria to ventricles, which is a failure to conduct rather than a slow node."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q09","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly supports which statement about a person whose atria are firing about three hundred times a minute?","choices":["The Purkinje network will turn away the extra impulses on its own.","The ventricles will follow every one of those impulses unless the atria are stopped.","The sinoatrial node will take control back as soon as the fibrous ring conducts.","Any degree of block at the node would make the situation more dangerous.","The ventricles are spared largely because the node passes only a fraction of them."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":5,"quote":"The node refuses on their behalf, passing only a fraction."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"nothing in the ventricles is equipped to refuse"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says the ventricles cannot follow either of those atrial rhythms and survive, and that nothing in them is equipped to refuse, so the node refuses on their behalf and passes only a fraction.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 7 says nothing in the ventricles is equipped to refuse, which is why the node has to do the refusing.","This ignores the rationing in paragraph 7, which is precisely what keeps the ventricles from following the atria.","Paragraph 5 says the fibrous ring carries no current, and paragraph 7 gives the node no role in restoring a normal rhythm.","Paragraph 7 treats the node's habit of holding impulses back as protective here, so slowing at the node is the opposite of dangerous.",null]},{"id":"rc-p12-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["Better battery chemistry is the single main reason implanted pacemakers eventually became practical devices.","Most conduction abnormalities that turn up on a tracing are harmless findings that call for no treatment of any kind.","The heart's own signal proved hard to imitate, and the effort to replace it kept relocating the hardest problem.","The atrioventricular node is the most fragile structure in the heart and the usual site of serious disease.","Self contained capsules placed inside the heart have now resolved the problems that every earlier design could not."],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"Every cardiac device ever built stands in for that internal source."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"The stubborn problem turned out to be the lead"},{"paragraph":18,"sentence":3,"quote":"Engineers spent five decades building a machine that could reproduce"}],"why":"The passage runs from how the heart makes and routes its own signal, through what happens when that routing fails, to the devices built to stand in, and it ends with the hard problem moving from the power source to the wire.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 14 does credit the lithium iodine cell, but paragraph 15 says the battery stopped being the hard part, so this covers only one stretch of the story.","Paragraph 9 makes that point about first degree block and Wenckebach, and paragraph 10 turns immediately to the blocks that do need a device.",null,"Paragraph 7 treats the node's narrowness as a safety feature, and the passage never calls it the most fragile structure or the usual site of disease.","Paragraph 17 says enthusiasm for those capsules has stayed measured, and that a capsule will still have to come out someday."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 7 is included mainly in order to","choices":["show that a feature that looks like a weakness is also the system's best protection","introduce the grading of blocks that the next two paragraphs go on to lay out in detail","explain why a disorganized atrial rhythm has to be treated as a medical emergency","argue that the heart would be better served by a faster route through the fibrous ring","prepare the reader for the external devices of the 1950s and the trouble they caused"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":7,"quote":"The most conspicuous bottleneck in the system is also its finest safety feature."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":6,"quote":"poor design for speed and excellent design for protection"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 explains the delay in terms of filling. Paragraph 7 adds the second job, rationing, and closes by saying the most conspicuous bottleneck in the system is also its finest safety feature.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 8 does that work, and paragraph 7 is still describing the normal node rather than any block.","Flutter and fibrillation are mentioned only to show what the node protects against, and treatment of them never comes up.","This inverts the paragraph, which praises the narrow slow passage instead of wishing it were faster.","Those devices arrive in paragraph 11, and paragraph 7 gives no hint of the history that follows."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["It alternates between individual patient cases and the laboratory findings that stand behind each of them.","It lays out a natural system, how it fails, the devices built to replace it, and where the difficulty settled.","It states an argument at the very start and then spends the rest of its length answering objections raised to it.","It follows one strict chronology from the nineteenth century forward to the present day without any digression.","It compares two competing devices point by point and then recommends one of them over the other."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"The signal behind each beat is manufactured inside the heart"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"When conduction along that route falters, clinicians call it a block"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"That is the problem the artificial pacemaker was built to solve."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"Here the story takes an unexpected turn."}],"why":"The first half builds the natural conduction system and the blocks that interrupt it, the middle traces the devices built to replace it, and paragraphs 15 through 17 show the hard problem moving from the generator to the lead.","distractorNotes":["No individual patient cases are presented anywhere in the passage.",null,"The passage is explanatory rather than argumentative, and no objection is ever raised and then answered.","The history in paragraphs 11 through 14 is chronological, but the first ten paragraphs are not a history at all.","Only one kind of device is traced here, and the passage recommends nothing to anyone."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's treatment of the external pacing machines of the 1950s is best described as","choices":["admiring of how elegant and comfortable they had become by then","dismissive of the physicians who were willing to use them on patients","candid about how crude they were while granting that they worked","neutral throughout, offering no evaluation of them in either direction","regretful that anyone attempted such a thing before better tools existed"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"they worked, which was the remarkable part"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"They were also brutal."}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says the machines worked and calls that the remarkable part, then in the next breath calls them brutal and describes burns, sedation, and a cabinet on wheels.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph describes burns, pain, sedation and a unit the size of a cabinet, none of which reads as elegant.","No criticism is aimed at the people using the machines. The judgment falls on the machines themselves.",null,"Words like brutal and remarkable are plain evaluations, so the paragraph is anything but neutral.","The passage treats these machines as a real step forward, and paragraph 12 builds directly on them."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward self contained capsules placed inside the heart is best described as","choices":["confident that they remove the last serious weakness left in the system","dismissive of them as a novelty with no real advantage to offer anyone","openly doubtful that they can pace a chamber reliably at all","interested while holding back full endorsement","worried that they will make lead extraction a far more common operation"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":17,"sentence":4,"quote":"enthusiasm for them has stayed measured rather than triumphant"}],"why":"Paragraph 17 explains why the capsules are attractive, since a device with no lead cannot suffer a lead fracture, and then notes that enthusiasm has stayed measured rather than triumphant and that the capsule will still have to come out someday.","distractorNotes":["The closing lines of paragraph 17 pull back from exactly that confidence by raising the question of retrieval.","The paragraph says the capsules have drawn such interest, which is not dismissal, and it names a genuine advantage.","Pacing reliability is never questioned. The reservation on offer is about getting the device out later.",null,"The passage suggests the opposite, since a capsule has no lead at all to be extracted."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports each of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["The sinoatrial node leads because it reaches threshold first, not because of any special standing.","A dropped beat can show up in a person with a perfectly healthy heart.","A ventricular escape rhythm is as steady and dependable as the node it replaces.","A cell with a solid electrolyte made a fully sealed pacemaker case possible.","A pacing lead has to bend with every beat for as long as it stays in place."],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":6,"quote":"The node leads by speed alone, not by rank","choice":0},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"one impulse fails and a beat is dropped","choice":1},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"both turn up in healthy people","choice":1},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"could therefore be sealed inside a welded titanium can","choice":3},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"A lead must flex with every heartbeat","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"prone to pausing long enough that the person faints where they stand","choice":2}],"why":"Paragraph 10 calls the escape rhythm slow, often only twenty to forty a minute, and prone to pausing long enough that the person faints, which is the opposite of dependable. The other four statements come from paragraphs 4, 9, 14 and 15.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 says the node leads by speed alone and not by rank, so this one is supported.","Paragraph 9 says the Wenckebach pattern, in which a beat is dropped, turns up in healthy people, so this one is supported.",null,"Paragraph 14 credits the solid electrolyte of the lithium iodine cell with allowing a welded titanium can, so this one is supported.","Paragraph 15 gives the flexing figures for a lead, about one hundred thousand times a day, so this one is supported."]},{"id":"rc-p12-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wants to know why the ventricles begin contracting near the bottom of the heart rather than at the top should look in","choices":["paragraph 3","paragraph 5","paragraph 7","paragraph 10","paragraph 16"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"hands it to ventricular muscle near the apex"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"The ventricles therefore contract from the bottom upward"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 traces the signal past the bundle of His and through the Purkinje network to ventricular muscle near the apex, which is why the squeeze runs from the bottom upward.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 is about how a single pacemaker cell drifts to threshold, not about where the wave travels.",null,"Paragraph 7 is about the node holding back a flood of atrial impulses.","Paragraph 10 covers the dangerous blocks and the escape rhythms that follow them.","Paragraph 16 is about the electrode tip and the energy a pulse needs."]}]},"rc-p13":{"id":"rc-p13","title":"The Bond That Made Nitrogen Scarce","topic":"nitrogen fixation and industrial ammonia synthesis","field":"chemistry","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1692,"paragraphs":[["Roughly seventy eight percent of every breath a person takes is nitrogen, and yet nitrogen is the nutrient that most often decides how much a field can yield.","A plant can stand in an ocean of the element it needs and still go hungry for it.","Sorting that out took chemistry most of a century, and the process that finally managed it now ranks among the largest industrial consumers of energy on the planet.","The line from one chemical bond to a coastal dead zone is shorter than it looks."],["Begin with the bond.","Two nitrogen atoms in the air are held by a triple bond, three shared pairs of electrons, and pulling that bond apart costs roughly nine hundred and forty five kilojoules per mole.","Few bonds in ordinary chemistry are stronger.","The molecule is also symmetrical from end to end, with no partially positive or partially negative region where a reagent could get a grip.","Nitrogen gas is therefore not unreactive because reacting is impossible.","Most of its reactions release energy overall.","It is unreactive because the first step, breaking that bond, sits behind a wall almost nothing at ordinary temperatures can climb."],["That distinction, between a reaction that cannot happen and a reaction that will not start, runs through the whole subject.","Lightning supplies enough local energy to force the issue, tearing nitrogen and oxygen apart and letting them recombine into oxides that rain out as nitrate.","Measured against what the world's crops pull out of the soil each season, that whole natural supply is a rounding error."],["The route that mattered for most of life's history is biological.","A scattered set of bacteria and archaea, called diazotrophs, carry an enzyme named nitrogenase that does at ordinary temperature and pressure what industry still cannot do without extreme conditions.","At its center sits a cluster of iron and molybdenum atoms that grips the nitrogen molecule and feeds electrons into it one at a time, weakening the bond in stages rather than snapping it in one blow.","The accounting is brutal.","Reducing one molecule of nitrogen gas to two molecules of ammonia consumes sixteen molecules of ATP, the cell's energy currency."],["Which sets up the central difficulty of biological fixation.","Sixteen ATP is an enormous bill, and the cheapest way any cell has to pay it is aerobic respiration, which requires oxygen.","Yet oxygen poisons nitrogenase.","Not in the mild, reversible way a competing molecule slows an enzyme and then lets go: oxygen attacks the exposed metal cluster and wrecks it, and the cell has to build a replacement from scratch.","A fixing organism therefore needs oxygen present in one compartment and absent in another, a few micrometers apart.","Everything interesting about the biology follows from that one conflict."],["Different lineages settled it differently.","Filamentous cyanobacteria build heterocysts, thick walled cells that slow gas entry and have the oxygen producing half of their photosynthetic machinery dismantled, so the fixing cell makes none of the gas that would destroy its enzyme.","Legumes, the family that includes soybeans, peas and clover, strike a bargain with soil bacteria instead.","The plant grows a nodule on its root, houses the bacteria inside, and floods it with leghemoglobin, an oxygen binding protein like the one that reddens blood.","Leghemoglobin does something more elegant than shutting oxygen out: it holds free oxygen in the nodule at a vanishingly low concentration while still ferrying a steady supply to the bacterial respiratory chain, so the bill gets paid and the enzyme survives."],["Farmers exploited the arrangement for thousands of years with no idea it existed.","Rotating a cereal with a legume, or plowing a stand of clover back into the ground, restored something to a tired field.","The name for the practice arrived long before the name for the mechanism, and for most of agricultural history the usable nitrogen supply was capped by whatever those bacteria, plus manure and a little lightning, happened to deliver."],["By the nineteenth century that cap was being felt.","European agriculture leaned on seabird guano shipped from the Peruvian coast and on nitrate mined in the Chilean desert, deposits that were rich, concentrated and finite.","In 1898 the president of the British Association for the Advancement of Science gave his annual address over to the arithmetic: wheat consumption was climbing, the nitrate beds would not last, and the shortfall would have to be met by pulling nitrogen out of the air.","Within fifteen years the chemists had answered him."],["Fritz Haber demonstrated the reaction on a laboratory bench in 1909, coaxing a slow drip of ammonia out of nitrogen and hydrogen passed over a heated catalyst.","Carl Bosch and his colleagues at a German chemical firm then spent four years turning that result into a factory, which meant inventing steel vessels that would not fail under sustained pressure and screening thousands of catalysts for one both active and affordable.","The first full scale works opened in 1913.","The modern version still runs near four hundred to five hundred degrees Celsius and one hundred and fifty to three hundred atmospheres, and it still uses iron."],["Those conditions are not arbitrary.","Combining nitrogen with hydrogen to make ammonia gives off heat, and it turns four molecules of gas into two.","Both facts argue for running the reaction cold and squeezed, since low temperature favors a heat releasing product and high pressure favors the side with fewer gas molecules.","The trouble is that a low temperature also means a bond nothing wants to break breaks even more slowly.","So the conditions are a deliberate compromise.","Engineers accept a hotter reactor than equilibrium would prefer, since a small share of a fast reaction beats a large share of one that never finishes, then raise the pressure to claw back some lost yield."],["The catalyst is the other half of the bargain, and it acts on the rate alone.","It cannot move where equilibrium lies; it only lowers the barrier to getting there.","An iron surface pulls the nitrogen molecule down flat and splits it into separate atoms held on the metal, after which hydrogen adds one atom at a time.","The working material is iron promoted with small quantities of potassium and aluminum oxides, additives that carry out no reaction themselves but keep the iron surface open and lift its activity.","Even so, one pass converts only ten to twenty percent of the feed, so the unreacted gas is cooled, stripped of its ammonia, and sent around again."],["The consequences were not subtle.","Nitrogen stopped being something a farm had to grow or a company had to mine and became something a factory could make from air, water and fuel.","World population stood near one and a half billion in 1900 and is near eight billion now, and by common estimate close to half the nitrogen in the protein of a person alive today entered the food chain through an ammonia reactor rather than a root nodule."],["It is tempting to stop there and file the process under unambiguous triumph, and that reading is too clean.","The trouble is not the reaction, which is remarkably good at what it does.","The trouble is everything downstream of the reactor.","Across the world's farms, well under half of the nitrogen applied to a field ends up inside the harvested crop.","The rest is lost, and those losses are not a rounding error.","A technology can be an extraordinary piece of chemistry and a careless piece of plumbing at once, and the honest description of synthetic nitrogen is that it is both."],["The leaking has a chemical reason.","Soil particles carry a net negative charge, so positively charged ammonium is held in place by plain attraction.","Soil bacteria, though, convert ammonium to nitrate within days, and nitrate is negative.","The soil repels it, and the next heavy rain carries it past the roots into groundwater or sideways into a ditch and a river.","Some of the surplus leaves as nitrous oxide instead, a gas that traps roughly three hundred times as much heat per molecule as carbon dioxide and that has become a leading threat to the ozone layer."],["What the nitrate does at the coast follows a sequence.","River water arrives loaded with nutrients and fertilizes a bloom of algae at the surface.","The algae grow, they die, and they sink.","Bacteria near the bottom decompose the falling material and consume dissolved oxygen as they work.","Meanwhile the warm fresh river water floats on colder, saltier seawater in a layer that resists mixing, so oxygen from the surface cannot get down to replace what the bacteria are using.","The bottom water goes hypoxic.","Fish that can swim leave; crabs, clams and worms that cannot, die.","Each summer the hypoxic zone at the mouth of the Mississippi covers several thousand square miles."],["The energy side of the ledger is just as awkward.","The hydrogen for ammonia is stripped out of natural gas with steam, so most of the world's fertilizer begins as fossil carbon and releases carbon dioxide before it ever reaches a field.","Ammonia synthesis accounts for somewhere between one and two percent of global energy use and a comparable share of industrial carbon emissions.","That is a large bill for one reaction, and not one that can simply be refused, because the alternative to the fertilizer is not a cleaner fertilizer but a great deal less food."],["Which is why the promising work aims at the losses, not the reaction.","Applying fertilizer in several small doses timed to the crop's demand, instead of one heavy dose in spring, leaves less nitrate sitting in wet soil with nothing to absorb it.","Compounds that slow the bacterial conversion of ammonium to nitrate buy the roots more time.","Cover crops take up leftover nitrogen through the winter, and legume rotations supply part of the need biologically.","Making the hydrogen by splitting water with renewable electricity would address the fuel side without touching the chemistry.","None of it repeals the triple bond.","That bond is why nitrogen was scarce and why the remedy is expensive, and that much has not changed since 1909."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p13-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to paragraph 2, nitrogen gas in the air resists reacting mainly because","choices":["its reactions with other elements take in more energy than they give off","breaking the bond between its two atoms demands an enormous first push","one end of the molecule is partially negative and pushes reagents away","it is far too dilute in the atmosphere for reactions to get started","its two atoms share two pairs of electrons instead of three"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":7,"quote":"breaking that bond, sits behind a wall almost nothing at ordinary temperatures can climb"},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"Most of its reactions release energy overall."},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"no partially positive or partially negative region"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 makes the point directly. Most nitrogen reactions release energy overall, so the block is not that reacting is impossible. The block is the first step, breaking a triple bond that costs roughly nine hundred forty five kilojoules per mole.","distractorNotes":["The passage says the opposite, that most of nitrogen's reactions release energy overall.",null,"Paragraph 2 calls the molecule symmetrical, with no partially positive or partially negative region at all.","Nitrogen is about seventy eight percent of the air, so being too dilute is not the problem.","A triple bond is three shared pairs rather than two, and that third pair is the whole difficulty."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q02","type":"detail","stem":"Paragraph 4 states that turning one molecule of nitrogen gas into ammonia costs the cell","choices":["two molecules of ATP, one for each ammonia made","eight molecules of ATP","sixteen molecules of ATP, but only in cells that lack oxygen","sixteen molecules of ATP","an amount of energy the passage treats as negligible"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"consumes sixteen molecules of ATP"}],"why":"This is a straight number check against paragraph 4. Reducing one nitrogen molecule to two ammonia molecules runs the cell sixteen ATP, which is why the passage calls the accounting brutal.","distractorNotes":["The passage never ties the ATP count to the number of ammonia molecules made, and the real figure is far higher.","Eight is not the number given, and it would make the cost sound modest rather than brutal.","The number is right, but the passage attaches no oxygen condition to it; the oxygen problem arrives in the next paragraph.",null,"Paragraph 4 calls the accounting brutal, which is the opposite of negligible."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to paragraph 6, leghemoglobin serves the root nodule by","choices":["sealing the nodule so that no oxygen at all can get in","thickening the nodule wall so that gases enter slowly","holding free oxygen very low while still feeding oxygen to the bacteria","converting ammonium into nitrate that the plant can absorb","supplying the electrons that nitrogenase pushes into the nitrogen molecule"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"holds free oxygen in the nodule at a vanishingly low concentration while still ferrying"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"more elegant than shutting oxygen out"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 draws the distinction for you. Leghemoglobin does something more elegant than shutting oxygen out: it keeps free oxygen vanishingly low in the nodule while still ferrying a steady supply to the bacteria so they can pay the ATP bill.","distractorNotes":["This is the reading the paragraph specifically rules out, since the bacteria still need oxygen to make their ATP.","That is the heterocyst solution used by cyanobacteria, not what leghemoglobin does.",null,"Soil bacteria make that conversion in paragraph 14, out in the field rather than inside the nodule.","Paragraph 6 gives leghemoglobin an oxygen handling job only, and says nothing about where the electrons come from."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q04","type":"detail","stem":"Paragraph 11 reports that one pass of the feed gas through the reactor","choices":["turns nearly all of the nitrogen and hydrogen into ammonia","converts only about ten to twenty percent of it","ruins the iron catalyst, which then has to be replaced","shifts the position of equilibrium toward more ammonia","removes any need for high pressure later on"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"one pass converts only ten to twenty percent of the feed"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 gives the figure at the end. A single pass converts only ten to twenty percent of the feed, which is why the leftover gas is cooled, stripped of its ammonia and sent around again.","distractorNotes":["If that were true there would be no reason to recycle the leftover gas, which the paragraph says is standard.",null,"The passage never says the catalyst is used up; it says the catalyst lowers the barrier to reaction.","Paragraph 11 states plainly that a catalyst cannot move where equilibrium lies, only how fast the reaction gets there.","High pressure is still part of the design in paragraph 10, and the low conversion is exactly why it is needed."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to paragraph 15, oxygen fails to reach the bottom water near the river mouth because","choices":["the algae at the surface use it up before any of it can sink","nitrate binds it chemically and pulls it out of solution","the bacteria near the bottom give off nitrous oxide in place of oxygen","wind stirs the surface layer downward only during winter","warm fresh water floats on colder salty water and resists mixing"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":5,"quote":"floats on colder, saltier seawater in a layer that resists mixing"}],"why":"Paragraph 15 puts the layering in the middle of the sequence. Warm fresh river water sits on colder, saltier seawater, and that boundary keeps surface oxygen from getting down to replace what the bacteria are using.","distractorNotes":["The algae fuel the problem by dying and sinking, and the oxygen is consumed by bacteria on the bottom.","Nitrate in this passage feeds the algal bloom, and no chemical binding of oxygen is described.","Nitrous oxide appears in paragraph 14 as a loss from farm soil, not as something released on the sea floor.","The passage never mentions wind or a winter cycle, so this comes from outside the text.",null]},{"id":"rc-p13-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 5, \"poisons\" most nearly means","choices":["makes unfit to eat","slows for a while and then lets go","damages beyond repair","coats with a trace impurity","makes unpleasant to the taste"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"oxygen attacks the exposed metal cluster and wrecks it"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"Not in the mild, reversible way"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 defines the word by contrast. Oxygen does not slow nitrogenase the way a competing molecule does and then release it; it wrecks the metal cluster so thoroughly that the cell has to build a new one.","distractorNotes":["That is the everyday sense of the word, and nothing in this sentence is being eaten.","The paragraph names that reversible behavior only in order to say this is not it.",null,"The passage describes real destruction of the metal cluster, not a film or a contaminant.","Taste plays no part here, since the subject is an enzyme being put out of action."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 11, \"promoted\" most nearly means","choices":["advertised to possible buyers","moved up to a higher rank","heated thoroughly before use","made more active by added substances","purified until no other element remains on the surface"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"keep the iron surface open and lift its activity"}],"why":"The sentence explains itself in paragraph 11. The iron carries small amounts of potassium and aluminum oxides, additives that run no reaction of their own but keep the surface open and lift the iron's activity.","distractorNotes":["That is the marketing sense of the word, which has no place in a description of a catalyst surface.","Rank belongs to people and jobs, while the paragraph is describing what additives do to a metal.","Heating is set by the operating temperature in paragraph 9, not by the word describing the additives.",null,"The additives are deliberately present, so the iron is not being cleaned of everything else."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly supports which conclusion about a fertilizer that stayed in the ammonium form longer than usual?","choices":["It would wash out of the soil more easily during a heavy rain","It would linger in the soil and reach the water less readily","It would stop soil bacteria from growing anywhere near the roots","It would remove the need to fertilize that field in later seasons","It would keep nitrous oxide from forming under any conditions"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"positively charged ammonium is held in place by plain attraction"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"the next heavy rain carries it past the roots into groundwater"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":3,"quote":"buy the roots more time"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 supplies both halves. Soil particles are negatively charged, so positive ammonium is held in place, while nitrate is negative and gets repelled and carried off. Ammonium that lingers is ammonium that stays put.","distractorNotes":["That is the behavior of nitrate, the negatively charged form the soil pushes away.",null,"Nothing in the passage says ammonium harms bacteria; paragraph 14 says bacteria convert it within days.","The passage never claims one application could carry a field through later seasons.","Paragraph 17 describes slowing that conversion, which is not the same as shutting off nitrous oxide entirely."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q09","type":"inference","stem":"It can be inferred from paragraph 16 that giving up synthetic fertilizer altogether would","choices":["leave the world with a great deal less food","have little effect on the food supply, since biology could take over at once","make the nitrogen in the air easier for crops to use directly","cut global energy use roughly in half","raise the share of applied nitrogen that crops take up"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"not a cleaner fertilizer but a great deal less food"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 closes on exactly this point. The bill for ammonia synthesis is large, but it cannot simply be refused, because the alternative to the fertilizer is not a cleaner fertilizer but a great deal less food.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 12 says close to half the nitrogen in human protein comes through the reactor, so the effect would be large.","Nothing about the triple bond changes, and paragraph 17 says none of the remedies repeal it.","The passage puts ammonia synthesis at one to two percent of global energy use, not half.","Uptake depends on how nitrate behaves in soil, and closing the factory would not change that chemistry."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Biological fixation in root nodules is more efficient than the industrial process and could replace it if farmers planted more legumes","Coastal dead zones are the single most serious environmental problem the world now faces","An unusually stable bond kept nitrogen scarce until industry learned to break it, a fix that feeds billions and leaks badly","Early twentieth century chemists solved a problem that farmers had already solved by rotating their fields","Ammonia synthesis should be shut down because its energy cost outweighs what it provides"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"The line from one chemical bond to a coastal dead zone is shorter"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"close to half the nitrogen in the protein of a person alive today"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":6,"quote":"an extraordinary piece of chemistry and a careless piece of plumbing at once"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":7,"quote":"That bond is why nitrogen was scarce and why the remedy is expensive"}],"why":"The passage runs from the strength of the triple bond, through the biological and industrial ways around it, to the food it produces and the nitrogen that escapes. Paragraph 13 states the balanced verdict: extraordinary chemistry, careless plumbing.","distractorNotes":["The passage never ranks the two for efficiency, and paragraph 16 says removing the factory would mean far less food.","Dead zones get one paragraph as a consequence, and no claim is made about their rank among world problems.",null,"Farmers raised the ceiling without removing it, since paragraph 7 says the supply stayed capped by what the bacteria delivered.","Paragraph 16 makes the opposite point, that this particular bill cannot simply be refused."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 6 is included mainly to","choices":["show two different ways organisms settle the conflict raised just before it","prove that legumes fix more nitrogen than cyanobacteria do","explain why farmers began rotating cereal crops with legumes in the first place","describe the color of a healthy root nodule","argue that biological fixation ought to replace industrial ammonia"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":6,"quote":"Everything interesting about the biology follows from that one conflict."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"Different lineages settled it differently."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"with no idea it existed"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 ends by saying everything interesting in the biology follows from one conflict, oxygen needed and oxygen fatal. Paragraph 6 then lays out two answers to it, the heterocyst and the nodule.","distractorNotes":[null,"The paragraph sets the two strategies side by side without comparing how much nitrogen either one fixes.","Crop rotation is taken up in the next paragraph, while paragraph 6 stays on the biology.","Nodule color is never mentioned, and a detail that small could not carry a paragraph.","The author never makes that argument, and paragraph 16 says the industrial supply cannot simply be given up."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes the way the passage is organized?","choices":["a list of the scientists who worked on nitrogen, in the order they worked","a comparison of two industrial processes that compete with each other today","a claim, a long objection raised against it, and a return to the claim unchanged","a series of regional case studies of how fertilizer is used","one chemical obstacle, its two answers, and the cost of the industrial one"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"Begin with the bond."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"The route that mattered for most of life's history is biological."},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"Within fifteen years the chemists had answered him."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"The trouble is everything downstream of the reactor."}],"why":"The passage opens on the triple bond in paragraph 2, works through biology in paragraphs 4 to 7 and industry in paragraphs 8 to 12, and spends paragraphs 13 to 17 on what the industrial answer costs.","distractorNotes":["Two chemists are named in passing, but the passage is built around a chemical problem rather than around people.","Only one industrial process is described, and nothing is set against it as a competitor.","The author does not return to the opening verdict unchanged, since paragraph 13 revises it on purpose.","No region is studied on its own, and the Mississippi appears once as an example of a general pattern.",null]},{"id":"rc-p13-q13","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 13, the author's attitude toward treating synthetic nitrogen as an unqualified success is best described as","choices":["fully in agreement with it","dismissive of the chemistry behind the process","alarmed enough to call for the process to be shut down","unwilling to accept it without naming what the process loses","indifferent to what happens to the nitrogen after it is spread"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":1,"quote":"that reading is too clean"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":6,"quote":"the honest description of synthetic nitrogen is that it is both"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"remarkably good at what it does"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 calls the triumphant reading too clean, then supplies the missing half, that well under half of applied nitrogen reaches the crop. The verdict at the end is both, not either.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph opens by calling that reading too clean, which is a refusal rather than agreement.","The reaction is called remarkably good at what it does, so the chemistry is not what draws criticism.","No shutdown is called for anywhere, and paragraph 16 says the fertilizer cannot simply be refused.",null,"Where the nitrogen goes afterward is the whole subject of the paragraph, so indifference does not fit."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude in paragraph 17 toward the measures described there is best characterized as","choices":["encouraged about the losses, clear that the chemistry is unchanged","confident that these steps will end coastal dead zones within a few years","doubtful that any of them is worth trying","irritated that farmers have not adopted them already","certain that they make the industrial process unnecessary"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":17,"sentence":1,"quote":"the promising work aims at the losses, not the reaction"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":6,"quote":"None of it repeals the triple bond."}],"why":"Paragraph 17 lists measures that all aim at the losses, which is a hopeful list, and then closes by saying none of it repeals the triple bond. Positive about the plumbing, honest about the chemistry.","distractorNotes":[null,"No timeline is offered, and the closing sentences hold back from promising a cure.","The paragraph calls this the promising work, which is the opposite of doubt.","Farmers are never faulted here, since the paragraph describes practices rather than blaming anyone.","Paragraph 16 says the fertilizer cannot simply be given up, so these measures work alongside the process."]},{"id":"rc-p13-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements about biological nitrogen fixation EXCEPT:","choices":["It runs at ordinary temperature and pressure.","It costs the cell a large amount of energy.","Oxygen can wreck the enzyme that carries it out.","Legumes shelter the bacteria that carry it out inside nodules grown on their roots.","It now supplies most of the nitrogen used by the world's crops."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"does at ordinary temperature and pressure","choice":0},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"consumes sixteen molecules of ATP","choice":1},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"oxygen attacks the exposed metal cluster and wrecks it","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"grows a nodule on its root, houses the bacteria inside","choice":3},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"through an ammonia reactor rather than a root nodule","choice":4},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":4,"quote":"legume rotations supply part of the need biologically","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraphs 4 through 7 support the other four statements. Paragraph 12 goes the other way on this one: by common estimate close to half the nitrogen in human protein now arrives through an ammonia reactor rather than a root nodule.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 says nitrogenase does at ordinary temperature and pressure what industry needs extreme conditions to do.","Paragraph 4 puts the cost at sixteen ATP for one nitrogen molecule and calls the accounting brutal.","Paragraph 5 says oxygen attacks the metal cluster and wrecks it, leaving the cell to build a replacement.","Paragraph 6 describes the legume nodule, with the bacteria housed inside it.",null]},{"id":"rc-p13-q16","type":"detail","stem":"In which paragraph would a reader find the source the hydrogen for ammonia is currently taken from?","choices":["paragraph 4","paragraph 9","paragraph 11","paragraph 16","paragraph 17"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"The hydrogen for ammonia is stripped out of natural gas with steam"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":5,"quote":"would address the fuel side"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 is where the fuel side is handled. The hydrogen is stripped out of natural gas with steam, which is why the fertilizer begins as fossil carbon.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 is about the enzyme nitrogenase, not about where industrial hydrogen comes from.","Paragraph 9 covers the history and the operating conditions, and mentions hydrogen only as a feed gas.","Paragraph 11 explains what the iron catalyst does, without saying where the hydrogen is obtained.",null,"Paragraph 17 offers splitting water as a possible future source, not the one in use now."]}]},"rc-p14":{"id":"rc-p14","title":"How Migrating Birds Find Their Way","topic":"avian migration and navigation","field":"animal behavior","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1696,"paragraphs":[["A blackpoll warbler weighs about twelve grams, less than four sheets of paper.","In autumn it leaves the spruce forest of eastern Canada, flies out over the Atlantic, and comes down three days later in northern South America.","Many of the birds making that crossing hatched a few months earlier.","No parent leads them, and they travel at night over water with nothing to steer by.","Most of them arrive.","The question is not whether such a bird navigates, but what it is reading while it does."],["The most informative early experiments did something blunt: they moved the birds.","An animal that merely holds an inherited heading for a set number of days should be unaffected by being carried sideways.","It will fly the same direction for the same time and finish somewhere new.","An animal that knows where the goal sits relative to itself should notice the displacement and turn to compensate."],["A large displacement study from the middle of the twentieth century did exactly this with European starlings.","Thousands were caught in the Netherlands during autumn passage, marked, carried several hundred kilometers to Switzerland, and released.","Starlings from that population normally winter in Britain and northern France, reached on a steady heading west and south.","The recoveries split cleanly by age.","Adults that had made the trip before turned and made for the usual wintering range.","The young birds, on their first migration, held the heading they had started with and were recovered in southern France and Spain, shifted by about the distance and direction they had been carried."],["That result has held up across species.","A bird on its first migration runs a short set of instructions: fly roughly this way, for roughly this long, then stop.","Heading and duration are inherited, and they suffice, because the route its ancestors flew ends somewhere a bird can survive.","After one full round trip the animal has something better.","It can be carried hundreds of kilometers off course and still correct, which means it has acquired an idea of where it is."],["That leaves the narrower question of how a bird points at all.","The oldest answer is the sun.","Using the sun as a compass sounds simple until one remembers that it moves.","Its bearing along the horizon shifts by roughly fifteen degrees an hour, so an animal treating the sun as a fixed marker is off by ninety degrees after six hours.","Birds get around this with an internal clock, correcting the sun's bearing against the time of day."],["The clock can be fooled, and fooling it gives one of the cleanest results in the field.","Hold a bird for several days in a room where the lights come on and go off six hours early, and its clock resets to the artificial day.","Release it under the real sun and it departs about ninety degrees from its proper heading, the error running in the direction the shift predicts.","Move the clock the other way and the error reverses.","The bird has simply been handed the wrong time."],["Night migrants use the stars.","A caged migrant in the restless state that comes over it in migration season hops and flutters toward the direction it would travel if the cage were open.","Put such a bird in a funnel shaped cage lined with paper that records where its feet land, and the marks pile up on one side, turning a private intention into a measurement.","Under a planetarium sky these birds orient to the projected stars, and rotating the projection rotates the birds."],["What a young bird brings to the sky is less than one might expect.","Hand raised birds that never see the sky turn do not develop a usable star compass at all.","Birds raised under a planetarium whose sky rotates around some ordinary star instead of Polaris treat that star's neighborhood as north thereafter.","What is inherited is not a picture of the constellations but a rule for pulling north out of them: watch the sky turn, find the point it turns around, call that the pole.","The pattern itself is learned."],["A third cue keeps working when the sky is covered.","Birds in closed cages with no view of sun or stars still orient, and coils that shift the field around the cage turn them predictably.","The avian magnetic compass does not behave like the one in a hiker's pocket.","It ignores polarity and reads inclination, the angle at which the field lines dip into the ground.","Such a bird does not tell north from south.","It tells poleward, where the lines plunge steeply, from equatorward, where they flatten.","Reverse the vertical part of the field and the bird reverses.","Reverse both parts at once, flipping the whole field, and it carries on as before."],["How a bird senses the field at all is where the two long running accounts part company.","The first says iron.","Small crystals of magnetite, an iron oxide, have been reported in the upper beak, wired to the brain through a branch of the trigeminal nerve, though the identity of those iron rich cells has been argued over at length.","A field twists such a crystal, and one tethered to a membrane could tug it and open a channel.","The prediction is sharp: a strong magnetic pulse should scramble the crystals and throw the sense off.","Pulsed birds do misorient, experienced ones more than young ones."],["The second account says light and chemistry.","In the retina sits a protein called cryptochrome.","When a photon strikes it, an electron jumps to a neighboring molecule, leaving two molecules each carrying one unpaired electron.","Those two electrons stay linked, and the surrounding field tips the pair between two states yielding different amounts of chemical product.","Because the outcome depends on the molecule's angle in the field, and because it sits in the eye, the bird may see direction as faint shading over whatever it looks at.","The compass would then need light, and fail in darkness and under some wavelengths.","In several species it works under blue and green light and fails under deep red.","It also runs on electron spins, which weak oscillating fields in the radio range can upset, fields far too feeble to move an iron crystal.","Birds exposed to them lose their bearings."],["It is tempting to treat these two as rivals awaiting a verdict, and the popular telling does.","That framing may be the trouble.","Nothing requires an animal to hold only one magnetic sense.","The eye based mechanism is good at angles and poor at measuring field strength, which makes it a serviceable compass and a useless meter.","The beak and nerve mechanism responds to strength rather than angle, which makes it a poor compass and a plausible instrument for reading position.","A bird carrying both would own a compass and part of a map."],["That distinction is the one most often blurred.","A compass answers a single question, which way is north.","It says nothing about where the traveler is standing.","To register a sideways displacement and correct for it, a bird needs a fix: an estimate of its own position relative to the goal.","A map of that kind must be built from something that changes predictably across the ground."],["Several candidates exist and not one is settled.","Field strength climbs from the magnetic equator toward the poles, and inclination runs from zero degrees at that equator to ninety at the poles, so two magnetic quantities vary with latitude.","They vary awkwardly.","The gradients are not at right angles everywhere, which makes a two coordinate position hard to extract, and the field drifts over decades.","The gradients are also noisy: iron in the crust can bend the field within a few kilometers, so an animal reading strength alone would sometimes place itself well away from where it stands.","Smell is the other serious candidate: homing pigeons often fail to return from unfamiliar sites when their sense of smell is blocked."],["The neatest test of a magnetic map leaves the bird where it is and moves the field instead.","A cage sits inside coils reproducing the strength and inclination of a place the animal has never been.","Some birds shift their heading as though they had been carried there, and in at least one species birds on their first migration do it too, which would mean a rough magnetic map is inherited rather than learned.","Other trials, in other species and other seasons, produce nothing."],["It is worth asking why a field this old, and this full of memorable results, has converged so slowly.","Part of the answer is redundancy.","A bird holding sun, star, and magnetic compasses can lose any one and keep flying, so removing a cue often changes nothing and proves nothing.","Part of it is effect size: a twenty degree shift in the mean heading of a dozen caged birds is a small signal, pulled from an animal whose behavior swings with season, fat reserves, age, and weather.","Part of it is that a striking result travels widely and a failed repetition rarely does."],["Cue conflict experiments show the trouble plainly.","Shift the magnetic field around a group of birds at sunset, the moment when several laboratories believe the compasses are calibrated against one another, and the birds sometimes adopt the new heading, sometimes ignore it, and sometimes adopt it and abandon it the next night.","Read generously, the pattern says birds weigh their cues rather than obeying any single one.","Read harshly, it says the experiments are not controlled well enough to tell those stories apart."],["The more useful question is probably not which cue a bird uses.","It is how an animal carrying several imperfect instruments, each wrong in its own way, combines them into one decision made at dusk with a fixed amount of fuel.","Tracking tags light enough to ride on a warbler are starting to return entire routes rather than a single arrow on a map, and entire routes would settle most of these arguments.","Until then the fair position is that the compasses are largely understood and the map is not."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p14-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, a bird whose light cycle has been moved six hours before release will","choices":["turn and fly in the direction opposite its normal heading","set off about ninety degrees away from its proper heading","keep its normal heading but leave several hours later than it should","orient normally as soon as the real sun becomes visible","wander without settling on any consistent direction at all"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"lights come on and go off six hours early"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"departs about ninety degrees from its proper heading"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 lays out the arithmetic for you: the sun moves about fifteen degrees an hour, six hours of clock shift adds up to about ninety degrees, and the bird departs that far off, in the direction the shift predicts.","distractorNotes":["A full reversal would be one hundred eighty degrees, and paragraph 6 gives about ninety.",null,"The shifted clock changes which way the bird points, not when it decides to leave.","Seeing the real sun is what creates the error, because the bird reads that sun against the wrong time of day.","The birds still pick a consistent direction after the shift, and only the direction is wrong."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q02","type":"detail","stem":"In the starling study, the young birds released in Switzerland","choices":["turned toward Britain the way the older birds did","were carried back to the Netherlands and released a second time","kept the original bearing and were found south of the usual wintering range","scattered so widely that no shared heading could be read from the recoveries","stopped migrating and stayed near the point where they were released"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":6,"quote":"held the heading they had started with and were recovered in southern France and Spain"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":3,"quote":"normally winter in Britain and northern France"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 splits the recoveries by age. The first time migrants held the heading they had started with and turned up in southern France and Spain, shifted by about the distance and direction of the truck ride.","distractorNotes":["Heading for the normal wintering area is what the adults did, and the age contrast is the whole point of the paragraph.","No bird in paragraph 3 is returned to the capture site; they were released in Switzerland and left there.",null,"The recoveries were consistent enough for the age split to be readable, so they were not scattered.","The young birds kept flying, which is why they were recovered hundreds of kilometers away."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q03","type":"detail","stem":"The passage indicates that the magnetic compass of a bird","choices":["tells true north from magnetic north","reverses when the whole field is flipped at once","reads the dip of the field lines rather than their polarity","measures how strong the field is rather than which way it runs","works only when the bird can also see the sun or the stars"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"It ignores polarity and reads inclination"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the avian compass ignores polarity and reads inclination, the angle at which the field lines dip into the ground, which is why it sorts poleward from equatorward rather than north from south.","distractorNotes":["True north never comes up; the contrast the passage draws is between polarity and dip angle.","Paragraph 9 says the bird carries on when the whole field is flipped; it is the vertical part alone that turns it around.",null,"Reading strength is what the beak and nerve mechanism is credited with in paragraph 12, and the passage calls that a poor compass.","Paragraph 9 introduces this cue precisely because it keeps working when the sky is covered."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage reports that weak oscillating fields in the radio range","choices":["throw off the birds although they are too weak to move an iron crystal","restore orientation in birds tested under deep red light","make the crystals in the beak respond more strongly","serve as the cue birds use to calibrate their compasses against one another at sunset","leave caged migrants entirely unaffected"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":8,"quote":"fields far too feeble to move an iron crystal"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":9,"quote":"Birds exposed to them lose their bearings."}],"why":"Paragraph 11 uses these fields as the sharp test of the light based account. They disturb electron spins, birds exposed to them lose their bearings, and they are far too feeble to move an iron crystal.","distractorNotes":[null,"Deep red light is where that compass fails in paragraph 11, and radio fields are not offered as a remedy.","The passage says the opposite: such fields are too weak to move an iron crystal, so the iron account predicts no effect.","Sunset calibration is a separate issue raised in paragraph 17, and nothing there involves radio fields.","Paragraph 11 says exposed birds lose their bearings in the cage, which is a measurable effect."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q05","type":"detail","stem":"Birds hand raised without ever seeing the night sky turn","choices":["fall back on the magnetic compass twice as often as other birds","treat whichever star is brightest as the pole","pick up the star compass later, during the first migration","orient to Polaris without having to learn anything","never develop a usable star compass"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":2,"quote":"do not develop a usable star compass at all"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 states it plainly: without seeing the sky rotate, the star compass does not develop at all, because what is inherited is the rule for finding the center of rotation rather than the pattern itself.","distractorNotes":["The passage never counts how often birds switch to the magnetic cue.","Brightness is never the criterion; the birds in paragraph 8 learn whichever star the artificial sky turns around.","Paragraph 8 says the compass does not develop at all, not that it shows up late.","Paragraph 8 says the pattern is learned, so no bird arrives already knowing which star marks the pole.",null]},{"id":"rc-p14-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 13, \"fix\" most nearly means","choices":["a workable solution to a difficult problem","a steady heading held without variation","a repair made to a sense that has been damaged","a landmark that stays in one place","an estimate of one's own position"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"an estimate of its own position relative to the goal"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 defines the word in the same sentence it uses it: an estimate of the bird's own position relative to the goal, which is the thing a compass alone cannot supply.","distractorNotes":["This general sense fits the sentence loosely, but the paragraph pins the word to position specifically.","A steady heading is what a compass gives, and paragraph 13 says that is exactly what is not enough.","This is an everyday meaning of the word, and nothing in paragraph 13 is broken or being mended.","Paragraph 1 points out there are no landmarks over open ocean, and the paragraph is about a measurement, not an object.",null]},{"id":"rc-p14-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 14, \"noisy\" most nearly means","choices":["loud enough to be heard over a distance","unusually strong in the neighborhood of the poles","shifting quickly from one season to the next","irregular enough in places to mislead a reading","carrying more information than an animal could possibly use"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"iron in the crust can bend the field within a few kilometers"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"would sometimes place itself well away from where it stands"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 explains the word right after using it: iron in the crust bends the field over a few kilometers, so a bird reading strength alone would sometimes place itself well away from where it actually stands.","distractorNotes":["This is the everyday meaning, and the paragraph is describing a magnetic field rather than sound.","Strength does climb toward the poles earlier in the paragraph, but that is a separate property from the one this word names.","The drift paragraph 14 mentions runs over decades, not from one season to the next.",null,"The problem described is that the field misleads, not that it offers too much to process."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage most strongly supports which conclusion about the inherited program a first time migrant follows?","choices":["It is abandoned once the bird reaches the wintering grounds","It works only while the bird stays on the ancestral route","It depends on the bird seeing the sun at some point in the flight","It is more accurate than what an experienced bird relies on","It supplies a position but leaves the direction to be worked out"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":3,"quote":"fly the same direction for the same time and finish somewhere new"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"because the route its ancestors flew ends somewhere a bird can survive"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":6,"quote":"shifted by about the distance and direction they had been carried"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 describes the program as a heading plus a duration, which works because the ancestral route ends somewhere survivable. Paragraph 3 shows what happens when that assumption breaks: carried sideways, the young birds ran the same instructions and landed somewhere else.","distractorNotes":["The passage credits the adult with an added ability after one round trip, not with dropping the inherited heading.",null,"Night migrants in paragraphs 7 and 9 have stars and a magnetic cue as well, so the sun is not required.","Paragraph 4 says the adult has something better, since it can be displaced and still correct.","This reverses the passage: the program gives a direction, and position is the thing it cannot give."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q09","type":"inference","stem":"It can be inferred from paragraph 12 that a bird equipped only with the eye based mechanism would","choices":["be unable to hold any heading after dark","lose all orientation under blue and green light","know which way to fly without knowing it had been moved","read its latitude more accurately than its direction","depend on other birds in the flock to correct its course"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"a serviceable compass and a useless meter"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"It says nothing about where the traveler is standing."}],"why":"Paragraph 12 calls the eye based mechanism a serviceable compass and a useless meter of field strength, and paragraph 13 says noticing a displacement takes a position estimate rather than a direction. Put together, that bird could point but could not place itself.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 12 calls that mechanism a serviceable compass, and paragraph 11 has it working under blue and green light.","Paragraph 11 reports that this compass works under blue and green light and fails under deep red.",null,"The paragraph says the reverse: it is poor at measuring field strength, which is what a latitude reading would need.","Nothing in the passage makes navigation a group effort, and paragraph 1 stresses that no parent leads these birds."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the main idea of the passage?","choices":["Birds use several imperfect cues, and the compasses are better understood than the map","Poor experimental control is the only reason the question remains open","The star compass is the single most important cue a night migrant has","Displacement experiments established that migration is entirely learned","Magnetic sensing in birds has now been traced conclusively to a single protein in the retina"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":6,"quote":"what it is reading while it does"},{"paragraph":18,"sentence":2,"quote":"several imperfect instruments, each wrong in its own way"},{"paragraph":18,"sentence":4,"quote":"the compasses are largely understood and the map is not"}],"why":"The passage moves through sun, stars, and magnetism, then separates a compass from a map in paragraph 13 and closes in paragraph 18 by saying the compasses are largely understood and the map is not.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 16 puts redundancy and small effect sizes ahead of method, so control is only part of the story.","The stars get one stretch of the passage among several, and no cue is ever ranked first.","Paragraph 4 gives the first migration an inherited heading and duration, so the passage splits inheritance and learning rather than choosing one.","The retinal protein is one of two accounts in paragraphs 10 and 11, and paragraph 12 declines to declare a winner, so nothing has been settled conclusively."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 13 is included mainly to","choices":["summarize the evidence for a sense of smell in homing pigeons","separate knowing a direction from knowing a position","explain why the funnel cage produces a readable record of intent","show that a compass is of no use over open water","argue that the magnetic compass is the most dependable cue of all"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":1,"quote":"That distinction is the one most often blurred."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"A compass answers a single question, which way is north."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"It says nothing about where the traveler is standing."}],"why":"Paragraph 13 draws the line the rest of the passage depends on: a compass answers which way is north, a map answers where you are, and correcting a displacement needs the second one. That sets up the unsettled map cues in paragraph 14.","distractorNotes":["Pigeons and smell appear in paragraph 14, one paragraph later.",null,"The funnel cage belongs to paragraph 7 and has nothing to do with this distinction.","The paragraph says a compass is not sufficient, not that it is useless, and paragraph 1 has birds crossing open ocean successfully.","No cue is ranked in paragraph 13, and paragraph 16 explains why ranking them is hard."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"The passage is organized mainly by","choices":["moving from laboratory work to field work and then back again","comparing birds with other migrating animals at each stage","listing the cues in the order they were discovered and ranking them by importance","opening with a puzzle, splitting it into two problems, then taking each in turn","presenting one decisive experiment and defending it against objections"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":6,"quote":"not whether such a bird navigates, but what it is reading while it does"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":1,"quote":"That leaves the narrower question of how a bird points at all."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"a bird needs a fix: an estimate of its own position relative to the goal"},{"paragraph":18,"sentence":4,"quote":"the compasses are largely understood and the map is not"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 poses the puzzle, paragraphs 2 through 4 use displacement to split it into holding a course and knowing a position, and the rest of the passage works through the compasses first and the map second.","distractorNotes":["Cages, coils, and planetariums run throughout, so the passage never divides itself that way.","No other migrating animal appears anywhere in the passage.","The cues do appear in a rough order, but the passage never ranks them, and paragraph 16 says redundancy makes ranking hard.",null,"Many experiments appear, and the passage weighs them against one another rather than defending any single one."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward treating the two magnetic accounts as rivals is best described as","choices":["enthusiastic agreement with that framing","confidence that the question has already been settled in one direction","indifference to which account proves correct","open hostility toward the iron based account","doubt that the contest is the right question to ask"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"It is tempting to treat these two as rivals awaiting a verdict"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"That framing may be the trouble."}],"why":"Paragraph 12 calls the rivalry tempting and then pushes back on it, pointing out that nothing requires one magnetic sense and that the two mechanisms suit different jobs.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 12 opens by calling that framing tempting and then argues against it.","Paragraph 12 ends with a proposal rather than a verdict, and paragraph 18 says the map is still unsettled.","The author is not indifferent; the paragraph proposes a specific division of labor between the two.","The iron based account is handed a serious role in paragraph 12, reading field strength for a map.",null]},{"id":"rc-p14-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In accounting for why the field has converged slowly, the author's tone is best described as","choices":["candid about the weaknesses without dismissing the work","defensive about criticism coming from outside the field","triumphant that the long argument has finally been won","unconcerned, on the view that answers will arrive on their own","scornful of the researchers doing the work"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"this full of memorable results"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"removing a cue often changes nothing and proves nothing"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"a striking result travels widely and a failed repetition rarely does"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 names real problems, including small effects and a published record tilted toward striking results, but it leads with redundancy in the birds themselves. The difficulty is treated as partly built into the subject.","distractorNotes":[null,"No outside critic appears, and paragraph 17 gives the harsh reading its own fair hearing.","Paragraph 18 closes by saying the map is still not understood.","The author treats the slowness as a real problem worth explaining rather than something to wait out.","Paragraph 16 puts redundancy and effect size ahead of any fault in the people doing the work."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Pigeons whose sense of smell is blocked often fail to return from unfamiliar sites","A bird that loses one compass can often keep going on another","Magnetic field strength varies with distance from the magnetic equator","A bird without its magnetic sense cannot orient at night","Adult starlings corrected course after being carried off their usual route"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"can lose any one and keep flying","choice":1},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"Field strength climbs from the magnetic equator toward the poles","choice":2},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"turned and made for the usual wintering range","choice":4},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":6,"quote":"often fail to return from unfamiliar sites when their sense of smell is blocked","choice":0},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"Night migrants use the stars.","choice":3},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"A bird holding sun, star, and magnetic compasses can lose any one and keep flying","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 16 makes the opposite point: the compasses are redundant, so a bird can lose one and keep flying, which is part of why removing a cue often proves nothing. The other four statements all sit on the page.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 14 gives exactly this result as the leading evidence for a sense of smell.","Paragraph 16 says a bird holding sun, star, and magnetic compasses can lose any one and keep flying.","Paragraph 14 says field strength climbs from the magnetic equator toward the poles.",null,"Paragraph 3 says the adults turned and made for the usual wintering range."]},{"id":"rc-p14-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for how a bird corrects for the sun's movement across the sky should turn to","choices":["paragraph 5","paragraph 11","paragraph 2","paragraph 3","paragraph 16"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"correcting the sun's bearing against the time of day"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 gives the problem and the solution together: the sun's bearing shifts about fifteen degrees an hour, and the bird corrects that bearing against an internal clock.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 11 covers the light based magnetic mechanism in the eye.","Paragraph 2 sets out the logic of moving birds and never mentions the sun.","Paragraph 3 reports the starling displacement and the split between adults and young birds.","Paragraph 16 explains why the field has been slow to settle."]}]},"rc-p15":{"id":"rc-p15","title":"The Calibration Problem in Radiocarbon Dating","topic":"radiocarbon dating and calibration","field":"history of science","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1703,"paragraphs":[["A few milligrams of charred wheat, scraped from the floor of a storeroom that burned three thousand years ago, can be persuaded to name the century of the fire.","The mechanism is almost too tidy.","Living things take in carbon.","A tiny, constant fraction of that carbon is unstable.","When the organism dies the intake stops and that fraction thins out on a fixed schedule.","Measure what is left, work backward, and the grain gives up its date.","The word carrying the trouble in that summary is constant."],["Carbon fourteen is not left over from the birth of the planet.","It is manufactured continuously overhead.","Cosmic rays, mostly fast protons from outside the solar system, smash into nuclei in the upper atmosphere and knock loose free neutrons.","A neutron that strikes a nitrogen fourteen nucleus can lodge there and expel a proton, leaving a nucleus of the same total mass but one fewer unit of positive charge: carbon fourteen.","It is soon oxidized, first to carbon monoxide and then, over weeks to months, to carbon dioxide, and within a few years mixing has carried it worldwide."],["Photosynthesis barely distinguishes the two forms.","A plant takes in carbon fourteen in close to the proportion the air offers, animals acquire it by eating plants, and every living body keeps topping up its supply for as long as it feeds.","The result is an internal ratio held near the atmosphere's own: roughly one carbon fourteen atom for every trillion atoms of ordinary carbon twelve.","Rare as that is, a gram of carbon from a living thing still emits just under fourteen beta particles a minute."],["Death closes the account.","Nothing new comes in, and the carbon fourteen already present disappears on its own timetable: a neutron inside the nucleus converts to a proton, an electron flies off, and the atom becomes nitrogen fourteen again, closing the loop that began in the upper air.","Half of any population of these atoms is gone in about 5,730 years.","There is a wrinkle, though.","The earliest measurements put the half-life near 5,568 years, and though the better figure has long been known, laboratories still report ages calculated from the original value, so dates published half a century apart stay comparable."],["What the clock can reach follows from the arithmetic.","After ten halvings only about a thousandth of the original carbon fourteen survives, which sets a practical ceiling near fifty thousand years.","The method is also particular about its subject, since it dates only carbon that was once exchanging with the atmosphere: charcoal, wood, seed, bone, shell, cloth and parchment.","It cannot date a flint blade.","It can date the soot on the hearth beside the blade, and then only if the association between the two is genuine."],["All of this rests on knowing how much carbon fourteen was in the air on the day the organism died, and the founding assumption was the simplest one available: that production and decay had long since come into balance, leaving the atmospheric level fixed.","That is a sensible first approximation, and it is wrong.","Production depends on how many cosmic rays get through.","The earth's magnetic field deflects them, and its strength has drifted substantially over the last ten thousand years.","The sun's outflowing wind deflects them as well, so an active sun suppresses production and a quiet sun raises it.","The carbon cycle adds a drift of its own, since the ocean holds far more carbon than the air and trades slowly."],["The discrepancy showed up early, from an awkward direction.","Objects from Egypt whose calendar dates were already fixed by written records came back from the laboratory too young, some by several centuries.","The conclusion was unavoidable: a radiocarbon year is not a calendar year, and the gap between the two is not a constant that can be subtracted once and forgotten."],["The correction came from trees.","In a temperate climate a tree lays down one growth ring a year, wide in a good season and narrow in a bad one, so a run of rings records a pattern of fat and lean years.","The same pattern appears in every tree that lived through those seasons, which lets a living tree be matched to an old beam, the beam to a buried log, and so on backward.","Stitched together, oak and pine from European sites carry an unbroken year by year record back more than twelve thousand years.","Each ring is a sealed sample of one known year's air."],["Measuring the carbon fourteen in rings of known age turns the problem into a graph, and the graph is not the straight line the founding assumption predicted.","It wobbles.","Decades of weak solar activity leave bumps in it, and in places the curve flattens into a plateau, a stretch where the atmospheric level barely changed, so that many different calendar years produce nearly the same radiocarbon result.","One notorious flat stretch covers roughly four centuries of the first millennium before the common era, from about 800 to 400.","A sample from anywhere inside it returns almost the same answer, and the calendar range that comes back is four centuries wide however careful the laboratory was."],["Trees run out before the method does.","For older material the curve is carried forward by archives that count years in their own way: corals dated independently by uranium and thorium, lake sediments that lay down a visible pair of layers each year, and layered stalagmites.","The internationally agreed curve now runs to about fifty five thousand years."],["Some carbon never came from the air at all, or came from it long ago, and organisms feeding on such a reservoir inherit its head start.","Deep ocean water has been out of contact with the atmosphere for centuries, so carbon drawn from the sea is already old when a fish or a clam builds tissue out of it.","Marine organisms therefore appear roughly four hundred years dead at the moment they actually die.","Fresh water can be worse: a lake fed by springs running through limestone carries dissolved carbonate with no carbon fourteen in it at all, and shells from such a lake have come back thousands of years too old."],["Human activity has since moved the level far more violently than the sun ever did.","Nuclear weapons tested in the open air through the 1950s and early 1960s released floods of neutrons, making carbon fourteen exactly as cosmic ray neutrons do.","By 1963 the northern hemisphere's atmospheric level had nearly doubled.","A treaty signed that year drove testing underground, and the excess has drained away since, until the air is close to where it stood before the tests.","That steep decline is a gift.","Each year on the descending limb carries its own value, so tissue grown in the last seventy years can often be placed within a year or two, which is how tooth enamel, laid down in childhood and never rebuilt, has been used to estimate a person's year of birth."],["A second human signal runs the opposite way.","Coal, oil and gas formed so long ago that every carbon fourteen atom in them decayed away ages back, which makes their carbon dead in the only sense that matters here: it carries no radiocarbon whatever.","Burning them pours dead carbon into the air and dilutes the living fraction, so the atmosphere takes on an apparent age it did not earn.","For decades the bomb excess masked the dilution.","With the bomb carbon nearly gone, dilution is now the dominant trend, and if emissions continue on their present course, cloth made later this century could carry the radiocarbon signature of something several hundred years old."],["The laboratory must also defend the sample itself, and the threats are lopsided.","Recent carbon is the dangerous intruder.","One percent of modern carbon mixed into a sample that is truly forty thousand years old brings back a reported age near thirty two thousand, an error of eight millennia.","One percent of dead carbon mixed into a modern sample shifts its reported age by about eighty years.","Rootlets, groundwater humic acids and old conservation glues all deliver carbon of the wrong age, so pretreatment is no formality: acid and alkali washes strip the soluble intruders, bone is reduced to collagen, wood to cellulose.","Sample choice matters just as much: the heartwood of a beam grew centuries before the tree was felled, and beams were often salvaged and reused, so a seed, which lives one season, answers a sharper question."],["The instrument changed as well.","Counting decays meant grams of purified carbon and days of waiting: at forty thousand years a whole gram yields about one decay every ten minutes.","Accelerator mass spectrometry counts the atoms themselves, so a milligram is enough."],["What the laboratory hands back, then, is not a year.","It is a measurement, reported in a form such as 2450 plus or minus 30 before present, where present is fixed by convention at 1950, a zero chosen because it sits just ahead of the bomb carbon.","The plus or minus describes the precision of the counting, and it is tempting to read it as the calendar answer's error bar.","It is not.","That symmetrical bell has to be pushed through the wobbling calibration curve first, and what emerges is lumpy: sometimes one narrow interval, sometimes two or three humps with unlikely gaps between them, sometimes a span wider than the laboratory's own uncertainty.","The intuition that a better measurement always buys a better date fails outright on a plateau, where halving the counting error barely narrows the calendar range."],["An honest radiocarbon date is therefore a probability distribution, normally quoted as the interval holding sixty eight percent of that probability and the wider one holding ninety five.","Where one layer sits demonstrably above another, statistical models can combine the dates with that order and tighten every range in the sequence.","And the atmosphere's refusal to hold still, the flaw that made all of this necessary, turned out to be worth having.","The calibration curve is itself a record of the sun, of the magnetic field and of the ocean.","The correction and the archive are the same object."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p15-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, carbon fourteen forms in the upper atmosphere when","choices":["nitrogen fourteen absorbs a passing electron and grows heavier by one unit of mass","a cosmic ray proton is captured directly by a carbon twelve nucleus","high energy radiation splits carbon dioxide molecules apart in the stratosphere","a free neutron enters a nitrogen fourteen nucleus and drives out a proton","particles in the solar wind attach a neutron to a nucleus of carbon thirteen"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":4,"quote":"A neutron that strikes a nitrogen fourteen nucleus can lodge there and expel a proton"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 walks the step through in order: cosmic rays knock free neutrons loose, and a neutron that strikes a nitrogen fourteen nucleus lodges there and expels a proton. Same total mass, one fewer unit of positive charge, which makes it carbon.","distractorNotes":["This runs the paragraph 4 decay step backward, and no electron is absorbed anywhere in the passage.","Cosmic rays start the chain, but the passage has them shattering nuclei to release neutrons rather than being captured by carbon.","Carbon dioxide is where the new atom ends up within hours, not the raw material it is made from.",null,"Paragraph 6 gives the solar wind the opposite role, deflecting cosmic rays and lowering production rather than building the isotope."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage says laboratories still calculate reported ages from a half-life near 5,568 years because","choices":["modern measurements have confirmed that this is the accurate figure","the shorter value offsets the effect of contamination by recent carbon","holding to the original value keeps dates published decades apart comparable","the calibration curve is built on the assumption that the atmosphere never changed","decay counting instruments cannot resolve the difference between the two figures"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"so dates published half a century apart stay comparable"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"though the better figure has long been known"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 calls it a wrinkle and gives the reason outright: the original value is kept so that dates published half a century apart stay directly comparable, even though about 5,730 years is the better figure.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 names 5,730 years as the better figure, so the value actually used is the one known to be slightly off.","Contamination is a paragraph 14 problem, handled by pretreatment, not by the choice of a half-life.",null,"Calibration exists precisely because the atmosphere did change, as paragraphs 6 and 9 both insist.","The passage never blames the instruments for this choice, since paragraph 4 gives comparability as the whole reason."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, a sample that is truly forty thousand years old but carries one percent modern carbon comes back with a reported age of about","choices":["eight thousand years","thirty nine thousand years","forty eight thousand years","thirty two thousand years","forty thousand years, since a one percent intruder is too small to matter"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"brings back a reported age near thirty two thousand"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 supplies that number to show how lopsided the threat is. One percent of modern carbon drags a forty thousand year old sample down to roughly thirty two thousand, an error of eight millennia.","distractorNotes":["Eight thousand is the size of the error, not the age that comes back.","A thousand year error would be the mild outcome, and the passage puts the shift at eight thousand years.","Modern carbon adds carbon fourteen, so it always makes a sample look younger rather than older.",null,"This is the intuition paragraph 14 is written to overturn, since a tiny intruder produces an enormous shift."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage attributes the fact that shells from a limestone fed lake can come back thousands of years too old to","choices":["the unusual pressure under which lake shells build their carbonate","the slow turnover of the deep ocean water that feeds the lake","dissolved carbonate in the spring water with no carbon fourteen","a locally high rate of carbon fourteen production over inland regions","contamination of the shells by rootlets and by groundwater"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"dissolved carbonate with no carbon fourteen in it at all"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 treats it as a reservoir effect. Springs running through limestone carry dissolved carbonate with no carbon fourteen in it at all, so shells built from that carbon start out looking ancient.","distractorNotes":["Pressure plays no part in anything the passage describes.","Deep ocean water is the marine case in the same paragraph, and a spring fed lake is a separate reservoir.",null,"Paragraph 2 says mixing carries the isotope worldwide within a few years, so no region has its own elevated supply.","Rootlets and groundwater are paragraph 14 contaminants, and those tend to add recent carbon, which makes a sample look younger."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, by 1963 the northern hemisphere's atmospheric carbon fourteen level had","choices":["returned to where it stood before the weapons tests began","roughly doubled","fallen by about half","stopped changing in any measurable way","risen by the equivalent of four hundred years of decay"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"had nearly doubled"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 gives the figure plainly: neutrons from open air weapons testing had nearly doubled the northern hemisphere's atmospheric level by 1963.","distractorNotes":["The return toward the pre-test level came afterward, once the treaty drove testing underground.",null,"Nothing halved, since the tests added carbon fourteen to the air rather than removing it.","The level was moving faster than at any other point in the passage, which is what makes the descending limb useful.","Four hundred years is the marine offset from paragraph 11, a different quantity in a different context."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 9, \"plateau\" most nearly means","choices":["a flat stretch where many calendar years share one radiocarbon value","a gap in the tree ring record that no surviving piece of timber can bridge","a broad raised area of level ground in a landscape","the highest value that the curve ever reaches","the age past which the method stops working entirely"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"many different calendar years produce nearly the same radiocarbon result"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 defines the word where it uses it: a stretch of curve where the atmospheric level barely changed, so many different calendar years produce nearly the same radiocarbon result.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 8 describes the tree ring record as unbroken, so no such gap is at issue.","This is the everyday landform sense the passage is borrowing from, not the sense it puts to work.","A flat stretch is level, not high, and nothing in paragraph 9 makes it a maximum.","A sample inside a plateau still returns an answer; the answer is just spread across four centuries."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 13, carbon called \"dead\" is carbon that","choices":["comes from organisms that stopped feeding only recently","can no longer take part in any chemical reaction","has been stripped out of a sample during pretreatment","sits sealed away from the air while it goes on decaying","contains no detectable carbon fourteen"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"it carries no radiocarbon whatever"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 states the sense it intends in the same sentence: coal, oil and gas are so old that every carbon fourteen atom in them decayed away long ago, so their carbon carries no radiocarbon whatever.","distractorNotes":["Recently dead material is the opposite case, since its carbon is still rich in carbon fourteen.","Fossil carbon burns readily, which is the whole problem, so chemical inertness is not the point.","Pretreatment in paragraph 14 removes intruders; it does not create the carbon the passage calls dead.","The point is that the decay finished ages ago, not that it is still running out of sight.",null]},{"id":"rc-p15-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The passage's account of contamination most strongly supports which statement about an ancient sample that takes up a trace of recent carbon","choices":["no pretreatment can remove an intruder of this kind","the sample will come back much younger than it really is","the error will match what an equal trace of ancient carbon would cause","the sample will come back older than it really is","the error will stay small because the intruding amount is small"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"Recent carbon is the dangerous intruder."},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"an error of eight millennia"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"shifts its reported age by about eighty years"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 runs the arithmetic in one direction only. Recent carbon adds carbon fourteen that the old sample should not have, so the reported age is pulled toward the present, by eight thousand years in the example given.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 14 describes acid and alkali washes stripping the soluble intruders, so such samples are not written off.",null,"The passage calls the threats lopsided and gives the two figures, eight thousand years against about eighty.","Adding carbon fourteen makes a sample look younger; only dead carbon can push an age older.","Paragraph 14 chooses the one percent example precisely because a tiny intruder produces an enormous shift."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Based on paragraph 9, two pieces of charcoal burned three hundred years apart, both inside that flat stretch, would most likely","choices":["come back three hundred years apart, matching their true separation","prove impossible for a laboratory to measure at all","differ by far more than the laboratory's stated uncertainty","land on two separate humps of one calibrated distribution","come back with radiocarbon results too close to tell apart"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":5,"quote":"A sample from anywhere inside it returns almost the same answer"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says a sample from anywhere inside the flat stretch returns almost the same answer, which is why the calendar range stays four centuries wide. Two samples three hundred years apart in there would be very hard to tell apart.","distractorNotes":["That is what a straight calibration line would deliver, and paragraph 9 says the curve is flat across this span instead.","The measurement itself is fine; it is the translation onto a calendar that stays wide.","A plateau compresses real differences rather than exaggerating them.","Separate humps belong to paragraph 16, where a wiggle splits a distribution, not to a flat stretch.",null]},{"id":"rc-p15-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the central point of the passage","choices":["Tree rings are the most valuable archive available anywhere in the earth sciences","The chemistry behind carbon fourteen production is more intricate than most people realize","Nuclear weapons testing did permanent damage to the usefulness of radiocarbon dating","Radiocarbon dating has been compromised by human pollution to the point where recent material can no longer be trusted","A radiocarbon date is a corrected range rather than a year, because the method's founding assumption is false"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":7,"quote":"The word carrying the trouble in that summary is constant."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"That is a sensible first approximation, and it is wrong."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"What the laboratory hands back, then, is not a year."},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":1,"quote":"An honest radiocarbon date is therefore a probability distribution"}],"why":"The passage moves in one direction throughout: a tidy mechanism, the constant level it quietly assumes, the evidence that the level was never constant, and then the corrections and limits that follow. Paragraphs 16 and 17 land it, calling the output a probability distribution rather than a year.","distractorNotes":["Tree rings supply the correction, but the passage never claims anything about their value to other sciences.","The production chemistry is only the opening move, and most of the passage is about calibration.","Paragraph 12 presents the spike as a dating opportunity, and says the excess has largely drained away since.","Paragraph 12 treats the bomb curve as a gift for recent material, so the passage argues nearly the reverse.",null]},{"id":"rc-p15-q11","type":"function","stem":"The author pairs the weapons testing spike with fossil fuel dilution mainly in order to show that","choices":["people have pushed the level both ways, with opposite effects on dating","burning fossil fuels released the neutrons that the weapons tests had produced","human activity has made modern samples impossible to date","the sun influences production more than any human activity does","the calibration curve has to be rebuilt from fresh tree rings every few decades"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":1,"quote":"A second human signal runs the opposite way."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":5,"quote":"That steep decline is a gift."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":5,"quote":"could carry the radiocarbon signature of something several hundred years old"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":6,"quote":"can often be placed within a year or two"}],"why":"Paragraphs 12 and 13 are deliberately mirrored. The tests raised the level and opened a way to date recent tissue to within a year or two, and fossil carbon lowers it and may take that ability away.","distractorNotes":[null,"The tests released the neutrons themselves, while fossil fuels release carbon that carries no radiocarbon at all.","Paragraph 12 makes recent material easier to date rather than impossible, so this overstates the case.","Paragraph 12 opens by saying human activity moved the level far more violently than the sun ever did.","Rebuilding the curve on a schedule is never mentioned anywhere in the passage."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best describes the way the passage is organized","choices":["a chronological history of the people who developed the technique","a mechanism, then the assumption behind it, then the corrections its failure forces","a list of laboratory procedures arranged from the least to the most expensive","a comparison of radiocarbon dating with two competing dating methods","a problem stated at the very outset and a proposed solution that the author finally rejects"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":2,"quote":"The mechanism is almost too tidy."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":1,"quote":"the founding assumption was the simplest one available"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"The correction came from trees."},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":3,"quote":"the flaw that made all of this necessary"}],"why":"Paragraphs 1 through 5 lay out a clean mechanism, paragraph 6 names the assumption it rests on and calls it wrong, and everything after that is correction and limitation. The last two paragraphs say what survives: a range with a probability attached.","distractorNotes":["Dates appear, but no researcher is named and the material is not arranged as a history.",null,"Pretreatment steps appear in paragraph 14, but cost is never discussed anywhere.","Uranium and thorium dating of corals appears once as a support for the curve, never as a rival.","The corrections are endorsed rather than rejected, and paragraph 17 calls the whole difficulty worth having."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the founding assumption that the atmospheric level never changed is best described as","choices":["convinced that it still holds well enough for most practical laboratory work","puzzled that anyone would ever have proposed it","accepting of it as a sensible start that evidence later overturned","scornful of the carelessness it represented","indifferent, since it has no bearing on how dates are produced now"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"That is a sensible first approximation, and it is wrong."}],"why":"Paragraph 6 gives both halves in one breath. It calls the assumption a sensible first approximation and then says flatly that it is wrong, which is respect for the reasoning together with a clear verdict on it.","distractorNotes":["The same paragraph says the assumption is wrong, and the rest of the passage corrects for it.","Calling something a sensible first approximation is the opposite of finding it baffling.",null,"The word sensible rules out scorn, and the passage treats the assumption as reasonable for its moment.","The failure of that assumption drives the entire calibration enterprise, so it could hardly matter more."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 16, the author's attitude toward the figure a laboratory reports is best described as","choices":["confident in the measurement and wary of how it gets read","impressed by how precisely a calendar answer can now be fixed","irritated with laboratories for publishing an uncertainty at all","doubtful that radiocarbon measurements carry real information","resigned to the method being replaced by something better"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"it is tempting to read it as the calendar answer's error bar"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"It is not."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"what emerges is lumpy"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 never questions the measurement. What it questions is the instinct to read the plus or minus as a calendar error bar, and it answers that instinct with three blunt words.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 16 argues the reverse, since better counting precision buys almost nothing on a plateau.","The uncertainty is welcomed as honest reporting, and paragraph 17 asks for the whole curve to be shown.","The passage treats the measurement as sound, and its caution is about interpretation rather than content.","No replacement is proposed anywhere, and paragraph 17 closes by calling the difficulty worth having."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["The calibration curve reaches further back in time than the tree ring record does on its own","A milligram of material can be enough for a determination today","Carbon fourteen production rises and falls with the activity of the sun","Halving a laboratory's counting error always halves the width of the calendar range","An organism that feeds from the sea can seem centuries old on the day it dies"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"an active sun suppresses production and a quiet sun raises it","choice":2},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"appear roughly four hundred years dead at the moment they actually die","choice":4},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"Trees run out before the method does.","choice":0},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"so a milligram is enough","choice":1},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":6,"quote":"halving the counting error barely narrows the calendar range","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 16 says the intuition that a better measurement always buys a better date fails outright on a plateau, where halving the counting error barely narrows the calendar range. The other four statements are stated in paragraphs 6, 11, 10 and 15.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 8 gives the trees about twelve thousand years, and paragraph 10 carries the curve out to about fifty five thousand.","Paragraph 15 credits accelerator mass spectrometry with bringing the sample down to a milligram.","Paragraph 6 says an active sun suppresses production and a quiet sun raises it, so this one is supported.",null,"Paragraph 11 puts marine organisms at roughly four hundred years dead at the moment they actually die."]},{"id":"rc-p15-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for how radiocarbon has been used to estimate a person's year of birth should turn to","choices":["paragraph 12","paragraph 14","paragraph 3","paragraph 7","paragraph 16"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":6,"quote":"has been used to estimate a person's year of birth"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 closes with it. Because each year on the declining bomb curve carries its own value, tooth enamel laid down in childhood and never rebuilt can be matched to a year of birth.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 14 is about contamination, pretreatment and choosing a short lived sample.","Paragraph 3 covers how living tissue takes carbon fourteen in, not how recent tissue is dated.","Paragraph 7 is about Egyptian objects coming back too young, which is where the calibration problem first surfaced.","Paragraph 16 explains what a reported measurement means, not what has been dated with it."]}]},"rc-p16":{"id":"rc-p16","title":"The Protein That Copies Its Own Shape","topic":"prions and templated protein misfolding","field":"history of science","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1663,"paragraphs":[["A ewe in a hill flock begins to rub.","She works her flank against a gate post until the wool comes away in patches and the skin beneath is raw.","Her gait goes wide and uncertain, she startles at nothing, and within a few months she is dead.","Shepherds in Britain were describing this pattern by the middle of the eighteenth century, and they named it for the rubbing: scrapie.","Almost nothing about it looked like an infection: no fever, no swelling, no pus, no rise in antibodies, none of the noise a body makes against an intruder.","Under the microscope the brain was riddled with holes, like a sponge."],["In the 1930s a simple experiment settled one question and opened a harder one.","Material from the brain of a sick sheep, injected into healthy sheep, gave them the disease.","Scrapie was transmissible, so something was being passed.","Yet that something behaved like nothing in the microbiology textbooks.","It slipped through filters fine enough to hold back bacteria.","It provoked no immune response at any point.","And it took its time: injected animals stayed well for a year, two years, sometimes longer, before the first symptom.","For three decades the agent was filed under a hopeful label, the slow viruses, and left there."],["Then came a run of experiments designed to measure the agent rather than to see it.","The standard way to size a viral genome was to bombard a sample and count survivors: radiation damages nucleic acid, and a larger genome offers a larger target.","Scrapie refused to cooperate.","Ultraviolet light at the wavelength nucleic acid absorbs most strongly, at doses that would cut the infectivity of an ordinary virus by a factor of thousands, barely touched it.","Ionizing radiation gave a target size far too small to hold any genome worth the name.","Enzymes that cut DNA and RNA did nothing measurable."],["The mirror image of that result was just as striking.","Treatments aimed at protein worked.","Proteases lowered the titer.","Phenol, strong urea, and detergents that unfold a polypeptide lowered it further.","Set the two results side by side and the conclusion was awkward but plain: whatever carried scrapie between animals was made of protein and carried no nucleic acid at all.","In 1967 a theoretical proposal suggested that a protein might replicate on its own.","Almost nobody built on it."],["Purification caught up in 1982, when fractions of infected hamster brain were enriched step by step and the infectivity tracked with a single small protein.","The particle got a name assembled out of proteinaceous and infectious: prion, and the protein itself was called PrP.","The disorienting finding came next.","The gene for PrP had not been smuggled in by the agent.","It sat in the animal's own chromosomes, on chromosome 20 in humans, and healthy uninfected animals transcribed it steadily, hanging the finished protein on the surface of their neurons by a lipid tail."],["The infected brain and the healthy brain held the same protein, from the same gene, with the same amino acids in the same order.","The difference was shape.","The normal cellular form is soluble, sits on the cell surface, is digested to nothing by a protease within minutes, and is roughly forty percent alpha helix with almost no beta sheet.","The disease form is insoluble, gathers into clumps and long fibrils, and runs near thirty percent helix with more than forty percent sheet.","Give it a protease and it does not disappear: the enzyme trims a loose tail and stops, leaving a resistant core of about twenty-seven to thirty kilodaltons out of a protein of thirty-three to thirty-five.","One chain, two folds, two fates."],["It is easy, looking back, to tell this as a story about a stubborn establishment and a lone heretic, and that telling is unfair.","The objection was not fussiness.","Biology had spent thirty years establishing that hereditary information lives in sequence, that sequence is copied by base pairing, and that protein is the end of the line.","An infectious protein with no genome was not a small amendment.","It asked for a second channel of heredity, one in which the thing copied was not a message at all.","Demanding extraordinary evidence for that was the correct first response, and the evidence took twenty years to assemble."],["What made the idea thinkable was a mechanism that requires no message to be copied.","The misfolded form is not a poison.","It is a mold.","A molecule already folded the wrong way binds a normal one, holds it against its own sheet face, and presses it into the same geometry.","The new convert is a mold in its turn.","Molecules stack into a fibril that grows at both ends, and when a fibril snaps, one growing surface becomes two.","Growth plus breakage gives an exponential rise in converting surfaces, and nothing anywhere is transcribed."],["That mechanism also explains the strange clock.","The slow part is the beginning, the improbable first seed, whether it arrives by injection or forms on its own in one cell out of billions.","Once enough seeds exist the curve turns steep and the brain fills quickly, which is why these diseases seem to arrive all at once after years of nothing.","Sheep incubate scrapie for two to five years.","In kuru, a human prion disease, some incubation periods ran past forty years."],["The sharpest objection to the protein idea was never the sequence rule.","It was strains.","Isolates of scrapie taken from different sources and passaged in mice bred to be genetically identical behaved as distinct and stable varieties.","One strain killed at about 150 days and another at more than 300, reproducibly, in the same inbred line.","Each left its own signature map of which brain regions turned spongy and which were spared.","A strain bred true through passage after passage.","That is the inheritance of a trait, and inheritance by an agent with no genes looked like a contradiction."],["The resolution is that the shape carries the trait.","A given strain is a particular fold, a particular way the sheet is stacked and the chain is tucked, and each fold templates its own arrangement onto the next molecule with high fidelity.","Strains can be told apart in the laboratory by the size of the fragment a protease leaves behind and by the pattern of sugars on the recovered protein.","The information is real and heritable.","It is simply written in geometry rather than in bases."],["Crossing between species revealed a second property.","Put a hamster prion into a mouse and most animals stay well, while the few that sicken take far longer than a mouse given a mouse prion.","Pass the agent again, now mouse to mouse, and the incubation shortens and settles at a new stable value.","The obstacle is real, but it is a steep hill rather than a wall, and it belongs to a pair rather than a species: what matters is the fit between the incoming shape and the host's own sequence, where a handful of residues can decide it.","The same host can be easy ground for one strain and nearly closed to another."],["Two episodes showed how partial that protection is.","Cattle in Britain developed a spongiform disease recognized in 1986 and traced to feed containing rendered tissue from other ruminants.","Ten years later a new pattern of human disease was reported in people who had eaten beef, and fewer than two hundred died of it in Britain.","Kuru, meanwhile, had spread decades earlier among the Fore of Papua New Guinea through mortuary practices in which the dead were eaten, and it faded once those practices stopped."],["Hospitals inherited a practical problem.","Sterilization protocols are built to destroy nucleic acid and unfold ordinary proteins.","A prion offers nothing of the first kind and is far too stable for the second.","A standard autoclave cycle, 121 degrees Celsius for fifteen minutes, does not reliably do it.","Published guidance calls instead for 134 degrees for eighteen minutes in a porous load autoclave, or an hour in one molar sodium hydroxide, or an hour in bleach at twenty thousand parts per million available chlorine.","Worse, the reagents that usually make a specimen safe do the opposite here.","Formaldehyde fixes the tissue, hardening it and sealing the infectivity inside, where it survives for years, and alcohol behaves much the same way.","Infectivity concentrates in brain, spinal cord, and eye, and instruments have carried it through ordinary cleaning, which is why high risk work leans on single use tools."],["For a long time this looked like one bizarre corner of pathology.","That has changed.","The same templating move turns up across a set of proteins with no relation to PrP.","Alpha synuclein in Parkinson disease, tau in Alzheimer disease, amyloid beta, TDP-43 in amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, and the expanded polyglutamine tracts of Huntington disease all misfold into sheet rich aggregates that seed more of themselves.","Inject affected brain extract into a healthy animal and matching deposits appear, then spread along connected circuits rather than by simple diffusion.","Tau and synuclein even come in strains, distinguishable by fold and by the damage each one leaves."],["Two cautions belong attached to that list.","The word infectious is doing much less work there than it appears.","Nothing indicates that Alzheimer disease or Parkinson disease passes between people in ordinary life, and treating a shared molecular move as evidence of contagion is the error the field works hardest to avoid.","The second caution runs the other way: self templating is not only a failure.","Yeast carry protein based traits inherited through the cytoplasm with no change to any gene, and a few animal proteins in memory and immune signaling switch on by assembling this way.","The sheep disease that broke the rules in the eighteenth century was the first visible case of something ordinary.","Many chains can fold more than one way, and one of those ways can copy itself."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p16-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, applying a protease to the disease form of PrP leaves behind","choices":["nothing, since the enzyme digests it completely","a soluble fragment that can no longer join a fibril","a resistant core of about twenty-seven to thirty kilodaltons","a fragment of thirty-three to thirty-five kilodaltons, the full length protein","a stretch that is roughly forty percent alpha helix"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":5,"quote":"leaving a resistant core of about twenty-seven to thirty kilodaltons"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 states it directly: the enzyme trims a loose tail and stops, leaving a resistant core of about twenty-seven to thirty kilodaltons. That paragraph carries several numbers, so the trick is matching the right one to the right thing.","distractorNotes":["Complete digestion is what happens to the normal cellular form in paragraph 6, not to the disease form.","Solubility belongs to the normal form, while the disease form stays insoluble and keeps gathering into fibrils.",null,"Thirty-three to thirty-five kilodaltons is the size of the whole protein before the enzyme trims it, not what survives.","Forty percent helix describes the normal fold in paragraph 6, and it is a structural figure rather than a fragment."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage reports that ultraviolet light tuned to the wavelength nucleic acid takes up best","choices":["destroyed scrapie infectivity at low doses","left scrapie infectivity nearly untouched","was never actually tested on scrapie","reduced scrapie infectivity by a factor of thousands","worked only when combined with enzymes that cut DNA and RNA"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"barely touched it"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"at doses that would cut the infectivity of an ordinary virus by a factor of thousands"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 says that doses high enough to cut an ordinary virus by a factor of thousands barely touched scrapie. That survival is the whole point of the experiment, since it argued against a nucleic acid genome.","distractorNotes":["Low doses did nothing and high doses did little more, which is the opposite of what this choice claims.",null,"Paragraph 3 describes that test being run, and its result is what pushed the field toward a protein.","A drop of that size is what the same dose did to an ordinary virus, which is the comparison the paragraph draws.","Enzymes that cut DNA and RNA had no measurable effect either, so pairing them changes nothing."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q03","type":"detail","stem":"Which treatment does the passage name as part of published guidance for prion contaminated instruments?","choices":["an autoclave cycle at 121 degrees for fifteen minutes","an hour in formaldehyde followed by alcohol","eighteen minutes at 134 degrees in a porous load autoclave","twenty minutes in bleach at two hundred parts per million chlorine","several rounds of ordinary cleaning between uses"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"134 degrees for eighteen minutes in a porous load autoclave"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"does not reliably do it"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 lists the accepted options, and 134 degrees for eighteen minutes in a porous load autoclave is the first of them. The others in that list are sodium hydroxide and much stronger bleach.","distractorNotes":["That is the standard cycle paragraph 14 names as the one that does not reliably work.","Formaldehyde and alcohol are named as making things worse, since they fix the tissue and seal infectivity inside.",null,"The bleach figure in paragraph 14 is twenty thousand parts per million for a full hour, far stronger and far longer.","Paragraph 14 says instruments have carried infectivity through ordinary cleaning, so that is the problem rather than the solution."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q04","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, one feature that made scrapie unlike a familiar infection was that it","choices":["produced a high fever within days","could not be transmitted by injection","was visible under an ordinary light microscope","was held back by filters that stop bacteria","provoked no immune response at any point"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":6,"quote":"It provoked no immune response at any point."},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"It slipped through filters fine enough to hold back bacteria."}],"why":"Paragraph 2 lists the oddities, and the absence of any immune response is one of them. Paragraph 1 makes the same point from the clinical side, noting no fever, no swelling, and no rise in antibodies.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 1 lists the absence of fever among the things that made scrapie look unlike an infection.","Injection of brain material did transmit it, which is the finding paragraph 2 opens with.","The agent was never seen, and paragraph 3 says the experiments were designed to measure it rather than to see it.","It slipped through those filters, which is how investigators knew it was smaller than bacteria.",null]},{"id":"rc-p16-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The evidence from scrapie strains passaged in genetically identical mice included","choices":["different incubation periods and different damage maps","different amino acid sequences in the host protein","a steady loss of infectivity with each passage","the appearance of new genes in the mouse genome","identical incubation periods in every case once the host line was inbred"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"One strain killed at about 150 days and another at more than 300"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"Each left its own signature map of which brain regions turned spongy"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 gives both signatures: one strain killing near 150 days and another past 300 in the same inbred line, and each strain leaving its own map of which brain regions turned spongy.","distractorNotes":[null,"The host protein sequence was the same in every mouse, which is exactly what made the result so puzzling.","Strains bred true through passage after passage rather than fading out.","No genetic change in the mice was involved, since the whole point was that the mice were identical.","The strains differed sharply in incubation, by more than a hundred fifty days in the example given."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 10, \"strains\" most nearly means","choices":["forces that stretch or deform a molecule","efforts made under difficult conditions","versions of the agent whose properties reproduce reliably","lines of laboratory mice bred over generations to be genetically identical","samples taken from a single infected animal"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":3,"quote":"behaved as distinct and stable varieties"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"A strain bred true through passage after passage."}],"why":"Paragraph 10 defines the word by what it describes: isolates that behave as distinct and stable varieties, each with its own incubation period and its own pattern of damage, holding true through passage after passage.","distractorNotes":["That is a mechanical sense of the word, and the paragraph is describing varieties of an agent rather than forces on a molecule.","The everyday sense does not fit a sentence about isolates that reproduce their own properties reliably.",null,"The inbred mice are the background the comparison was run in, not what the word names.","A single sample could contain any variety, so this names the act of collecting rather than the behavior being described."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 14, \"fixes\" most nearly means","choices":["repairs damage done to the tissue","settles on one value something that had been variable","attaches the specimen to a slide","hardens and preserves the tissue chemically","removes the infectious material from the tissue"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":7,"quote":"fixes the tissue, hardening it and sealing the infectivity inside"}],"why":"The same sentence in paragraph 14 supplies the meaning: formaldehyde hardens the tissue and seals the infectivity inside, where it survives for years. The word describes chemical preservation, not repair.","distractorNotes":["Repair is the everyday sense, and the paragraph is describing a treatment that makes the hazard worse rather than better.","That sense of the word belongs to prices and schedules, not to what formaldehyde does to a specimen.","Mounting is a real laboratory step, but the sentence is about hardening tissue and trapping what is inside it.",null,"The sentence says the reverse: the infectivity survives for years inside the treated tissue."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The account of incubation in paragraph 9 most strongly suggests that","choices":["symptoms track how much conversion has occurred, not time since exposure","the agent lies dormant until a fever activates it","younger animals incubate the disease longer than older ones do","most exposed animals never accumulate any misfolded protein","the immune system holds the agent in check for many years before it finally fails"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":2,"quote":"The slow part is the beginning, the improbable first seed"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"Once enough seeds exist the curve turns steep and the brain fills quickly"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the slow step is forming the improbable first seed, and that once enough seeds exist the curve turns steep and the brain fills quickly. Timing therefore follows the amount of templating, which is why the disease seems to arrive all at once.","distractorNotes":[null,"No fever appears anywhere in this passage, and paragraph 1 lists its absence as one of the original puzzles.","Age is never raised as a variable in the account of incubation.","Paragraph 9 says the first seed is improbable, not that seeding usually fails outright.","Paragraph 2 says the agent provokes no immune response at all, so there is nothing holding it in check."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Based on paragraph 12, which step would most likely shorten the incubation period of a prion in a new host species?","choices":["raising the dose of the first inoculation until every animal sickens","passing the agent through several generations of that host","using a host whose own PrP gene has been deleted","selecting a strain that has never been passaged before","storing the inoculum at a lower temperature before use"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"Pass the agent again, now mouse to mouse, and the incubation shortens"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 describes exactly this: pass the agent again in the new host and the incubation shortens and settles at a new stable value. The shape adapts to the host sequence it is now copying itself onto.","distractorNotes":["Dose is not the variable paragraph 12 identifies, since the shortening comes with repeated passage in the new host.",null,"A host with no PrP of its own would supply nothing for the template to convert, which is the reverse of what is wanted.","Being new to the host is what makes a first passage long, so an unpassaged strain would not help.","Storage conditions of the inoculum are never mentioned as affecting incubation."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"The central point of the passage is that","choices":["scrapie has been recognized in British sheep since the eighteenth century and is still untreatable","sterilization practice in hospitals has had to change because of prion diseases","a protein can copy information in its shape, and this is not rare","the central dogma of molecular biology has turned out to be false in its essentials","an infectious agent can have strains without any genetic differences"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":7,"quote":"Many chains can fold more than one way, and one of those ways can copy itself."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"It is simply written in geometry rather than in bases."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"The same templating move turns up across a set of proteins with no relation to PrP."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":6,"quote":"the first visible case of something ordinary"}],"why":"The passage moves from one sheep disease to a general property of proteins, ending in paragraphs 15 and 16 with a list of other proteins that template their own shape and with a normal cellular version of the same trick.","distractorNotes":["Scrapie opens the passage as an entry point, and the argument travels well past sheep.","Sterilization gets one paragraph as a practical consequence rather than being the point the passage builds toward.",null,"Paragraph 7 treats the sequence rule as sound and hard won, and the passage adds a second channel rather than overturning the first.","Strains are one striking piece of evidence in paragraph 10, offered in support of the larger claim rather than as the claim."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q11","type":"function","stem":"Paragraph 13 is included mainly in order to","choices":["show that the species barrier can be crossed under real conditions","argue that beef is unsafe to eat","explain how rendered animal feed is manufactured","date the first recognition of scrapie in Britain","establish that kuru and the cattle disease were caused by the very same strain"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":1,"quote":"Two episodes showed how partial that protection is."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"it is a steep hill rather than a wall"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 ends by calling the barrier a steep hill rather than a wall, and paragraph 13 supplies two real episodes, cattle in Britain and kuru among the Fore, in which the agent did move. It is evidence for the point just made.","distractorNotes":[null,"The paragraph reports a past epidemic and its toll without making any recommendation about what to eat.","Feed is named as the route of spread in a single clause, and the manufacturing process is never described.","That date is in paragraph 1, and paragraph 13 deals entirely with the twentieth century.","The two episodes are presented as separate events in different species, with no claim that one strain caused both."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["a list of prion diseases followed by their treatments","a debate between two camps, with each given equal space","a chronological history of one laboratory's work","a practical guide to sterilization, with background supplied first","a puzzle set out, narrowed by evidence, then resolved and widened"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"settled one question and opened a harder one"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"the conclusion was awkward but plain"},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"The resolution is that the shape carries the trait."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":3,"quote":"The same templating move turns up across a set of proteins with no relation to PrP."}],"why":"The passage opens with a disease nothing explained, spends the middle paragraphs eliminating a genome and building a mechanism, then widens in paragraphs 15 and 16 to other proteins that do the same thing.","distractorNotes":["Treatments never appear, and the passage offers nothing on curing any of these diseases.","Only one position is developed, and the opposing view in paragraph 7 is explained rather than argued for at length.","No laboratory is followed, and the passage moves across decades, species, and proteins rather than tracking one group.","Sterilization occupies a single paragraph well after the argument is built, and it is a consequence rather than the destination.",null]},{"id":"rc-p16-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the scientists who first rejected the idea of an infectious protein is best described as","choices":["amused by their confusion","sharply critical of their caution","wholly indifferent to their position","admiring of their eventual conversion","sympathetic to their reasoning"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"that telling is unfair"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":6,"quote":"Demanding extraordinary evidence for that was the correct first response"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 calls the heretic story unfair, says the objection was not fussiness, and ends by calling the demand for extraordinary evidence the correct first response. That is a defense of the doubters.","distractorNotes":["Nothing in paragraph 7 treats the objection as confusion, and amusement is not the note the paragraph strikes.","Criticism is what the paragraph sets out to correct, and it calls their caution correct.","Indifference would mean no judgment at all, and the paragraph makes one in its final sentence.","No conversion is described, and the paragraph praises the initial resistance rather than any later change of mind.",null]},{"id":"rc-p16-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 16, the author's attitude toward calling Alzheimer disease infectious is best described as","choices":["enthusiastic about a bold new framing","uneasy with the word and careful to limit it","confident that the disease will prove contagious","dismissive of the entire line of research as a distraction","neutral, reporting the claim without any comment"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"The word infectious is doing much less work there than it appears."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"treating a shared molecular move as evidence of contagion is the error the field works hardest to avoid"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 opens with a caution, says the word is doing much less work there than it appears, and calls the slide from shared mechanism to contagion the error the field works hardest to avoid.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph opens with a warning rather than with enthusiasm about the framing.",null,"The author states that nothing indicates these diseases pass between people in ordinary life.","Paragraph 15 treats the seeding work as solid and interesting, so the research itself is not being brushed aside.","Comment is exactly what the paragraph offers, naming the loose use of the word as an error."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage supports all of the following statements EXCEPT:","choices":["Healthy animals that have never been exposed still make PrP","Formaldehyde treatment can leave infectivity intact for years","The disease form of PrP contains more beta sheet than the normal form","A prion strain carries a short stretch of its own nucleic acid","Breaking a fibril increases the number of surfaces that can convert"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"healthy uninfected animals transcribed it steadily","choice":0},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":7,"quote":"sealing the infectivity inside, where it survives for years","choice":1},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"almost no beta sheet","choice":2},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"more than forty percent sheet","choice":2},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"when a fibril snaps, one growing surface becomes two","choice":4},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":5,"quote":"carried no nucleic acid at all","choice":3}],"why":"The passage argues the opposite at length: paragraph 3 reports that nucleic acid damaging treatments did not touch the agent, and paragraph 11 says the strain information is written in geometry rather than in bases. The other four statements all sit on the page.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 5 says the gene sits in the animal's own chromosomes and that healthy uninfected animals transcribe it steadily.","Paragraph 14 says formaldehyde hardens the tissue and seals the infectivity inside, where it survives for years.","Paragraph 6 contrasts almost no sheet in the normal fold with more than forty percent in the disease fold.",null,"Paragraph 8 says that when a fibril snaps, one growing surface becomes two."]},{"id":"rc-p16-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wanted to know why a prion takes longer to cause disease the first time it enters a new species should turn to","choices":["paragraph 3","paragraph 8","paragraph 10","paragraph 12","paragraph 15"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":2,"quote":"the few that sicken take far longer than a mouse given a mouse prion"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 is the one that crosses species: a hamster prion in a mouse, the long and incomplete first passage, and the shortening that follows on later passage in the new host.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 is the radiation work that argued against a genome, with no mention of species.","Paragraph 8 gives the templating mechanism in general terms and never brings in a second species.","Paragraph 10 concerns strains within one host species, mice bred to be identical.",null,"Paragraph 15 extends the mechanism to other proteins and does not return to crossing species."]}]},"rc-p17":{"id":"rc-p17","title":"What the Ice Remembers and What It Loses","topic":"ice cores as a record of past atmospheres","field":"earth science","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1694,"paragraphs":[["A drill working down through an ice sheet returns cylinders of ice about ten centimeters across, cut into meter lengths and boxed for the trip to a freezer laboratory.","What arrives looks like plumbing stock.","What it holds is an archive.","Every cylinder holds snow that fell on a particular set of days, the dust and salt and ash that fell with it, and, sealed inside small bubbles, a sample of the air the planet was breathing some time after that snow landed.","That last clause carries the first complication: the ice and the air trapped inside it are not the same age."],["Turning snow into ice is slow mechanical work.","Fresh polar snow arrives with a density near one tenth that of water, most of its volume empty space between delicate crystals.","Under the weight of each new season those crystals round off, press together, and weld at their contact points, a process called sintering.","The intermediate material, denser than snow and not yet ice, is firn.","Firn is granular, porous, and connected: its pore space forms continuous channels that run all the way to the surface.","Solid glacial ice has a density of about 0.917 grams per cubic centimeter."],["Because those channels stay open, the upper firn breathes.","Air from above moves down through it and back up, driven by pressure changes at the surface and by molecular diffusion, so the gas in open pores thirty meters down is very nearly modern.","How far that exchange reaches depends on temperature and snowfall.","On the cold, dry interior of East Antarctica the firn column can run past a hundred meters.","On a warm coastal dome with heavy accumulation it may be only fifty.","At the base of the column sits a lock in zone, a stack of layers dense enough that vertical mixing effectively stops."],["Below the lock in zone, at a density near 0.83 grams per cubic centimeter, the remaining pores pinch shut and the air in them becomes a set of isolated bubbles.","That moment of closure, not the moment the snow fell, is the birthday of the gas sample.","The ice around a bubble has sat there as long as it took the firn column to bury it, so the air is always younger than the ice holding it.","The size of that age offset varies enormously, from a few decades at a wet coastal site to several thousand years at the coldest, driest inland sites during a glacial period."],["The offset is not a nuisance to be waved away.","It is the central obstacle to the question the cores are most often asked.","Anyone asking whether carbon dioxide rose before or after a temperature change is comparing a measurement made on the gas with one made on the ice, and the two sit on different clocks.","Correcting for the difference requires a model of how the firn column behaved in the past, which itself requires estimates of past temperature and snowfall.","The uncertainty in that correction is often larger than the lead or the lag under debate."],["Closure also blurs.","Air in the open firn is stirred over years to centuries before it is sealed, and bubbles do not all close at the same depth, so a laboratory measures a weighted average across a window of time rather than a snapshot of one year.","A sharp spike lasting a decade is flattened into a gentle rise, or lost.","The same mixing has a use, since methane spreads between the hemispheres within a couple of years and its swings can be matched from core to core."],["The ice itself carries a second archive that owes nothing to bubbles.","Windblown mineral dust records the dryness and circulation of distant continents.","Sea salt tracks storms and sea ice.","Sulfate spikes mark explosive eruptions, ammonium marks biomass burning, and beryllium-10, produced when cosmic rays strike the upper atmosphere, tracks the magnetic activity of the sun.","Not everything survives equally well.","Greenland ice carries enough calcium carbonate dust to react with acids already in the ice and manufacture carbon dioxide inside the core itself, which is why Antarctic ice is the reference archive for past carbon dioxide."],["The best known measurement made on the ice rather than the gas is the isotope ratio of the water molecules.","Most water is built from the lighter atoms, oxygen-16 and ordinary hydrogen, but a small fraction carries oxygen-18 or deuterium, the heavier form of hydrogen.","Heavy molecules evaporate slightly less readily and condense slightly more readily.","As an air mass travels from a warm ocean toward a cold pole it snows away its moisture, and each round of condensation removes heavy molecules first, leaving the vapor lighter.","The colder the final condensation, the more of that stripping has happened, and the lighter the snow that falls."],["The chain of reasoning turns a chemical ratio into a thermometer.","The calibration in common use is a spatial one.","Measure the isotope ratio of recent snow at many sites across an ice sheet, plot each value against that site's modern mean annual temperature, and take the slope of the resulting line.","Applying that slope downward through a core converts an isotope profile into a temperature history."],["The step from a line across space to a line through time is where care is required, and it is worth being blunt about what the reading assumes.","It assumes that snow fell in the same season then as now, that it came from the same ocean regions at the same sea surface temperatures, and that the ice surface stood at the same elevation.","None of these holds exactly.","If a colder climate suppresses winter snowfall, the surviving layers are weighted toward summer and the record reads warm.","If the ice sheet stood taller, its surface was colder for reasons unrelated to the climate at sea level.","The thermometer is not wrong, but it is conditional, and reading it as a direct account of air temperature claims more than it can deliver."],["What rescues it is that the ice offers thermometers of other kinds.","An ice sheet conducts heat poorly, so a borehole holds a faint temperature profile left by past surface conditions, which can be inverted for a coarse history.","During an abrupt warming, the sudden temperature contrast across the firn column sorts nitrogen and argon isotopes by mass, leaving a fingerprint in the trapped gas that records the size of the temperature jump directly.","Applied to Greenland, these methods indicated a change from the last glacial period to the present close to twice what the spatial slope alone suggested."],["Dating begins with counting.","Near the top of a core the layers are visible, bands of coarse summer crystals alternating with fine winter ones.","Deeper down, chemistry keeps the calendar.","Dust and sodium peak in one season, hydrogen peroxide and the water isotopes in another, and a probe drawn along the core measures electrical conductivity, which traces acidity layer by layer.","Counting works where snowfall is heavy.","Central Greenland receives roughly twenty centimeters of ice equivalent accumulation a year, enough that single years stay thick enough to resolve well back into the last glacial period.","Error accumulates as the count runs, from a year or two per thousand in recent millennia to hundreds by forty thousand."],["Counting alone would drift, so the count is pinned.","A large explosive eruption injects sulfate into the stratosphere and deposits it in both hemispheres within a couple of years, leaving one horizon that shows up in Greenland and Antarctic cores alike.","Eruptions with known historical dates anchor the recent count outright.","Farther back, sharp beryllium-10 peaks produced by extreme solar events appear in the ice and in the radiocarbon of tree rings at the same moment, tying two independent chronologies together."],["The deepest ice is the least trustworthy, and the reasons compound.","An ice sheet spreads under its own weight, so every annual layer is stretched sideways and thinned as it is buried.","A layer twenty centimeters thick at the surface may be a centimeter thick halfway down and a fraction of a millimeter near the bed.","Once layers are that thin, diffusion of water molecules through the solid smears the isotope cycles until annual structure is gone.","Geothermal heat can melt the bottom away, removing the oldest ice.","Worse, ice near bedrock flows around obstacles and can fold back over itself, stacking older ice above younger.","Two deep Greenland cores drilled near one another agreed for most of their length and disagreed in the bottom tenth, which is how that disturbance was found."],["Below the depth where counting fails, chronology comes from models of ice flow and from matching features in the record to the known rhythm of the orbital cycles of Earth.","This is a reasonable procedure and a weaker one, and the resulting ages carry uncertainties of thousands of years.","It is why the long Antarctic records, which reach back eight hundred thousand years, are used for what they are good at, the size and ordering of slow glacial cycles, rather than for the fine timing of a single event."],["All of this shapes what the record can say about rates.","Smoothing in the firn and thinning at depth push the same way: both hide fast changes and preserve slow ones.","A hundred year excursion inside old, low accumulation ice would leave little trace.","The record therefore sets a floor under past rates of change rather than a ceiling, and a claim that nothing in the past matched the speed of the present rise is only as strong as the resolution of the ice behind it.","That claim holds up because it does not rest on the deep cores.","Sites with heavy snowfall resolve the last two thousand years to a decade or better, and across that stretch the record is flat."],["None of this makes the archive less valuable.","Its limits are unusually well characterized.","The age offset can be modeled, the smoothing estimated, the thinning calculated, and the disturbed sections identified and set aside.","An archive whose distortions are known and bounded is worth far more than one that merely looks clean."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p17-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the air held inside a bubble in an ice core dates from","choices":["the season in which the snow now surrounding it first fell","the moment the pores in the firn pinched shut and sealed it off","the year the core was drilled and lifted to the surface","the time when the firn column at that site first began to form","the point at which the ice reached a density of 0.917 grams per cubic centimeter"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":2,"quote":"That moment of closure, not the moment the snow fell, is the birthday of the gas sample."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"the remaining pores pinch shut and the air in them becomes a set of isolated bubbles"}],"why":"Paragraph 4 says the moment of closure, not the moment the snow fell, is the birthday of the gas sample. Below the lock in zone the pores pinch shut and the air becomes isolated bubbles.","distractorNotes":["The passage states the opposite, that the snow and the gas carry different dates and the gas is the younger of the two.",null,"Drilling only recovers the sample. The passage dates the gas to closure in the firn, far below the modern surface.","The firn column at a site is far older than any single bubble in it, and paragraph 4 ties the gas age to pore closure.","The number 0.917 is the density of solid glacial ice given in paragraph 2, not the close off density of about 0.83 named in paragraph 4."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage says that acidity is traced layer by layer in a core by measuring","choices":["the density of each band of crystals in the ice","the concentration of sea salt held in the ice","the ratio of oxygen-18 to ordinary oxygen-16","the amount of windblown mineral dust deposited in each layer","electrical conductivity along the core"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":4,"quote":"measures electrical conductivity, which traces acidity layer by layer"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 lists the seasonal markers and then says a probe drawn along the core measures electrical conductivity, which traces acidity layer by layer.","distractorNotes":["Density tells you how far snow has turned into ice, which paragraph 2 covers, not how acidic a layer is.","Sea salt appears in paragraph 7 as a tracer of storms and sea ice, not as the acidity measurement.","The isotope ratio is the temperature proxy and one of the seasonal markers, not the way acidity is read.","Dust is named as a seasonal marker and as a continental tracer, but the acidity reading is the conductivity probe.",null]},{"id":"rc-p17-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, Antarctic ice rather than Greenland ice serves as the reference record for past carbon dioxide because","choices":["Greenland cores rarely reach back far enough to cover a full glacial cycle of the kind Antarctic ice records","the firn column in Greenland is too shallow to seal any gas at all","carbonate dust in Greenland ice reacts with acids in the ice and makes carbon dioxide inside the core","Greenland receives too little snowfall for its annual layers to be counted","carbon dioxide mixes between the hemispheres far too slowly to ever reach Greenland"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":6,"quote":"react with acids already in the ice and manufacture carbon dioxide inside the core itself"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 says Greenland ice carries enough calcium carbonate dust to react with acids already in the ice and manufacture carbon dioxide inside the core itself.","distractorNotes":["The passage never compares the two regions on length of record. The objection it raises is chemical contamination inside the core.","Greenland firn seals gas normally. Paragraph 7 objects to a reaction in the ice, not to a failure to trap bubbles.",null,"Paragraph 12 says the opposite, that central Greenland's snowfall is heavy enough to make layer counting work well.","Paragraph 6 says methane crosses between the hemispheres in a couple of years, so slow mixing is not the problem raised here."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage presents the sorting of nitrogen and argon isotopes in the firn as a way to","choices":["measure the size of an abrupt temperature jump directly","date the gas trapped at the bottom of the firn column","identify which season a given layer of snow fell in","detect the sulfate that a distant volcanic eruption left behind in the ice","estimate how much snow fell at a site during a glacial period"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"records the size of the temperature jump directly"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 says that during an abrupt warming the temperature contrast across the firn sorts nitrogen and argon by mass, leaving a fingerprint in the trapped gas that records the size of the jump directly.","distractorNotes":[null,"The sorting records a temperature change, not an age. Dating comes from counting and flow models in paragraphs 12 through 15.","Seasonal identification comes from dust, sodium, hydrogen peroxide and the water isotopes in paragraph 12.","Volcanic sulfate is a dating tie point in paragraph 13 and has nothing to do with gas isotope sorting.","Snowfall estimates feed the firn model in paragraph 5. The isotope sorting described here reports a temperature change."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q05","type":"detail","stem":"The passage says that sharp peaks of beryllium-10 are useful for dating because they","choices":["appear only in ice that has never been disturbed by flow","mark the season in which each annual layer was deposited","record the total amount of sulfate a large eruption released","show up in the ice and in the radiocarbon of tree rings at the same moment","accumulate in the ice at a steady rate that can be converted directly into an age"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"appear in the ice and in the radiocarbon of tree rings at the same moment"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 says beryllium-10 peaks from extreme solar events appear in the ice and in tree ring radiocarbon at the same moment, tying two independent chronologies together.","distractorNotes":["The passage never links beryllium-10 to undisturbed ice. Its value is that it shows up in two separate archives at once.","Seasonal marking is done by dust, sodium, hydrogen peroxide and the water isotopes, as paragraph 12 explains.","Sulfate is the volcanic marker described just before this. Beryllium-10 comes from cosmic rays and solar events.",null,"This describes a steady radiometric clock. The passage values beryllium-10 for matching two chronologies, not for decaying predictably."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 3, \"breathes\" most nearly means that the upper firn","choices":["expands and contracts as the temperature at the surface rises and falls","releases the gases that were sealed into it during earlier seasons of snowfall","exchanges air with the atmosphere through its connected pore spaces","supports living communities of microbes within its open pores","loses mass steadily to evaporation from the surface of the snow"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":2,"quote":"Air from above moves down through it and back up"},{"paragraph":2,"sentence":5,"quote":"its pore space forms continuous channels that run all the way to the surface"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 explains the word right after using it: air moves down and back up through the open channels, so the gas thirty meters down is very nearly modern.","distractorNotes":["The word here is about air moving in and out, not about the firn changing volume.","Nothing is sealed in the upper firn yet. Paragraph 4 places trapping below the lock in zone.",null,"The passage never mentions anything living in the firn. The image is entirely about air moving in and out.","The passage describes firn gaining mass and compacting under new snow, not wasting away."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 16, \"floor\" most nearly means","choices":["an upper bound on how fast past change could have been","a lower bound on how fast change could have been","the lowest temperature ever reached in the polar regions","the base of the ice sheet where it rests on bedrock","the deepest section of a core that can still be counted"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"sets a floor under past rates of change rather than a ceiling"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"both hide fast changes and preserve slow ones"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 says smoothing and thinning hide fast change, so the record can show that change was at least this fast without ruling out something faster. A floor is the lower bound, and the paragraph sets it against a ceiling.","distractorNotes":["That is the ceiling the paragraph says the record does not give, and a floor works the other way.",null,"Here the word limits a rate of change, not a temperature.","This is the literal sense of the word, but the paragraph is discussing limits on a rate, not the bottom of an ice sheet.","Depth comes up nearby, though the phrase is about how fast past change could have been, not about where counting stops."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q08","type":"inference","stem":"It can be inferred from paragraph 5 that a conclusion about whether carbon dioxide rose before or after a warming is most trustworthy when it is drawn from","choices":["the deepest ice available at a site, since it covers by far the longest span of time","a single core, so that only one chronology is involved","gas measurements alone, with the ice measurements set aside entirely","an interior site where the firn column is unusually deep and cold","a site where the ice age and the gas age are close together"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":3,"quote":"the two sit on different clocks"},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":5,"quote":"The uncertainty in that correction is often larger than the lead or the lag under debate."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":4,"quote":"from a few decades at a wet coastal site to several thousand years"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says the correction for the age offset is often larger than the lead or lag being argued about, so the smaller that offset is, the less the answer depends on the model.","distractorNotes":["Length of record does not help with sequencing, and paragraph 14 adds that the deepest ice is the least trustworthy of all.","Using one core does not shrink the offset between the two clocks inside that same core.","The comparison needs both, since the temperature signal is read from the ice and the gas is read from the bubbles.","A deeper firn column takes longer to seal air, which makes the offset larger and the correction harder.",null]},{"id":"rc-p17-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 10 suggests that a temperature history built from water isotopes could be misleading if","choices":["the core was drilled at a site with unusually heavy snowfall","the laboratory measured deuterium instead of oxygen-18","the surrounding ice contained unusually large amounts of volcanic sulfate","the height of the ice sheet surface changed over the period covered","the bubbles in the core closed off at a shallower depth than usual"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"If the ice sheet stood taller, its surface was colder for reasons unrelated to the climate at sea level."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"that the ice surface stood at the same elevation"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 lists constant surface elevation as one of the assumptions behind the reading, then notes that a taller ice sheet had a colder surface for reasons unrelated to the climate at sea level.","distractorNotes":["Heavy snowfall helps the record by improving resolution. The worry in this paragraph is season, moisture source, and elevation.","The passage treats deuterium and oxygen-18 as two versions of the same signal, not as a source of error.","Sulfate is a dating marker in paragraph 13 and is never said to distort the isotope thermometer.",null,"Close off depth changes the age of the gas, which is paragraph 4, not the isotope ratio measured in the ice itself."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best states the central point of the passage?","choices":["Ice cores have proven beyond argument that carbon dioxide and temperature have always risen and fallen together","The water isotope thermometer should be abandoned in favor of borehole and trapped gas methods","Ice holds a usable record of past air, but each step from snowfall to number adds a limit to account for","Deep drilling in Antarctica has extended the climate record much further back than drilling in Greenland","Layer counting is the only dependable way to assign an age to anything found inside an ice core"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"What it holds is an archive."},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"That last clause carries the first complication"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"The thermometer is not wrong, but it is conditional"},{"paragraph":17,"sentence":4,"quote":"An archive whose distortions are known and bounded is worth far more than one that merely looks clean."}],"why":"The passage traces snow into ice, then into a readable record, and at every stage names what is lost or shifted. Paragraph 17 states the point outright: the archive is valuable because its distortions are known and bounded.","distractorNotes":["The passage says the ordering of the two is exactly what the age offset makes hard to establish.","Paragraph 11 presents those methods as checks that correct the isotope reading, not as replacements for it.",null,"The length of the Antarctic record is one detail in paragraph 15, not the argument the whole passage builds.","Paragraphs 13 and 15 add volcanic horizons, solar events and flow models as further ways of assigning ages."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q11","type":"function","stem":"The main function of paragraph 11 in the passage is to","choices":["introduce a second and more reliable method for dating the layers in a core","show that the record carries its own checks on the isotope reading","argue that measurements made on the ice are more reliable than measurements made on the gas","explain why the deepest sections of a core cannot be counted year by year","describe how heat from the interior of the Earth reaches the base of an ice sheet"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"What rescues it is that the ice offers thermometers of other kinds."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"close to twice what the spatial slope alone suggested"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 ends by calling the isotope thermometer conditional. Paragraph 11 opens with what rescues it and gives two other thermometers, one of which revised the Greenland estimate by about a factor of two.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 11 is about temperature, not age. Dating begins in the paragraph that follows it.",null,"One of the two checks it describes, the isotope sorting of nitrogen and argon, is a measurement made on the trapped gas.","That problem belongs to paragraph 14, which takes up the difficulties of the deepest ice.","Geothermal heat appears in paragraph 14 as a way the oldest ice is lost, not as the subject of this paragraph."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which of the following best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["It traces snow into ice, then how the record is read and dated, and finally what it can claim","It compares the Greenland and Antarctic drilling programs and judges which of them is better","It states a conclusion about past climate and then works backward through the evidence supporting it","It moves through the seasons of a single year of snowfall and then repeats the cycle at greater depth","It alternates between describing a measurement and describing an eruption that confirms it"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":1,"quote":"Turning snow into ice is slow mechanical work."},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":1,"quote":"The chain of reasoning turns a chemical ratio into a thermometer."},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":1,"quote":"Dating begins with counting."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"All of this shapes what the record can say about rates."}],"why":"The passage runs in order: snow to firn to sealed bubbles in paragraphs 2 through 6, then the isotope thermometer and the dating methods, then the deepest ice, and finally what the record can and cannot say about rates.","distractorNotes":[null,"Both regions appear, but as sources of different kinds of information, and they are never ranked against each other.","No conclusion is stated up front. The passage builds its cautions as it goes and gathers them at the end.","Seasonal layering appears only as a dating method in paragraph 12, not as the plan of the passage.","Volcanic eruptions appear once, as a way of pinning a layer count, not as a recurring pattern."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the water isotope thermometer in paragraph 10 is best described as","choices":["dismissive of a method that rests on assumptions that no one is able to test","enthusiastic about a technique that requires no further checking","indifferent to whether the method gives an accurate temperature","puzzled by a result that nobody working in the field has managed to explain","accepting of the method while insisting that its assumptions be stated"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"The thermometer is not wrong, but it is conditional"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"it is worth being blunt about what the reading assumes"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 lists what the reading assumes, says none of it holds exactly, and then says plainly that the thermometer is not wrong but conditional. That is acceptance with conditions attached.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph says the thermometer is not wrong, and the next paragraph shows the assumptions being tested.","The paragraph spends most of its length on what the reading takes for granted, which is not enthusiasm.","Accuracy is the entire concern of the paragraph, and the author cares enough to list what can bias it.","Nothing here is left unexplained. The author explains each assumption and how it can skew the result.",null]},{"id":"rc-p17-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In paragraph 16, the author treats the claim that no past rise matched the speed of the present one as","choices":["an overstatement the ice record cannot support at all","a conclusion that the deepest Antarctic cores establish most firmly","a question that further deep drilling will have to settle one way or another","sound, but only because well resolved records stand behind it","less interesting than the question of how the layers are counted"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":5,"quote":"That claim holds up because it does not rest on the deep cores."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":6,"quote":"resolve the last two thousand years to a decade or better"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 says the claim is only as strong as the resolution of the ice behind it, then says it holds up because it rests on heavy snowfall sites that resolve the last two thousand years to a decade or better.","distractorNotes":["The author says the claim holds up, and names the evidence that carries it.","The paragraph says the claim survives precisely because it does not rest on the deep cores.","The author treats the question as already answered by the well resolved recent record.",null,"Counting is discussed earlier as a tool. Here the author is judging the rate claim itself, not ranking the two topics."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage names all of the following as reasons the deepest ice is hard to trust EXCEPT","choices":["layers are thinned by the spreading of the ice sheet until single years are very thin","the bubbles at that depth have leaked their gas into the nearby ice","diffusion of water molecules through solid ice erases the annual isotope cycles","heat from below can melt away the oldest ice at the base","flow around obstacles near bedrock can place older ice above younger ice"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":2,"quote":"every annual layer is stretched sideways and thinned as it is buried","choice":0},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"diffusion of water molecules through the solid smears the isotope cycles","choice":2},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"Geothermal heat can melt the bottom away","choice":3},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":6,"quote":"stacking older ice above younger","choice":4}],"why":"Paragraph 14 lists thinning, isotope diffusion, basal melting and folding near bedrock. Leaking bubbles are never mentioned anywhere in the passage.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 14 describes exactly this thinning, from twenty centimeters at the surface to a fraction of a millimeter near the bed.",null,"Paragraph 14 names this smearing as one reason annual structure disappears at depth.","Paragraph 14 says geothermal heat can melt the bottom away and remove the oldest ice.","Paragraph 14 describes ice folding back over itself near bedrock and stacking older ice above younger."]},{"id":"rc-p17-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for an explanation of how a volcanic eruption helps fix the age of a layer would find it in","choices":["paragraph 3, on how deep the open firn column runs","paragraph 7, on what the ice carries besides trapped air","paragraph 10, on the assumptions that sit behind the isotope thermometer","paragraph 13, on the tie points that keep a layer count from drifting","paragraph 14, on the problems that affect the deepest ice"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"Eruptions with known historical dates anchor the recent count outright."},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"leaving one horizon that shows up in Greenland and Antarctic cores alike"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 explains that a large eruption lays down one sulfate horizon in both hemispheres, and that eruptions with known historical dates anchor the recent count.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 is about how far the firn exchanges air with the atmosphere, not about dating.","Paragraph 7 mentions sulfate as a marker of eruptions but does not explain how it pins a chronology.","Paragraph 10 is about reconstructing temperature, not about assigning ages to layers.",null,"Paragraph 14 explains why deep layers are unreliable, not how eruptions anchor a count."]}]},"rc-p18":{"id":"rc-p18","title":"What Venom Has Actually Given Medicine","topic":"venom as a source of medicines","field":"pharmacology","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1693,"paragraphs":[["Every few years a headline announces that the next great painkiller is swimming in a tide pool or coiled under a rock.","Venom is chemistry built to shut down a nervous system or a circulatory system in seconds, and the molecules that do it are extraordinarily good at their jobs.","Researchers like to call venom a library that evolution has already screened against the very proteins medicine wants to control.","The premise is sound.","The output is thin.","After five decades of effort, the list of approved drugs that trace back to venom is short enough to recite from memory."],["The first thing to correct is the singular noun: there is no such thing as the toxin in a snake or a scorpion.","A venom is a mixture of dozens to hundreds of components: enzymes that break down tissue, salts and small molecules that cause pain and swelling, and, of most interest to drug developers, short folded peptides that bind ion channels and receptors.","Cone snails are the extreme case.","More than seven hundred species are known, one species may produce well over a hundred peptides, and the mixture shifts depending on whether the animal is hunting or defending.","The components are not redundant: some paralyze, some block escape reflexes, and several do little alone and much together."],["The peptides are small, usually ten to forty amino acids.","What makes them durable is not length but stitching.","Cysteine residues along the chain pair off into disulfide bonds that lash distant parts of the sequence together.","In one common arrangement, the inhibitor cystine knot, three such bonds build a compact core in which one bond threads through the ring formed by the other two.","The result behaves less like a floppy string than like a bead of hardened resin, resistant to heat, to acid, and to the enzymes that destroy ordinary peptides."],["That rigid core explains the selectivity that first drew pharmacologists in.","The cysteines and the fold they create stay conserved; the residues on the loops between them do not.","Venom genes duplicate readily, and the copies accumulate changes on the exposed surface while the skeleton holds still.","Prey evolve resistance, predators need fresh keys, and the cycle repeats.","What comes out is a set of peptides that can tell nearly identical proteins apart."],["Nearly identical is not an exaggeration.","Humans carry nine subtypes of voltage-gated sodium channel, built alike and sharing much of their sequence, yet sitting in different tissues: one in a pain-sensing nerve, one in heart muscle, one in skeletal muscle.","A drug that blocks all of them stops the heart.","Chemists have spent decades trying to build small molecules that touch one subtype and spare the rest, with limited success, because the pockets they bind are so alike.","Venom peptides sidestep that by not using a pocket.","They lie across the outer face of the channel and read a patch of its surface, and small differences there decide whether the peptide sticks."],["Here the story usually stops being told carefully.","Binding tightly to a human channel is a laboratory result, not a therapy, and the distance between them is where nearly every venom program has died.","It helps to ask what the animal was selecting for.","A predator needs a dose delivered by needle into tissue, acting within seconds, on an animal it will eat and never see again.","Nothing pressed it toward a molecule safe at a steady dose for six months, able to survive a stomach, or careful with the one tissue a patient cannot lose.","Evolution screened for the predator's problem.","The patient's problem was never on the test."],["The practical form of that mismatch is the window.","Every drug has a range between the amount that helps and the amount that harms, and a compound's usefulness depends far more on the width of that range than on its strength at the low end.","Venom peptides tend to have narrow ones.","A molecule bred to be lethal at vanishingly small concentrations does not become gentle once purified and named.","Several candidates worked as designed in patients and were dropped anyway, because the dose that eased symptoms sat close to the dose that brought dizziness, confusion, or a fall in blood pressure."],["Against that, the record of approved drugs is short but real.","Captopril reached patients in 1981 as the first approved drug to block angiotensin converting enzyme.","It began with peptides in a Brazilian pit viper's venom that lowered blood pressure by prolonging a signaling molecule the enzyme destroys.","Chemists did not develop the peptide; they worked out how it met the enzyme, then built a small molecule that met it the same way and could be swallowed.","Eptifibatide, approved in 1998, is a seven-residue ring modeled on a rattlesnake venom protein that keeps platelets from clumping; tirofiban, cleared the same year, hits the same target with a synthetic molecule of similar shape.","Exenatide, approved in 2005, is a thirty-nine residue peptide from the saliva of the Gila monster; it lowers blood sugar through the same receptor as a human hormone whose natural half-life is about two minutes, and because the lizard version resists the enzyme that clips the human one, it lasts for hours."],["The purest case is ziconotide, a twenty-five residue peptide with three disulfide bonds, identical to one made by a fish-hunting cone snail.","It blocks a calcium channel on the spinal neurons that relay pain upward.","Approved in the United States in 2004, it eases pain when opioids have failed.","It is also close to unusable.","Swallowed, it is destroyed.","In a vein, it is filtered out by the kidney within minutes, and it could not cross into the central nervous system anyway.","The only route that works is continuous infusion through a catheter threaded into the fluid around the spinal cord, fed by a pump implanted under the skin.","Even then the window is narrow enough that the dose must be raised slowly over weeks."],["So the tally is roughly a dozen, most of them injected, and several not peptides at all but small molecules designed after a peptide showed the way.","Set that against the pool.","More than two hundred thousand animal species are thought to be venomous, and the number of distinct venom peptides has been put in the millions.","Well under one percent have ever been tested against a human target.","Both halves of that comparison get quoted, usually to argue that a breakthrough is near.","The same numbers read the other way: an enormous input has produced a small output, and the reasons are not mysterious."],["Most of those reasons concern getting the molecule where it must go.","A peptide taken by mouth meets stomach acid and then a wall of digestive enzymes, and whatever survives is too large and too polar to cross the gut lining.","Injection solves that and creates a second problem: small peptides slip through the filter in the kidney and are gone from the blood in minutes.","Targets inside the brain or spinal cord sit behind a barrier peptides do not cross.","Any foreign sequence may also be recognized by the immune system, which can neutralize the drug or attack the patient's own tissue.","None of these are failures of potency."],["There is a tool for each problem, which is why the field has not given up.","A peptide can be closed into a ring, hung with a chain of polyethylene glycol, or rebuilt from mirror-image amino acids, each change buying time against the enzymes or the kidney.","Each change also alters the surface that made the peptide selective, so the rebuilt molecule often has to prove itself again."],["It is worth being blunt about the framing that keeps the field oversold.","Calling venom a library is a metaphor doing a great deal of unearned work.","A library implies that the hard part, generating good candidates, is finished, and that what remains is retrieval.","What evolution supplied is a very large set of molecules optimized for an unrelated purpose, in which a handful happen to be useful and there is no index.","Counting sequences is not counting leads.","The gap between a peptide that exists and one that can be given to a patient is where the work lives, and being natural does not narrow it."],["The technical picture has changed far more than the clinical one.","For most of the twentieth century the limit was supply: a researcher milked animals, separated the pooled venom by chromatography, tested the fractions in a bioassay, and needed a great deal of raw material to isolate a usable quantity of one component.","Sequencing the venom gland changed that: reading the messenger RNA there yields the sequence of everything the animal is building, including peptides far too scarce to isolate by hand.","The bottleneck moved rather than vanished.","Thousands of sequences now arrive with no indication of what any of them binds, and testing one peptide against a panel of ion channels stays slow and costly even with automated instruments."],["The pain channel called Nav1.7 shows the pattern in miniature.","People born without a working copy feel no pain and are otherwise largely ordinary, which makes it about as well validated a target as exists.","Spider and scorpion venoms gave up peptides that block it with great potency, programs were funded, candidates entered trials, and the results have been repeatedly disappointing.","The channel may yet yield.","What it has not done is yield easily."],["The fair summary is neither the press release nor the dismissal.","Venom has given medicine a small number of genuinely useful drugs and a much larger number of research tools: a peptide that blocks one channel subtype and nothing else is how a physiologist proves what that subtype does.","Its most reliable contribution has been information rather than product: a demonstration that a protein can be hit selectively, which chemists can then pursue with molecules that behave better in a body.","Captopril is the template, not ziconotide.","The animal did not make a drug.","It made an argument that a drug was possible, and the rest of the work belongs to people."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p18-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, the only delivery route that works for ziconotide is","choices":["a tablet taken by mouth on a regular daily schedule","an injection into a vein, repeated several times through the day","a steady infusion into the fluid that bathes the spinal cord","a patch worn on the skin above the part of the body that hurts","a single injection into the muscle at the site of the pain"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":7,"quote":"continuous infusion through a catheter threaded into the fluid around the spinal cord"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":6,"quote":"it is filtered out by the kidney within minutes"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 walks through the routes that fail and lands on the one that works: a catheter threaded into the fluid around the spinal cord, fed by a pump implanted under the skin.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 9 says that swallowed, the peptide is destroyed.","The same paragraph rules a vein out, since the kidney clears the peptide within minutes and it cannot reach the central nervous system.",null,"No skin patch appears anywhere in the passage; the routes weighed are the mouth, a vein, and the space around the spinal cord.","A muscle injection is never mentioned, and any injection into the body still leaves the peptide facing the kidney and the barrier around the nervous system."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage says the peptide found in Gila monster saliva outlasts the human hormone it mimics because it","choices":["resists the enzyme that clips the human version","binds a different and considerably more durable receptor","is far smaller than the human hormone it stands in for","is taken by mouth rather than injected into the patient","carries three disulfide bonds that hold its shape together"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"the lizard version resists the enzyme that clips the human one"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"through the same receptor as a human hormone"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 gives the reason outright: the human hormone has a natural half-life of about two minutes, and the lizard version lasts for hours because it resists the enzyme that clips the human one.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 8 states that it works through the same receptor as the human hormone, not a different one.","Size is never offered as the reason, and the passage puts the peptide at thirty-nine residues without comparing it to the hormone.","Nothing says this drug is swallowed, and paragraph 10 notes that most venom-derived drugs are injected.","Three disulfide bonds belong to ziconotide in paragraph 9, not to the peptide from the lizard."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q03","type":"detail","stem":"As the passage describes it, the inhibitor cystine knot consists of","choices":["a single disulfide bond looping the two ends of the chain together","three disulfide bonds lying side by side along the outer surface of the peptide","a ring of cysteines that grips the ion channel directly","three disulfide bonds, one threaded through the ring made by the other two","a knot of hydrogen bonds formed between neighboring amino acids"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"one bond threads through the ring formed by the other two"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 defines the arrangement exactly: three disulfide bonds build a compact core, and one of them threads through the ring formed by the other two.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 describes three bonds in this arrangement, not one.","The number is right and the geometry is not, since one bond passes through the ring the other two make.","The cysteines hold the peptide's own shape in paragraph 3; the surface that meets a channel is described in paragraph 5.",null,"Paragraph 3 names disulfide bonds between cysteine residues, not hydrogen bonds."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage attributes the trouble chemists have had in targeting a single sodium channel subtype to","choices":["the small number of subtypes they have available to work with","the close similarity of the pockets that small molecules bind","the barrier that keeps drugs out of the brain and spinal cord","the speed with which the body clears small molecules from the blood","the immune response that a foreign molecule provokes in the patient"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":5,"sentence":4,"quote":"because the pockets they bind are so alike"}],"why":"Paragraph 5 says chemists have had limited success because the pockets those molecules bind are so alike from one subtype to the next.","distractorNotes":["Nine subtypes is a fact from paragraph 5, but their number is never treated as the obstacle.",null,"That barrier is a peptide delivery problem in paragraph 11, not the reason a small molecule cannot single out one subtype.","Rapid clearance in paragraph 11 applies to small peptides in the kidney, and it is a dosing problem rather than a targeting one.","Immune recognition appears in paragraph 11 as a hazard for foreign sequences, not as the reason subtypes are hard to tell apart."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, sequencing the venom gland made it possible to obtain","choices":["a finished drug with no further chemistry required of anyone","a reliable prediction of which channel each peptide will block","a short and manageable list of the most promising candidates","a way to test many peptides at once against a panel of channels","the sequences of peptides too scarce to isolate by hand"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"including peptides far too scarce to isolate by hand"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"with no indication of what any of them binds"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"stays slow and costly even with automated instruments"}],"why":"Paragraph 14 says that reading the gland's messenger RNA yields the sequence of everything the animal is building, including peptides far too scarce to isolate by hand.","distractorNotes":["The same paragraph says the bottleneck moved rather than vanished, and a sequence still has to be made and tested.","Paragraph 14 says the opposite, that thousands of sequences arrive with no indication of what any of them binds.","Sequencing produced thousands of candidates, which paragraph 14 presents as the new problem rather than a solution.","Testing against a panel of ion channels is called slow and costly in paragraph 14, even with automated instruments.",null]},{"id":"rc-p18-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 7, \"window\" most nearly means","choices":["the span between a dose that helps and a dose that harms","the period during which a drug stays active inside the body","the opening in a channel through which a peptide passes","the brief chance a research team has to publish a finding","the range of patients for whom a treatment has been approved"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":2,"quote":"a range between the amount that helps and the amount that harms"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"The practical form of that mismatch is the window."}],"why":"Paragraph 7 defines the term as it goes: every drug has a range between the amount that helps and the amount that harms, and venom peptides tend to have narrow ones.","distractorNotes":[null,"A stretch of time is the everyday sense of the word, but paragraph 7 is measuring amounts of drug rather than hours.","Paragraph 5 has peptides lying across a channel's outer face rather than passing through an opening, and paragraph 7 is about dosing.","Nothing in the passage concerns publishing; the paragraph is about the gap between a helpful dose and a harmful one.","Which patients may receive a drug never comes up in paragraph 7, which is about how much of it is safe to give."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 13, \"library\" most nearly means","choices":["a place where research findings are stored and looked up","an orderly record of every venom peptide yet reported","a ready stock of candidate molecules to be drawn on","a set of instructions an organism follows to build a protein","a collection of research tools kept on hand for teaching"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":3,"quote":"generating good candidates, is finished, and that what remains is retrieval"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"a library that evolution has already screened"}],"why":"Paragraph 13 takes the word apart as the field uses it: a library implies that good candidates have already been generated and that what remains is retrieval, which is what a waiting stock of compounds would mean.","distractorNotes":["That is the everyday sense, and the author's complaint is precisely that venom is not a place where usable answers sit waiting.","An orderly record is the part the author denies, since the same paragraph says there is no index.",null,"The passage never uses the word for genetic instructions; the metaphor concerns a supply of compounds.","Research tools do appear in paragraph 16, but the metaphor in paragraph 13 is about candidate drugs, not teaching."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The account of captopril and tirofiban in paragraph 8 most strongly supports which conclusion?","choices":["Most of the drugs that trace back to venom can be taken by mouth.","A venom molecule can lead to a medicine containing none of the venom.","Small molecules reach targets that venom peptides are unable to reach.","Peptides are on the whole safer for patients than synthetic molecules.","Copying a venom peptide exactly is the surest route to an approved drug."],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"Chemists did not develop the peptide"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":4,"quote":"built a small molecule that met it the same way and could be swallowed"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":5,"quote":"hits the same target with a synthetic molecule of similar shape"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":1,"quote":"most of them injected"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 says the chemists did not develop the pit viper peptide but built a small molecule that met the enzyme the same way, and tirofiban does the same at the platelet target, so the venom supplied the model rather than the product.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 10 says most of the approved drugs are injected.",null,"Tirofiban aims at the same target as the peptide-based drug beside it, so the two are not reaching different places.","Paragraph 7 gives venom peptides narrow windows and paragraph 11 lists the trouble they bring, so the passage does not treat them as the safer option.","Paragraph 8 makes the opposite point, since the chemists set the peptide aside and built something that could be swallowed."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 12 suggests that after a peptide has been closed into a ring to protect it from enzymes, a researcher would most likely have to","choices":["raise the dose to make up for the potency lost in the change","set the molecule aside, since a closed ring cannot bind a channel","repeat the gland sequencing to confirm the change was made","check whether it still tells its target from related proteins","move the drug from injection to some form taken by mouth"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"alters the surface that made the peptide selective"},{"paragraph":12,"sentence":3,"quote":"the rebuilt molecule often has to prove itself again"}],"why":"Paragraph 12 says each such change alters the surface that made the peptide selective, so the rebuilt molecule has to prove itself again, and paragraph 4 explains that this surface is what does the discriminating.","distractorNotes":["Lost potency is not what paragraph 12 warns about; the risk it names is lost selectivity.","That goes well past the passage, which presents ring closure as a tool that works and carries a cost worth checking.","Sequencing in paragraph 14 is a way of learning what an animal makes, not a way of confirming a chemical modification.",null,"Nothing in paragraph 12 promises that these changes let a peptide survive the gut, and paragraph 11 names more than one obstacle there."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["Venom research has absorbed decades of effort and should now give way to other approaches.","The chief obstacle to venom-derived medicine has always been the difficulty of collecting enough raw venom to work with.","Cone snails and spiders offer the most promising starting points for the drugs of the future.","Venom peptides matter chiefly because they showed that ion channels come in many related forms.","Venom offers unusually selective molecules, yet the gap between potency and medicine keeps the drug list short."],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"The premise is sound."},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":5,"quote":"The output is thin."},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"Binding tightly to a human channel is a laboratory result, not a therapy"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":6,"quote":"The gap between a peptide that exists and one that can be given to a patient"},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":4,"quote":"The bottleneck moved rather than vanished."}],"why":"The passage moves from why venom peptides are so selective, through why potency alone does not make a drug, to the short list of approvals and the reasons behind it, which is what this choice says.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 16 refuses that dismissal and credits venom with real drugs and a larger number of research tools.","Paragraph 14 says supply was the old limit and that gland sequencing removed it, so the current bottleneck lies elsewhere.","Cone snails and spiders appear as examples, and the passage never ranks sources by how promising they are.","Paragraph 16 gives research tools a supporting role, and the point there is that a peptide proves what a subtype does.",null]},{"id":"rc-p18-q11","type":"function","stem":"The author's main purpose in describing ziconotide at length is to","choices":["show a peptide that works as intended and is still barely usable","argue that opioids ought to be replaced with peptides wherever that is possible","demonstrate that cone snail venom is the most potent venom yet measured","illustrate how quickly gland sequencing can turn a peptide into a medicine","explain why disulfide bonds leave a peptide too rigid to act on a channel"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"it eases pain when opioids have failed"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"It is also close to unusable."},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":4,"quote":"Captopril is the template, not ziconotide."}],"why":"Paragraph 9 grants that the drug eases pain when opioids have failed, then lists everything that makes it close to unusable, which makes it the clearest case of the gap the passage is built around.","distractorNotes":[null,"Paragraph 9 mentions opioids only to say who the drug is for, and it makes no recommendation about replacing them.","The passage never ranks venoms by strength, and potency is not why this drug is featured.","Gland sequencing belongs to paragraph 14, and ziconotide is presented as a problem case rather than a quick success.","Paragraph 3 presents disulfide bonds as what makes a peptide durable, and ziconotide blocks its channel perfectly well."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["A history of one drug, followed by predictions about several others to come","A popular claim, the biology behind it, the obstacles and the record, a revised verdict","A list of unsolved problems, each paired with a solution already in use today","A comparison of two competing schools of thought, with the evidence for each weighed in turn","A summary of laboratory methods, followed by instructions for improving on them"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":1,"quote":"Every few years a headline announces"},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"That rigid core explains the selectivity"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":2,"quote":"where nearly every venom program has died"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":1,"quote":"the record of approved drugs is short but real"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"The fair summary is neither the press release nor the dismissal."}],"why":"Paragraph 1 opens with the familiar claim, paragraphs 2 through 5 explain why the peptides are so selective, the middle paragraphs turn to what stands in the way and what has actually been approved, and paragraph 16 delivers a reworked judgment.","distractorNotes":["Captopril and ziconotide are examples along the way, and the passage makes no predictions about drugs to come.",null,"Paragraph 12 does pair tools with problems, but that is one paragraph, and most of what the passage raises goes unsolved.","No two camps are set against each other; the author is arguing with one widely repeated framing.","Methods appear only in paragraph 14, and the passage never tells researchers how to do their work."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q13","type":"tone","stem":"The author's attitude toward the claim that evolution has already screened venom for useful drugs is best described as","choices":["openly contemptuous of the researchers who reach for the phrase","untroubled, since the phrase captures the situation well enough","bluntly skeptical of a phrase claiming more than it earns","puzzled by a comparison whose meaning is difficult to make out","enthusiastic about the promise the phrase is meant to convey"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":2,"quote":"a metaphor doing a great deal of unearned work"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":1,"quote":"the framing that keeps the field oversold"},{"paragraph":1,"sentence":4,"quote":"The premise is sound."}],"why":"Paragraph 13 calls the metaphor a great deal of unearned work and says counting sequences is not counting leads, which is criticism of the phrase rather than of the science or the people using it.","distractorNotes":["The criticism falls on the framing, not the people, and paragraph 16 credits the field with real results.","Paragraph 13 is built entirely on the objection that the phrase is misleading.",null,"The author states plainly what the phrase implies and why that implication is wrong, so nothing here is unclear.","Paragraph 1 reports that enthusiasm as something other people express, and paragraph 13 pushes back against it."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q14","type":"tone","stem":"In the final paragraph, the author's assessment of what venom has given medicine is","choices":["confident that a major new drug is now close at hand","resigned to the view that nothing further will come of the work","nostalgic for the days of milking animals and running bioassays","defensive about the money and effort the field has already absorbed","measured, crediting real gains and leaving the rest to chemists"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":16,"sentence":1,"quote":"neither the press release nor the dismissal"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":2,"quote":"a small number of genuinely useful drugs"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":3,"quote":"which chemists can then pursue with molecules that behave better in a body"},{"paragraph":16,"sentence":6,"quote":"the rest of the work belongs to people"}],"why":"Paragraph 16 sets aside both the press release and the dismissal, credits venom with useful drugs and research tools, and ends by saying the remaining work belongs to people.","distractorNotes":["The paragraph makes no prediction of a breakthrough and explicitly sets the press release aside.","The same sentence sets the dismissal aside, and the paragraph goes on to list genuine contributions.","Paragraph 14 treats milking and bioassays as a limit that was overcome, with no fondness attached.","Cost never comes up anywhere in the passage, and the author is not defending anyone.",null]},{"id":"rc-p18-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage names all of the following as obstacles to using a venom peptide as a medicine EXCEPT","choices":["destruction by acid and enzymes along the digestive tract","rapid removal from the blood by the filter in the kidney","the barrier that surrounds the brain and the spinal cord","the difficulty of working out a peptide's sequence","recognition of the peptide by the patient's immune system"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":2,"quote":"meets stomach acid and then a wall of digestive enzymes","choice":0},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"slip through the filter in the kidney and are gone from the blood in minutes","choice":1},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":4,"quote":"sit behind a barrier peptides do not cross","choice":2},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":5,"quote":"recognized by the immune system","choice":4},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":3,"quote":"yields the sequence of everything the animal is building","choice":3},{"paragraph":14,"sentence":5,"quote":"Thousands of sequences now arrive","choice":3}],"why":"Paragraph 11 lists digestion, kidney clearance, the barrier around the nervous system, and immune recognition, while paragraph 14 says sequence is now the easy part, since reading the gland's messenger RNA supplies it.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 11 opens with stomach acid and a wall of digestive enzymes.","Paragraph 11 says small peptides slip through the kidney's filter and are gone from the blood in minutes.","Paragraph 11 says targets in the brain or spinal cord sit behind a barrier peptides do not cross.",null,"Paragraph 11 warns that a foreign sequence may be recognized by the immune system."]},{"id":"rc-p18-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader looking for the year a venom-derived treatment for blood sugar was approved should turn to","choices":["paragraph 2","paragraph 5","paragraph 8","paragraph 11","paragraph 14"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"Exenatide, approved in 2005"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"it lowers blood sugar"}],"why":"Paragraph 8 is the roll call of approved drugs with their years, and it places the thirty-nine residue peptide from Gila monster saliva at 2005.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 2 describes what a venom contains, with cone snails as its example.","Paragraph 5 is about sodium channel subtypes and why they are hard to tell apart.",null,"Paragraph 11 lists the delivery problems peptides face and names no drug at all.","Paragraph 14 covers how venom peptides are found, from milking animals to sequencing the gland."]}]},"rc-p19":{"id":"rc-p19","title":"Why Anaesthesia Still Lacks an Explanation","topic":"the unsettled mechanism of general anaesthesia","field":"neuroscience","source":"migrated:acethedat-prep","words":1701,"paragraphs":[["Every year tens of millions of people lie down in operating rooms, receive a drug, lose consciousness within seconds, remain still while their bodies are opened, and wake afterward with nothing to report about the interval.","The procedure is also remarkably safe, since death attributable to the anaesthetic itself is now counted in cases per hundreds of thousands.","What is strange about this success is that no one can say, in the way a pharmacologist can say how a blood pressure drug works, what general anaesthesia is at the level of molecules.","The state is defined by what it prevents rather than by what it does."],["The first serious answer arrived at the turn of the twentieth century.","Working separately, Hans Horst Meyer and Charles Ernest Overton noticed that if one lines up the anaesthetic agents then available and measures how readily each dissolves in olive oil, that ranking predicts almost exactly how little of each is needed to put an animal under.","The relationship held across more than four orders of magnitude of potency.","A gas that barely dissolves in oil, such as nitrous oxide, has to be given at enormous concentrations.","An agent that dissolves greedily works at a whisper."],["The inference seemed forced upon them.","If oil solubility predicts potency, the drug must be working somewhere oily, and the oiliest structure in a neuron is the lipid membrane wrapped around it.","The idea hardened into a single unified account: anaesthetic molecules dissolve into the bilayer, swell or disorder it, and the ion channels embedded there stop working properly.","One observation appeared to seal the case.","Animals anaesthetized and then held under helium at roughly a hundred atmospheres begin to move again, as though the pressure were squeezing the membrane back toward its original volume.","One site, one mechanism, no receptors required."],["The account began to fail on its own terms.","The first problem was quantitative.","Warming a membrane by a single degree disorders the bilayer more than a clinical dose of anaesthetic does, yet a mildly feverish patient does not fall asleep.","The second problem was geometric.","Isoflurane exists as two mirror image forms, identical in every property a bath of oil could distinguish, including solubility.","In animals the two differ in potency, one being roughly twice as effective as the other.","Oil cannot tell left from right.","Something with a handed shape, which inside a cell means a protein, must be reading the molecule."],["Then came the compounds that refused to cooperate.","Chemists built halogenated molecules whose oil solubility predicted that they ought to be powerful anaesthetics, and several of them are not.","An animal breathing one of these still withdraws from a surgical stimulus at concentrations far above the predicted threshold.","These so called non-immobilizers are not inactive, and several of them impair an animal's memory for what happened while it breathed them.","A compound that erases the record of an event without abolishing the response to it is by itself an argument that the old correlation was never a law of nature."],["The gentlest blow was the most damaging.","Firefly luciferase, the enzyme that makes a firefly glow, is a soluble protein floating in water with no membrane anywhere near it.","Anaesthetics switch it off, and they do so in nearly the order of their clinical potency.","If a lone protein in a test tube reproduces the century old correlation, the correlation never pointed at lipid in particular.","It pointed instead at a general fact about these molecules.","They are small, greasy and weakly sticky, and they lodge in any pocket that is oily on the inside, whether in a bilayer or in the folded interior of a protein.","After that, the lipid theory lost its purchase."],["The field turned to proteins and found too many of them.","Propofol, etomidate, the barbiturates and the volatile ethers all enhance the GABA type A receptor, the chloride channel that carries most of the brain's inhibition, so that each pulse of inhibition lasts longer than it should.","Glycine receptors, which do similar work in the cord, are enhanced too.","Ketamine, nitrous oxide and xenon leave GABA largely alone and instead block the NMDA glutamate receptor, cutting excitation rather than boosting inhibition.","Volatile agents also open two pore domain potassium channels, which make a neuron harder to fire, and they suppress the pacemaker channels that keep thalamic cells rhythmic.","Mice lacking one of those channels need more agent than their littermates, yet they can still be anaesthetized."],["Set the drugs side by side and the problem is visible at a glance.","Xenon is a single atom with a full outer shell that forms almost no chemical bonds, and it is a complete anaesthetic.","Nitrous oxide is three atoms in a line.","Propofol is an oily phenol delivered as a white emulsion.","Etomidate is a small ring compound, alphaxalone is a steroid, and the volatile agents are short carbon chains hung with fluorine and chlorine.","In any other drug class the list would be absurd, since molecules that act at one receptor usually share a recognizable shape.","These share almost nothing beyond a mild reluctance to stay dissolved in water."],["Depth, a word used constantly in the operating room, is a metaphor doing a great deal of work.","Its most precise definition is a number called minimum alveolar concentration, or MAC: the concentration of an inhaled agent in the lungs at which half of patients will not move when the skin is cut.","For isoflurane that is a little over one percent, for sevoflurane about two percent, for desflurane about six, and for xenon roughly two thirds of the breath.","For nitrous oxide it is slightly more than a whole atmosphere, which is why that gas cannot carry an anaesthetic by itself.","MAC is both useful and blunt.","It describes a population rather than the patient on the table, and the only thing it measures is whether a body moves."],["That last point matters.","Whether a body moves is decided largely below the neck.","In classic animal preparations where the blood supply to the brain and to the rest of the body could be separated, delivering the agent to the brain alone took far more of it to stop movement, and animals whose cords had been cut off from the brain needed about the same concentration as intact ones.","Immobility is mostly a spinal effect.","Unresponsiveness to speech, the thing a patient would call being under, depends on the loops between thalamus and cortex and appears at perhaps a third to a half of MAC.","Amnesia appears earlier still, at something like a quarter of MAC, and leans on the hippocampus and the amygdala.","Three effects, three doses, three places."],["General anaesthesia, then, is not one state but a bundle of them, assembled in practice out of several drugs at once: one for unconsciousness, an opioid for the reflex responses to injury, and often a muscle relaxant for stillness.","The relaxant is the awkward member of the set.","It paralyzes the patient and so removes the very sign, movement, on which the standard measure of depth was built."],["This is why monitoring depth is harder than it looks.","No instrument reads experience.","The available monitors take the electroencephalogram from a few forehead electrodes, compress it through an algorithm fitted to recordings from patients given particular drugs, and return one number between zero and one hundred.","Those numbers behave well for propofol and the ethers and badly for the rest.","Ketamine, nitrous oxide and xenon can drive the index upward, toward the appearance of wakefulness, in a patient who is entirely unresponsive.","The raw trace is more honest and less convenient.","Propofol produces large slow waves and a band of alpha rhythm that migrates to the front of the head, and at deeper levels the trace breaks into bursts separated by silence."],["One old technique makes the difficulty concrete.","Before the muscle relaxant is given, a cuff is inflated on the upper arm so the drug never reaches the hand, and the anaesthetist can then ask the patient to squeeze.","A minority of patients squeeze on command during surgery, and almost none of them remember anything afterward.","Whether such a patient is conscious in any sense that ought to trouble us is not something a monitor can settle, and honest disagreement about it runs through the literature.","Reports of awareness with recall are rare, on the order of one case in twenty thousand when the count relies on patients volunteering the experience, and perhaps twenty or thirty times higher when every patient is asked directly with a structured questionnaire.","The distance between those two figures is itself a finding."],["Why has a mechanism been so slow to arrive?","Partly because the targets are redundant.","Delete one of them in a mouse and the anaesthetic usually still works, shifted a little along the dose axis, which is what one expects of a drug that binds many things weakly.","There is a celebrated exception.","Etomidate acts at a pocket in one GABA type A subunit, and changing a single amino acid in that pocket leaves mice the drug can no longer keep still.","That is as clean a result as this field has produced, and it holds for one drug rather than for the state.","A further puzzle is that waking is not induction played backwards, since animals emerge at lower concentrations than were needed to put them under."],["None of this unsettles the practice, and it should not.","Anaesthesia is a recipe that works, refined over a century and a half by watching patients rather than by deducing consequences from a theory, and the refinement has been extraordinarily successful.","Medicine has often run this way: lithium and aspirin were in confident use long before anyone could say why they worked.","The incompleteness is a research program rather than a warning.","The situation does have a particular flavor.","The field has learned to produce on demand, and by the millions, a condition it cannot yet define, and the most economical description of what these drugs do is still a list of the things a patient cannot do while they are in the blood."]],"questions":[{"id":"rc-p19-q01","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, what did Meyer and Overton find when they compared the anaesthetic agents available in their day?","choices":["Agents that dissolved most readily in water were the ones that could be given to an animal in the smallest doses","How readily an agent dissolved in olive oil predicted how little of it was needed to anaesthetize an animal","Heavier molecules were consistently more potent than lighter ones","Every agent worked by binding the same receptor in the nerve membrane","The agents varied so widely that no single ordering of them could be found"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":2,"sentence":2,"quote":"how readily each dissolves in olive oil, that ranking predicts almost exactly how little"}],"why":"Paragraph 2 states it directly: ranking the agents by how readily each dissolves in olive oil predicts almost exactly how little of each is needed. The measurement was solubility in oil, and the finding was a prediction of potency.","distractorNotes":["The passage names olive oil, not water, and it later calls these molecules reluctant to stay dissolved in water.",null,"Molecular weight never enters the comparison; the only property measured was solubility.","The lipid account that grew out of the finding was attractive precisely because it needed no receptor at all.","The opposite is stated. The ranking held across more than four orders of magnitude of potency."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q02","type":"detail","stem":"The passage reports that anaesthetized animals held under helium at roughly a hundred atmospheres","choices":["stopped breathing and had to be supported by a ventilator","needed a far larger dose of agent for the rest of the experiment","showed no measurable change of any kind","began to move again, which was read as pressure restoring the membrane's volume","lost the response to a surgical stimulus that they had still shown a moment earlier"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":3,"sentence":5,"quote":"begin to move again, as though the pressure were squeezing the membrane back"}],"why":"Paragraph 3 gives this as the observation that seemed to seal the lipid case: under high pressure the animals begin to move again, as though the membrane were being squeezed back toward its original volume.","distractorNotes":["Breathing is not what the passage describes. The reported change was the return of movement.","No later dosing is mentioned. The observation was about what the pressure did at the time.","The whole force of the result was that something did change, which is why it looked like proof.",null,"This runs the effect backwards. Pressure brought movement back rather than taking a response away."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q03","type":"detail","stem":"According to paragraph 9, which agent has a minimum alveolar concentration greater than a whole atmosphere?","choices":["isoflurane","sevoflurane","desflurane","the noble gas xenon","nitrous oxide"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":4,"quote":"For nitrous oxide it is slightly more than a whole atmosphere"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":3,"quote":"for xenon roughly two thirds of the breath"}],"why":"Paragraph 9 says the MAC of nitrous oxide is slightly more than a whole atmosphere, which is the reason that gas cannot carry an anaesthetic by itself.","distractorNotes":["Isoflurane is a little over one percent, the smallest figure in the list.","Sevoflurane is about two percent, far below a full atmosphere.","Desflurane is about six percent, still a small fraction of the breath.","Xenon is roughly two thirds of the breath, high but under one atmosphere.",null]},{"id":"rc-p19-q04","type":"detail","stem":"The passage groups ketamine, nitrous oxide and xenon together because these three","choices":["block a glutamate receptor instead of strengthening inhibition","make each pulse of the brain's inhibition last longer than it should","act on the glycine receptors of the spinal cord","open potassium channels that make a neuron harder to fire","are the only agents that can be used without a muscle relaxant"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":7,"sentence":4,"quote":"instead block the NMDA glutamate receptor, cutting excitation rather than boosting inhibition"}],"why":"Paragraph 7 sets these three apart from the rest: they leave GABA largely alone and block the NMDA glutamate receptor, cutting excitation rather than boosting inhibition.","distractorNotes":[null,"That is what the GABA type A group does, and the passage contrasts these three against that group.","Glycine receptors appear in the same paragraph, but they are enhanced by the other group of drugs.","Two pore domain potassium channels are attributed to the volatile agents, not to these three.","The passage never connects any drug to doing without a relaxant."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q05","type":"detail","stem":"According to the passage, unresponsiveness to speech appears at roughly what fraction of MAC?","choices":["about a quarter","about one tenth","somewhere between a third and a half","the full value of MAC and nothing less","roughly twice the value of MAC"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":5,"quote":"appears at perhaps a third to a half of MAC"},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":6,"quote":"Amnesia appears earlier still, at something like a quarter of MAC"}],"why":"Paragraph 10 places the three effects on a dose ladder. Unresponsiveness to speech appears at perhaps a third to a half of MAC, above amnesia and below immobility.","distractorNotes":["A quarter of MAC is where amnesia appears, one rung lower on the same ladder.","No figure that low is given for any of the three effects.",null,"Full MAC is the immobility figure, the concentration at which half of patients do not move.","Nothing in the passage puts any of the three effects above MAC."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q06","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 6, the statement that the lipid theory lost its purchase most nearly means that it lost its","choices":["commercial backing from the companies that made the drugs","physical grip on the membranes it described","place in the historical record of the field","power to explain what the evidence showed","supporters among the chemists who first proposed it"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"the correlation never pointed at lipid in particular"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":7,"quote":"the lipid theory lost its purchase"}],"why":"The sentence closes a paragraph about what the old correlation could and could not account for. Purchase here is the hold an idea has on the evidence, so losing it means losing explanatory power.","distractorNotes":["Purchase can mean something bought, but no money appears anywhere in this paragraph.","A theory is an idea and not an object, and what slipped was its hold on the evidence.","The passage keeps discussing the theory afterward, so it was not erased from the record.",null,"Who supported the theory is never the subject here. What it could account for is."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q07","type":"vocab","stem":"As used in paragraph 9, \"blunt\" most nearly means","choices":["worn down by repeated use","incapable of fine distinctions","plainly and even harshly worded","slow to take effect in the body","not sharp enough to cut tissue"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":9,"sentence":6,"quote":"It describes a population rather than the patient on the table"},{"paragraph":9,"sentence":6,"quote":"the only thing it measures is whether a body moves"}],"why":"The next sentence in paragraph 9 explains the word: MAC describes a population rather than the patient on the table, and it measures only whether a body moves. That is a measure too coarse to separate cases.","distractorNotes":["Nothing suggests the measure has eroded over time. It is coarse from the start.",null,"That is the everyday sense of blunt applied to speech, and here the word is judging a measurement.","Speed of onset is not what the sentence weighs. It weighs how much MAC can tell you.","The literal edge of a blade has no place here, since the word describes a criterion."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q08","type":"inference","stem":"The firefly luciferase result best supports which conclusion about the old solubility correlation?","choices":["It rested on measurements that later turned out to be mistaken","It applies only to agents that are gases at room temperature","It shows that enzymes rather than ion channels are the real targets","It holds for proteins in a test tube but breaks down in the tissue of a living animal","It fits protein binding just as well, so it cannot single out lipid"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":6,"sentence":4,"quote":"the correlation never pointed at lipid in particular"},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":6,"quote":"whether in a bilayer or in the folded interior of a protein"}],"why":"Paragraph 6 draws exactly this lesson. A protein with no membrane near it reproduces the correlation, so the correlation was never evidence for lipid in particular, only for these molecules lodging in any oily pocket.","distractorNotes":["The passage never doubts the measurements. It doubts what they were pointing at.","Nothing restricts the correlation to gases, and propofol is described as an oily liquid.","Luciferase is used as a demonstration, not offered as a target in the brain.","The passage does not set the test tube against the animal here. It uses the test tube to reread the correlation.",null]},{"id":"rc-p19-q09","type":"inference","stem":"Paragraph 11 suggests that a paralyzed patient creates a particular difficulty because","choices":["the sign that the standard measure of depth depends on can no longer be seen","muscle relaxants lower the concentration of agent that a patient turns out to need","relaxants are known to erase the memory of the procedure","the opioid in the mixture becomes unnecessary once movement has stopped","paralysis makes the electroencephalogram impossible to record"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":11,"sentence":3,"quote":"removes the very sign, movement, on which the standard measure of depth was built"}],"why":"Paragraph 11 links two facts: MAC is built on movement, and the relaxant removes movement. With the sign gone, the standard measure has nothing left to read.","distractorNotes":[null,"The passage says nothing about relaxants changing how much agent is required.","Amnesia is credited to the anaesthetic at low doses, not to the relaxant.","Each drug in the bundle keeps its own job, and the opioid's job is the reflex response to injury.","The monitors described next read the brain's electrical signal, which paralysis does not silence."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q10","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which statement best expresses the central point of the passage?","choices":["The lipid membrane theory has been replaced by a settled account built on one protein target","Anaesthesia carries hidden dangers that patients are never told about, and the profession has been slow to admit it","Anaesthesia works safely at scale yet is not fully understood, with many targets and effects that come apart","The main obstacle to understanding anaesthesia is that operating room monitors are not accurate enough","Because different drugs act at different sites, anaesthetists ought to give only one agent at a time"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"what general anaesthesia is at the level of molecules"},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"The field turned to proteins and found too many of them."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":7,"quote":"Three effects, three doses, three places."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":6,"quote":"a condition it cannot yet define"}],"why":"Every section of the passage feeds this claim: the safety record in paragraph 1, the collapse of one theory, the crowd of protein targets, and the separation of immobility, unconsciousness and amnesia, closing with a procedure that works without being defined.","distractorNotes":["Half right. The lipid theory did fall, but the passage insists that no single settled target replaced it.","The passage says the opposite about safety and treats the incompleteness as ordinary rather than concealed.",null,"Monitoring is one section of the argument rather than the argument. The trouble starts with the drugs.","The passage treats the multi drug bundle as normal practice and never recommends dropping it."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q11","type":"function","stem":"The author lists xenon, nitrous oxide, propofol, etomidate, alphaxalone and the volatile agents in paragraph 8 mainly to","choices":["rank the agents by how safe each one is in practice","show that molecules with nothing structurally in common produce the same state","explain why the volatile agents became the most widely used members of the group","argue that only one drug on the list is a true anaesthetic","demonstrate that every agent named acts at the GABA type A receptor"],"key":1,"proof":[{"paragraph":8,"sentence":6,"quote":"molecules that act at one receptor usually share a recognizable shape"},{"paragraph":8,"sentence":7,"quote":"These share almost nothing beyond a mild reluctance to stay dissolved in water."}],"why":"Paragraph 8 ends by noting that in any other drug class such a list would be absurd, because molecules acting at one receptor usually share a shape. The list exists to make the chemical variety impossible to miss.","distractorNotes":["Safety appears in the first and last paragraphs, and no ranking of these drugs is given anywhere.",null,"Popularity is never the subject. The paragraph is about chemical variety.","Xenon is called a complete anaesthetic in this very paragraph, so no member is being disqualified.","Paragraph 7 sorts the drugs by target and places ketamine, nitrous oxide and xenon somewhere else."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q12","type":"mainidea","stem":"Which best describes how the passage is organized?","choices":["A puzzle is posed, one explanation rises and collapses, a messier replacement is laid out, and the consequences are weighed","Two rival drugs are compared point by point and one is judged the better choice","A single patient's case is described and then generalized to the whole of surgery","A chronological list of discoveries is given with no argument running through it","A problem in the operating room is described in detail and then a particular piece of monitoring equipment is recommended to solve it"],"key":0,"proof":[{"paragraph":1,"sentence":3,"quote":"What is strange about this success"},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":6,"quote":"One site, one mechanism, no receptors required."},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":1,"quote":"The account began to fail on its own terms."},{"paragraph":7,"sentence":1,"quote":"The field turned to proteins and found too many of them."},{"paragraph":11,"sentence":1,"quote":"not one state but a bundle of them"}],"why":"Paragraph 1 poses the puzzle, paragraphs 2 and 3 build the lipid account, paragraphs 4 through 6 dismantle it, paragraphs 7 through 13 lay out the crowded replacement, and paragraphs 14 and 15 weigh what the gap means.","distractorNotes":[null,"No two drugs are set against each other as choices. The drugs are grouped by target and by chemistry.","There is no case history anywhere in the passage.","The dates do run forward, but each step is used as evidence in an argument about mechanism.","The monitors are described and criticized, and no particular device is endorsed."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q13","type":"tone","stem":"In the final paragraph, the author's attitude toward the gap between practice and explanation is best described as","choices":["alarmed at what the gap means for patients","dismissive of the research that seeks to close it","indifferent to whether patients are safe","untroubled by it and curious about what remains","triumphant about how much has been settled"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":15,"sentence":1,"quote":"None of this unsettles the practice, and it should not."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":4,"quote":"The incompleteness is a research program rather than a warning."},{"paragraph":15,"sentence":5,"quote":"The situation does have a particular flavor."}],"why":"Paragraph 15 opens by saying none of this unsettles the practice and calls the incompleteness a research program rather than a warning, while still pointing out that the field cannot yet define what it produces.","distractorNotes":["Alarm is ruled out by the first line of the paragraph, which says the practice is not unsettled.","Calling the gap a research program endorses the work rather than waving it off.","Safety is the first thing the paragraph credits, so indifference does not fit.",null,"The paragraph ends on what the field still cannot define, which is not a victory lap."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q14","type":"tone","stem":"The author's handling of the two awareness figures in paragraph 13 is best described as","choices":["confident that the lower figure is the accurate one","convinced that the higher figure proves patients are routinely awake and aware during surgery","impatient with colleagues who keep raising the question","certain that the difference is a simple error of record keeping","deliberate about leaving the disagreement open and treating the gap as informative"],"key":4,"proof":[{"paragraph":13,"sentence":4,"quote":"honest disagreement about it runs through the literature"},{"paragraph":13,"sentence":6,"quote":"The distance between those two figures is itself a finding."}],"why":"Paragraph 13 gives both numbers, says honest disagreement about the question runs through the literature, and then calls the distance between the two figures a finding in its own right.","distractorNotes":["Both numbers are given without either being endorsed, and the disagreement is called honest.","Almost none of the patients who respond remember anything, and the author declines to say what that amounts to.","No irritation toward anyone appears here. The open question is presented as worth having.","The gap is called a finding, which makes it informative rather than a mistake in the records.",null]},{"id":"rc-p19-q15","type":"except","stem":"The passage presents all of the following as trouble for the lipid membrane theory EXCEPT","choices":["a single degree of warming disorders a bilayer more than a clinical dose does","two mirror image forms of one agent differ in potency","pressure restores movement in an animal that has been anaesthetized","a soluble enzyme with no membrane is switched off in the order of clinical potency","some highly oil soluble compounds fail to keep an animal from moving"],"key":2,"proof":[{"paragraph":4,"sentence":3,"quote":"Warming a membrane by a single degree disorders the bilayer more than a clinical dose","choice":0},{"paragraph":4,"sentence":6,"quote":"the two differ in potency, one being roughly twice as effective","choice":1},{"paragraph":6,"sentence":3,"quote":"they do so in nearly the order of their clinical potency","choice":3},{"paragraph":5,"sentence":2,"quote":"ought to be powerful anaesthetics, and several of them are not","choice":4},{"paragraph":3,"sentence":4,"quote":"One observation appeared to seal the case.","choice":2}],"why":"Pressure reversal is the one item on this list that the passage offers as support for the lipid theory, in paragraph 3, not as evidence against it. The other four all appear in paragraphs 4 through 6 as failures.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 4 gives this as the first quantitative failure of the theory.","Paragraph 4 gives this as the geometric failure, since oil cannot tell one mirror form from the other.",null,"Paragraph 6 uses luciferase to show that the correlation never pointed at lipid in particular.","Paragraph 5 gives the non-immobilizers, whose oil solubility predicts a potency they do not have."]},{"id":"rc-p19-q16","type":"detail","stem":"A reader who wanted to find out which part of the nervous system decides whether a surgical patient moves should turn to","choices":["paragraph 3","paragraph 7","paragraph 9","paragraph 10","paragraph 14"],"key":3,"proof":[{"paragraph":10,"sentence":2,"quote":"Whether a body moves is decided largely below the neck."},{"paragraph":10,"sentence":4,"quote":"Immobility is mostly a spinal effect."}],"why":"Paragraph 10 is where the three effects are assigned to places. It says immobility is mostly a spinal effect and describes the preparations that separated brain from cord.","distractorNotes":["Paragraph 3 covers the lipid theory and the pressure observation.","Paragraph 7 lists molecular targets rather than regions of the nervous system.","Paragraph 9 defines MAC by whether a body moves, but it never says where that decision is made.",null,"Paragraph 14 is about redundant targets and the etomidate exception."]}]}},"order":["rc-o01","rc-o02","rc-o03","rc-o04","rc-o05","rc-o06","rc-p01","rc-p02","rc-p03","rc-p04","rc-p05","rc-p06","rc-p07","rc-p08","rc-p09","rc-p10","rc-p11","rc-p12","rc-p13","rc-p14","rc-p15","rc-p16","rc-p17","rc-p18","rc-p19"],"sections":[{"id":"rc-s1","title":"Altitude, Stripes, Deep-Sea Light","passages":["rc-o01","rc-o06","rc-p03"]},{"id":"rc-s2","title":"Mirror Molecules, Wolves, Prions","passages":["rc-o04","rc-o02","rc-p16"]},{"id":"rc-s3","title":"Phages, Insulin, Nitrogen","passages":["rc-o05","rc-o03","rc-p13"]},{"id":"rc-s4","title":"Tardigrades, Batteries, Radiocarbon","passages":["rc-p01","rc-p09","rc-p15"]},{"id":"rc-s5","title":"Pacemakers, Ash, Gene Editing","passages":["rc-p12","rc-p05","rc-p11"]},{"id":"rc-s6","title":"Fungal Trade, Concrete, Sleep","passages":["rc-p10","rc-p02","rc-p08"]},{"id":"rc-s7","title":"Resistance Genes, Ice Cores, Venom","passages":["rc-p04","rc-p17","rc-p18"]},{"id":"rc-s8","title":"Plaque, Shells, Anaesthesia","passages":["rc-p06","rc-p07","rc-p19"]}]};
